\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s a fraught moment\u8217\'20\'19: Omicron puts brakes on US return-to-office plans\b0\par\par\uc2 Melody Schreiber \par \uc2 916 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2021\par 17:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employers are pausing efforts to call remote workers back in amid a renewed push for strikes and unionization\par \par \uc2 Large US companies are now pulling back on plans to return to in-person work in light of the Omicron variant\u8217\'20\'19s rapid spread across America.\par \par \uc2 Employers planning to call remote workers back into the office in the new year are now pausing those efforts, and they are wary of setting new return dates only to push them back once again in the face of continued uncertainty and risks from the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic is also driving changes in how in-person employees work, with a renewed push for strikes and unionization across several industries where workers have frequently faced long hours and unsafe conditions.\par \par \uc2 Alphabet\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Google\uc2 , Meta, \uc2 Apple\uc2 , Uber, \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , \uc2 Ford\uc2 , \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , \uc2 DocuSign\uc2  and \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  are among the companies that have delayed returns to the office.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s warranted, given the uptick that we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen in cases,\u8221\'20\'1D Bradford Bell, director of the Center for Advanced Human Resource Studies at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ILR School, said. The delays also depend on where the offices are located, he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re very much looking at this on a location-by-location basis.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Apple CEO \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  told employees in a memo on Wednesday that the partial return to offices, planned for 1 February, was now pushed to ??a \u8220\'20\'1Cdate yet to be determined\u8221\'20\'1D, after several other delays over the past two years. Cook offered employees a $1,000 bonus to help improve their home offices.\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s offices remain open and employees are still going in to work in countries with lower Covid rates, he said. But he specifically pointed to the global rise in cases and the emergence of the Omicron variant as a cause for concern.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  and Uber were among the first to announce the indefinite delays in early December after planning for early January returns. \uc2 Google\uc2  planned to reopen offices fully no later than 10 January, but has now delayed that deadline indefinitely, though offices in several places have already reopened.\par \par \uc2 Meta was planning to open early in 2022 but is now allowing workers to come back as late as June. Janelle Gale, the company\u8217\'20\'19s vice-president of human resources, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/coronavirus-pandemic-business-lifestyle-health-937d4c71e44781cf1d25ce6b56141eb0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Csome aren\u8217\'20\'19t quite ready to come back\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  went fully {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2020/10/01/twitter-work-from-home/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 remote forever}}}\uc2  in October.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s offices will open in February, but workers can stay remote for the entirety of next year \u8211\'20\'13 one of the longest delayed returns, partly due to worries over Omicron and other variants that may emerge.\par \par \uc2 Fidelity Investments\uc2  said on Monday it was pausing its return-to-work plans. \uc2 DocuSign\uc2  has delayed its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/12/11/business/return-to-office-2022.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fourth attempt}}}\uc2  to return.\par \par \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s corporate employees were going back to the office in 2022 in a hybrid approach, but now the company is waiting to see what the new plan should be.\par \par \uc2 Ford\uc2  planned to return with a hybrid work model for employees working at the headquarters in January, but that is now planned for March.\par \par \uc2 Notably, hourly employees at \uc2 Ford\uc2  returned to in-person work in May 2020. And gig workers at Uber, \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  and elsewhere have continued working through the pandemic, despite frequently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2021/12/08/the-state-of-gig-work-in-2021/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 feeling unsafe}}}\uc2  on the job.\par \par \uc2 About half of full-time workers surveyed in the US said their jobs could be done remotely, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gallup.com/workplace/357779/bet-desks-empty.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according}}}\uc2  to a Gallup poll. And nearly one-third of the workers said they never wanted to work in the office; the majority wanted a hybrid model.\par \par \uc2 In September, 45% of full-time employees were working remotely some or all of the time, with nine out of 10 remote workers wanting to continue that way, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/355907/remote-work-persisting-trending-permanent.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according}}}\uc2  to a Gallup poll.\par \par \uc2 One-third of employees who could work remotely are doing so, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/coronavirus-pandemic-business-lifestyle-health-937d4c71e44781cf1d25ce6b56141eb0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according}}}\uc2  to a survey from Willis \uc2 Towers Watson\uc2 , a global advisory firm. That number was expected to decline in 2022, before the advent of Omicron.\par \par \uc2 One of the challenges for companies is \u8220\'20\'1Ctrying to build momentum in terms of getting people back into the office\u8221\'20\'1D, Bell said. Companies are worried that if they open their offices before employees are ready, the momentum will stall \u8211\'20\'13 and they\u8217\'20\'19re wary of forcing anyone back with office mandates. \u8220\'20\'1CSo they\u8217\'20\'19re waiting for the right time to really make that happen,\u8221\'20\'1D Bell said.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic could bring lasting changes to the ways we work \u8211\'20\'13 for those who have jobs that can be done remotely.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s certainly a real issue\u8221\'20\'1D for those who can\u8217\'20\'19t work remotely, Bell said. \u8220\'20\'1CI think that\u8217\'20\'19s why we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen an uptick in strikes and other things over the last several months where people are wanting \u8211\'20\'13 not necessarily a level playing field, but want their sacrifices recognized by their employers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In-person employees should continue to have support for working safely, including masking, distancing and vaccination mandates.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll those things matter,\u8221\'20\'1D Bell said.\par \par \uc2 The US is experiencing record-low unemployment even as cases surge.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a fraught moment, and it\u8217\'20\'19s a time where I think the power dynamic has really shifted, with the Great Resignation and the labor shortages,\u8221\'20\'1D Bell said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s put labor in the driver\u8217\'20\'19s seat,\u8221\'20\'1D with moves like the first Starbuck union, in Buffalo, New York, he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEmployees realize that they, to a certain degree, have the upper hand and so can really start bringing these issues to the forefront.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211217ehch002bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
