\par\par \uc2 Money\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18At 75, I still have to work\u8217\'20\'19: millions of Americans can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to retire\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,263 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 December 2021\par 10:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Number of US workers aged 75 and up expected to increase 96.5% over next decade as some say \u8216\'20\'18we must work until we die\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Maria Rios, 75, has worked as a food prep worker with contractor HMS Host at Phoenix Sky Harbor international airport for 17 years, where she makes $14.50 an hour. Her husband, who is retired, only receives about $400 a month in social security benefits. Rios would like to retire also, but she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have the option even as she is battling ovarian cancer.\par \par \uc2 When Covid-19 hit the US, Rios was one of thousands of workers in the food service industry who were furloughed, and was only recalled back to work a few months ago. While receiving unemployment assistance, Rios and her husband had to rely on food banks to have enough food to eat, and because she lost her health insurance along with her job, she had to skip cancer treatments until she was able to get Medicare, but still paid hundreds of dollars out of pocket for treatments.\par \par \uc2 Her situation means that at a time when many might assume she should be enjoying a well-earned rest, she is still forced to work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt 75 years old, I\u8217\'20\'19m forced to still have to work to try to make ends meet. The healthcare the company provides is way too expensive and they\u8217\'20\'19ve yet to provide a more affordable health insurance plan,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rios, who pays about $200 a month for her health insurance. \u8220\'20\'1C It\u8217\'20\'19s also very important for people to be able to have a pension too, to be able to retire with dignity so people aren\u8217\'20\'19t forced to be in a position where I am right now, 75 years old and still having to work\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 She even recently went on strike with her co-workers for 10 days, fighting for higher wages, more affordable healthcare and a pension.\par \par \uc2 Media reports of older workers have often been framed as feel-good stories, such as a viral news report of an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.today.com/food/89-year-old-pizza-delivery-man-gets-surprise-12k-tip-t192448"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 89-year-old pizza delivery man}}}\uc2  who received a $12,000 tip raised by a customer out of remorse, as he works 30 hours a week because he can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to retire on social security benefits alone. Or an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ChloeTeboe/status/1286232536176308225"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 84-year-old woman}}}\uc2  who started a new job as a motel housekeeper in Maine in July 2020. Or an 81-year-old woman in Ohio who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/11/28/us/labor-shortage-restaurant-retiree-works/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 volunteered}}}\uc2  to start working at her favorite restaurant in November 2021 because it shut down temporarily due to an inability to hire and retain enough staff.\par \par \uc2 But the grim reality is millions of Americans are working into their senior years because they can\u8217\'20\'19t afford not to have a job.\par \par \uc2 Over the next decade, the number of workers ages 75 and older is expected to increase in the US by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/opub/ted/2021/number-of-people-75-and-older-in-the-labor-force-is-expected-to-grow-96-5-percent-by-2030.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 96.5}}}\uc2  %, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , with their labor force participation rate projected to rise from 8.9% in 2020 to 11.7% by 2030, a rate that has steadily increased from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/opub/ted/2019/labor-force-participation-rate-for-workers-age-75-and-older-projected-to-be-over-10-percent-by-2026.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 4.7%}}}\uc2  in 1996.\par \par \uc2 By 2040, the US population of adults ages 65 and older is expected to increase to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://acl.gov/aging-and-disability-in-america/data-and-research/projected-future-growth-older-population#:~:text=Aging%20%26%20Disability%20in%20America,-%2D%20Left%20Nav%20%2D&text=In%202019%2C%20there%20were%2054.1,and%2094.7%20million%20by%202060.&text=Both%20populations%20are%20growing%2C%20and,growing%20demographics%20in%20the%20country."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 80.8 million}}}\uc2  from 54.1 million in 2019.\par \par \uc2 The number of workers who retired during the pandemic was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2021/08/23/1028993124/these-older-workers-hadnt-planned-to-retire-so-soon-the-pandemic-sped-things-up"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about 2 million}}}\uc2  more than expected. 50.3% of US adults ages 55 and older said they were out of the labor force due to retirement in the third quarter of 2021, compared to 48.1% in the third quarter of 2019, according to an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2021/11/04/amid-the-pandemic-a-rising-share-of-older-u-s-adults-are-now-retired/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 analysis}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2 . Though in recent months, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/11/17/retirees-are-unretiring-and-thats-good-for-the-labor-market.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unretirement rate}}}\uc2  of US workers has gradually increased toward pre-pandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 As the ageing US population grows, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/the-state-of-american-retirement-savings/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 participation}}}\uc2  in retirement plans has declined since 2000. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/the-state-of-american-retirement-savings/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nearly half}}}\uc2  of all families in the US have no retirement savings at all and inequality among Americans based on retirement savings is greater than income inequality. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncoa.org/article/get-the-facts-on-economic-security-for-seniors"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Over 15 million}}}\uc2  adults ages 65 and older are economically insecure, with incomes below 200% of the federal poverty line, with Black, Hispanic and women ages 65 and older more likely to live in poverty.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have no savings, no assets, I don\u8217\'20\'19t even own the home I\u8217\'20\'19ve been renting for 15 years,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Lisa Natale, 65, a chiropractor in Hawaii who put herself through school as a single mother. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no way I could afford to retire.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 With the average estimated social security retirement benefit in 2021 at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aarp.org/retirement/social-security/questions-answers/maximum-ss-benefit.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $1,543 a month}}}\uc2 , even with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/10/17/people-will-get-bigger-social-security-checks-in-2022-how-to-prepare.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 5.9%}}}\uc2  cost of living adjusted increase for 2022, millions of Americans who rely on social security benefits are forced to continue working past retirement age in order to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 Kathy Luebbe, a 69-year-old teacher in the Cincinnati, Ohio, area, has continued working through retirement age because social security benefits and her partial pension only provide $2,600 a month, which is not enough to cover her bills and student loan payments.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have to work just to keep paying my regular bills,\u8221\'20\'1D said Luebbe.\par \par \uc2 Ted Newman of Columbus, Ohio, is a retired state government employee, but continued working full-time through his retirement, along with his wife, for the past eight years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have been working full-time for the last 8 years since retiring, and my wife also. Her yearly salary went almost completely to pay student debt and I worked to have extras for the home,\u8221\'20\'1D said Newman.\par \par \uc2 When Covid-19 hit, his wife stopped working and he shifted to part-time due to health conditions. They paid about $1,000 toward parent plus loans for their daughter\u8217\'20\'19s tuition for years, but still owe the principal of the loan.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow our income is cut in half and our loans will be coming due again in 2022,\u8221\'20\'1D added Newman. \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t live long enough to pay the debt and plan to let it go to default because I can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to pay it any more.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As the US population ages, with millions of Americans having no retirement savings, the number of older Americans with student debt, either for themselves or for children, is on the rise.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marketplace.org/2021/06/21/nearly-9-million-older-americans-still-have-student-loan-debt/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nearly 9 million}}}\uc2  Americans ages 50 and older still have student debt, and the amount owed by this demographic is growing faster than any other age group. In 2015, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://money.com/older-borrowers-student-debt-retirement/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 40,000 Americans}}}\uc2  had their social security retirement benefits garnished for student loans.\par \par \uc2 Jane Switchenko, 63, of Massachusetts, and her 68-year-old husband cannot afford to retire, in part because of parent plus student loans they took out for their children to attend college.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy husband and I must work until we die. Parent plus loans take over $1,000 a month, every month, for a decade now and we continue to see a rising balance,\u8221\'20\'1D said Switchenko. \u8220\'20\'1CNot only can we not retire, we cannot afford to fix our old house \u8211\'20\'13 we are desperate for new windows, have a leaking chimney and need new electrical work done.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CWe work hard. We are frugal, but we\u8217\'20\'19re getting up there and have nothing to look forward to because of these loans. We have five grandchildren and don\u8217\'20\'19t know what we\u8217\'20\'19ll be able to buy for Christmas.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This article was written with the support of a journalism fellowship from\par \par \uc2 the \uc2 Gerontological Society of America\uc2 ,\par \par \uc2 the Journalists Network on Generations and the RRF Foundation for Aging\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211213ehcd001hi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
