\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  threatens graduate workers with replacement if they continue strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 870 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 December 2021\par 17:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The students are striking over better pay, healthcare and third-party arbitration for harassment and discrimination complaints\par \par \uc2 About 3,000 graduate workers at \uc2 Columbia University in New York City\uc2 , who have been on strike since 3 November, recently received an email from the university human resources department threatening the workers with replacement if they continue striking.\par \par \uc2 The strike is the largest active strike in the US.\par \par \uc2 The email, sent on 2 December by Columbia University human resources vice-president Dave Driscoll, informed workers their positions would be replaced if they continue striking past 10 December.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur interpretation of this email is that it\u8217\'20\'19s basically a threat. They are saying here\u8217\'20\'19s the date, December 10. If you\u8217\'20\'19re still exercising your right to engage in protected activity on or after December 10 there\u8217\'20\'19s no guarantee that you\u8217\'20\'19re going to get your job back. So we think that\u8217\'20\'19s the intention of the threat, but we also think that what they\u8217\'20\'19re doing is unlawful,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ethan Jacobs, a graduate student worker in the philosophy department at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  and a member of the GWC-UAW Local 2110 bargaining committee.\par \par \uc2 Jacobs described the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/02-CA-286589"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unfair labor practice charges}}}\uc2  filed by the union against the university with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 . They include the university enacting a wage freeze and changing wage disbursement schedules earlier this year without negotiating with the union after its members {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.columbiaspectator.com/news/2021/05/02/dissatisfied-with-contract-negotiations-and-bargaining-committee-gwc-uaw-student-workers-vote-to-reject-tentative-agreement/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rejected }}}\uc2  a tentative agreement after a strike in the 2021 spring semester.\par \par \uc2 According to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/strikes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Labor Relations Act}}}\uc2 , workers who strike to protest unfair labor practices cannot be discharged or permanently replaced. Jacobs expressed the union\u8217\'20\'19s intent to file additional charges with the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  over the university\u8217\'20\'19s threat to replace workers on strike.\par \par \uc2 Graduate workers have been on strike over improved compensation to cover the high cost of living in New York City, a third-party arbitration process for harassment and discrimination complaints and improved healthcare plans for student workers, including dental care and vision coverage.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Columbia University\uc2  operates like these early 20th century company towns. For example, I live in an apartment that is owned by Columbia, so the university is also my landlord. They are the source of my health insurance, the source of my job, the source of my academic progress. In nearly every aspect of my life, Columbia has some sort of say in how that goes and that\u8217\'20\'19s really problematic,\u8221\'20\'1D added Jacobs.\par \par \uc2 Over the past several years, graduate student workers at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  have fought for a \uc2 first union\uc2  contract with the university, after securing a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/sections/thetwo-way/2016/08/23/491090762/nlrb-rules-graduate-students-are-employees-with-the-right-to-unionize"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruling}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  in 2016 that affirmed graduate students are employees with the right to unionize.\par \par \uc2 Workers are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/11/24/nyregion/columbia-grad-student-strike.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pushing}}}\uc2  for a wage floor of $45,000 annually for first-year doctoral students on one-year appointments and a minimum hourly wage of $26 for hourly workers. Current wages vary by department from as low as $29,000 annually for student workers at the School of Social Work to $41,500 for engineering student workers. The hourly minimum wage at the school is $15.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe pay structure we have now is the subject of our second unfair labor practice charge. We used to get paid in a lump sum at the beginning of the semester. Our pay was never enough to begin with, but people could budget around it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jonathan Ben-Menachem, a graduate student worker in the Department of Sociology, who noted student workers have been forced to sign an attestation form that they were not striking in order to receive pay during the strike.\par \par \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  received returns on their endowment of 32.3% in fiscal year 2021, increasing the school\u8217\'20\'19s endowment value to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.columbiaspectator.com/news/2021/10/27/columbia-endowment-soars-up-323-percent-to-1435-billion-as-markets-recover-from-covid-19-recession/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $14.35bn}}}\uc2  and ending the fiscal year with a $150m operating surplus, recovering from initial financial losses incurred by the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Tamara Hache, a graduate student worker in the Latin American and Iberian cultures department and an international student, expressed concerns over the threat of replacement and its impact on the university as a whole, including international students who cannot work outside the university.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis measure the university is trying to carry out, of this threat, would be incredibly destructive for everyone at the university. It wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t just affect us as graduate student workers, but everyone. The quality of our students\u8217\'20\'19 education and our departments would be terribly impacted,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hache.\par \par \uc2 In response to the threat of replacement, the union is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScNzzN0rJqsoL1foVBt3og65eYBTI_9zTHmUOxZ7maJk23_sQ/viewform"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rallying}}}\uc2  support from undergraduate students, parents, alumni, faculty and the public, and have held {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SW_Columbia/status/1468068462702604291"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protests}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  said in a statement, \u8220\'20\'1CIn the face of enormously trying circumstances created by the strike, our first priority is the academic progress of our students, particularly undergraduates whose classes are being disrupted. The message sent last week to the union bargaining committee explaining the university\u8217\'20\'19s approach to spring appointments and teaching assignments was necessary to fulfill that commitment. Replacing instructors who leave the classroom is permitted by US labor law. With respect to striking student workers who return to work after December 10, we will make every effort to provide them with suitable positions, as available.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211209ehc9002pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
