\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Kellogg to replace 1,400 strikers as deal is rejected\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff and agencies \par \uc2 637 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 December 2021\par 15:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Strike, which began in October, expected to continue as workers seek significant raises, saying they work 80-hour weeks\par \par \uc2 Kellogg has said it is permanently replacing 1,400 workers who have been on strike since October, a decision that comes as the majority of its cereal plant workforce rejected a deal that would have provided 3% raises.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bakery, Confectionary, Tobacco Workers and Grain Millers (BCTGM) \uc2 International Union said an overwhelming majority of workers had voted down the five-year offer.\par \par \uc2 The decision follows months of bitter disagreement between the company and the union. The rejected offer would have provided cost of living adjustments in the later years of the deal and preserved the workers\u8217\'20\'19 current healthcare benefits. But workers say they deserve significant raises because they routinely work more than 80 hours a week, and they kept the plants running throughout the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Employees have been striking since 5 October at plants in Michigan, Nebraska, Pennsylvania and Tennessee. They make all of the company\u8217\'20\'19s well-known brands of cereal, including \uc2 Apple\uc2  Jacks and Frosted Flakes. The strike is expected to continue.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe members have spoken. The strike continues,\u8221\'20\'1D the union president, Anthony Shelton, said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe International Union will continue to provide full support to our striking \uc2 Kellogg\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  members.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Workers say they are also protesting planned job cuts and offshoring, and a proposed two-tier system that gives newer workers at the plants less pay and fewer benefits. Speaking {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/oct/07/kelloggs-workers-strike-offshore-jobs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to the Guardian}}}\uc2  in October, Trevor Bidelman, president of BCTGM Local3G and a fourth-generation employee at the Kellogg plant in Battle Creek, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/michigan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Michigan}}}\uc2 , described it as a \u8220\'20\'1Cfight for our future\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is after just one year ago, we were hailed as heroes, as we worked through the pandemic, seven days a week, 16 hours a day. Now apparently, we are no longer heroes,\u8221\'20\'1D said Bidelman. \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t have weekends, really. We just work seven days a week, sometimes 100 to 130 days in a row. For 28 days, the machines run, then rest three days for cleaning. They don\u8217\'20\'19t even treat us as well as they do their machinery.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kellogg said it would now move forward with plans to start hiring permanent replacements for the striking workers. The company has already been using salaried employees and outside workers to keep the plants operating during the strike.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile certainly not the result we had hoped for, we must take the necessary steps to ensure business continuity,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Chris Hood\uc2 , president of Kellogg North America. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have an obligation to our customers and consumers to continue to provide the cereals that they know and love.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Rutgers University professor Todd Vachon, who teaches classes about labor relations, said he was not sure the company would be able to hire enough workers to replace the ones who are out on strike in the current economy, and Kellogg may have a hard time finding people willing to cross a picket line.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBy voting \u8216\'20\'18no\u8217\'20\'19, the workers are making a strong statement that they are not satisfied by the agreement, but they are also signaling they believe they have the leverage that\u8217\'20\'19s needed to win more,\u8221\'20\'1D Vachon said.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, about 600 food workers went on strike at a \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  plant in Topeka, Kansas, and 1,000 others walked off the job at five Nabisco plants across the US. In another recent strike, more than 10,000 Deere workers secured 10% raises and improved benefits, but those gains came after the workers remained on strike for a month and rejected two offers from the company.\par \par \uc2 The offer that Kellogg workers rejected was the first one they have voted on since the strike began.\par \par \uc2 Michael Sainato contributed reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211208ehc8001p8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
