\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Kansas sisters break new ground for Black women founders in tech \u8211\'20\'13 and beyond\b0\par\par\uc2 Kelli Mar\u237\'00\'EDa Korducki \par \uc2 717 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 December 2021\par 13:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Angela Muhwezi-Hall and Deborah Gladney are pioneering a startup that links jobseekers to opportunities in the midwest\par \par \uc2 This year, sisters Angela Muhwezi-Hall and Deborah Gladney from Wichita, Kansas, became the first Black women entrepreneurs in the state to raise $1m in seed funding for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://getquickhire.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 QuickHire}}}\uc2 , an app that serves as a \u8220\'20\'1Ccareer discovery platform\u8221\'20\'1D for frontline service economy workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is really cool,\u8221\'20\'1D says Gladney \u8220\'20\'1CWe pride ourselves on being underserved founders who are serving underserved workers in an underserved geographic region. We\u8217\'20\'19re hoping to shed light on opportunities can arise outside of traditional areas. If we can help remove barriers for people, even by being the first or just being able to have people come alongside us, that is what we\u8217\'20\'19re all about.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As with so many innovative tech startups, the story of QuickHire begins with an a-ha moment.\par \par \uc2 A few years ago, Muhwezi-Hall was working as an educational adviser in Los Angeles when a high-schooler approached her for help finding a job. She quickly learned that seeking and applying for work in blue-collar industries hadn\u8217\'20\'19t changed much since Muhwezi-Hall\u8217\'20\'19s own high school years a decade earlier. More often than not, young jobseekers still needed to scan their neighborhood businesses for \u8220\'20\'1Chiring\u8221\'20\'1D signs, enter the business, and fill out a paper application.\par \par \uc2 Not long afterwards, Muhwezi-Hall approached her sister with the seed of an idea: what if the two of them started a business that made it easier to apply for jobs? \u8220\'20\'1CWe sat on it for quite some time, but we\u8217\'20\'19d always go back to it. Like, \u8216\'20\'18it would be so great if people could apply by swiping,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D recalls Muhwezi-Hall.\par \par \uc2 This April, QuickHire officially launched with Gladney and Muhwezi-Hall as, respectively, CEO and COO. With its current emphasis on the retail, restaurant and hospitality industries, the app went live just in time to help local businesses find enthusiastic workers amid a nationwide labor shortage. So far, QuickHire has attracted more than 12,000 jobseekers and listings from 70 local businesses.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe really pride ourselves on making sure that we connect the right people to the right jobs, and making it very efficient,\u8221\'20\'1D says Muhwezi-Hall. \u8220\'20\'1CSo we were able to find success pretty early on, on the employer side. Some of our first employers, actually, are now joining our team because they really loved their experience with QuickHire and think that what we\u8217\'20\'19re doing is important.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 QuickHire\u8217\'20\'19s mission to extend opportunities to underserved workers is a personal one for its sister-founders. As the youngest of five children born to Ugandan immigrants in the midwest, the sisters spent their formative years in a number of \u8220\'20\'1Clittle small towns in Kansas\u8221\'20\'1D, says Muhwezi-Hall, where they were often \u8220\'20\'1Cthe only person of color in a room\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt prepared us for being in this tech world,\u8221\'20\'1D says Muhwezi-Hall. \u8220\'20\'1CWe had that work ethic that our parents ingrained in us, to make sure that we don\u8217\'20\'19t take anything for granted, make sure that we put our best foot forward, so that people see why we are at the table and why we deserve to be there.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gladney notes that their parents are excited about the doors that QuickHire could open up for other immigrant families, children of immigrants and Black women founders in Kansas. \u8220\'20\'1CI think that that is what brings them a lot of pride, that we\u8217\'20\'19re able to impact and have an influence on a mission that\u8217\'20\'19s bigger than us,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 The sisters are also tremendously proud of each other.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s just amazing to see the growth in each other through this journey,\u8221\'20\'1D says Muhwezi-Hall. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve always said that Deborah is a phenomenal leader. It\u8217\'20\'19s amazing to be able to watch your sister become the CEO that you always knew was there, knowing that she\u8217\'20\'19s got this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gladney agrees, and says that co-founding a company with her sister is a professional advantage. \u8220\'20\'1CEven though it\u8217\'20\'19s our first time building a company,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, \u8220\'20\'1CAngie and I have been building together all of our lives, just in other ways.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211204ehc4001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
