\par\par \uc2 Life and style\par \b \uc2 Portugal banned bosses from texting employees after work. Could it happen in the US?\b0\par\par\uc2 Adrienne Matei \par \uc2 886 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 November 2021\par 14:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Countries protecting people\u8217\'20\'19s right to a healthy work-life balance are responding appropriately to modern work culture\par \par \uc2 Employers in Portugal will now face fines if they attempt to contact remote workers after hours, thanks to a new law.\par \par \uc2 The legislation was conceived by Portugal\u8217\'20\'19s ruling Socialist party to improve work-life balance for the country\u8217\'20\'19s remote workforce, which expanded due to Covid-19, and to make Portugal a more attractive base for international \u8220\'20\'1Cdigital nomads\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 people who travel while telecommuting.\par \par \uc2 Portugal isn\u8217\'20\'19t the only country modernizing its labor laws; citizens of France, Spain, Belgium, Slovakia, Italy, the Philippines, Argentina, India and more, all currently enjoy \u8220\'20\'1Cthe right to disconnect\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 or abstain without punishment from working and communicating with their employers during designated rest periods.\par \par \uc2 In 2013, Germany\u8217\'20\'19s employment ministry implemented a ban on employers contacting workers outside of contracted hours, and several of the country\u8217\'20\'19s large employers, including \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  and Daimler, have instituted policies intended to limit the number of emails employees receive outside of work hours, too.\par \par \uc2 The provincial government of Ontario is also introducing legislation that would require employers to establish a written policy setting generous \u8220\'20\'1Cexpectations about response time for emails and encouraging employees to turn on out-of-office notifications when they aren\u8217\'20\'19t working\u8221\'20\'1D, according to a government {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.ontario.ca/en/release/1001033/ontario-creating-a-better-future-for-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 release}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Could this ever happen in the United States?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think that we\u8217\'20\'19ll see a firm requirement of employers to not at all contact employees during non-work hours,\u8221\'20\'1D says Orly Lobel, professor of law at the University of San Diego.\par \par \uc2 While California does have laws against employers forcing employees to work overtime, and mandates that all overtime be paid, Lobel thinks that adopting and enforcing rules about work hours on a federal level would be overly complex and contradictory to the nature of globalized professional work, in which urgent tasks inevitably crop up and must be dealt with by someone.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI just don\u8217\'20\'19t see it as a frontier right now,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, \u8220\'20\'1Cwhen there are so many things that could be better.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have a long history in the United States of not having policies that make it easier to work and have a life, or work and have a family,\u8221\'20\'1D says Julie Kashen, director of women\u8217\'20\'19s economic justice at the Century Institute.\par \par \uc2 Despite paid family and medical leave being one of the most popular bipartisan policies, the fact that Congress is struggling to get it through 28 years after passing an unpaid version \u8220\'20\'1Cdoes not bode well for the US taking additional steps in our laws to respect boundaries,\u8221\'20\'1D says Kashen.\par \par \uc2 Historically, the US has emphasized productivity, profits and the bottom line above all else, Kashen says. It relies on a capitalist system that has never involved an equitable, inclusive vision \u8220\'20\'1Cthat really addresses all of the different realities of being a human being\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The countries that are actively protecting people\u8217\'20\'19s right to a healthy work-life balance are responding appropriately to modern work culture. The mass adoption of technologies have created \u8220\'20\'1Calways on\u8221\'20\'1D conditions in which off-hours and weekends can be work time as well. Today\u8217\'20\'19s workers need help reinstating the parameters between work and life that social and technological change have eroded.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s little doubt that people without sufficient worker protections are getting fed up and burned out. That\u8217\'20\'19s especially true since the pandemic collapsed the distinction between work and home. It\u8217\'20\'19s not surprise then that the US is also in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/10/19/the-great-resignation-why-people-are-quitting-their-jobs.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 midst}}}\uc2  of a \u8220\'20\'1Cgreat resignation\u8221\'20\'1D, with 4.3 million Americans quitting their jobs this August alone.\par \par \uc2 Lobel and Kashen believe America\u8217\'20\'19s most realistic path towards enforcing firm work-life boundaries will have to come from corporations instituting more accommodating policies themselves.\par \par \uc2 There are practical reasons for corporations to apply such policies. Many studies, including 2017 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.baua.de/EN/Topics/Work-design/Working-time/_functions/Publications-search_Formular.html?gtp=8701218_list%253D3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 research}}}\uc2  by the German Federal Institute for Occupational Health and Safety, and a 2018 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://vtx.vt.edu/articles/2018/08/pamplin-employer-emailexpectations.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  from the Pamplin College of Business, \uc2 Virginia Tech\uc2 , support the fact that after-hours workplace communication produces stress, negatively affects employee mental health, and can spill over into affecting employees\u8217\'20\'19 families.\par \par \uc2 Competitive workplaces are already becoming increasingly flexible to attract desirable candidates, and that trend can be reasonably expected to continue and incorporate more stipulations about the right to disconnect.\par \par \uc2 Yet while the corporate route may be most realistic, it isn\u8217\'20\'19t the only feasible way for Americans to secure protected off-time.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is absolutely legally possible for US lawmakers to pass a law similar to the one passed in Portugal,\u8221\'20\'1D says Veena Dubal, professor of law at the University of California, Hastings College.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe impediment is twofold. One is rooted in the power of industry; the business lobby would never allow something like this to pass. The second is simply that US lawmakers and courts have historically been very reticent to interfere in business decisions,\u8221\'20\'1D she explains.\par \par \uc2 In the US, the presumption has long been that the property right of capital outweighs the labor rights of workers. \u8220\'20\'1CBut this is a presumption, not a legal restriction or boundary,\u8221\'20\'1D says Dubal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe can transform this presumption through a cultural shift that might emerge through and with the labor movement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211115ehbf001e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
