\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Inmate executed in Oklahoma\u8217\'20\'19s first lethal injection in six years\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff and agencies \par \uc2 682 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2021\par 14:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 John Marion Grant, convicted of stabbing a prison cafeteria worker, was the first since a series of flawed executions in 2014 and 2015\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma has executed a man for the 1998 stabbing death of a prison cafeteria worker, the state\u8217\'20\'19s first lethal injection following a six-year moratorium, after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  on Thursday cleared the way for the execution to proceed.\par \par \uc2 John Marion Grant, 60, was declared dead at 4.21pm after being strapped to a gurney inside the execution chamber and given a lethal injection of three drugs.\par \par \uc2 Grant was the first inmate to be executed since a series of flawed lethal injections in 2014 and 2015. He was serving a 130-year prison sentence for several armed robberies when witnesses say he dragged Gay Carter, a prison cafeteria worker, into a mop closet and stabbed her 16 times with a homemade shank. He was sentenced to death in 1999. The state twice denied Grant\u8217\'20\'19s request for clemency.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma moved forward with the execution on Thursday after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 , in a 5-3 decision, lifted stays of execution that were put in place on Wednesday for Grant and another death row inmate, Julius Jones, by the 10th US circuit court of appeals.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma had one of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s busiest death chambers until problems in 2014 and 2015 led to a de facto moratorium. Richard Glossip was just hours away from being executed in September 2015 when prison officials realized they received the wrong lethal drug. It was later learned the same wrong drug had been used to execute an inmate in January 2015. In 2014, another botched execution saw Clayton Lockett struggle on a gurney before dying 43 minutes into his lethal injection.\par \par \uc2 While the moratorium was in place, Oklahoma moved ahead with plans to use nitrogen gas to execute inmates, but ultimately scrapped that idea and announced last year that it planned to resume executions using the same three-drug lethal injection protocol that was used during the flawed executions. The three drugs are: midazolam, a sedative, vecuronium bromide, a paralytic, and potassium chloride, which stops the heart.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma prison officials recently announced that they had confirmed a source to supply all the drugs needed for Grant\u8217\'20\'19s execution plus six more that are scheduled to take place through March.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CExtensive validations and redundancies have been implemented since the last execution in order to ensure that the process works as intended,\u8221\'20\'1D said the Department of Corrections in a statement.\par \par \uc2 More than two dozen Oklahoma death row inmates are part of a federal lawsuit challenging the state\u8217\'20\'19s lethal injection protocols, arguing that the three-drug method risks causing unconstitutional pain and suffering. A trial is scheduled for early next year.\par \par \uc2 Grant and his attorneys did not deny that he killed Carter, but argued that important facts about the crime and Grant\u8217\'20\'19s troubled childhood were never presented to the jury. They maintained that Grant developed deep feelings for Carter and was upset when she fired him after he got in a fight with another kitchen worker.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJurors never heard that Mr Grant killed Ms Gay Carter while in the heat of passion and despair over the abrupt end of the deepest and most important adult relationship of his life,\u8221\'20\'1D his attorneys wrote in his clemency application.\par \par \uc2 Carter\u8217\'20\'19s daughter, Pam Carter, who also worked at the prison and was there the day her mother was killed, rejected the idea that her mother and Grant had anything more than a professional relationship and urged state officials to move forward with the execution.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI understand he\u8217\'20\'19s trying to save his life, but you keep victimizing my mother with these stupid allegations,\u8221\'20\'1D she told the pardon and parole board this month. \u8220\'20\'1CMy mother was vivacious. She was friendly. She didn\u8217\'20\'19t meet a stranger. She treated her workers just as you would on a job on the outside. For someone to take advantage of that is just heinous.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211101ehb1002pc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
