\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 US would only quit Iran nuclear deal if Tehran were to renege, Biden pledges\b0\par\par\uc2 Patrick Wintour in Rome \par \uc2 1,042 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2021\par 20:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 President makes commitment alongside Germany, France and UK not to repeat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s walkout on agreement\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has given a pledge that if the US returns to the Iran nuclear agreement, it will only subsequently leave if Tehran clearly breaks the terms of the deal.\par \par \uc2 The US president made the commitment, which addresses one of Iran\u8217\'20\'19s key negotiating demands, in a joint statement issued with Germany, France and the UK. The statement followed a meeting on the margins of the G20 in Rome attended by Biden, Germany\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2 , France\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  and Britain\u8217\'20\'19s Boris Johnson.\par \par \uc2 The key paragraph of a lengthy statement read: \u8220\'20\'1CWe welcome President Biden\u8217\'20\'19s clearly demonstrated commitment to return the US to full compliance with the JCPOA [joint comprehensive plan of action] and to stay in full compliance, so long as Iran does the same.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The pledge was welcomed by \uc2 Mikhail Ulyanov\uc2 , the Russian ambassador to multilateral bodies in Vienna, where talks on the future of the Iran nuclear deal have been on pause since June.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the talks Iran has been seeking an undertaking, ideally legally underpinned, that if Iran returns to the deal, future US administrations will not repeat the walkout of the previous president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which was accompanied by the imposition of tighter economic sanctions on Iran\u8217\'20\'19s financial institutions and political bodies.\par \par \uc2 Biden is hamstrung in the value of the guarantees he can give to Iran because the nuclear deal is not a signed treaty endorsed by the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , and he cannot bind the hands of future US administrations.\par \par \uc2 However, the Biden pledge, underwritten by the main three European powers, may be a sign that the US wants to create a more positive atmosphere before the resumption of the Vienna talks that Iran has \u8211\'20\'13 after much pressure and delay \u8211\'20\'13 promised will happen at some point in November. There have already been six rounds of talks, but they broke off in June to allow the new Iranian government, led by the president, \uc2 Ebrahim Raisi\uc2 , to review its negotiating strategy.\par \par \uc2 During a speech to the G20 summit in Rome on Sunday, Biden said the US will respond to Iranian attacks on US interests in countries such as Syria, but implied that America preferred using economic leverage rather than military force if Iran refused to return to the negotiating table in Vienna to discuss the future of the 2015 nuclear deal.\par \par \uc2 He added the US was still paying the price of bad choices made by the Trump administration, including the decision to quit the nuclear deal in 2018. But he insisted he and European leaders at the summit had agreed diplomacy was the best way forward in handling Iran over the future of the nuclear deal. He said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe came together to reiterate our shared belief that diplomacy is the best way to prevent Iran from getting a nuclear weapon and discussed how best to encourage Iran to resume serious good faith negotiations\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It is not clear if his remarks means the US would disapprove if Israel launched military strikes against Iran if Israel believed Iran was close to being able to assemble the material needed to make a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 The US secretary of state, \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 , said on Sunday that the United States was \u8220\'20\'1Cabsolutely in lock step\u8221\'20\'1D with Britain, Germany and France on getting Iran back into a nuclear deal, but added it was unclear if Tehran was willing to rejoin the talks in a \u8220\'20\'1Cmeaningful way\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt really depends on whether Iran is serious about doing that,\u8221\'20\'1D Blinken said of rejoining the nuclear talks. \u8220\'20\'1CAll of our countries, working by the way with Russia and China, believe strongly that that would be the best path forward,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut we do not yet know whether Iran is willing to come back to engage in a meaningful way,\u8221\'20\'1D Blinken said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut if it isn\u8217\'20\'19t, if it won\u8217\'20\'19t, then we are looking together at all of the options necessary to deal with this problem.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The shift in the US tone comes a week after a cyber-attack disabled Iran\u8217\'20\'19s petrol stations, an attack attributed on Sunday by Brig Gen Gholamreza Jalali, the head of Iran\u8217\'20\'19s civil defence organisation, to Israel and the US.\par \par \uc2 The west has become increasingly concerned that Iran\u8217\'20\'19s delay is a subterfuge, and that Tehran has been using the pause to strengthen its stockpile of uranium, weaken the \uc2 UN\uc2  nuclear inspections process and become more familiar with the use of advanced centrifuges that can produce highly enriched uranium. The US is concerned that Iran\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cbreakout\u8221\'20\'1D time for developing a nuclear weapon is reducing and that the value of returning to the deal is fast diminishing. It has been looking at alternative options if it decides that diplomacy will not work.\par \par \uc2 In a bid to entrench a revived deal further, Iran is also seeking pledges from the \uc2 EU\uc2  about its response if the US was to walk out of the deal a second time.\par \par \uc2 It wants firm pledges that the \uc2 EU\uc2  would put more money into Instex, the trading device that the \uc2 EU\uc2  set up to ensure Iran and the \uc2 EU\uc2  could evade US sanctions. It also wants the \uc2 EU\uc2  to allow China and Russia to join the scheme. Instex has largely been inoperable ever since it was set up by the \uc2 EU\uc2  with fanfare more than four years ago.\par \par \uc2 The US E3 joint statement issued in Rome at the margins of the G20 summit also contained no demand that Iran, as part of any agreement, commit to follow-on negotiations about what the west regards as its destabilising behaviour in the region. The Iranian foreign minister, \uc2 Hossein Amir-Abdollahian\uc2 , said in an interview published at the weekend that there was no need for the negotiation and the simplest solution was for Biden to issue an executive order saying he was returning to the nuclear deal and lifting sanctions.\par \par \uc2 Amir-Abdollahian said his administration was embracing a balanced foreign policy, a phrase implying that Iran is going to deprioritise relations with the west, including the main European powers.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211031ehav000xh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
