\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Can\u8217\'20\'19t find workers? Why not pay more instead of exploiting children and migrants\b0\par\par\uc2 Van Badham \par \uc2 938 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2021\par 04:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The creative energy found by employers to avoid wage rises is yet another reminder the promises made to workers by neoliberal economics are lies, lies, lies\par \par \uc2 The senate of the US state of Wisconsin has just approved a bill allowing 14-year-old children to engage in paid work until {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com.au/labor-shortage-wisconsin-senate-jobs-work-teenagers-child-labor-hours-2021-10"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as late as 11pm}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Health authorities recommend that children in this age group should be getting 8-10 hours of sleep a night. Children who do not experience problems with concentration, memory, organising tasks, thinking creatively, regulating their emotions, distraction, irritation, hyperactivity, restlessness, poor mental health, poor growth, weight gain and reduced school performance.\par \par \uc2 Screw that, seems to be the message from the hotel and tourism lobbyists who campaigned for the change, which goes against federal child labour laws in the US. These already allow children to be shifted for up to three hours a day on school days, up to eight hours a day on non-school days for as many as six days a week.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBusinesses throughout the state see a massive increase in traffic during the summer tourist season,\u8221\'20\'1D explained a spokesperson pushing for child night-workers, \u8220\'20\'1Cso much so that it can be difficult to find employees to work odd hours and seasonal times.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I mean, they could offer to pay adults more money, but that\u8217\'20\'19s just not how modern capitalism is choosing to roll.\par \par \uc2 Not only in the US, but also in the UK and Australia, the creative energy found by employers to avoid paying wage rises is reminding working people \u8211\'20\'13 yet again \u8211\'20\'13 that the promises made to them by neoliberal economics were all a wobbling jelly tower of lies, lies, lies.\par \par \uc2 The shift from rigid post-second world war industrial standards to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://newrepublic.com/article/162623/milton-friedman-legacy-biden-government-spending"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 free market neoliberal model}}}\uc2  began in the 1970s, with great fanfare of how deregulated workplaces and contracting models would allow labour market \u8220\'20\'1Cflexibility\u8221\'20\'1D and for businesses to more easily meet commercial needs. The dead giveaway that this was not perhaps in workers\u8217\'20\'19 interests was the massive amount of regulation curiously imposed on trade unions at the same time.\par \par \uc2 The promise made to workers was that in a market-based economy governed by the dynamics of supply and demand, workers would be able to freely negotiate individual work contracts based on their value within those dynamics.\par \par \uc2 Of course, low-wage growth has just happened to be the cumulative result, with the risk of \u8211\'20\'13 again, highly regulated \u8211\'20\'13 brutal, punitive conditions of unemployment playing a very useful role for employers in scaring the bejeezus out of anyone who may dare ask for a payrise. Work-for-the-dole is not designed to be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2019/jul/26/having-a-constant-pool-of-unemployed-workers-is-deliberate-policy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a competitive option}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The ructions to the economies of the US, UK and Australia in the wake of coronavirus disruption may have led some to believe that the time was \u8211\'20\'13 finally \u8211\'20\'13 ripe for workers to make their own insistences of value and be paid accordingly.\par \par \uc2 You\u8217\'20\'19d be wrong. In the US, there is an actual labour shortage \u8211\'20\'13 an excellent piece in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/archive/2021/10/how-do-you-make-7-million-workers-disappear/620475/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Atlantic}}}\uc2  last week measured the seven-million-worker-sized gap in their present labour market with the analogy of every single working person in the state of Pennsylvania vanishing overnight. Simply, Americans are refusing to return to workplace conditions they no longer accept. The phenomenon\u8217\'20\'19s been called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/archive/2021/10/great-resignation-accelerating/620382/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Great Resignation}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And yet the response to it by employers isn\u8217\'20\'19t to make workplace conditions competitive. It\u8217\'20\'19s to strong-arm legislatures into things like supporting the workplace exploitation of children \u8211\'20\'13 into the hospitality industry no less, which is a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.smh.com.au/interactive/2017/harassment-in-hospitality/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 super safe}}}\uc2  place for a 14-year-old girl to be working in a service capacity on a Friday night, except it really isn\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 The UK, of course, willingly legislated itself into its own labour shortage. The Brits were warned by militant communist journal, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/425f42c8-9dea-11e7-8cd4-932067fbf946"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Financial Times}}}\uc2 , in 2017 that as British industries were \u8220\'20\'1Caddicted to cheap labour\u8221\'20\'1D shipped in from poorer EU countries, the cold turkey of \u8220\'20\'1Cgetting Brexit done\u8221\'20\'1D would provoke some proper jonesing. Now it\u8217\'20\'19s 2021, and shortages of not only lorry drivers but also farm and food processing workers have provoked such supply problems that British supermarkets are placing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/oct/22/supermarkets-using-cardboard-cutouts-to-hide-gaps-left-by-supply-issues"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 photographs of food}}}\uc2  on shelves to hide the food that isn\u8217\'20\'19t there.\par \par \uc2 Are employers finally recognising the value of these services and remunerating their true worth? In desperation, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://californianewstimes.com/uk-retailers-step-up-battle-to-lure-lorry-drivers/543923/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 some}}}\uc2  are \u8211\'20\'13 but not all, and not at the rate required to meet the shortfall. Proposed remedies to the situation from the corporate-loving Tory government have so far included: offering Brexit-hypocritical short-term visas to restore EU workers to the jobs, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/politics/military-drivers-petrol-stations-fuel-b1926186.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deploying the army}}}\uc2  and using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://metro.co.uk/2021/09/30/dominic-raab-ex-prisoners-and-asylum-seekers-should-fill-uk-staff-shortages-15339584/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prison labour}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Is Australia any different? Only in that our labour shortages are presently more patchy. The \uc2 Australian Chamber of Commerce and Industry{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2021/oct/21/labour-shortages-may-cause-farmers-to-lose-crops-and-consumers-to-face-food-price-hikes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called for the numbers}}}\uc2  of skilled migrants to be doubled to 200,000 people a year. And a terrifying ABC piece recently celebrated the introduction of a Hunger Games employment paradigm for local strawberry picking. Ten strawberry pickers were to be given a one in a hundred chance of winning a $100,000 bonus in a lottery if they signed on to the job. Employers could have, of course, just paid those people an extra $1,000. I guess they didn\u8217\'20\'19t want the employees to share collective confidence in their own labour value.\par \par \uc2 That would rather cause the whole rotten system to come crashing down, wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t it? And that \u8211\'20\'13 as the neoliberals and employers know \u8211\'20\'13 is the truth that lies beneath.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211028ehas000ma\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
