\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Striketober\u8217\'20\'19 is showing workers\u8217\'20\'19 rising power \u8211\'20\'13 but will it lead to lasting change?\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,681 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2021\par 21:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A post-pandemic labor shortage has given workers leverage but experts doubt it will lead to a sustained rise in union membership\par \par \uc2 US labor unions have been on the defensive for decades but this October there has been a surprising burst of worker militancy and strikes as workers have gone on the offensive to demand more. Experts are predicting more actions to come but whether \u8220\'20\'1CStriketober\u8221\'20\'1D can lead to permanent change remains an open question.\par \par \uc2 The scale of industrial action is truly remarkable. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/oct/14/john-deere-workers-strike-contract-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ten thousand John Deere}}}\uc2  workers have gone on strike, 1,400 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/oct/07/kelloggs-workers-strike-offshore-jobs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kellogg workers}}}\uc2  have walked out, as well as a walkout threatened by more than 30,000 \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  workers, all inflamed by a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/oct/01/us-labor-unrest-unions-strikes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 profound disconnect}}}\uc2  between labor and management.\par \par \uc2 Many frontline workers \u8211\'20\'13 after working so hard and risking their lives during the pandemic \u8211\'20\'13 say they deserve substantial raises along with lots of gratitude. With this in mind and with myriad employers complaining of a labor shortage, many workers believe it\u8217\'20\'19s an opportune time to demand more and go on strike. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t hurt that there\u8217\'20\'19s a strongly pro-union president in the White House and there\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://news.gallup.com/poll/354455/approval-labor-unions-highest-point-1965.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more public support for unions than in decades}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But some corporations are acting as if nothing has changed and they can continue corporate America\u8217\'20\'19s decades-long practice of squeezing workers and demanding concessions, even after corporate profits have soared.\par \par \uc2 This attitude doesn\u8217\'20\'19t sit well with Chris Laursen, who earns $20.82 an hour after 19 years at Deere\u8217\'20\'19s farm equipment factory in Ottumwa, Iowa. Laursen is upset that Deere is offering just a one-dollar-an-hour raise and wants to eliminate pensions for future hires even when Deere anticipates a record $5.7bn in profits this year, more than double last year\u8217\'20\'19s earnings.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe were deemed essential workers right out of the gate,\u8221\'20\'1D Laursen said, noting that many workers racked up lots of overtime during the pandemic. \u8220\'20\'1CBut then they came with an offer that was appallingly low. It was a slap in the face of the workers who created all the wealth for them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Many Deere workers complain that the company offered only a 12% raise over six years, which they say won\u8217\'20\'19t keep pace with inflation, even as the CEO\u8217\'20\'19s pay rose {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.execpay.org/news/deere-and-co-2020-compensation-335"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 160% last year to $16m}}}\uc2  and dividends were raised 17%. Deere\u8217\'20\'19s workers voted down the company\u8217\'20\'19s offer by 90% before they went on strike at 14 factories on 14 October, their first walkout in 35 years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe really showed up during the pandemic and kept building equipment for them,\u8221\'20\'1D Laursen said. \u8220\'20\'1CNow we want something back. The stars are finally lined up for us, and we had to bring the fight.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Thomas Kochan, an MIT professor of industrial relations, agreed that it was a favorable time for workers \u8211\'20\'13 many corporations have substantially increased pay in response to the labor shortage. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s clear that workers are much more empowered,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re empowered because of the labor shortage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kochan added: \u8220\'20\'1CThese strikes could easily trigger more strike activity if several are successful or perceived to be successful.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Robert Bruno, a labor relations professor at the University of Illinois, said workers have built up a lot of grievances and anger during the pandemic, after years of seeing scant improvement in pay and benefits. Bruno pointed to a big reason for the growing worker frustration: \u8220\'20\'1CYou can definitely see that American capitalism has reigned supreme over workers, and as a result, the incentive for companies is to continue to do what\u8217\'20\'19s been working for them. It\u8217\'20\'19s likely that an arrogance sets in where companies think that\u8217\'20\'19s going to last for ever, and maybe they don\u8217\'20\'19t read the times properly.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kevin Bradshaw, a striker at Kellogg\u8217\'20\'19s factory in Memphis, said the cereal maker was being arrogant and unappreciative. During the pandemic, he said, Kellogg employees often worked 30 days in a row, often in 12-hour or 16-hour shifts.\par \par \uc2 In light of this hard work, he derided Kellogg\u8217\'20\'19s contract offer, which calls for a far lower scale for new hires. \u8220\'20\'1CKellogg is offering a $13 cut in top pay for new workers,\u8221\'20\'1D Bradshaw said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey want a permanent two-tier. New employees will no longer receive the same amount of money and benefits we do.\u8221\'20\'1D That, he said, is bad for the next generation of workers.\par \par \uc2 Bradshaw, vice-president of the \uc2 Bakery, Confectionery, Tobacco Workers and Grain Millers\uc2  union local, noted that it made painful concessions to Kellogg in 2015. \u8220\'20\'1CWe gave so many concessions, and now they\u8217\'20\'19re saying they need more,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a real smack in the face during the pandemic. Everyone knows that they\u8217\'20\'19re greedy and not needy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kellogg said its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/10/06/business/kellogg-cereal-plants-strike/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 compensation is among the industry\u8217\'20\'19s best}}}\uc2  and its offer will help the company meet competitive challenges. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/money/business/2021/10/14/strike-begins-deere-after-workers-reject-contract-90-margin/8440145002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Deere said it was determined to reach an agreement }}}\uc2  and continue to make its workers \u8220\'20\'1Cthe highest paid employees in the agriculture industry\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 There are many strikes beyond Deere and Kellogg. More than 400 workers at the Heaven Hill bourbon distillery in Kentucky have been on strike for six weeks, while roughly 1,000 Warrior Met coalminers in Alabama have been on strike since April. Hundreds of nurses at Mercy hospital in Buffalo went on strike on 1 October, and 450 steelworkers at Special Metals in Huntington, West Virginia, also walked out that day. More than 30,000 nurses and other healthcare professionals at \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  on the west coast have voted to authorize a strike.\par \par \uc2 Sixty thousand Hollywood production employees threatened to go on strike last Monday, unhappy that film and TV companies were not taking their concerns about overwork and exhaustion seriously. But seeing that the union was serious about staging its first-ever strike, Hollywood producers flinched, agreed to compromises, and the two sides reached a settlement.\par \par \uc2 Noting that \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2 , a non-profit, had amassed $45bn in reserves, Belinda Redding, a Kaiser nurse in Woodland Hills, California, said, \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve been going all out during the pandemic. We\u8217\'20\'19ve been working extra shifts. Our lives have been turned upside down. The signs were up all over saying, \u8216\'20\'18Heroes Work Here\u8217\'20\'19. And the pandemic isn\u8217\'20\'19t even over for us, and then for them to offer us a 1% raise, it\u8217\'20\'19s almost a slap in the face.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Redding is also fuming that management has proposed hiring new nurses at 26% less pay than current ones earn \u8211\'20\'13 which she said would ensure a shortage of nurses. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s hard to imagine a nurse giving her all when she\u8217\'20\'19s paid far less than other nurses,\u8221\'20\'1D Redding said.\par \par \uc2 Kaiser said that its employees earn 26% more than average market wages and that its services would become unaffordable unless it restrains labor costs.\par \par \uc2 On a smaller scale but in an industry in increasing demand, striking workers at one of the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest bourbon producers were scheduled to vote on a new contract on Saturday, a day after announcing a tentative agreement with Heaven Hill, the producer of Evan Williams bourbon.\par \par \uc2 About 420 members of United Food and Commercial Workers Local 23D went on strike about six weeks ago, forming picket lines at the company\u8217\'20\'19s operations in Bardstown, Kentucky, with the dispute revolving around healthcare and scheduling.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, many non-union workers \u8211\'20\'13 frequently dismayed with low pay, volatile schedules and poor treatment \u8211\'20\'13 have quit their jobs or refused to return to their old ones after being laid off during the pandemic. In August, 4.2 million workers quit their jobs, part of what has been called the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/jul/03/us-jobs-report-june-trend"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Great Resignation}}}\uc2 . Some economists have suggested this is a quiet general strike with workers demanding better pay and conditions. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are using exit from their jobs as a source of power,\u8221\'20\'1D Kochan said.\par \par \uc2 As for unionized workers, some labor experts see parallels between today\u8217\'20\'19s burst of strikes and the much larger wave of strikes after the first and second world wars. As with the pandemic, those catastrophic wars caused many Americans to reassess their lives and jobs and ask: after what we\u8217\'20\'19ve been through, don\u8217\'20\'19t we deserve better pay and conditions?\par \par \uc2 Professor Bruno said that in light of today\u8217\'20\'19s increased worker militancy, unionized employers would have to rethink their approach to bargaining \u8220\'20\'1Cand take the rank and file pretty seriously\u8221\'20\'1D. They can no longer expect workers to roll over or to strong-arm them into swallowing concessions, often by threatening to move operations overseas.\par \par \uc2 Bruno questioned whether the surge in strikes will be long-lasting. He predicts that the improvements in pay and job quality will be long-lasting, adding that that was more likely than unions substantially increasing their membership. He said that if workers see others winning better wages and conditions through strikes, that will raise unions\u8217\'20\'19 visibility and lead to more workers voting to join unions.\par \par \uc2 Despite the recent turbulence, Ruth Milkman, a sociologist of labor at \uc2 City University of New York\uc2 , foresees a return to the status quo. \u8220\'20\'1CI think things will go back to where they were once things settle down,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe labor shortage is not necessarily going to last.\u8221\'20\'1D She sees the number of strikes declining once the labor shortage ends.\par \par \uc2 In her view, union membership isn\u8217\'20\'19t likely to increase markedly because \u8220\'20\'1Cthey\u8217\'20\'19re not doing that much organizing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a little\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 like the unionization efforts at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in Buffalo and at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cbut it\u8217\'20\'19s not as if there\u8217\'20\'19s some big push.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A big question, Milkman said, was how can today\u8217\'20\'19s labor momentum be sustained? She said it would help if Congress passed the Protecting the Right to Organize Act, which would make it easier to unionize workers. That law would spur unions to do more organizing and increase their chances of winning union drives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat would be a real shot in the arm,\u8221\'20\'1D Milkman said.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press contributed reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211023ehan000me\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
