\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 Netflix\uc2  employees join wave of tech activism with walkout over Chappelle controversy\b0\par\par\uc2 Kari Paul in San Francisco \par \uc2 1,404 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 October 2021\par 22:58 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Slew of walkouts by tech workers, unthinkable mere years ago, shows workers \u8216\'20\'18now understand their labor power\u8217\'20\'19, expert says\par \par \uc2 Employees at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/netflix"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Netflix}}}\uc2  halted work on Wednesday and staged a protest outside the company\u8217\'20\'19s Los Gatos, California, headquarters to condemn the streaming platform\u8217\'20\'19s handling of complaints against Dave Chappelle\u8217\'20\'19s new special.\par \par \uc2 The actions \u8211\'20\'13 which hundreds participated in \u8211\'20\'13 are the latest in a string of highly visible organizing efforts in the tech sector, as workers increasingly take their grievances about company policies and decisions public.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThree years ago, a worker walkout at a major tech company would have been unthinkable,\u8221\'20\'1D said Veena Dubal, a labor law professor at the University of California, Hastings. \u8220\'20\'1CWhite-collar workers across the world now understand their labor power, and their ability to change the unethical practices of their employer by withholding their labor.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the transgender employee resources group behind the walkout released a list of specific demands of \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , including more funding for trans creators, recruiting more diverse employees and flagging anti-trans content on the platform.\par \par \uc2 Tensions at \uc2 Netflix\uc2  started in early October, when \uc2 Netflix\uc2  leaders doubled down on their support for the comedian Dave Chappelle following criticism from viewers, the queer media watchdog Glaad as well as some employees that Chappelle\u8217\'20\'19s new show contained jokes that were anti-trans.\par \par \uc2 As internal criticism grew, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  leaders continued to defend the special. \uc2 Reed Hastings\uc2 , the co-chief executive, reportedly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/10/15/business/dave-chappelle-netflix-hannah-gadsby.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  on an internal message board: \u8220\'20\'1CI do believe that our commitment to artistic expression and pleasing our members is the right long-term choice for \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , and that we are on the right side, but only time will tell.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ted Sarandos\uc2 , the other co-CEO, claimed in an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://variety.com/2021/film/news/ted-sarandos-dave-chappelle-defense-1235088647/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 email obtained by Variety}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CWhile some employees disagree, we have a strong belief that content on screen doesn\u8217\'20\'19t directly translate to real-world harm.\u8221\'20\'1D He added: \u8220\'20\'1CAdults can watch violence, assault and abuse \u8211\'20\'13 or enjoy shocking standup comedy \u8211\'20\'13 without it causing them to harm others.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Sarandos memo in particular fueled the walkout, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/digital/netflix-dave-chappelle-walkout-1235031048/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Hollywood Reporter}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe memo was very disrespectful,\u8221\'20\'1D a staffer told the outlet on the condition of anonymity. \u8220\'20\'1CIt didn\u8217\'20\'19t invite a robust conversation about this hard topic, and that\u8217\'20\'19s normally how things go.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  temporarily suspended Terra Field, a trans employee, who had tweeted that Chappelle \u8220\'20\'1Cattacks the trans community, and the very validity of transness\u8221\'20\'1D and tied such comments to real-world violence. The company said Field was suspended because she had attended a meeting she was not invited to, but it later conceded she had \u8220\'20\'1Cno ill intent\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Netflix{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theverge.com/2021/10/15/22728337/netflix-fires-organizer-trans-employee-walkout-dave-chappelle"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fired }}}\uc2  another trans worker who had been involved in organizing the walkout on allegations of leaking internal documents to the press.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe understand this employee may have been motivated by disappointment and hurt with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/netflix"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Netflix}}}\uc2 , but maintaining a culture of trust and transparency is core to our company,\u8221\'20\'1D a Netflix spokesperson told \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  about that decision last week.\par \par \uc2 The employee on Tuesday identified themself as B Pagels-Minor in an interview with the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and denied \u8220\'20\'1Cleaking sensitive information to the press\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Social media {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/events/420594006168281/?acontext=%7B%22ref%22%3A%2252%22%2C%22action_history%22%3A%22[%7B%5C%22surface%5C%22%3A%5C%22share_link%5C%22%2C%5C%22mechanism%5C%22%3A%5C%22share_link%5C%22%2C%5C%22extra_data%5C%22%3A%7B%5C%22invite_link_id%5C%22%3A248797827301147%7D%7D]%22%7D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 event}}}\uc2  pages for the walkout have advertised a rally outside the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  headquarters in Los Angeles featuring public figures and speakers.\par \par \uc2 Staffers participating in the virtual walkout have vowed to halt work and focus on efforts to support the trans community.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A wave of worker walkouts\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 In this week alone, there are protests at \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , the grocery delivery platform {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/oct/15/instacart-workers-strike-pay"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Instacart}}}\uc2  and at \uc2 Facebook\uc2  by its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theverge.com/2021/10/19/22726915/facebook-content-moderators-protest-low-wages"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 content moderators}}}\uc2 . Uber drivers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2019/may/08/uber-drivers-strike-over-pay-and-conditions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 globally}}}\uc2  went on strike in 2019. Hundreds of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/recode/2019/9/20/20874497/amazon-climate-change-walkout-google-microsoft-strike-tech-activism"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 walked out}}}\uc2  to protest against the company\u8217\'20\'19s climate policies in 2019.\par \par \uc2 Walkouts have become an increasingly common tactic among tech employees. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are seeing a wave of them,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jess Kutch, executive director of the Solidarity Fund, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://coworkerfund.org/contribute-to-the-emergency-fund/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 raises money}}}\uc2  to support employees engaged in workplace organizing \u8211\'20\'13 including at \uc2 Netflix\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  employees were among the first to deploy the strategy on a large scale in 2018, when more than 20,000 workers around the world walked out over the news that the company had given a $90m severance package to an executive who was forced to step down over sexual misconduct allegations (which he has denied).\par \par \uc2 The incensed workers decried a culture of silence about sexual harassment and systemic racism and demanded \uc2 Google\uc2  make concrete changes to address such issues within the company. In particular, they targeted \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s use of forced arbitration \u8211\'20\'13 a practice common in the tech industry in which workers settle legal disputes in a private forum, making it almost impossible for workers to sue their bosses in court and keep repeat offenders from being publicly recognized.\par \par \uc2 The November 2018 action changed the way workers in the tech industry organize, experts said. \u8220\'20\'1CWorkers are observing their peers to see what is effective in moving decision makers, and replicating that in their own companies,\u8221\'20\'1D Kutch said.\par \par \uc2 Kutch noted tech employees studied other protest movements to determine the most effective forms of action, learning, for example, to release specific demands tied to their walkouts. \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a degree of depth, commitment and planning that was not present even just a few years ago,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Organizers have particularly taken aim at the tools tech companies had long used to keep dissent internal. Faced with employee pressure, companies such as \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 eBay\uc2  were compelled to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2018/11/19/google-facebook-airbnb-employees-can-now-sue-over-sexual-harassment.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 end}}}\uc2  forced arbitration practices.\par \par \uc2 Employees have also fought companies\u8217\'20\'19 use of non-disclosure agreements, or NDAs, which were initially meant to protect trade secrets, but later allowed companies to keep accusations of wrongdoing from becoming public.\par \par \uc2 Last month, California {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/oct/08/california-companies-can-no-longer-silence-workers-in-victory-for-tech-activists"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed}}}\uc2  a law that makes it illegal for firms to prevent employees from speaking out about such issues through the use of NDAs.\par \par \uc2 Organizing gained another boost when the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2  and protests laid bare some of the huge inequities in tech and revealed the power of protest to change them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWorkers woke up at that moment to the fact that if employers are able to discriminate against any one part of the workforce, it hurts everyone,\u8221\'20\'1D said Anastasia Christman, senior policy analyst at the National Employment Law Project.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere have been isolated examples of this kind of thing for years, but employees are increasingly using the leverage of their labor to stand up for diversity and equity,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 The price of whistleblowing\par \par \uc2 For some employees, the price of speaking out has been steep. Leaked memos {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2020/apr/02/amazon-chris-smalls-smart-articulate-leaked-memo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 showed}}}\uc2  that in early 2020, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  discussed smearing a warehouse worker who spoke out against the company\u8217\'20\'19s Covid-19 practices and was later fired. (\uc2 Amazon\uc2  said the employee was fired for putting other employees at risk of Covid-19.) In September 2021, \uc2 Amazon{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/09/29/amazon-settles-with-employees-who-said-they-were-fired-over-activism.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reached a settlement}}}\uc2  with two other employees who said they had been fired over their climate activism within the company.\par \par \uc2 Other whistleblowers have narrated how their lives were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/oct/08/tech-whistleblowers-facebook-frances-haugen-amazon-google-pinterest"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 upended}}}\uc2  by speaking out against major tech companies. The worker behind the walkouts at \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Claire Stapleton\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2019/jun/07/google-walkout-organizer-claire-stapleton-resigns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 left the company}}}\uc2  after 12 years of working there, due to perceived retaliation for her role in organizing.\par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  told \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  in an email that it \u8220\'20\'1Crespect[s] the decision of any employee who chooses to walk out\u8221\'20\'1D and recognizes \u8220\'20\'1Cwe have much more work to do both within \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and in our content\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe value our trans colleagues and allies, and understand the deep hurt that\u8217\'20\'19s been caused,\u8221\'20\'1D the spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 In a public blogpost, Field outlined much of the vitriol she has sustained for speaking out about the special. She said she did not necessarily want the show removed from the platform, but wanted accountability from \uc2 Netflix\uc2  to its workers and viewers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve spent years building out the company\u8217\'20\'19s policies and benefits so that it would be a great place for trans people to work,\u8221\'20\'1D she wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CA place can\u8217\'20\'19t be a great place to work if someone has to betray their community to do so.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Netflix CEO Sarandos told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/digital/netflix-ted-sarandos-dave-chappelle-walkout-1235033910/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Hollywood Reporter}}}\uc2  on Tuesday that he handled the situation poorly, but that he remains supportive of Chappelle\u8217\'20\'19s work. He said that his previous memos \u8220\'20\'1Clacked humanity\u8221\'20\'1D, and did not acknowledge that \u8220\'20\'1Ca group of our employees were in pain\u8221\'20\'1D, but said that his stance \u8220\'20\'1Chadn\u8217\'20\'19t changed\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211020ehak000mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
