\par\par \uc2 Culture\par \b \uc2 Trevor Noah on \u8216\'20\'18Strike-tober\u8217\'20\'19: \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s not a step workers take lightly\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Adrian Horton \par \uc2 759 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 October 2021\par 15:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Late-night hosts discuss workers from healthcare to Hollywood demanding better conditions, and global supply chain issues\par \par \uc2 Trevor Noah\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJobs: it\u8217\'20\'19s how Americans prove they deserve healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D joked Trevor Noah at the outset of Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s Daily Show, in which he discussed the wave of labor unrest in America\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/oct/01/us-labor-unrest-unions-strikes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStrike-tober\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Companies from John Deere to Kellogg\u8217\'20\'19s have been underpaying and overworking their employees, and workers are finally saying enough is enough.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a look at Strike-tober. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/DkIpwWFF2e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/DkIpwWFF2e}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 The Daily Show (@TheDailyShow) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TheDailyShow/status/1450650790855598082?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 20, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith more job openings than ever and more people quitting than ever, workers suddenly find themselves with a lot of leverage,\u8221\'20\'1D he continued as tens of thousands of workers from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/oct/15/hollywood-workers-pay-conditions-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hollywood}}}\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2021/jul/30/us-nurses-strike-covid-coronavirus-conditions-understaffing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 healthcare}}}\uc2  push for better conditions and pay. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd they\u8217\'20\'19re using it to demand things like better pay, more flexible hours and cancelling the annual company Squid Game.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Not all companies are agreeing to the demands. Last week, over 10,000 workers at farm equipment John Deere {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/oct/14/john-deere-workers-strike-contract-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 went on strike}}}\uc2 , the first by the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  in 35 years. More than 24,000 workers at the healthcare conglomerate \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  voted to authorize a strike, and workers at the cereal giant Kellogg also walked off work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s none of the bad industries you wish would go on strike,\u8221\'20\'1D Noah lamented. \u8220\'20\'1CYou know the people who collect student loans? They never go on strike. Or the telemarketers? Come on, you guys deserve better pay!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On a more serious note, \u8220\'20\'1Cgoing on strike is not a step that workers take lightly\u8221\'20\'1D, he added. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a major decision, you know? You risk your job, you lost out on pay, you have to protest in front of your workplace but you can\u8217\'20\'19t go in to pee. Which means that when people do go on strike, they probably have pretty good reasons for doing it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 John Deere, for example, reported profits of $4.7bn in the first three quarters of 2021, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s22.q4cdn.com/253594569/files/doc_downloads/2021/08/Deere-Company-Q3-2021-10-Q.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 spent}}}\uc2  over $1.7bn on stock buybacks in the first nine months and paid out $761m in dividends to shareholders; CEO John C May\u8217\'20\'19s salary grew by 160% during the pandemic. But the company planned to eliminate pensions for new workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome of these CEOs get so greedy that they become shortsighted,\u8221\'20\'1D said Noah. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause if they thought about it, they would realize they could probably get away with exploiting their workers for longer if they just exploited them a little less.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI hope that companies start treating their employees like people, and not just money-making machines. And if they don\u8217\'20\'19t do that, then they should at least be honest in their TV commercials,\u8221\'20\'1D he concluded over an amended Frosted Flakes ad boasting the brand as the \u8220\'20\'1Conly cereal made with the tears of the people who made it!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Stephen Colbert\par \par \uc2 And on the Late Show, Stephen Colbert discussed the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/oct/02/supply-chain-world-economy-energy-labour-transport-covid"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 global supply chain issues}}}\uc2  plaguing American supermarkets. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause of Covid lockdowns and labor shortages and a lack of shipping containers, everything you want is either not made yet, stuck on a boat, or waiting for a trucker who can\u8217\'20\'19t drive because the Gatorade bottle he needs to pee in is stuck on a boat or hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been made yet,\u8221\'20\'1D he joked.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow, global supply chain problems are leading to empty shelves at grocery stores. It\u8217\'20\'19s so bad that \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  has had to change their name to \u8216\'20\'18Part Foods\u8217\'20\'19.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Supply chain issues have left shelves bare of fish sticks, frozen meals and, in a \u8220\'20\'1Cdevastating blow to divorced dads\u8221\'20\'1D, Marie Callender\u8217\'20\'19s pot pies. \u8220\'20\'1CBrace yourselves, ladies and gentlemen, because America is running low on \uc2 Ben & Jerry\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Colbert said. \u8220\'20\'1CI scream, you scream, we all scream \u8216\'20\'18where\u8217\'20\'19s the fucking ice cream?!\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The company said it will focus on producing its most popular flavors \u8211\'20\'13 including Colbert\u8217\'20\'19s own Americone Dream \u8211\'20\'13 but \u8220\'20\'1Cyou might be in trouble if you prefer one of \uc2 Ben & Jerry\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  less popular flavors, like Chunks of Real Monkey, Cherry Orbach, and Inattentive Hubby,\u8221\'20\'1D he joked.\par \par \uc2 The most concerning blow, he continued, is a shortage of wine. \u8220\'20\'1CFrozen foods? Fine. Candy? Who needs it. But a wine shortage? Me and my book club are buying zip ties and storming the Capitol.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The shortage is due in part to a \u8220\'20\'1Cglass bottle crunch\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cincidentally, \u8216\'20\'18glass bottle crunch\u8217\'20\'19 is one of \uc2 Ben & Jerry\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  least popular flavors,\u8221\'20\'1D Colbert quipped.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211020ehak001gy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
