\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Hollywood strike averted after union and producers reach last-minute deal\b0\par\par\uc2 Staff and agencies \par \uc2 551 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2021\par 10:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Up to 60,000 behind-the-scenes workers had been set to strike from Monday over claims of excessive hours and unliveable wages\par \par \uc2 An 11th-hour deal between producers and the union representing 60,000 film and television workers has averted a strike that threatened to cause widespread disruption in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 The International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees, which includes camera operators, makeup artists, sound technicians and others, said negotiators agreed to a new three-year contract on Saturday, ahead of a Monday deadline that would have seen them walk off the job.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a Hollywood ending,\u8221\'20\'1D Matthew Loeb, president of the union, said in an emailed statement. \u8220\'20\'1COur members stood firm. They\u8217\'20\'19re tough and united.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe went toe to toe with some of the richest and most powerful entertainment and tech companies in the world, and we havenow reached an agreement with the AMPTP that meets our members\u8217\'20\'19 needs.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/861fwvQNii"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/861fwvQNii}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 IATSE // #IASolidarity (@IATSE) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/IATSE/status/1449544996764409860?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 17, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The workers still must vote to approve the deal, but the strike has been called off with the tentative agreement, avoiding a serious setback for an industry that had just gotten back to work after long pandemic shutdowns.\par \par \uc2 Jarryd Gonzales, spokesperson for the Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers, which represented the studios and other entertainment companies in negotiations, confirmed the agreement to the Associated Press.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGood for @IATSE for standing your ground. And don\u8217\'20\'19t forget we got your back anytime you need us,\u8221\'20\'1D comedian, actor and writer Patton Oswalt said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Another actor, comic and writer, Yvette Nicole Brown, tweeted \u8220\'20\'1C#UnionStrong!\u8221\'20\'1D along with a link to a story reporting the agreement.\par \par \uc2 The effects of the strike would have been immediate, with crews not only on long-term productions but daily series including network talkshows walking off their jobs.\par \par \uc2 The union represents cinematographers, camera operators, set designers, carpenters, hair and makeup artists and many others.\par \par \uc2 Union members said previous contracts allowed their employers to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/oct/13/film-industry-workers-authorize-strike-negotiations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 force them to work excessive hours}}}\uc2  and deny them reasonable rest via meal breaks and sufficient time off between shifts.\par \par \uc2 Leaders said the lowest paid crafts were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/07/hollywood-film-industry-union-wages-conditions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 receiving unliveable wages}}}\uc2  and streaming outlets including \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , Apple and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  were allowed to work them even harder for less money.\par \par \uc2 Details of the new contracts were not immediately revealed.\par \par \uc2 The union reported on 4 October that its members had voted overwhelmingly to authorise a strike, setting off industry-wide fears, but negotiations immediately resumed between the union and producers.\par \par \uc2 A Monday strike deadline was set on Wednesday when talks stagnated, but the union said subsequent negotiations were productive.\par \par \uc2 It would have been the first nationwide strike in the 128-year history of the alliance, and would have affected not just the Los Angeles area and New York but growing production hubs like Georgia, New Mexico and Colorado.\par \par \uc2 During negotiations, many prominent names in entertainment spoke out in favour of the union\u8217\'20\'19s demands, including Octavia Spencer, Mindy Kaling and Jane Fonda. The \uc2 Directors Guild of America\uc2  issued a statement of solidarity too, signed by the likes of Steven Spielberg, Christopher Nolan, Barry Jenkins, Ron Howard and Ava DuVernay.\par \par \uc2 Associated Press and Reuters contributed to this report\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211018ehai00163\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
