\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We are not machines\u8217\'20\'19: Hollywood workers poised to strike for better conditions\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,031 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2021\par 16:55 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Union leaders say a strike will start Monday if there is no deal with studios as workers describe low pay and grueling days without breaks\par \par \uc2 At the start of the pandemic, Hollywood productions abruptly shut down, leaving many workers out of work before things began to resume with Covid-19 safety protocols in place.\par \par \uc2 Since then, workers in Hollywood say they have worked long schedules and endured increased workloads, including staggering work because of social distancing; wearing and distributing personal protective equipment through long work days; and regularly getting tested for Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe were working at breakneck speeds, and that was something that was supposed to have changed. We were supposed to have the time we needed to work in that kind of environment,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mike Loomer, a set dresser in Hollywood and International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees (IATSE) Local 44 member.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe only thing that changed is what we had to endure to make the product that they had to have to get out for the public to see.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Studios will again go quiet around the US as IATSE leaders say its 60,000 members will go on strike from 12.01 PT on Monday if a deal is not reached with employers. It would be the first such national strike since the IATSE was formed 128 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Members of the union include cinematographers, camera operators, set designers, carpenters, hair and makeup artists, animators and many other workers \u8211\'20\'13 and action would disrupt productions in major industry hubs from Los Angeles to Atlanta and New York.\par \par \uc2 Loomer said workers in Hollywood have dealt with the added stress of worrying about contracting Covid-19, and seeing production days increase from 12 hours to 14-hour and 16-hour days, working through meal breaks.Workers are leaving sets late Friday nights into early Saturday morning.\par \par \uc2 Union members {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/culture/archive/2021/10/iatse-strike-hollywood-labor-conditions/620350/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 say }}}\uc2  these grueling schedules have led to car accidents during work commutes and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instagram.com/ia_stories/?hl=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 caused or worsened}}}\uc2  medical problems.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat ramp-up in production just made the work and life situation unbearable. We can\u8217\'20\'19t work at that feverish pace. We are not machines. We are people that need rest,\u8221\'20\'1D Loomer said. \u8220\'20\'1CWorking in Hollywood is not just wrap parties and red-carpet events. It is a factory job, where we move the factory and set it up every day before we do our shift. The amount of physical labor that is required to make an hour of television \u8230\'20\'26 it\u8217\'20\'19s unbelievable to the average person.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Thousands of workers in the US film and TV industry are fighting to change these difficult schedules and improve wages and benefits in new union contract negotiations, as entertainment streaming services have ramped up production throughout the pandemic while reaping a surge in profits. Streaming services in the US {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/543719-streaming-service-subscriptions-reached-11b-globally-during-pandemic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  a 32% increase in subscriptions last year, and the US home and entertainment market saw a $30bn rise in revenue in 2020, a 21% increase from 2019.\par \par \uc2 On 4 October, thousands of film and TV workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://iatse.net/by-a-nearly-unanimous-margin-iatse-members-in-tv-and-film-production-vote-to-authorize-a-nationwide-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted}}}\uc2  to authorize their union, the International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees, to grant the IATSE president, Matthew Loeb, the authority to call a strike. Negotiations for a new three-year union contract with the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  (AMPTP) have been going on since May.\par \par \uc2 It would be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/10/08/opinion/hollywood-iatse-movies-amazon-streaming.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the largest private sector strike }}}\uc2  since 74,000 \uc2 General Motors\uc2  workers went on strike in 2007. A work stoppage would shut down film and television production across the US.\par \par \uc2 Other Hollywood unions, including Sag-Aftra and the Writers Guild of America, several celebrity actors, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://de6167c5-9f06-4e54-81fb-73ab5a07b817.filesusr.com/ugd/a37828_1c2a622a43584099bbf4b8d401265431.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 elected officials }}}\uc2  have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2021/09/hollywood-strike-dga-sag-aftra-wga-east-teamsters-back-iatse-1234844027/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 publicly supported}}}\uc2  IATSE\u8217\'20\'19s demands for a new, fair union contract in the wake of the strike authorization vote.\par \par \uc2 The threat of a strike among Hollywood workers and other large groups of workers around the US have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/unitehere/status/1447664653002289152"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inspired calls }}}\uc2  from labor unions to take advantage of the opportunity presented by the collective action and energy from these labor actions.\par \par \uc2 Some 53,411 workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://iatse.net/by-a-nearly-unanimous-margin-iatse-members-in-tv-and-film-production-vote-to-authorize-a-nationwide-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cast}}}\uc2  ballots in the strike authorization vote, with 98.68% voting in favor. Thirty-six local unions participated in the vote, including 13 locals on the west coast and 26 locals in Georgia, Illinois, New Mexico and Louisiana, with all locals voting at least by 96% in favor of the strike authorization.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday Loeb said the union will continue bargaining with producers on core issues, such as reasonable rest periods, meal breaks and a living wage for those on the bottom of the wage scale. \u8220\'20\'1CHowever, the pace of bargaining doesn\u8217\'20\'19t reflect any sense of urgency,\u8221\'20\'1D Loeb said. \u8220\'20\'1CWithout an end date, we could keep talking forever. Our members deserve to have their basic needs addressed now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The union {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2021/10/iatse-strike-picket-signs-contract-talks-1234854187/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prepared}}}\uc2  picket signs over the past weekend in anticipation of the strike.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2021/10/hollywood-strike-threat-iatse-producers-contract-1234852900/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 According to union officials}}}\uc2 , some movement has been made in negotiations, with a strike action as the last option if they feel employers refuse to move further on contract priorities unless forced to do so.\par \par \uc2 Contract negotiations are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2021/10/fifth-day-of-do-or-die-iatse-contract-talks-end-for-the-day-will-resume-monday-1234853391/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ongoing}}}\uc2 , and though no details have been publicly announced, the industry and unions are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://variety.com/2021/film/news/iatse-talks-resume-monday-1235085410/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bracing}}}\uc2  for a possible work stoppage if an agreement isn\u8217\'20\'19t reached soon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe product that we\u8217\'20\'19re making is much more valuable, but yet the contracts have not changed. If anything, they\u8217\'20\'19ve gone in reverse, so our labor is worth more, but we\u8217\'20\'19re not getting paid more, and that\u8217\'20\'19s not fair,\u8221\'20\'1D said Victor Bouzi, a sound mixer and IATSE local 695 member in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 He said that through the pandemic, he has been working so much that he often has nightmares about work. But film industry workers are standing up to these conditions as part of a broader labor movement fight against multibillion-dollar companies like \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , Apple, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , which are deeply embedded in the entertainment industry.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe workers are getting squeezed more and more for their labor as the profits are getting higher,\u8221\'20\'1D added Bouzi. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s the cost. Our mental health to make this product that we\u8217\'20\'19re not being fully compensated for, and people are starting to understand that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211015ehaf0015x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
