\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Largest US union federation\u8217\'20\'19s first female president takes charge at critical moment\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,431 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2021\par 11:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Liz Shuler\uc2  leads \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  as public approval for organized labor is on the rise, young workers are flocking into unions and millions seek better conditions\par \par \uc2 For 12 years, \uc2 Liz Shuler\uc2  was No 2 to \uc2 Richard Trumka\uc2 , president of the AFL-CIO, main union federation in the US, but after Trumka\u8217\'20\'19s unexpected death in August, Shuler became the its first-ever female president at a time that organized labor in the US faces deep problems \u8211\'20\'13 and extraordinary opportunities.\par \par \uc2 Many union members are hoping that Shuler, a well-liked, hard-working 51-year-old, will figure out how to capitalize on those opportunities. Public approval for organized labor in the US has climbed to its highest level in more than 50 years, as many young workers are flocking into unions and millions of overstressed, underpaid frontline workers are impatient to improve their lot.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe biggest challenge that labor faces is whether we\u8217\'20\'19re ready for the opportunities in front of us,\u8221\'20\'1D said April Sims, secretary-treasurer of the \uc2 Washington State Labor Council\uc2 , a federation of that state\u8217\'20\'19s unions. \u8220\'20\'1CUnion favorability is at a long-time high. We\u8217\'20\'19ve learned from Covid how much our society and economy depend on workers. We probably have the most pro-labor president since FDR. The challenge for us as a labor movement is, are we able to take advantage of this moment?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Shuler \u8211\'20\'13 and all of US labor \u8211\'20\'13 face some daunting obstacles.\par \par \uc2 A mere 6% of private-sector workers are in unions, the lowest level in a century. The Republican party is intent on weakening unions, and most US corporations \u8211\'20\'13 led by behemoths Amazon and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 are fiercely opposed to unionization.\par \par \uc2 Among many union leaders, there is an eagerness, sometimes bordering on desperation, to reverse labor\u8217\'20\'19s decades-long decline. \u8220\'20\'1CMy cry for 30 years is our biggest challenge is that collective bargaining has been systematically rubbed out \u8211\'20\'13 private-sector collective bargaining now covers just 6% of workers, having dropped from 30,\u8221\'20\'1D said Larry Cohen, former president of the Communications Workers of America and now chair of Our Revolution, a progressive group spun out of Bernie Sanders\u8217\'20\'19 2016 campaign. \u8220\'20\'1CI think \uc2 Liz Shuler\uc2  and the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  have to be almost singularly focused on that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There are many encouraging stories of workers pushing to unionize, Cohen said, pointing, for example, to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  employees in Buffalo. Shuler will undoubtedly back these efforts, Cohen said, but he said she needs to press the nation\u8217\'20\'19s unions to do more to support and expand organizing.\par \par \uc2 Bill Fletcher Jr, a former AFL-CIO education director and now executive editor of Global African Worker, said labor leaders need to see that unions are often using timeworn strategies to fight the last war. The field of battle has changed vastly for labor, Fletcher said. There are far more gig workers, tech workers and immigrant workers; corporations have grown more sophisticated in battling unions; and young workers are showing more interest in unionizing than in decades.\par \par \uc2 This is a completely different labor movement and what does that mean for the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Bill Fletcher\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s what \uc2 Liz Shuler\uc2  and other labor leaders have to think about,\u8221\'20\'1D Fletcher said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a completely different labor movement and what does that mean for the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 ?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 To help labor win on today\u8217\'20\'19s new battlefield, Fletcher said the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  and its member unions should marshal large amounts of resources to ensure some breakthrough victories \u8211\'20\'13 perhaps some big organizing wins in the south. That would create momentum and optimism and inspire more workers to turn to unions. Fletcher also called on Shuler to push for labor to do far more large-scale organizing, to unionize thousands of workers at a time.\par \par \uc2 After Trumka died on 5 August, a few union leaders floated the idea of electing a caretaker AFL-CIO president until the federation holds its convention next June. Some who backed the caretaker idea voiced concern that Shuler was not inspiring or visionary enough at a time labor could use lots more visibility and inspiration. Some leaders also voiced concern that Schuler, unlike Trumka \u8211\'20\'13 who once headed the United Mine Workers \u8211\'20\'13 had never led a union or a strike. Before becoming the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s secretary-treasurer, Shuler was an assistant to the president of the International Brotherhood of Electrical Workers.\par \par \uc2 But many union presidents brushed those concerns aside when they overwhelmingly elected Schuler on 20 August to fill Trumka\u8217\'20\'19s term through June. Many labor leaders like Shuler, respect her hard work, and believe that she can bring people together. Moreover, many felt there shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be a divisive leadership fight right after Trumka died.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLiz was a very effective second to Trumka,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2 , president of the American Federation of Teachers. As secretary-treasurer, Shuler oversaw the federation\u8217\'20\'19s finances and its outreach program to young workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople trust her because they know she will listen before she acts,\u8221\'20\'1D Weingarten said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a great amount of trust because of her collaborative nature. She doesn\u8217\'20\'19t see collaboration as kumbaya. She sees collaboration as a way to move a very disparate federation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  is a federation of 57 unions and nearly 10 million union members. It includes industrial unions like the steelworkers, public employee unions like the teachers, and unions representing the construction trades \u8211\'20\'13 generally the federation\u8217\'20\'19s most conservative unions. Some union leaders fear that Shuler is tied too closely with the conservative building trades.\par \par \uc2 But Jimmy Williams, president of the International Union of Painters, said, \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s ridiculous to say she\u8217\'20\'19s controlled by the building trades unions. Far too often things get made of where an individual comes from. Liz has spent the last 12 years at the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , representing union members as a whole.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trumka had signaled that he wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t run for another four-year term, and it was widely thought that two candidates would vie to succeed him: Shuler and \uc2 Sara Nelson\uc2 , president of the Association of Flight Attendants. Nelson has enthusiastic support among many rank-and-file workers because of her progressive politics, up-against-the-boss rhetoric and her gifts as a speaker.\par \par \uc2 Nelson declined to be interviewed, but one labor leader who knows her said she might run next June, in part to press her case that the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  needs to do far more organizing and mobilizing of workers.\par \par \uc2 We need more innovation and creativity. We can\u8217\'20\'19t continue to do things the way we\u8217\'20\'19ve always done them.\par \par \uc2 April Sims\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a longstanding debate about whether the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  should mainly service its member unions and do lobbying for them or whether it should be a forceful leader, pressing unions to do more on organizing, politics, immigrant rights and civil rights. Some union presidents want Schuler to just serve member unions, but others say that to reverse labor\u8217\'20\'19s slide, she needs to turn the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  into a more muscular institution that plays a bigger role in organizing and politics.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople grow into roles, and I think she\u8217\'20\'19ll grow into this role,\u8221\'20\'1D Weingarten said.\par \par \uc2 One challenge Shuler faces is everyone has advice for her.\par \par \uc2 Cohen says she needs to build a bigger tent, to reach out more to non-union workers and to bring into the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  three large unions not in the federation: the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , the \uc2 National Education Association\uc2  and the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Washington state\u8217\'20\'19s Sims said, \u8220\'20\'1CWe need more innovation and creativity. We can\u8217\'20\'19t continue to do things the way we\u8217\'20\'19ve always done them. We have to challenge ourselves to think more outside the box.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Williams of the painters\u8217\'20\'19 union said more union organizing and more political organizing were needed. \u8220\'20\'1CThe two go hand in hand,\u8221\'20\'1D he said \u8211\'20\'13 labor-friendly lawmakers are needed to defend against GOP attacks and to enact laws making it easier to unionize. \u8216\'20\'18The biggest challenge is how to organize our members post-Trump, to reorganize them around the truth and values.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rick Levy, president of the Texas AFL-CIO, said labor needed to do more to defend America\u8217\'20\'19s democracy. \u8220\'20\'1CIn terms of our main challenge, it\u8217\'20\'19s that things are falling apart, our democracy is falling apart,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe challenge is, how do we be a force and really become a much stronger movement in the face of these relentless attacks on the very things we have taken for granted, things that we care about.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211014ehae0015t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
