\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Is America experiencing an unofficial general strike?\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Reich \par \uc2 795 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2021\par 10:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Across the country, people are refusing to return to backbreaking or mind-numbing low-wage jobs\par \par \uc2 Last Friday\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report from the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2  elicited a barrage of gloomy headlines. The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  emphasized \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/10/08/business/economy/jobs-report-september-2021.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 weak}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D jobs growth and fretted that \u8220\'20\'1Chiring challenges that have bedeviled employers all year won\u8217\'20\'19t be quickly resolved,\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Crising wages could add to concerns about inflation.\u8221\'20\'1D For CNN, it was \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/10/08/economy/september-jobs-report/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 another disappointment}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. For {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2021-10-08/payrolls-growth-in-u-s-misses-big-for-a-second-straight-month"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bloomberg}}}\uc2  the \u8220\'20\'1CSeptember jobs report misses big for a second straight month\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The media failed to report the big story, which is actually a very good one: American workers are now flexing their muscles for the first time in decades.\par \par \uc2 You might say workers have declared a national general strike until they get better pay and improved working conditions.\par \par \uc2 No one calls it a general strike. But in its own disorganized way it\u8217\'20\'19s related to the organized strikes breaking out across the land \u8211\'20\'13 Hollywood TV and film crews, John Deere workers, Alabama coal miners, Nabisco workers, Kellogg workers, nurses in California, healthcare workers in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 Disorganized or organized, American workers now have bargaining leverage to do better. After a year and a half of the pandemic, consumers have pent-up demand for all sorts of goods and services.\par \par \uc2 But employers are finding it hard to fill positions.\par \par \uc2 Last Friday\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report showed the number of job openings at a record high. The share of people working or actively looking for work (the labor force participation rate) has dropped to 61.6%. Participation for people in their prime working years, defined as 25 to 54 years old, is also down.\par \par \uc2 Over the past year, job openings have increased 62%. Yet overall hiring has actually declined.\par \par \uc2 What gives?\par \par \uc2 Another clue: Americans are also quitting their jobs at the highest rate on record. The Department of Labor reported on Tuesday that some 4.3 million people quit their jobs in August. That comes to about 2.9% of the workforce \u8211\'20\'13 up from the previous record {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/jolts_06082021.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 set in April}}}\uc2 , of about 4 million people quitting.\par \par \uc2 All told, about 4 million American workers have been leaving their jobs every month since the spring.\par \par \uc2 These numbers have nothing to do with the Republican bogeyman of extra unemployment benefits supposedly discouraging people from working. Reminder: the extra benefits ran out on Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 Renewed fears of the Delta variant of Covid may play some role. But it can\u8217\'20\'19t be the largest factor. With most adults now vaccinated, rates of hospitalizations and deaths are way down.\par \par \uc2 My take: workers are reluctant to return to or remain in their old jobs mostly because they\u8217\'20\'19re burned out.\par \par \uc2 Some have retired early. Others have found ways to make ends meet other than remain in jobs they abhor. Many just don\u8217\'20\'19t want to return to backbreaking or mind-numbing low-wage shit jobs.\par \par \uc2 The media and most economists measure the economy\u8217\'20\'19s success by the number of jobs it creates, while ignoring the quality of those jobs. That\u8217\'20\'19s a huge oversight.\par \par \uc2 Years ago, when I was secretary of labor, I kept meeting working people all over the country who had full-time work but complained that their jobs paid too little and had few benefits, or were unsafe, or required lengthy or unpredictable hours. Many said their employers treated them badly, harassed them, and did not respect them.\par \par \uc2 Since then, these complaints have only grown louder, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com/labor-shortage-workers-quitting-quit-job-pandemic-covid-survey-monster-2021-7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 polls}}}\uc2 . For many, the pandemic was the last straw. Workers are fed up, wiped out, done-in, and run down. In the wake of so much hardship, illness and death during the past year, they\u8217\'20\'19re not going to take it anymore.\par \par \uc2 In order to lure workers back, employers are raising wages and offering other inducements. Average earnings rose 19 cents an hour in September and are up more than $1 an hour \u8211\'20\'13 or 4.6% \u8211\'20\'13 over the last year.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, that\u8217\'20\'19s not enough.\par \par \uc2 Corporate America wants to frame this as a \u8220\'20\'1Clabor shortage.\u8221\'20\'1D Wrong. What\u8217\'20\'19s really going on is more accurately described as a living-wage shortage, a hazard pay shortage, a childcare shortage, a paid sick leave shortage, and a healthcare shortage.\par \par \uc2 Unless these shortages are rectified, many Americans won\u8217\'20\'19t return to work anytime soon. I say it\u8217\'20\'19s about time.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2 , a former US secretary of labor, is professor of public policy at the University of California at Berkeley and the author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.amazon.com/Saving-Capitalism-Many-Not-Few/dp/0345806220"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Saving Capitalism: For the Many, Not the Few}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.amazon.com/gp/product/052552049X/ref=dbs_a_def_rwt_bibl_vppi_i0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Common Good}}}\uc2 . His new book, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.amazon.com/System-Who-Rigged-How-Fix/dp/0525659048"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The System: Who Rigged It, How We Fix It}}}\uc2 , is out now. He is a Guardian US columnist. His newsletter is at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://robertreich.substack.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 robertreich.substack.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211013ehad001jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
