\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 New York City delivery workers win rights to better tips, bathrooms and more\b0\par\par\uc2 Kari Paul \par \uc2 753 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2021\par 18:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A package of bills targeting app-based companies such as \uc2 Grubhub\uc2  and Doordash will also set minimum pay\par \par \uc2 New York City lawmakers have passed a historic package of bills to improve labor conditions for gig economy and food delivery workers.\par \par \uc2 The first of its kind legislation, which targets app-based delivery companies such as \uc2 Grubhub\uc2 , Uber Eats, and Doordash, will set minimum pay, allow workers to keep more of their tips, and limit how far workers can be asked to travel for deliveries. It will also guarantee workers access to bathrooms \u8211\'20\'13 a problem that has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/v7ggz4/gig-workers-say-most-restaurants-have-banned-them-from-using-their-bathrooms"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 long plagued people in the gig economy}}}\uc2  and has been exacerbated by Covid-19 restrictions.\par \par \uc2 City council speaker Corey Johnson, speaking in a press conference following the vote on Thursday, said the package would give workers the \u8220\'20\'1Crights they deserve\u8221\'20\'1D and inspire future legislation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNew York will now be the first city in the country to make sure delivery workers are not exploited \u8211\'20\'13 to make sure people are treated with dignity and respect, that they get their wages, and that they are not exploited by these multibillion dollar corporations,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The legislation was written in collaboration with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://losdeliveristasunidos.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Los Deliveristas Unidos}}}\uc2  (LDU), a collective of mostly immigrant app delivery workers who have long pushed for living wages, bathroom access, and the right to organize.\par \par \uc2 The legislation was passed as Covid-19 has increased consumer reliance on delivery services, causing the sector to grow enormously in the past year. Uber Eats, the food delivery segment of the ride-hailing company Uber, grew by 190% in 2020, adding 36,000 couriers in New York City alone.\par \par \uc2 But the growing army of workers found themselves on the front lines of a pandemic with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/article/us-health-coronavirus-delivery-drivers-i/delivery-drivers-face-pandemic-without-sick-pay-insurance-sanitizer-idUSKBN21C1CJ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 no health benefits}}}\uc2  and little job security. Many complain they are unable to access bathrooms and often cannot see or access the tips that customers add to orders.\par \par \uc2 Excluding tips, the median hourly wage for delivery workers in New York City was $7.94 in 2020 according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gothamist.com/food/report-nyc-food-delivery-workers-face-low-pay-high-risks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a study}}}\uc2  from the Worker\u8217\'20\'19s Justice Project. The hourly net pay when including tips was still below New York\u8217\'20\'19s $15 minimum wage, at an average of $12.21.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman from \uc2 Grubhub\uc2  said the company supported the bills, calling them \u8220\'20\'1Ccommon-sense steps to support the delivery workers who work hard every day for New York\u8217\'20\'19s restaurants and residents\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEnsuring they receive a living wage and have access to restrooms isn\u8217\'20\'19t just a good idea \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s the right thing to do,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  has also expressed support of the legislation.\par \par \uc2 Cities are increasingly cracking down on the gig economy. Chicago {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://chicago.eater.com/2021/8/27/22644787/chicago-grubhub-doordash-lawsuit-third-party-delivery"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sued}}}\uc2  food delivery apps in August for misleading consumers, restaurants, and workers \u8211\'20\'13 including \u8220\'20\'1Cusing consumer tips to pay itself rather than its drivers\u8221\'20\'1D. In June, San Francisco {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sf.eater.com/2021/6/23/22546595/permanent-food-delivery-fee-cap-san-francisco"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted}}}\uc2  to cap delivery app fees charged to restaurants at 15%.\par \par \uc2 But some of the companies targeted by these efforts are also fighting back. California in 2020 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2020/feb/07/uber-ab5-changes-drivers-california"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed}}}\uc2  a law entitling drivers to benefits and better pay, which industry giants such as Uber quickly countered with their own bill exempting themselves from the legislation. Uber now plans to appeal after that bill, Prop 22, was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/california/newsletter/2021-08-23/proposition-22-lyft-uber-decision-essential-california"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled unconstitutional}}}\uc2  in August.\par \par \uc2 Workers themselves are making their own efforts, demanding the benefits afforded to full-time employees including better pay and the right to organize.\par \par \uc2 Starting this week, contractors at \uc2 Instacart\uc2  have called on customers to boycott the app as they demand better working conditions. In June, Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/jul/21/uber-lyft-drivers-strike-app-based-work-gig-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 participated}}}\uc2  in a day-long strike to demand the right to organize.\par \par \uc2 Worker advocates say the New York bills are a good start but do not address some broader concerns about the gig economy in the US, and that more comprehensive legislation on a national scale is needed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is an excellent step in the right direction, but I am concerned that a piecemeal approach to addressing these serious issues is going to take the wind out of the movement towards basic employment rights for these workers,\u8221\'20\'1D said Veena Dubal, a professor of employment law at University of California, Hastings.\par \par \uc2 She added that delivery workers are at a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brooklynpaper.com/five-delivery-workers-have-died-on-the-job-in-brooklyn-in-past-year-according-to-scathing-new-report/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 very high risk}}}\uc2  of injury and should be entitled to workers\u8217\'20\'19 compensation and healthcare when they are hurt on the job.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is better than nothing in the short term, but lawmakers should not think by passing these bills they are doing enough,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210924eh9o0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
