\par\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 UK politics: Starmer says talks with unions will continue on his plans for Labour reform \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Andrew Sparrow \par \uc2 10,183 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2021\par 16:39 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Sir \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  suggests trade unions that support Labour have not yet backed his plans to change the party\u8217\'20\'19s leadership election rulesStarmer says talks with unions will continue on plans for Labour reformSummary of \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s TV interview in WashingtonPMQs - snap verdictOfgem boss says global gas price hike \u8216\'20\'18far, far above\u8217\'20\'19 any forecastsKwarteng says consumers should prepare for \u8216\'20\'18longer-term high prices\u8217\'20\'19Eustice implies Biden does not fully understand \u8216\'20\'18complicated\u8217\'20\'19 NI issue\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 Afternoon summary\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Prof Chris Whitty\uc2 , England\u8217\'20\'19s chief medical officer, has justified the plan to vaccinate 12- to 15-year-olds, telling MPs it is almost inevitable that children will get infected, and that half of them have probably had coronavirus already. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/22/joe-biden-northern-ireland-covid-coronavirus-pmqs-boris-johnson-keir-starmer-latest-updates-politics-live?page=with:block-614b2c378f08b228c9498701#block-614b2c378f08b228c9498701"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  2.18pm}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/22/joe-biden-northern-ireland-covid-coronavirus-pmqs-boris-johnson-keir-starmer-latest-updates-politics-live?page=with:block-614b47128f084a3056b454e4#block-614b47128f084a3056b454e4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  4.15pm.)}}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2021/sep/22/unions-concerns-could-delay-keir-starmers-labour-rules-shake-up"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Keir Starmer could be forced to delay plans for Labour party reforms amid resistance from key trade unions, who have said they need more time to consult on plans to rewrite the leadership election rules.}}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2021/sep/22/gordon-brown-universal-credit-cut-vindictive-and-indefensible"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gordon Brown has called the government\u8217\'20\'19s decision to cut \u163\'00\'A320 a week from universal credit the most \u8220\'20\'1Csocially divisive and morally indefensible\u8221\'20\'1D policy he has witnessed in UK politics, saying it was being pursued with open disregard for its impact.}}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/sep/22/19m-uk-homes-left-without-supplier-after-two-more-gas-firms-go-bust"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The UK gas crisis has claimed another two energy companies, bringing the total number of households that have lost their supplier this year to almost 2m.}}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2021/sep/22/angela-rayner-targets-dominc-raab-holiday-fiasco-deputies-pmqs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Angela Rayner has accused the government of failing the British people over the looming energy crisis in a prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s questions notable for its stand-in participants and a series of jibes about the holiday habits of her opponent, Dominic Raab.}}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2021/sep/22/boris-johnson-talks-up-solid-steps-on-us-trade-as-lamb-deal-agreed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The US will start to allow imports of UK lamb for the first time in decades, Boris Johnson has said, as he used his trip to Washington to talk up what he termed \u8220\'20\'1Csolid incremental steps\u8221\'20\'1D in mutual trade, in the absence of a formal bilateral deal.}}}\par \par \uc2 * Johnson has jokingly dismissed French concern about the new Australia/UK/US defence partnership, Aukus, telling President Macron he should \u8220\'20\'1Cprenez un grip about this and donnez moi un break\u8221\'20\'1D. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/22/joe-biden-northern-ireland-covid-coronavirus-pmqs-boris-johnson-keir-starmer-latest-updates-politics-live?page=with:block-614b237a8f087b5c6fb4addb#block-614b237a8f087b5c6fb4addb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1.51pm.)}}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/sep/22/brazils-health-minister-tests-positive-for-covid-after-meeting-maskless-johnson"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brazil\u8217\'20\'19s health minister, Marcelo Queiroga, has tested positive for Covid and gone into isolation, 24 hours after meeting a maskless Boris Johnson and other British officials in New York.}}}\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s all from me for today. But our Covid coverage continues on our global live blog. It\u8217\'20\'19s here.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2021/sep/22/coronavirus-live-news-brazil-health-minister-tests-positive-at-un-india-urges-uk-to-resolve-quarantine-dispute"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Covid live: Germany to end quarantine pay for unvaccinated workers; no plans to relax France health pass restrictions}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , the former US secretary of state, in a procession today as she received an honorary degree at \uc2 Oxford University\uc2 . Photograph: Steve Parsons/PA\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 These are from the \uc2 BBC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Iain Watson on Sir \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s stand-off with the unions over his party reform plans.\par \par \uc2 enltrAt {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Keir_Starmer?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @Keir_Starmer}}}\uc2  's crunch summit with the unions I am told there were 'no takers' for his plan to change {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/UKLabour?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @uklabour}}}\uc2  's leadership rules. Some opposed in principle but logistics mattered as much as politics for others\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 iain watson (@iainjwatson) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/iainjwatson/status/1440685404760330253?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 22, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThey hadnt seen the full proposals until today and they made it clear they needed to discuss them more widely so urged him to delay his proposals until after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/UKLabour?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @uklabour}}}\uc2  conference\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 iain watson (@iainjwatson) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/iainjwatson/status/1440685406593318927?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 22, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe meeting i am told was 'cordial'and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Keir_Starmer?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @Keir_Starmer}}}\uc2  seemed to be in 'listening mode' but not clear yet if he will delay The \uc2 GMB union\uc2  wasn't present - and the Labour leadership were hoping for their support\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 iain watson (@iainjwatson) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/iainjwatson/status/1440685408593973262?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 22, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrLooks like no final decision has been taken by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Keir_Starmer?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @Keir_Starmer}}}\uc2  to take his proposals to conference He is making it clear he will discuss changes and is open to suggestions ahead of a crucial {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/UKLabour?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @uklabour}}}\uc2  NEC meeting on Friday\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 iain watson (@iainjwatson) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/iainjwatson/status/1440705777157640203?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 22, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe proposals in some form will go to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/UKLabour?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @uklabour}}}\uc2  's NEC on Friday night Only then will a decision be taken on whether to risk a conference vote\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 iain watson (@iainjwatson) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/iainjwatson/status/1440712903418540040?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 22, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 Starmer says talks with unions will continue on his plans for Labour reform\par \par \uc2 Sir \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  has in effect confirmed that the trade unions that support Labour have not as yet backed his plans to change the party\u8217\'20\'19s leadership election rules. Following a meeting with them earlier (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/22/joe-biden-northern-ireland-covid-coronavirus-pmqs-boris-johnson-keir-starmer-latest-updates-politics-live?page=with:block-614b4fbc8f084a3056b4556f#block-614b4fbc8f084a3056b4556f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 4.50pm)}}}\uc2 , he issued this statement.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s TULO [trade union and Labour party liaison organisation] meeting was a welcome opportunity to set out some of the rule changes I believe will strengthen our party, our link with the unions and our ability to win the next election.\par \par \uc2 We had a positive conversation and I look forward to continuing those conversations through the coming days because the principles are important and we have to look at how we need to change to win again.\par \par \uc2 I said yesterday this was never a take it or leave it conversation. I am continuing to take suggestions and have discussions about how we do everything we need to in order to make the Labour party the party of working people again.\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer. Photograph: Peter Morrison/PA\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 Union opposition means Starmer may have to delay vote on Labour's leadership election rules\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer could be forced to delay plans for Labour party reforms amid resistance from key trade unions, who have said they need more time to consult on plans to rewrite the leadership election rules.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  understands that Unite, one of the most powerful unions affiliated with Labour, told Starmer that he should delay any vote on a return to the electoral college system of voting, which is fiercely opposed by the party\u8217\'20\'19s left.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEveryone was agreed on that point,\u8221\'20\'1D another union source said. \u8220\'20\'1CEach union has a different approach to these ideas but in principle we do not want to be bounced into them. Keir was very much in listening mode.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starmer has argued that switching to an electoral college system to decide future party leaders will give greater sway to millions of trade union members, but some on the left of the party have said it would ignite a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://viewer.gutools.co.uk/politics/2021/sep/21/major-row-brewing-over-plans-to-change-way-labour-elects-party-leader"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Ccivil war\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  and hand more power to MPs at the expense of ordinary members.\par \par \uc2 The move would be a return to Labour\u8217\'20\'19s old system under which MPs, party members and trade unions each had a third of the votes for a new leader. It was abolished by \uc2 Ed Miliband\uc2  in 2014 in favour of giving each member of the party and its affiliates one vote on any candidate on the ballot paper. Candidates must acquire the support of 10% of MPs plus some support from constituency parties and trade unions to get on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Starmer met union leaders to try to persuade them to back the proposals at lunchtime and \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  understands the Unite delegation argued that he should delay the changes even if it meant organising a special conference session to approve them later.\par \par \uc2 The Labour leader told the meeting he would need to time to consider whether a delay was acceptable, according to two sources.\par \par \uc2 During the discussions, key unions including Unite, Unison and \uc2 Usdaw\uc2  are said to have told Starmer they were open to discussion about rule changes for leadership elections but felt blindsided by the proposals.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 The UK has updated {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://coronavirus.data.gov.uk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 its Covid dashboard.}}}\uc2  There have been 34,460 new cases, but the total number of cases over the past seven days is marginally down \u8211\'20\'13 by 0.4% \u8211\'20\'13 compared with the previous week.\par \par \uc2 Hospital admissions are down by 13.8% week on week, although these figures only go up to last Saturday, because UK hospital admission figures are always a few days late. There were 747 admissions on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 And today\u8217\'20\'19s figures show there have been 166 more Covid deaths. Week on week, deaths are up by 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 Covid dashboard Photograph: Gov.UK/Covid dashboard\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 This is from Rym Momtaz, Politico\u8217\'20\'19s France correspondent, on \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cdonnez-moi un break\u8221\'20\'1D comment about \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  earlier. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/22/joe-biden-northern-ireland-covid-coronavirus-pmqs-boris-johnson-keir-starmer-latest-updates-politics-live?page=with:block-614b237a8f087b5c6fb4addb#block-614b237a8f087b5c6fb4addb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  1.51pm}}}\uc2 .) Bloomberg has written it up under the headline \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2021-09-22/johnson-says-time-for-france-to-get-a-grip-on-submarine-spat?srnd=politics-vp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Boris Johnson mocks French outrage over loss of submarine deal}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltrWow.\par \par \uc2 Is the Brexit domestic politics shtick worth it for \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  to irreparably blow up the relationship with France?\par \par \uc2 France remains the only \uc2 EU\uc2  member state with real military and diplomatic weight, and that the UK needs. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/AUKUS?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #AUKUS}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/pSFeTQCMgA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/pSFeTQCMgA}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Rym Momtaz ??? ????? (@RymMomtaz) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RymMomtaz/status/1440687762953277450?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 22, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 Around half of children have already had coronavirus, MPs told\par \par \uc2 Here is the PA Media story from the opening of the education committee hearing with \uc2 Prof Chris Whitty\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 About half of children have already had Covid-19 and the rest are more likely to get it without a vaccine, England\u8217\'20\'19s chief medical officer has suggested.\par \par \uc2 Prof Chris Whitty\uc2  warned that \u8220\'20\'1Cquite a lot of damage\u8221\'20\'1D could still occur over the winter months.\par \par \uc2 He told MPs: \u8220\'20\'1CLet\u8217\'20\'19s make an assumption that the great majority of children who\u8217\'20\'19ve not currently had Covid-19 are going to get it at some point over the next period.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt won\u8217\'20\'19t be necessarily in the next two or three months but they will get it sooner or later.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But addressing the Commons education committee, Whitty added: \u8220\'20\'1CVaccination will reduce that risk.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 England\u8217\'20\'19s chief medical officer was facing questions from MPs about the inclusion of children in the government\u8217\'20\'19s Covid-19 vaccination programme.\par \par \uc2 When asked what proportion of children had already had Covid-19, he replied: \u8220\'20\'1CIt varies by age and it does also vary by setting, but I think if we go for roughly half I think that is a reasonable stab at this.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s half over the period of the entire epidemic to date, and we\u8217\'20\'19ve got quite a way to run.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re running into winter so there\u8217\'20\'19s still quite a lot of damage that could be done in terms of disruption.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 England\u8217\'20\'19s deputy chief medical officer \uc2 Prof Jonathan Van-Tam\uc2  added that because the Delta variant is so infectious \u8220\'20\'1Cwe are not looking at a theoretical risk\u8221\'20\'1D of children aged 12 to 17 becoming infected.\par \par \uc2 He said: \u8220\'20\'1CI think it is really quite inevitable that they will be so at some point.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Prof Van-Tam warned that these pupils could become infected during their GCSEs and A-levels when it is \u8220\'20\'1Cextremely inconvenient to be laid low\u8221\'20\'1D with a cough, fever, and respiratory symptoms.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , Speaker of the US House of Representatives, with \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  on his visit to the US Capitol today. Photograph: Rex/\uc2 Shutterstock\uc2  Johnson admiring the bust of Winston Churchill at the US Capitol. Photograph: Kevin Dietsch/Getty Images\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back in the education committee, \uc2 Prof Chris Whitty\uc2  explains how the \u8220\'20\'1CGillick competence\u8221\'20\'1D test is applied when clinicians have to decide whether or not to offer treatment, like the Covid vaccine, to a child under the age of 16. He reads from Lord Scarman\u8217\'20\'19s ruling in the original 1980s law lords case that decided this.\par \par \uc2 Scarman said:\par \par \uc2 As a matter of law the parental right to determine whether or not their minor child below the age of 16 will have medical treatment terminates if and when the child achieves a sufficient understanding and intelligence to enable him or her to understand fully what is proposed.\par \par \uc2 Whitty says above the age of 16 there is a general assumption that teenagers are competent to decide for themselves, and above the age of 18 there is an absolute assumption, he says.\par \par \uc2 And he says under the age of 12 there is an absolute assumption against the child being competent to decide.\par \par \uc2 Lord Scarman, architect of the Gillick competence test. Photograph: David Rose/The Independent/Rex/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 Health service in Northern Ireland facing pressures 'like never before', minister warns\par \par \uc2 Robin Swan, health minister in the Northern Ireland executive, has issued {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.niassembly.gov.uk/news-and-media/press-releases/session-2021-2022/joint-statement-from-minister-of-health-robin-swann-mla-chairperson-of-the-assembly-health-committee-colm-gildernew-mla-and-deputy-chairperson-of-the-assembly-health-committee-pam-cameron-la/?utm_campaign=health-service-statement&utm_medium=social-media-organic&utm_source=twitter&utm_content=press-release"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a statement}}}\uc2  with the chair and deputy chair of the Northern Ireland assembly\u8217\'20\'19s health committee saying the health service in the region faces \u8220\'20\'1Can extremely challenging winter\u8221\'20\'1D. They urge people to get vaccinated if they have not already, and suggest that former health workers might want to return to the \uc2 NHS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here is an excerpt:\par \par \uc2 Our health and social care staff and services are currently facing pressures like never before.\par \par \uc2 The devastating impact of the past 20 months is compounded by the likelihood of an extremely challenging winter \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 We also recognise the great potential of enabling those with relevant experience, skills and knowledge to rejoin the workforce through workforce appeals and by providing additional support to our HSC workforce at this difficult time.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 Asked about anti-vax campaigners, Whitty says they represents a \u8220\'20\'1Ctiny fraction\u8221\'20\'1D of the population. He says when people are seeking health advice, they should take advice from people who are \u8220\'20\'1Cknowledgable, logical and kind\u8221\'20\'1D. He says from what he has seen of the anti-vaxxers\u8217\'20\'19 material, they don\u8217\'20\'19t have any of these three qualities.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back in the education committee, \uc2 Prof Chris Whitty\uc2  says at this stage 12- to 15-year-olds are only getting one dose of vaccine. He says the scientists are looking at the data from other countries, where vaccinating children is ahead of the UK, before a decision is taken on whether they should have a second dose.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  attending a meeting today in Washington with the Senate majority leader, Chuck Schumer (right), Senate minority leader, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  (2nd right), and other members of the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 . Photograph: Michael Reynolds/EPA\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 Two more energy supplies go out of business\par \par \uc2 Avro Energy\uc2  and Green Supplier Limited have become the fourth and fifth energy suppliers to go out of business in September as a rise in gas prices puts pressure on the sector, PA Media reports. PA says:\par \par \uc2 Ofgem\uc2  said that it would ensure that Avro\u8217\'20\'19s 580,000 domestic gas and electricity customers, and Green\u8217\'20\'19s 255,000 households would be protected.\par \par \uc2 The regulator will choose a new supplier for the households, and said customers should wait to be contacted by their new supplier.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI want to reassure customers of \uc2 Avro Energy\uc2  and Green Supplier Limited that they do not need to worry. Under our safety net we\u8217\'20\'19ll make sure your energy supplies continue,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Ofgem\uc2  director of retail Neil Lawrence said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you have credit on your \uc2 Avro Energy\uc2  or Green Supplier Limited account this is protected and you will not lose the money that is owed to you.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 Schools in England are bracing themselves for food shortages, with some already forced to adapt lunch menus because of problems in the supply chain.\par \par \uc2 The headteachers\u8217\'20\'19 union, the \uc2 Association of School and College Leaders\uc2  (ASCL), said schools had reported problems with the supply of some meat products, which had had to be replaced on lunch menus, and a number of school catering companies have confirmed that supply shortages are already affecting school canteen menus.\par \par \uc2 Hayley Dunn, ASCL\u8217\'20\'19s business leadership specialist, said:\par \par \uc2 We have heard of schools having to make adaptations to their menus because of problems in the supply chain. This relates to certain meat products and has necessitated replacing options with other choices.\par \par \uc2 We have no indication of how widespread this is and we have not so far received reports of problems more serious than this, but we will monitor the situation and pick it up with the government if necessary. Any disruption to the food supply chain is obviously of concern.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.19pm BST\par \par \uc2 Prof Jonathan Van-Tam\uc2  tells the education committee that data from healthcare workers puts the chance of re-infection among people who have had Covid before at between 5% and 15%.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back at the education committee, \uc2 Prof Chris Whitty\uc2 , the chief medical officer for England, says vaccination has a smaller benefit for children who have already had Covid than it does for children who haven\u8217\'20\'19t. But he says it is not true to say it offers them no benefit.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 Gordon Brown says universal credit cut \u8216\'20\'18vindictive and indefensible\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Gordon Brown has called the government\u8217\'20\'19s decision to cut \u163\'00\'A320 a week from universal credit the most \u8220\'20\'1Csocially divisive and morally indefensible\u8221\'20\'1D policy he has witnessed in UK politics, saying it is being pursued with open disregard for its impact. He has set out his argument in an article for \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  available here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/sep/22/20-benefit-cut-universal-credit-welfare-state-poorest-people-cast-aside-gordon-brown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The \u163\'00\'A320 benefit cut is the most morally indefensible thing I\u8217\'20\'19ve seen in politics | Gordon Brown}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 The education committee has now suspended its proceedings for 15 minutes because there is a vote in the Commons.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back in the education committee, Prof Wei Shen Lim, chair of the Joint Committee on Vaccination and Immunisation\u8217\'20\'19s Covid committee, says adults are well protected for six to nine months by the vaccine. With children, the protection is likely to be as good or better.\par \par \uc2 Dr Camilla Kingdon, president of the \uc2 Royal College of Paediatrics and Child Health\uc2 , says children launch an \u8220\'20\'1Cexceptionally good immune response\u8221\'20\'1D, and so their protection is likely to last nine months.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 The industry body representing nightclubs is launching a legal challenge to the Scottish government\u8217\'20\'19s plans for vaccine passports.\par \par \uc2 The requirement for people over the age of 18 to show proof they have been double vaccinated to gain entry to nightclubs and large-scale events comes into force in Scotland on 1 October.\par \par \uc2 But the Night Time Industries Association Scotland says the plans were made with no meaningful consultation, are \u8220\'20\'1Cdeeply flawed and incoherent\u8221\'20\'1D, and that the definition of \u8220\'20\'1Cnightclub\u8221\'20\'1D set out by \uc2 Nicola Sturgeon\uc2  is likely to impact thousands of other bars and pubs.\par \par \uc2 The NTIA Scotland has now instructed lawyers to seek a judicial review of the proposals.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Sturgeon said a \u8220\'20\'1Cpragmatic approach\u8221\'20\'1D would be encouraged, \u8220\'20\'1Cso that businesses can make sensible judgements.\u8221\'20\'1D But the Scottish Chambers of Commerce responded that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe practical application of what is being asked is not workable in the timelines being proposed\u8221\'20\'1D, raising concerns that the definition could also cover a range of other hospitality venues\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 Dr Caroline Johnson (Con) was your recommendation about vaccinating teenagers entirely a medical decision, or was it influenced by politics.\par \par \uc2 Whitty says it was entirely medical. He says there is no point giving professional advice if it is influenced by politics. He says it is for ministers to take account political considerations when taking final decisions.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 Tom Hunt (Con) asks to what extend the recommendation of the chief medical officers was based just on health benefits to 12 to 15-year-olds.\par \par \uc2 Whitty says the chief medical officers were \u8220\'20\'1Creally clear\u8221\'20\'1D that their recommendation would only be based on what was best for children in that age group.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Van-Tam says he can understand why people worry that pupils who do not get vaccinated might end up being discriminated against at school. But he says he has never heard of this happening in relation to children and the flu vaccine.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 Robert Halfon (Con), the committee chair, says in its statement in early September the JCVI said a school vaccination programme would interfere with children\u8217\'20\'19s schooling.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/jcvi-statement-september-2021-covid-19-vaccination-of-children-aged-12-to-15-years/jcvi-statement-on-covid-19-vaccination-of-children-aged-12-to-15-years-3-september-2021"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 It said: }}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1CDelivery of a Covid-19 vaccine programme for children and young people is likely to be disruptive to education in the short term, particularly if school premises are used for vaccination.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Q: What analysis has been done of how much disruption there might be?\par \par \uc2 Lim says the JCVI did not try to quantify this.\par \par \uc2 Whitty says the comparison was not between vaccination and nothing else happening; the comparison is between vaccination, and children getting infected.\par \par \uc2 He says if you look at it like this, a vaccination programme leads to fewer school days being missed.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 Arrival of Delta variant meant it was 'inevitable' children would be infected, MPs told\par \par \uc2 Back at the education committee Van-Tam says the Delta variant is 60% more infectious or more than the Alpha variant circulating at the start of the year, or possibly more.\par \par \uc2 He says that means the risk of children being infected is not theoretical.\par \par \uc2 He says it is \u8220\'20\'1Creally quite inevitable\u8221\'20\'1D that children will be infected. That means they could be infected at some point \u8220\'20\'1Cnot of their choosing\u8221\'20\'1D, he says, such as when they are taking exams.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Turing away from the education committee, my colleague Jessica Elgot says the unions want to delay a Labour decision on changing the leadership election rules.\par \par \uc2 enltrNew - I understand Unite has won some consensus from other unions that party reforms should NOT be voted on this weekend and that there should be more time to consider. Source tells me Starmer said he would need time think about this and come back to them.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jessica Elgot (@jessicaelgot) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/jessicaelgot/status/1440663396639121411?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 22, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.11pm BST\par \par \uc2 MPs take evidence from \uc2 Chris Whitty\uc2 , \uc2 Jonathan Van-Tam\uc2  and JCVI chair on vaccinating children\par \par \uc2 The Commons education committee is now taking evidence on vaccinating children.\par \par \uc2 The witnesses are: \uc2 Prof Chris Whitty\uc2 , the chief medical officer for England; \uc2 Prof Jonathan Van-Tam\uc2 , his deputy; Dr Camilla Kingdon, presiden of the \uc2 Royal College of Paediatrics and Child Health\uc2  (RCPCH); Prof Wei Shen Lim, chair, of the Joint Commitee on Vaccination and Immunisation\u8217\'20\'19s Covid committee; and Prof Keith Willett, head of Covid-19 and flu vaccine deployment at \uc2 NHS England\uc2  and \uc2 NHS\uc2  Improvement.\par \par \uc2 Whitty starts by explaining the decision to recommend vaccinating 12 to 15-year-old.\par \par \uc2 He says the Joint Committee on Vaccination and Immunisation gave {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/jcvi-statement-september-2021-covid-19-vaccination-of-children-aged-12-to-15-years/jcvi-statement-on-covid-19-vaccination-of-children-aged-12-to-15-years-3-september-2021"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 advice on this.}}}\par \par \uc2 It said the benefits of vaccination for 12 to 15-year-olds were marginally greater than the risks.\par \par \uc2 But they said it was not the JCVI\u8217\'20\'19s job to take into account wider societal benefits. But it recommended that chief medical officers take a decision, taking into account a wider definition of health benefits.\par \par \uc2 He explains how the chief medical officers went about this, and he stresses that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/13/uk-covid-live-news-coronavirus-vaccine-passports-boris-johnson-lockdown?page=with:block-613f755f8f08fea27f0e6337#block-613f755f8f08fea27f0e6337"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 their recent recommendation }}}\uc2  represented the mid-point in medical opinion.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 PM says he did not talk about Northern Ireland with Biden - after No 10 said he did\par \par \uc2 In his interview \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  claimed Northern Ireland did not come up in his talks with President Biden yesterday. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/22/joe-biden-northern-ireland-covid-coronavirus-pmqs-boris-johnson-keir-starmer-latest-updates-politics-live?page=with:block-614b237a8f087b5c6fb4addb#block-614b237a8f087b5c6fb4addb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1.51pm.)}}}\uc2  But the Downing Street read-out from the talks issued last night said the topic was raised. It included this paragraph.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister updated President Biden on the developments with respect to the Northern Ireland protocol since they last met in June. The leaders agreed on the importance of protecting peace in Northern Ireland.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 Johnson urges Macron to 'donnez moi un break' over Aukus row\par \par \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  has been speaking to reports in Washington. Here are the main points.\par \par \uc2 * Johnson dismissed George Eustice\u8217\'20\'19s claim that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  does not fully understand the Northern Ireland protocol issue. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/22/joe-biden-northern-ireland-covid-coronavirus-pmqs-boris-johnson-keir-starmer-latest-updates-politics-live?page=with:block-614adf0c8f087b5c6fb4a941#block-614adf0c8f087b5c6fb4a941"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 9.31am.)}}}\uc2  Asked if agreed with Eustice, Johnson replied:\par \par \uc2 No. The president actually in our meeting yesterday, I don\u8217\'20\'19t think it came up at all. We had a meeting of over 90 minutes and it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t raised.\par \par \uc2 * He said Washington has agreed to lift a ban on British lamb. Asked about the trade relationship with the US, and the admission that there will be no UK-US free trade deal soon, he said:\par \par \uc2 I can tell you today that what we\u8217\'20\'19re going to get from the United States now is a lifting of the decades old ban, totally unjustified, discriminating on British farmers and British lamb.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s about time too. And what we\u8217\'20\'19re wanting to do is make solid incremental steps in trade.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is not doing free trade deals around the world right now but I\u8217\'20\'19ve got absolutely every confidence that a great deal is there to be done.\par \par \uc2 And there are plenty of people in that building behind me [Capitol Hill] who certainly want a deal.\par \par \uc2 This is from the Critic\u8217\'20\'19s Robert Hutton.\par \par \uc2 enltr"Actually, she said it's not me, she just doesn't want a serious relationship with anyone at the moment." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/KhpWXw0l7N"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/KhpWXw0l7N}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Robert Hutton (@RobDotHutton) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RobDotHutton/status/1440650490291757056?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 22, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 * He urged \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  to \u8220\'20\'1Cdonnez moi un break\u8221\'20\'1D over the Aukus row. Asked about ongoing French anger over the deal, he resorted to franglais, saying:\par \par \uc2 I just think it\u8217\'20\'19s time for some of our dearest friends around the world to prenez un grip about this and donnez moi un break.\par \par \uc2 Because this is fundamentally a great step forward for global security. It\u8217\'20\'19s three very like-minded allies standing shoulder to shoulder creating a new partnership for the sharing of technology.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not exclusive. It\u8217\'20\'19s not trying to shoulder anybody out. It\u8217\'20\'19s not adversarial towards China, for instance.\par \par \uc2 * He dismissed suggestions the gas price crisis could lead to panic buying. Asked if he thought this might happen, he said:\par \par \uc2 I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that will happen. I think we\u8217\'20\'19ve got very good supply chains, as I\u8217\'20\'19ve been saying over the last few days, and what we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing is the growing pains of a global economy recovering rapidly from Covid.\par \par \uc2 * He said he was pleasantly surprised by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2021/sep/22/developing-nations-welcome-us-climate-finance-pledge-but-warn-more-is-needed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s climate finance pledge. }}}\uc2  He said:\par \par \uc2 Yesterday the president came through with something that really exceeded our expectations. I said on the plane out that we had a six in 10 chance of success on that. Maybe I undercooked it. They really surprised us all on the upside.\par \par \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  outside Capitol Hill today. Photograph: Stefan Rousseau/PA\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Near the start of his hearing with the Commons business committee, \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2 , the business secretary, suggested the government was considering a Spanish-style windfall tax on gas traders. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/22/joe-biden-northern-ireland-covid-coronavirus-pmqs-boris-johnson-keir-starmer-latest-updates-politics-live?page=with:block-614b0beb8f087b5c6fb4ac2a#block-614b0beb8f087b5c6fb4ac2a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12pm.) }}}\uc2  Later in the hearing Kwarteng clarified his position, saying he was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot a fan\u8221\'20\'1D of windfall taxes. He said:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m not a fan of windfall taxes, let me just get that straight.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, it\u8217\'20\'19s an entire system and we have to think about how we can get the energy system as a whole to help itself.\par \par \uc2 As PA Media reports, Kwarteng also said the government would struggle to waive rules which ban one energy supplier from supplying more than a quarter of UK households. But he said that even in the worst-case predictions that only a handful of energy suppliers might be left by the end of the year, it is unlikely that the limit will be breached. He said:\par \par \uc2 In a scenario where you have ten companies, it\u8217\'20\'19s difficult to see one having 25%, but I will definitely cross that bridge when I come to it.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 My colleague Jessica Elgot says that, although Unite are opposed to Sir \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s plans to change the Labour leadership election rules, two of the other biggest unions that support the party are thought to be in favour.\par \par \uc2 enltrNEW - I understand Unite are to propose a delay to the party reforms proposed by Keir Starmer and ask for a special conference later in the year to discuss them.\par \par \uc2 Whispers though that Unison and GMB are already onboard with the changes.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jessica Elgot (@jessicaelgot) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/jessicaelgot/status/1440635305749475331?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 22, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Scotland\u8217\'20\'19s health secretary, \uc2 Humza Yousaf\uc2 , has countered opposition suggestions that military support should be deployed in field hospitals. Yousaf told BBC Radio Scotland\u8217\'20\'19s Good Morning Scotland programme that \u8220\'20\'1Cwe don\u8217\'20\'19t have the workforce\u8221\'20\'1D for field hospitals. \u8220\'20\'1CI definitely wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t rule it out entirely but we have to look at whether or not we would end up pulling people out of acute sites at the moment to staff those beds,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 On the same programme, Brigadier Ben Wrench, commander of Joint Military Command Scotland, confirmed the army will deploy 114 drivers and support staff to assist the ambulance service from this weekend, with a further 111 personnel to staff mobile testing units from next Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 He underlined that military staff would \u8220\'20\'1Cnot be undertaking any clinical duties\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 Rayner challenges Raab at PMQs over cost of living crisis\par \par \uc2 Here is the PA Media take on the Raab/Rayner exchanges at PMQs.\par \par \uc2 Dominic Raab\uc2  has faced Labour calls to guarantee no-one will lose their energy supply or be pushed into fuel poverty this winter.\par \par \uc2 Amid the ongoing energy crisis, Labour deputy leader \uc2 Angela Rayner\uc2  repeatedly sought assurances over supplies while also pressing Raab to cancel the removal of the \u163\'00\'A320-a-week uplift to Universal Credit.\par \par \uc2 Rayner at one stage asked Raab if he would make the lives of working people easier or harder, with the deputy prime minister beginning his reply with a fact check on the Chevening grace-and-favour mansion.\par \par \uc2 Rayner had accused Raab of \u8220\'20\'1Ccomplaining\u8221\'20\'1D about having to share the \u8220\'20\'1C115-room taxpayer-funded mansion\u8221\'20\'1D with foreign secretary Liz Truss. Raab noted Chevening is funded by a charity.\par \par \uc2 The duo traded barbs at PMQs as \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  is still in the United States following his visit to New York and Washington.\par \par \uc2 Speaking in the Commons, Rayner accused the Government of hitting the pockets of working families by \u8220\'20\'1Ccutting the income of a worker on \u163\'00\'A318,000 a year by over \u163\'00\'A31,100\u8221\'20\'1D with tax rises.\par \par \uc2 Rayner said: \u8220\'20\'1CThat is almost exactly the same as an average annual energy bill. Just as energy prices are ballooning they have chosen to take the money that could cover a year\u8217\'20\'19s worth of bills out of the pockets of working people.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe deputy prime minister has said the solution is for people to work harder. So, can he tell us how many days a worker on the minimum wage would have to work this year in order to afford a night at a luxury hotel, say in Crete?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Raab criticised Labour\u8217\'20\'19s economic record before adding: \u8220\'20\'1CUnder this government we cut income tax, saving every worker \u163\'00\'A31,200 each year, we have introduced and extended the national living wage so full-time workers are 4,000 better-off each year, we have doubled the free childcare for working parents worth up to 5,000 for every child every year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rayner countered: \u8220\'20\'1CHe talks about the economy, he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t even know how much his own holiday cost.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo, let me tell him. A worker on the minimum wage would need to work an extra 50 days to pay for a single night at his favourite resort, probably even more if the sea was open.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Labour deputy leader added: \u8220\'20\'1CThe prime minister has said it is just a short-term problem and we will leave it to the market to fix.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo can the deputy prime minister guarantee that no-one will lose their gas or energy supply or be pushed into fuel poverty this winter?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rayner went on to joke there was a \u8220\'20\'1Cshortage of hot air this week\u8221\'20\'1D, with Raab \u8220\'20\'1Cdoing his best\u8221\'20\'1D to shore up supplies in Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s absence.\par \par \uc2 Raab replied: \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no shortage of hot air on that side of the benches.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 PMQs - snap verdict\par \par \uc2 That was a comfortable win for \uc2 Angela Rayner\uc2  - but in a PMQs that overall felt somewhat undercharged. Colleagues who were sitting in the chamber tell me it felt a bit subdued, and the exchanges did not get much beyond ritual knockabout.\par \par \uc2 But, in terms of knockabout, Rayner was good. She focused on cost of living issues but her questions were loaded with barbed asides and snarky jokes that landed well in chamber - although perhaps it was all just a bit too pantomime. (Those watching from the press gallery tell me Raab did not seem to mind the jokes and seemed to find them funny.) Like Sir \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  last week, Rayner asked about the real-terms impact of the universal credit cut on low-paid workers. But, unlike Starmer, she personalised it in class terms, mocking Raab over his expensive holiday in Crete, and also for squabbling over access to Chevening. Because of her working class credentials are clearly stronger than Starmer\u8217\'20\'19s (her accent makes a difference), she can carry this off with conviction, and it worked well.\par \par \uc2 Should Starmer try this approach himself? Some in Labour would like him to inject a big of class politics into his attacks on Johnson, but what works for Rayner as a one-off probably wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t for Starmer. He is less comfortable than his deputy with going personal in this way, and class warfare is obviously not his style. Also, the electoral gains may be limited; voters tend not to mind people going on nice holidays, because they like to do so themselves.\par \par \uc2 While Rayner rose to the occasion, it was surprising that Raab did not do so himself. He did not say anything daft, but his response to Rayner were formulaic and dull. He only seemed to light up right at the end, when asked about a topic in his new portfolio, justice. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/22/joe-biden-northern-ireland-covid-coronavirus-pmqs-boris-johnson-keir-starmer-latest-updates-politics-live?page=with:block-614b14fe8f08b228c9498573#block-614b14fe8f08b228c9498573"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12.38pm.) }}}\uc2  At the time of the reshuffle there was some comment about how he and Rayner were similar, in that they both emerged from frontbench reorganisations with impressive-sounding new job titles. But, in Rayner\u8217\'20\'19s case, title inflation coincided with her position strengthening, or at least being consolidated. Raab was demoted. He knows it, and today it seemed to show.\par \par \uc2 Angela Rayner\uc2  at PMQs Photograph: Parliament/Jessica Taylor\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 Anna McMorrin\uc2  (Lab) asks if Raab will finally deliver a victims\u8217\'20\'19 bill in his new role as justice secretary. It was originally promised six years ago.\par \par \uc2 Raab says he will. He looked at plans for this on day one in his new post, he says.\par \par \uc2 But he says he is sorry Labour voted against the police, crime, sentencing and courts bill. You cannot stand up for victims unless you support tough sentencing, he says.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Derek Thomas\uc2  (Con) says he and fellow Cornish MPs want the seasonal agricultural workers visa scheme renewed.\par \par \uc2 Raab says the home secretary is very mindful of these concerns.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 Rachel Hopkins (Lab) asks what the government is doing to protect the automative sector.\par \par \uc2 Raab says Hopkins was wrong to suggest the government was not helping workers. It paid the wages of 12m workers during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 Siobhan Baillie (Con) asks what the government is doing to make homes more energy efficient.\par \par \uc2 Raab says by 2025 all new homes will be required to have low-carbon heating.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 Sir \uc2 George Howarth\uc2  (Lab) asks if Raab agrees that town halls know better than Whitehall when it comes to levelling up. Will the government hold a summit with town hall leaders on this.\par \par \uc2 Raab welcomes Howarth back to the Commons after his illness. He says he supports the spirit of what Howarth said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Margaret Ferrier (Ind) asks what the government is doing to ensure toys are safe.\par \par \uc2 Raab says he has young children and understands the concerns. Product safety regulation is being reviewed to ensure it protects consumers.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 Carla Lockhart (DUP) says, far from defending the Belfast agreement, the Northern Ireland protocol is the biggest threat to it. A solution must be found in weeks, not months?\par \par \uc2 Raab says a \u8220\'20\'1Csmart, pragmatic\u8221\'20\'1D approach is the only way to resolve this.\par \par \uc2 He says, notwithstanding the reporting, he knows that President Biden understands the UK\u8217\'20\'19s position, because he was there at the \uc2 G7\uc2  summit in Carbis Bay when this was discussed.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s an implied rebuke to George Eustice. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/22/joe-biden-northern-ireland-covid-coronavirus-pmqs-boris-johnson-keir-starmer-latest-updates-politics-live?page=with:block-614adf0c8f087b5c6fb4a941#block-614adf0c8f087b5c6fb4a941"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 9.31am.)}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 Dehenna Davison (Con) says the death of her father from a single-punch assault contributed to her becoming an MP. Will Raab support one-punch awareness week?\par \par \uc2 Raab says he will, and that he will attend a reception to promote this.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 Barry Sheerman\uc2  (Lab) asks if Raab still believes in redisribution and levelling up. And will he keep Channel 4 in Leeds.\par \par \uc2 Raab says the government put the infrastructure bank in Leeds because it loves Leeds.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 Deidre Brock\uc2  (SNP) asks what the government has against the Scottish government requiring net zero and real living wages in its green port.\par \par \uc2 Raab says net zero is at the heart of everything the \uc2 UK government\uc2  does, and that it has put up the national living wage.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 Andy Carter (Con) asks Raab to back plans for a new hospital in Warrington.\par \par \uc2 Raab says applications have been received for the latest developments in the hospital programme. There will be a decision by spring 2022, he says.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 Stephen Farry (Alliance) asks how the government has any credibility on climate change when the Cambo oil field development is going ahead.\par \par \uc2 Raab says the government has a landmark North Sea transition plan\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 Kirsten Oswald, the deputy SNP leader at Westminster, asks about energy bills and the cost of living. She says people will be hundreds of pounds worse off next year. This is a Tory cost of living crisis, she says. She says the universal credit cut should be abandoned.\par \par \uc2 Raab lists a string of measures that will help people on low incomes. The crucial thing is that we have rising employment and rising wages, he says.\par \par \uc2 Oswald says warm words will not heat homes. More families will be pushed into crisis, he says. She says Citizens Advice have warned about a tsunami hitting families. Will the government consider an emergency energy payment?\par \par \uc2 Raab says the energy price cap will help 15m families, to the tune of \u163\'00\'A3100 each. And he says the SNP should recognise the help Scotland is getting from the British army.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Rob Butler (Con) asks Raab if he agrees that, far from being a fifth wheel, the UK is at the heart of global security.\par \par \uc2 Raab does agree, and he defends the Aukus deal.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Rayner jokes about how there was meant to be a shortage of hot air this week. She asks for an assurance that no workers employed by energy companies will end up unemployed.\par \par \uc2 Raab says there is no shortage of hot air on the Labour benches. He says the government has secured carbon dioxide supplies. If we had listened to Labour, the economy would not have opened up.\par \par \uc2 Rayner says Raab should go back to his sun lounger and let her take over. This is a Conservative party that does not care about working people. People are worried about having to heat their homes as Raab is complaining about having to share his taxpayer-funded mansion [Chevening, a grace-and-favour mansion] with the foreign secretary.\par \par \uc2 Raab says Chevening is funded by a charity, and the taxpayer does not contribute a penny. He says Labour\u8217\'20\'19s plan to nationalise energy companies would put an extra \u163\'00\'A32,000 on bills, according to the CBI. He repeats the point about the UK still being in lockdown if Labour had been in power.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 Rayner says someone on \u163\'00\'A318,000 will lose more than \u163\'00\'A31,000. She says that is almost exactly the same as an average energy bill. How many extra days would someone have to work to be able to afford a night in a luxury hotel (like Raab\u8217\'20\'19s in the summer).\par \par \uc2 Raab says when Labour is in power, taxes go up and the economy gets worse.\par \par \uc2 Rayner says Raab does not even know the cost of his own holiday. She says a worker on the minimum wage would have to work an extra 50 days to afford one night at Raab\u8217\'20\'19s hotel.\par \par \uc2 Raab quotes Rayner saying previously that working people want opportunities, not a hand-out. That is what they are getting from this government, she says.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 Angela Rayner\uc2 , the deputy leader, offers her commiserations to the PM after he flew to the US and \u8220\'20\'1Cmade zero progress on the trade deal he promised us\u8221\'20\'1D. Does Raab still believe British workers are among the worst idlers in the world?\par \par \uc2 That is a quote from Britannia Unchained, a book Raab co-wrote some years ago with fellow Tory MPs. They used the phrase to make a point about poor British productivity.\par \par \uc2 Raab defends the PM\u8217\'20\'19s trip, and does not refer to the book.\par \par \uc2 Rayner asks how much the universal credit cut will cost workers?\par \par \uc2 Raab dodges the question, and says the government\u8217\'20\'19s economic plan is working.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 PMQs\par \par \uc2 Dominic Raab\uc2 , the deputy PM, is taking PMQs on behalf of \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 , who is still in the US.\par \par \uc2 Asked about face-to-face GP appointments, Raab thanks GPs for the work they have done during the pandemic. Appointment numbers are back at pre-pandemic levels, he says. He says patients should see doctors face to face when they need to.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back at the business committee, \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2 , the business secretary, said the government was considering a windfall tax on traders who have made big profits from the rising gas price.\par \par \uc2 Asked about reports the government was considering this, he said the government was looking at \u8220\'20\'1Call options\u8221\'20\'1D and that in Spain {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/daily-tax-report-international/spain-to-cap-windfall-energy-profits-as-rally-hits-inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  a measure of this kind has been introduced.}}}\uc2  He said the Spain plan involved looking at the energy system as a whole.\par \par \uc2 We are looking at all options. What they are doing in Spain is recognising that it\u8217\'20\'19s an entire system - the energy system is an entire system. I am in discussion with \uc2 Ofgem\uc2  and other officials, looking at all options.\par \par \uc2 UPDATE: Later Kwarteng said he was not a fan of windfall taxes. See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/22/joe-biden-northern-ireland-covid-coronavirus-pmqs-boris-johnson-keir-starmer-latest-updates-politics-live?page=with:block-614b1fd28f087b5c6fb4ad94#block-614b1fd28f087b5c6fb4ad94"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1.35pm.}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.56am BST\par \par \uc2 George Eustice, the environment secretary, sounded more like a DUP politician than a Conservative party politician in what he said about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s understanding of Northern Ireland this morning (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/22/joe-biden-northern-ireland-covid-coronavirus-pmqs-boris-johnson-keir-starmer-latest-updates-politics-live?page=with:block-614adf0c8f087b5c6fb4a941#block-614adf0c8f087b5c6fb4a941"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 9.31am),}}}\uc2  and that was highlighted by what Edwin Poots, the Stormont agriculture minister who led the DUP for about a month in the summer, told Radio Ulster today. Poots made almost exactly the same point Eustice did. He said:\par \par \uc2 As far as we\u8217\'20\'19re concerned, I think [the Biden administration] need to actually understand what the Belfast agreement actually does say and go and read it. If they do that, they will recognise that the Northern Ireland protocol is actually damaging to the Belfast agreement. It is creating a border where there shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be one and therefore the protocol has to go in order to meet the requirements of the Belfast agreement.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.43am BST\par \par \uc2 Turning away from the committee hearing, Labour has challenged the government to explain what will happen to carbon dioxide supplies if high gas prices are still a problem after three weeks, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2021/sep/22/bailout-of-us-carbon-dioxide-supplier-will-cost-uk-taxpayers-many-millions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 when the CF Industries bailout ends. }}}\uc2 Ed Miliband\uc2 , the shadow business secretary, said:\par \par \uc2 We welcome that this short-term deal has been struck, but the government must urgently engage with unions and the wider manufacturing industry, and explain the contingency plans in place in case issues are not resolved in three weeks.\par \par \uc2 Crucially, the government cannot keep blaming surging gas prices and supply chain chaos on external forces. It is a decade of Conservative missteps that has left the UK so exposed and vulnerable, without the diverse, resilient energy system we need to protect us from global volatility. It is businesses, consumers and families that are now paying the price.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.39am BST\par \par \uc2 Kwarteng says competition is essential for the gas market. He does not want to return to a \u8220\'20\'1Ccosy oligopoly\u8221\'20\'1D, where a small number of companies would be able to set the price.\par \par \uc2 He says he wants to kill the perception that small companies are necessarily bad and big companies good.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.36am BST\par \par \uc2 Q: What is going to happen to the warm homes discount?\par \par \uc2 Kwarteng says that is a matter for the budget.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.35am BST\par \par \uc2 Kwarteng says consumers should prepare for 'longer-term high prices'\par \par \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2 , the business secretary, is now giving evidence to the business committee about gas supply.\par \par \uc2 Darren Jones\uc2 , the Labour chair, asks what the PM meant yesterday when he said this would be temporary.\par \par \uc2 Kwarteng says that the gas prices has spiked, but that you would expect it to revert to the mean.\par \par \uc2 But he says customers should prepare for long-term high prices.\par \par \uc2 UPDATE: Kwarteng said:\par \par \uc2 I think \u8216\'20\'18temporary\u8217\'20\'19 means that it\u8217\'20\'19s a position where the price has spiked considerably... I think it has quadrupled in the last six months, seven months.\par \par \uc2 You would expect normally that the price would revert to the mean, it\u8217\'20\'19s not something that we think is going to be sustainable.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, we have to prepare for longer-term high prices.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.30am BST\par \par \uc2 At the business committee Alan Brown (SNP) asked the Ofgem CEO, \uc2 Jonathan Brearley\uc2 , about claims that the crisis could see the number of energy companies in the UK reduced to about 10.\par \par \uc2 Brearley said that the situation was \u8220\'20\'1Chighly changeable\u8221\'20\'1D and that, as a result, he did not think predictions like that were credible.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.25am BST\par \par \uc2 Emma Pinchbeck, chief executive of supplier trade body Energy UK, told the Commons business committee, that the Government and \uc2 Ofgem\uc2  were warned as early as two years ago that the energy sector was fragile. She explained:\par \par \uc2 I took this job a year ago. When I was hired, the chairman of Energy UK said that your biggest challenge is going to be the vulnerability of the retail market. I know that for a year or more before that my team have been making the case to the regulator and the government that the sector is fragile...\par \par \uc2 A lot of that is about market design. No competitive market would be making an average return of minus 1%. There\u8217\'20\'19s a short-term crisis here, which is in some ways out of our control, it\u8217\'20\'19s to do with the gas prices, but it\u8217\'20\'19s been exacerbated and arguably caused by our regulatory design. That is a resilience and security of supply risk in the future. It\u8217\'20\'19s terrible news for customers in the long run.\par \par \uc2 When we get through this, whatever support we put in place in the short term to make sure that customers are looked after, we desperately need to stop dismissing retailers when they say the market design is not fit for purpose, the market design is harming customers, the market design means we\u8217\'20\'19re not making any margin and the market design leaves us vulnerable and fragile.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.15am BST\par \par \uc2 Ofgem\uc2  boss says global gas price hike 'far, far above' any forecasts\par \par \uc2 Brearley told MPs that \uc2 Ofgem\uc2  does try to forecast what will happen to price of gas, but that what was happening now was beyond what was expected. He said:\par \par \uc2 We do do forecasts of the overall gas price, and we do look at the market projections at the time, but this is a discontinuity. It is far, far above any of our even most conservative projections. Because it\u8217\'20\'19s not just the issue of supply; it is supply plus the demand factors, plus some what sound like quite granular trade issues, around simply the number of ships that you can get on the water, all the issues around ports at the moment. So it is a continuation of events that have led to this, and it is beyond what any of us thought would happen at this time of year.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.02am BST\par \par \uc2 Ofgem\uc2  boss implies millions of people could be affected by energy companies going bust\par \par \uc2 Brearley told MPs that \u8220\'20\'1Cwell above\u8221\'20\'1D hundreds of thousands of customers could see their energy supplier go bust. He said:\par \par \uc2 We do expect a large number of customers to be affected, we\u8217\'20\'19ve already seen hundreds of thousands of customers affected, that may well go well above that. It\u8217\'20\'19s very hard for me to put a figure on it.\par \par \uc2 UPDATE: This is from Paul Kelso, \uc2 Sky\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s business correspondent.\par \par \uc2 enltrAt {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CommonsBEIS?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @CommonsBEIS}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ofgem?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @ofgem}}}\uc2  CEO \uc2 Jonathan Brearley\uc2  declines to put a figure on how many people could be signed to failing energy providers, beyond "many more" than hundreds of thousands. Privately industry estimates it could be six million households {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/xTBuidolU3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/xTBuidolU3}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Paul Kelso (@pkelso) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/pkelso/status/1440617355948670978?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 22, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.08am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.01am BST\par \par \uc2 Ofgem\uc2  boss says latest price hike unprecedented\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Brearley\uc2 , the Ofgem CEO, told the business committee that what was happening to gas prices was unlike previous price hikes. He said:\par \par \uc2 I do think this is a different kind of change. The sector has faced shocks, and in fact we\u8217\'20\'19ve talked about how we managed through the Covid crisis, which had a big impact on the energy sector overall.\par \par \uc2 But when you see that change - I encouraging you just to have a look at the change in gas price - it really is something that we don\u8217\'20\'19t think we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen before at this pace.\par \par \uc2 Brearley said this would feed through to customers. And he said many suppliers were under \u8220\'20\'1Chuge pressure\u8221\'20\'1D as a result.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.47am BST\par \par \uc2 Ofgem\uc2  boss casts doubt on PM's claim gas price crisis only temporary\par \par \uc2 The Commons business committee is taking evidence now from \uc2 Ofgem\uc2 , the energy regulator, on the gas price crisis.\par \par \uc2 Darren Jones\uc2  (Lab), the chair, started by asking why \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  felt confident saying yesterday that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/21/boris-johnson-brexit-uk-us-trade-biden-energy-covid-coronavirus-live-latest-updates?page=with:block-6149dafd8f084d4bbdefe9d2#block-6149dafd8f084d4bbdefe9d2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 this crisis would just be temporary.}}}\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Brearley\uc2 , the Ofgem CEO, says gas prices are six times what they were last year. They rose 70% in August, he said.\par \par \uc2 He said many factors contributed to that, including increasing global demand and some restrictions with supply. \u8220\'20\'1CSo it is very, very hard to predict how long that will last,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Jones asked again, if that was the case, why Johnson said yesterday the problem would only be temporary.\par \par \uc2 Brearley said that, in commodity markets, when you see a price spike, \u8220\'20\'1Chistory has suggested that those spikes do go away\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Brearley\uc2  Photograph: Parliament TV\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.20am BST\par \par \uc2 John McDonnell\uc2 , who was shadow chancellor when \uc2 Jeremy Corbyn\uc2  was Labour leader, told the Today programme this morning that he thought Sir \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  was making a \u8220\'20\'1Chuge mistake\u8221\'20\'1D with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2021/sep/21/major-row-brewing-over-plans-to-change-way-labour-elects-party-leader"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 his plans to change the leadership election rules.}}}\uc2  He said:\par \par \uc2 [Starmer] was elected on one member, one vote. He never mentioned any of these reforms in that election itself. He is opening himself up to challenges of dishonestly on this.\par \par \uc2 If it comes to the point where our members can\u8217\'20\'19t trust him, you know what the Tories are going to say, if your members can\u8217\'20\'19t trust you, how can the electorate.\par \par \uc2 Ned Simons from HuffPost has more from McDonnell\u8217\'20\'19s interview {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/entry/keir-starmer-labour-leadership-rule-change_uk_614ade7fe4b0efa77f86e0d7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here.}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.14am BST\par \par \uc2 Priti Patel\uc2 , the home secretary, has also welcomed the granting of the injunction against the motorway protesters.\par \par \uc2 enltrI can confirm this Government and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NationalHways?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @NationalHways}}}\uc2  are taking action to bring the ongoing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/M25?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #M25}}}\uc2  protests to end with an important injunction so people can get moving again.\par \par \uc2 We will not tolerate lives being put at risk.\par \par \uc2 Those who continue to do so risk imprisonment.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Priti Patel\uc2  (@pritipatel) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/pritipatel/status/1440590416613502982?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 22, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.13am BST\par \par \uc2 Grant Shapps, the transport secretary, told the transport committee this morning that the injunction granted against the M25 protesters (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/22/joe-biden-northern-ireland-covid-coronavirus-pmqs-boris-johnson-keir-starmer-latest-updates-politics-live?page=with:block-614aeb378f08b228c94982af#block-614aeb378f08b228c94982af"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  9.38am) }}}\uc2  should bring an end to the demonstrations. He said:\par \par \uc2 It barely goes without saying, it\u8217\'20\'19s irresponsible, dangerous and completely counterproductive. It\u8217\'20\'19s unacceptable and I hope the injunction will bring it to a close.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the process there was a somewhat different approach being taken. Yesterday the police were on the scene much more quickly. The injunction will greatly strengthen their hand...\par \par \uc2 We will review the powers because clearly it\u8217\'20\'19s unacceptable for people to be able to walk on to not just a major highway but a motorway, stop traffic, be released the next day and do the same thing again. An injunction may just be an interim way of doing that.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.47am BST\par \par \uc2 New Unite general secretary Sharon Graham says she won't attend Labour conference - but it's 'not a snub'\par \par \uc2 Sharon Graham, the new Unite leader, has said she will not be attending this year\u8217\'20\'19s Labour conference. Explaining her decision, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-58644894"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she told the BBC:}}}\par \par \uc2 We shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t always do what we have always done just because we have always done it.\par \par \uc2 I am days into my leadership - we currently have 16 industrial disputes going on, from \uc2 Tesco\uc2  drivers to \uc2 Weetabix\uc2  to locksmiths in Scunthorpe and Wolverhampton.\par \par \uc2 What I need to do is be with those workers in dispute and personally take leadership.\par \par \uc2 Graham said this was \u8220\'20\'1Cdefinitely not a snub\u8221\'20\'1D to Sir \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 , the Labour leader. She said she had had a meeting with him on Monday that she described as \u8220\'20\'1Ccordial\u8221\'20\'1D. But that was before Starmer announced plans to change the Labour leadership elections rules - plans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2021/sep/21/boris-johnson-brexit-uk-us-trade-biden-energy-covid-coronavirus-live-latest-updates?page=with:block-6149c0c88f086e40d2e8cc7e#block-6149c0c88f086e40d2e8cc7e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 that Graham criticised yesterday.}}}\par \par \uc2 Graham also told the \uc2 BBC\uc2  that, if there is a vote at conference on David Evans, the party\u8217\'20\'19s general secretary, Unite would vote to remove him. That was because of the way he was implementing redundancies, she said.\par \par \uc2 Sharon Graham. Photograph: Sharon Graham Campaign/PA\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.47am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.38am BST\par \par \uc2 Shapps says protesters could face jail for blocking roads after injunction granted\par \par \uc2 Grant Shapps, the transport secretary, has announced that National Highways have been granted a court injunction against environmental protesters. That means activists who block roads could face jail.\par \par \uc2 enltrInvading a motorway is reckless & puts lives at risk. I asked National Highways to seek an injunction against M25 protestors which a judge granted last night. Effective later today, activists will face contempt of court with possible imprisonment if they flout.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Rt Hon Grant Shapps MP (@grantshapps) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/grantshapps/status/1440587104069709838?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 22, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.31am BST\par \par \uc2 George Eustice implies \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  does not fully understand \u8216\'20\'18complicated\u8217\'20\'19 Northern Ireland issue\par \par \uc2 Good morning. Yesterday \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  told \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  at the White House about his concerns that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/21/biden-tells-johnson-trade-tensions-must-not-threaten-peace-in-northern-ireland"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the UK\u8217\'20\'19s stance on the Northern Ireland protocol could undermine the Good Friday agreement.}}}\uc2  It is a concern shared by Democrats and Republicans in Washington, and one that has been expressed many times. Johnson told Biden diplomatically that he too wanted to protect the Good Friday agreement. But in an interview this morning George Eustice, the environment secretary, said that Biden was \u8220\'20\'1Cwrong\u8221\'20\'1D about this, and he implied the US president did not understand the issue.\par \par \uc2 Eustice told Sky News:\par \par \uc2 [Biden] is probably at the moment just reading the headlines, reading what the \uc2 EU\uc2  is saying, reading what Ireland might be saying, which is that they would like the Northern Ireland protocol to work in the way the \uc2 EU\uc2  envisage.\par \par \uc2 We think he is wrong because the truth is that unless we have a sustainable solution that enables trade to continue between GB and Northern Ireland then we are going to have issues, and that itself would become a challenge to the Belfast agreement.\par \par \uc2 We will obviously explain to the United States effectively it is tantamount to saying that potatoes grown in one part of the United States can\u8217\'20\'19t be sold in another part of the United States.\par \par \uc2 When you explain some of those provisions in detail, it is understood by the \uc2 US government\uc2  that that clearly does not make any sense and therefore should be revisited.\par \par \uc2 Eustice also said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cvery complicated\u8221\'20\'1D, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not sure he does fully appreciate all of that\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Eustice was just saying what cabinet ministers think. But it is unusual for any of them to be so blunt (and patronising) about the US president in public.\par \par \uc2 As my colleague Rowena Mason reports, in his interviews this morning Eustice also said the bailout of the private US firm that supplies carbon dioxide to the food industry will run into \u8220\'20\'1Cmany millions of pounds\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2021/sep/22/bailout-of-us-carbon-dioxide-supplier-will-cost-uk-taxpayers-many-millions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bailout of US CO2 supplier will cost UK taxpayers \u8216\'20\'18millions\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 Here is the agenda for the day.\par \par \uc2 9.30am: Grant Shapps, the transport secretary, gives evidence to the Commons transport committee.\par \par \uc2 9.30am: Matthew Roycroft, permanent secretary at the Home Office, gives evidence to the Commons home affairs committee.\par \par \uc2 10.30am: \uc2 Ofgem\uc2 , Energy UK and National Energy Action give evidence to the Commons business committee about the UK gas market. At 11.30am \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2 , the business secretary, gives evidence.\par \par \uc2 12pm: \uc2 Dominic Raab\uc2 , the deputy prime minister, faces \uc2 Angela Rayner\uc2 , Labour\u8217\'20\'19s deputy leader, at PMQs.\par \par \uc2 Lunchtime (UK time): \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  is due to give media interviews in the US.\par \par \uc2 2pm: \uc2 Prof Chris Whitty\uc2 , the chief medical officer for England, \uc2 Prof Jonathan Van-Tam\uc2 , his deputy, and Prof Wei Shen Lim, chair of the JCVI\u8217\'20\'19s Covid sub-committee, give evidence to the Commons education committee about vaccinating children.\par \par \uc2 Later Johnson will be addressing the \uc2 UN general assembly\uc2  in New York, but his speech is not expected until about 9pm UK time.\par \par \uc2 For the latest Covid developments, do read our global live blog.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2021/sep/22/coronavirus-live-news-brazil-health-minister-tests-positive-at-un-india-urges-uk-to-resolve-quarantine-dispute"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Coronavirus live news: Brazil health minister tests positive at UN; Australia could reopen borders \u8216\'20\'18by Christmas\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 I try to monitor the comments below the line (BTL) but it is impossible to read them all. If you have a direct question, do include \u8220\'20\'1CAndrew\u8221\'20\'1D in it somewhere and I\u8217\'20\'19m more likely to find it. I do try to answer questions, and if they are of general interest, I will post the question and reply above the line (ATL), although I can\u8217\'20\'19t promise to do this for everyone.\par \par \uc2 If you want to attract my attention quickly, it is probably better to use \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . I\u8217\'20\'19m on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AndrewSparrow"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @AndrewSparrow}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Alternatively, you can email me at andrew.sparrow@theguardian.com\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.40am BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210922eh9m0015q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
