\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re trying to survive\u8217\'20\'19: workers face cuts as US public sector lags in recovery\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,201 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2021\par 09:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Working in US public sector feels untenable for many amid lack of benefits, low pay and lack of general investment\par \par \uc2 Mopping, sweeping, waxing floors, trash removal, sanitizing, scrubbing bathrooms and cleaning student apartments.\par \par \uc2 For $10.65 an hour, Nelly Nunez and Pamala Greathouse have worked as janitorial custodians at the \uc2 University of New Mexico\uc2  in Albuquerque through the pandemic as essential workers, facing extra workloads, understaffing and Covid-19 safety concerns, while struggling to make ends meet on low pay.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re trying to survive and we\u8217\'20\'19re living from paycheck to paycheck just to pay our bills and to stay above water,\u8221\'20\'1D said Greathouse. \u8220\'20\'1CEverything has gone up in pricing, groceries, gas, everything. With the cost of living rising as fast as it is, we\u8217\'20\'19re still at the same pay rate.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nunez and Greathouse are far from alone. Workers in the public sector around the United States have faced drastic cuts and layoffs, leaving workers even more understaffed, underpaid and overworked through the Covid-19 pandemic than they were before. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/03/business/economy/august-2021-jobs-report.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 About 815,000 jobs}}}\uc2  have been lost in the American public sector since the start of the pandemic and job recovery in the public sector has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewtrusts.org/en/research-and-analysis/articles/2021/09/14/state-and-local-government-job-growth-lags-as-economy-recovers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lagged}}}\uc2  behind the private sector.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a crisis in the public sector in this country in all public services,\u8221\'20\'1D said Margaret Cook, vice-president of CWA-PHEW (\uc2 Communications Workers of America\uc2  Public, Healthcare and Education Workers).\par \par \uc2 Local and state governments have made significant cuts in anticipation of budget shortfalls caused by Covid-19, but even as federal assistance has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/blog/up-front/2020/12/23/why-is-state-and-local-employment-falling-faster-than-revenues/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 helped}}}\uc2  offset shortfalls, workers are still bearing the brunt of the pandemic\u8217\'20\'19s impact in the form of increased workloads, lack of benefits, low pay and a lack of general investment in public services in favor of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/archive/2020/10/public-infrastructure-civil-rights/616671/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 austerity and privatization}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For many, working in the public sector feels almost untenable.\par \par \uc2 Nunez, who has worked for 16 years at the public university in New Mexico, is charged for parking and pays for health insurance coverage, leaving her with little left to take care of herself and her family. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not enough to make ends meet or support our families,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nunez. \u8220\'20\'1CMost people here have to work two jobs, because we can\u8217\'20\'19t get by on working for the university alone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cook said that local unions and workers are fighting for a $15 an hour minimum wage and to pressure local and state governments to use federal aid to support and compensate workers in the public sector who have continued to keep public services running through the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 No worker should make below $15 an hour\par \par \uc2 Margaret Cook, vice-president of CWA-PHEW\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow is the time to make sure they\u8217\'20\'19re compensated for the type of care and responsibility they hold,\u8221\'20\'1D said Cook. \u8220\'20\'1CNo worker should make below $15 an hour. That is just the minimum threshold that we believe a worker should be able to work a full-time job and not rely on food stamps from the state if they work for the state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Louise Irizarry, 59, worked for eight years at \uc2 Kennesaw State University\uc2 , a public university in Georgia, as an administrative specialist in the student affairs department. She was one of several workers at the university who were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://theksusentinel.com/2020/09/01/university-lays-off-24-employees-amid-pandemic/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  laid off}}}\uc2  in late 2020 in anticipation of budget shortfalls, despite {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.kennesaw.edu/stories/2020/ksu-fall-2020-enrollment-increase.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 record }}}\uc2  student enrollment at the school and a decision to provide {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fox5atlanta.com/news/former-georgia-educator-weighs-in-on-state-budget"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bonuses}}}\uc2  to all university workers shortly after the cuts, citing unanticipated funds.\par \par \uc2 Some of the lowest-paid workers in public higher education systems, who are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/cuts-to-the-state-and-local-public-sector-will-disproportionately-harm-women-and-black-workers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disproportionately}}}\uc2  women and Black workers, have faced the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2020/11/17/higher-ed-job-loss/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most consistent and drastic}}}\uc2  layoffs and cuts through the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 When she was laid off, Irizarry was less than two years away from her pension vesting at the 10-year mark. Even though there have been hundreds of job openings at the university since the layoff, she has struggled to find a comparable position, though laidoff employees are supposed to receive priority in new hirings.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve lost my pension,\u8221\'20\'1D said Irizarry, who has only been able to find two part-time jobs with no benefits to replace the full-time job she lost. She is still trying to regain employment at the university, while her subsidized Cobra health insurance is due to expire soon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have a chronic illness and to trying to get healthcare has been just impossible,\u8221\'20\'1D added Irizarry. \u8220\'20\'1CThe university had opportunities where they had a chance to be able to fix this and make right by it and they haven\u8217\'20\'19t.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Other workers in the public sector who have kept their jobs through the pandemic have faced greater workloads, while struggling to care for sick family members and take care of children who have been in and out of remote learning as schools and daycare centers have either been shut down or have periodically closed for quarantines.\par \par \uc2 Kristen McManis works as an operator at a 24/7 distribution center for a utilities company owned by the city of Gainesville, Florida.\par \par \uc2 Through the pandemic she has worked hundreds of hours of overtime, while she and her coworkers have struggled to care for family and children. Family and medical leave was initially offered to employees, but then expired after workers were discouraged from using it. She had to donate her own personal leave time to coworkers who couldn\u8217\'20\'19t access childcare or had to take care of sick family members.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve been donating a lot of my leads to my co-workers who have been sick, and they\u8217\'20\'19ve exhausted all of their leaves. I\u8217\'20\'19m constantly getting requests to donate my time to them. These are people that I care about,\u8221\'20\'1D said McManis, who has also struggled to see her family and protect at-risk family members from Covid, while working erratic schedules and through holidays.\par \par \uc2 Louise Ortiz has worked at the Museum of Indian Arts and Culture, operated by the state government in Santa Fe, New Mexico, for eight years. She makes $12.75 an hour and relies on Medicaid for health coverage because she can\u8217\'20\'19t afford the insurance offered through her employer.\par \par \uc2 She continued to work during the pandemic while the museum was shut down, but received no extra compensation for the additional duties she took on, which included contact tracing, assisting the billing department and configuring a new computer system, even as much higher paid employees were doing the same work.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Ortiz was diagnosed with breast cancer and is still recovering, while working in-person at the museum.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t make ends meet. I can\u8217\'20\'19t support my child. I can\u8217\'20\'19t get an apartment for me and my daughter. I can\u8217\'20\'19t survive. I\u8217\'20\'19m in the process of trying to get a car so I can get another full-time job, because I need another full-time job in order to survive,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ortiz. \u8220\'20\'1CI go to work every day, I put in my 40 hours a week, I do what I\u8217\'20\'19m supposed to do. I love my job. But it would be nice if I could get paid for it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210922eh9m0018j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
