\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Garment workers in America\u8217\'20\'19s fashion capital may make just $6 an hour. A new law could change that\b0\par\par\uc2 Paulina Velasco in Los Angeles \par \uc2 936 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2021\par 10:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 California measure would end practice of paying workers per piece made and put brands on the hook for wage theft claims\par \par \uc2 Los Angeles is considered the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://garmentworkeract.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 center}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.labor.ucla.edu/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/DirtyThreads_FINAL_web_single.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 of garment}}}\uc2  manufacturing in America, employing over 40,000 people to make clothing for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.labor.ucla.edu/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/DirtyThreads_FINAL_web_single.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fashion}}}\uc2  brands including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fashionista.com/2017/07/made-in-la-los-angeles-clothing-fashion-labels"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Reformation}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.labor.ucla.edu/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/DirtyThreads_FINAL_web_single.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Forever 21, Charlotte Russe and Wet Seal}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But for decades those workers have functioned under a system that sees them paid per piece they construct, meaning they often earn less than $6 per hour, according {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.labor.ucla.edu/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/DirtyThreads_FINAL_web_single.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to a University of California, Los Angeles, study}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 less than half of California\u8217\'20\'19s minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 Now, a new law could change that.\par \par \uc2 California lawmakers recently approved the Garment Worker Protection Act, which would eliminate the piece rate system and ensure workers are paid an hourly minimum wage. The bill would also expand who is liable for stolen wages \u8211\'20\'13 meaning a brand like Charlotte Russe, for example, would share responsibility for paying out wage theft claims filed by workers who make their clothing in factories such as the ones in downtown LA. Right now, those claims are filed against the factories themselves but can languish for years before being paid out, if they ever are.\par \par \uc2 Marissa Nuncio, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://garmentworkercenter.org/marissa-nuncio/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 director}}}\uc2  of the Garment Worker Center and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://calmatters.org/explainers/california-legislature-bills-passed-2021/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 co-sponsor}}}\uc2  of the bill, says this latter part is key, because brands have long been able to shield themselves by shifting the blame on to the factories that make their clothing. \u8220\'20\'1CThrough subcontracting, they\u8217\'20\'19re really able to buffer themselves and evade any kind of legal liability for improper wages,\u8221\'20\'1D Nuncio says.\par \par \uc2 Garment workers and advocates say the current system has been designed to incentivize rapid production while exploiting workers \u8211\'20\'13 most of whom are immigrants and women of color.\par \par \uc2 Workers such as Santiago Puac, an immigrant from Guatemala who has been in the garment industry for 17 years. Pauc works on a single-needle sewing machine, attaching zippers and labels to dresses and other small finishing details. It\u8217\'20\'19s a job he says has taken a lot of skill to learn, since such details can be complicated.\par \par \uc2 But the work pays him just 15 cents or less per piece, meaning even on highly productive days he\u8217\'20\'19s only taking home $75 a day.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt would be such a big change in my life,\u8221\'20\'1D Puac told \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  in Spanish of the proposed law. \u8220\'20\'1CKnowing I could have a secure income, a set number of work hours, and earn a just wage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Garment Worker Protection Act has already cleared the state legislature and is now awaiting the signature of Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , who has until October to approve it.\par \par \uc2 The bill, which garment workers helped craft, would add heft to an existing law, passed in 1999, that also sought to address wage theft in the industry by setting up a restitution fund for wage theft claims that fashion companies pay into. But advocates say fashion brands have spent 20 years circumventing it by hiring contractors and claiming that they do not fall under its definition of \u8220\'20\'1Cgarment manufacturer\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202120220SB62"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bill}}}\uc2  seeks to sharpen that definition by making brands share joint liability with their contractors for unpaid wages, other compensation, interest, and penalties.\par \par \uc2 The author of the bill, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sd24.senate.ca.gov/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 state senator}}}\uc2 Mar\u237\'00\'EDa Elena Durazo\uc2 , says it originally intended to make brands fully liable for lost wages, but was later amended.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAlthough personally I think they should be liable for everything, it gives recognition that the immediate employer would face the penalties and the brands would face the lost wages,\u8221\'20\'1D says Durazo.\par \par \uc2 While the \uc2 California Chamber of Commerce\uc2 , a business lobbying organization, has labeled the bill a \u8220\'20\'1Cjobs killer\u8221\'20\'1D, many fashion brands have come out in support of it, saying the bill will not only help workers but level the playing field between companies that pay a living wage and those that don\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 In July, a coalition of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://garmentworkeract.org/open-letter-to-the-california-chamber-of-commerce"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at least 70}}}\uc2  fashion businesses wrote an open letter to the Chamber of Commerce saying: \u8220\'20\'1CThe sustainable business community wants a level playing field, enabled by strong labor laws, because we believe it is crucial for California fashion to flourish well into the 21st century.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Senator Durazo says these business owners are often women whose brand relies on their image of environmental sustainability and caring about their employees. Nuncio put it more bluntly: \u8220\'20\'1CThey don\u8217\'20\'19t want to compete with sweatshop users.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Covid-19 pandemic has also added urgency to the issue. Thousands continued to work throughout California\u8217\'20\'19s lockdowns, with many shifting to making masks. Poor working conditions {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/jul/11/coronavirus-los-angeles-mask-making"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 caused many}}}\uc2  garment workers to contract the virus.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe pandemic exposed the inequities in our economy and rather than turn our backs on those workers, now\u8217\'20\'19s the time to fix it,\u8221\'20\'1D says Durazo. \u8220\'20\'1CIf we want to bring real meaning to the word \u8216\'20\'18essential workers\u8217\'20\'19, then let\u8217\'20\'19s do that by helping them support their families.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Puac says the financial boost would help him better support his six children, most of whom still live in Guatemala. \u8220\'20\'1CI could be with my family more, eat nutritious food,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, adding that fashion brands should be excited for workers to have more money in their pockets; they might even spend it on new clothes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think the brand selling the clothing also benefits, because having a minimum wage would mean more of us buying their products. All of us that will have money because we make the wage we deserve, we too will have money to spend.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210918eh9i0018h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
