\par\par \uc2 Global development\par \b \uc2 US restaurant workers are getting stiffed. It\u8217\'20\'19s time for employers to pay up\b0\par\par\uc2 Gene Marks \par \uc2 773 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2021\par 16:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The tipped wage scale allows restaurants to pay workers less than the legal minimum wage. Some unscrupulous owners nevertheless try to get away with paying even less\par \par \uc2 This may come as a shock to some but there\u8217\'20\'19s a different minimum wage for restaurant employees than for workers in most other industries. In Pennsylvania, for example, that minimum wage is just $2.83 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Every statein the US has these \u8220\'20\'1Ctipped wages\u8221\'20\'1D. According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.minimum-wage.org/tipped"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 minimumwage.org}}}\uc2 , the tipped wage is as little as $2.13 an hour in 19 states and as high as $10 in New York.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s how it works in Pennsylvania: restaurant owners here have to pay their workers just $2.83 an hour (the federal minimum) as long as those employees receive enough tips so that their total hourly wage exceeds the state (and national) minimum of $7.25.\par \par \uc2 There is a logic to this. State-based tipped wage rules are designed to allow restaurant owners to lower their payroll costs and let customers make up the difference.\par \par \uc2 To me, it\u8217\'20\'19s all still woefully too little, but most restaurants take advantage of these tipped wage rules. And who can blame them? It\u8217\'20\'19s legal and common and a significant help towards keeping overhead (and prices) under control. You would think that having to pay an employee a mere $2.83 an hour is enough of a saving. Unfortunately for some restaurants owners it\u8217\'20\'19s not. So they steal.\par \par \uc2 For example, there\u8217\'20\'19s the Bottle Bar East restaurant in Philadelphia, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bizjournals.com/philadelphia/news/2021/09/01/fishtown-bar-back-pay-wages-federal-investigation.html?"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last week}}}\uc2  was found in violation of these wage (and other) rules and ordered by a federal judge to pay $246,457.99 in back wages, as well as an equal amount of liquidated damages and civil money penalties to 73 bartenders, servers, cooks and dishwashers. Some employees were owed as much as $42,000. I live in Philly. I won\u8217\'20\'19t be eating there.\par \par \uc2 I love Italian food, but I won\u8217\'20\'19t be eating at Maggiano\u8217\'20\'19s near the convention center in Philadelphia either. Why? Because, back in April, the geniuses running that location {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bizjournals.com/philadelphia/news/2021/04/12/maggianos-center-city-wage-violations-restaurant.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 were ordered}}}\uc2  to pay $116,308 in back wages for wage theft that affected 82 workers. The restaurant chain {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.restaurantbusinessonline.com/top-500-chains-2020/maggianos-little-italy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 grossed more}}}\uc2  than $400m in sales in 2019.\par \par \uc2 Business owners are being accused of stealing from their employees wherever you look.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ny.eater.com/2021/8/16/22626956/cecil-steakhouse-harlem-class-action-lawsuit-time-shaving-minimum-wage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 class-action lawsuit}}}\uc2  filed in New York against a popular steakhouse alleges \u8220\'20\'1Cwage theft and other illegal practices that shortchanged staffers\u8217\'20\'19 pay\u8221\'20\'1D, which affected about 50 current and former employees. A San Francisco restaurant {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com/z-and-y-restaurant-san-francisco-chinatown-stolen-tips-wages-2021-8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed to pay}}}\uc2  22 workers roughly $73,000 each for wage violations in a settlement to avoid a costly lawsuit. Two well-known eateries in Nashville {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tennessean.com/story/money/2021/03/18/nashville-restaurant-workers-awarded-628-k-wage-theft-lawsuit-settlements/4734302001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed earlier}}}\uc2  this year to pay a combined $1.03m to settle separate lawsuits alleging tip and wage theft. A casino in Pennsylvania {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mcall.com/business/mc-biz-wind-creek-bethlehem-class-action-settlement-tipped-workers-20210430-gtoe4vywxbcnbdoyrsg3ajak3m-story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed to pay}}}\uc2  $6m to settle a class action lawsuit alleging a failure to pay its tipped employees the proper amount under federal and state law.\par \par \uc2 There are explanations for these shenanigans, and none of them good. Maybe the pandemic put so much pressure on some restaurants that the managers thought that shortchanging employees could help them survive financially. Perhaps their point-of-sale systems were inadequately set up to track tipped wages. Or maybe these same managers were simply taking advantage of their employees\u8217\'20\'19 trust and thought they could get away with it.\par \par \uc2 One thing\u8217\'20\'19s for sure: those employers are stupid.\par \par \uc2 Haven\u8217\'20\'19t you seen the news? Good employees are in short supply and that situation isn\u8217\'20\'19t going to significantly change any time in the near future. Not only that but thanks to the pro-labor Biden administration, you can count on an uptick in the enforcement of wage and other employment rules during the coming years. If you own a restaurant or a business that employs tipped workers, you better make sure you\u8217\'20\'19re educating each and every one of them as to how their pay rate is calculated. You better make sure your systems are in order. You better pay special attention to \u8220\'20\'1Cpooled tips\u8221\'20\'1D and overtime pay. Oh, and one other thing: you better make sure not to steal from your workers.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a very bad idea to play games with your employees\u8217\'20\'19 wages. Getting caught will result in significant penalties and a lot of bad press. But even more importantly it\u8217\'20\'19s almost guaranteed to ensure that the best workers will avoid working for you. And customers will avoid you too.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210912eh9c000um\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
