\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Revealed: \uc2 Google\uc2  illegally underpaid thousands of workers across dozens of countries\b0\par\par\uc2 Julia Carrie Wong \par \uc2 2,296 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2021\par 17:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Documents show company dragged feet to correct disparity after learning it was failing to comply with laws in UK, Europe and Asia\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  has been illegally underpaying thousands of temporary workers in dozens of countries and delayed correcting the pay rates for more than two years as it attempted to cover up the problem, \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  can reveal.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  executives have been aware since at least May 2019 that the company was failing to comply with local laws in the UK, Europe and Asia that mandate temporary workers be paid equal rates to full-time employees performing similar work, internal \uc2 Google\uc2  documents and emails reviewed by \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  show.\par \par \uc2 But rather than immediately correct the errors, the company dragged its feet for more than two years, the documents show, citing concern about the increased cost to departments that rely heavily on temporary workers, potential exposure to legal claims, and fear of negative press attention.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  executives and attorneys at one point pursued a plan to come into compliance slowly and at the least possible cost to itself, despite acknowledging that such a move was not \u8220\'20\'1Cthe correct outcome from a compliance perspective\u8221\'20\'1D and could place the staffing companies it contracts with \u8220\'20\'1Cin a difficult position, legally and ethically\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  admitted the failures and said it would conduct an investigation after being contacted by \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile the team hasn\u8217\'20\'19t increased the comparator rate benchmarks for some years, actual pay rates for temporary staff have increased numerous times in that period,\u8221\'20\'1D said Spyro Karetsos, Google\u8217\'20\'19s chief compliance officer, in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CMost temporary staff are paid significantly more than the comparator rates.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNevertheless, it\u8217\'20\'19s clear that this process has not been handled consistent with the high standards to which we hold ourselves as a company. We\u8217\'20\'19re doing a thorough review, and we\u8217\'20\'19re committed to identifying and addressing any pay discrepancies that the team has not already addressed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd we\u8217\'20\'19ll be conducting a review of our compliance practices in this area. In short, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to figure out what went wrong here, why it happened, and we\u8217\'20\'19re going to make it right.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A whistleblower represented by Whistleblower Aid has filed a complaint about the alleged violations with the \uc2 US Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While international labor law is not under the purview of the SEC, the complaint alleges that \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s failure to disclose the pay parity liabilities, which it estimates could amount to $100m, constitute material misstatements in its quarterly financial reports, a violation of US securities law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe disclosure makes it clear that \uc2 Google\uc2  hasn\u8217\'20\'19t just broken labor laws around the world, but has misled investors about major legal and financial liabilities,\u8221\'20\'1D said John Tye, founder and chief disclosure officer of Whistleblower Aid. \u8220\'20\'1CThe lawful, anonymous whistleblower disclosure is a critical step toward ensuring that \uc2 Google\uc2  is held to account. We urge the SEC to bring an enforcement action against \uc2 Google\uc2 , and protect the rights of investors to receive complete and accurate information.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The issue stems from \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s extensive reliance on what it calls its \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2018/dec/11/google-tvc-full-time-employees-training-document"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 extended workforce}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, as well as the plethora of local laws that govern how such workers must be treated in the dozens of countries where \uc2 Google\uc2  operates. \uc2 Google\uc2  maintains a workforce of more than 100,000 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2019/may/28/a-white-collar-sweatshop-google-assistant-contractors-allege-wage-theft"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 temps, vendors and contractors}}}\uc2  who are not directly employed by the company, but who perform work on its behalf, on tasks ranging from food service and security to coding and data analysis.\par \par \uc2 The vast majority of these workers are \u8220\'20\'1Cvendors\u8221\'20\'1D who work on long-term projects that are wholly managed by the vendor company, such as content moderation, and have little interaction with \uc2 Google\uc2  employees.\par \par \uc2 But at any given time, \uc2 Google\uc2  also employs thousands of temporary workers. While they are paid by staffing agencies, temps report directly to \uc2 Google\uc2  managers. The departments that rely most heavily on temporary workers include recruiting, marketing and \uc2 Waymo\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s autonomous vehicle subsidiary. Globally, \uc2 Google\uc2  spends about $800m annually on temporary workers.\par \par \uc2 The use of contingent workers is common in the tech industry, but \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s reliance on a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2018-07-25/inside-google-s-shadow-workforce?sref=6flipp83"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shadow workforce}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D that outnumbers its direct employee base has long drawn criticism from the company\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2019/apr/02/google-workers-sign-letter-temp-contractors-protest"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 own employees}}}\uc2 , as well as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/05/technology/senate-google-temp-workers.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 politicians}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2019/sep/23/google-pittsburgh-union-roots-transform-tech-industry"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 labor unions}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 More than 30 countries, including the UK, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ec.europa.eu/social/main.jsp?catId=706&langId=en&intPageId=207"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 EU member states}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www2.staffingindustry.com/row/Editorial/Daily-News/India-Supreme-Court-declares-temporary-employees-to-be-paid-on-par-with-regular-workers-Times-of-India-39839"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 India}}}\uc2  and Taiwan, have enacted \u8220\'20\'1Cpay parity\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Cequal treatment\u8221\'20\'1D laws that require temporary workers to be treated equally to full-time employees (FTEs) who perform the same or similar work, according to \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s internal guidance on the laws. The US, where the majority of \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s temps are based, does not have any such protections.\par \par \uc2 In general, these laws require equivalent treatment of temps and FTEs when it comes to pay, hours, breaks, vacation, holidays and certain perks. In the UK, pay parity is required after a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nidirect.gov.uk/articles/agency-workers-entitled-equal-treatment-after-12-weeks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12-week qualifying period}}}\uc2 , for example, while in Ireland it is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/employment/types_of_employment/agency_workers.html#"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mandated}}}\uc2  from the first day of work. Some countries also mandate equivalency for benefits and bonuses.\par \par \uc2 The staffing agency and the hirer \u8211\'20\'13 in this case, \uc2 Google\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 share the responsibility for ensuring equitable treatment, but it is the hirer\u8217\'20\'19s responsibility to provide the agency with accurate data about the pay rates.\par \par \uc2 In 2012 and 2013, \uc2 Google\uc2  undertook an exercise to map all its temporary roles in EMEA (Europe, Middle East and Africa) with the comparable \uc2 Google\uc2  full-time roles, according to the internal memo. The exercise produced \u8220\'20\'1Ccomparator\u8221\'20\'1D rates that \uc2 Google\uc2  then provided to the staffing agencies that recruit and employ its temporary staff. The company performed a similar exercise for temp roles in APAC (Asia Pacific) in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Since those exercises were carried out, compensation for full-time \uc2 Google\uc2  employees has increased substantially. The median total compensation rose more than 38%, from $197,274 to $273,493, between 2017 and 2020, according to SEC filings.\par \par \uc2 But the comparator rates were never updated. \u8220\'20\'1CIdeally this would be done annually,\u8221\'20\'1D reads the internal \uc2 Google\uc2  memo on the issue, which acknowledges that it is \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s obligation to keep the information up to date.\par \par \uc2 By 15 May 2019, the problem was known to Adrienne Crowther, the executive responsible for \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s extended workforce team (xWS), her comments on the internal memo show.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYikes! This seems WAY too old,\u8221\'20\'1D she wrote in response to the \u8220\'20\'1C2012-2013\u8221\'20\'1D date of the comparator data.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAgreed,\u8221\'20\'1D responded Alan Barry, a compliance manager for Google\u8217\'20\'19s extended workforce team. Barry went on to suggest that xWS \u8220\'20\'1Crefresh\u8221\'20\'1D the data as part of a larger project working on ensuring accurate mapping between temporary and full-time positions.\par \par \uc2 Scale of the problem\par \par \uc2 Given the high turnover rate of temporary workers, the number of workers affected by \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s failure could be in the tens of thousands. One document shows that, as of 16 February 2021, \uc2 Google\uc2  had 1,030 temporary workers in countries with pay parity laws, the largest numbers in the UK (343), India (222), Ireland (207) and Germany (59).\par \par \uc2 That document \u8211\'20\'13 an audit of countries with pay parity laws performed for \uc2 Google\uc2  by Deloitte \u8211\'20\'13 highlighted another problem with the company\u8217\'20\'19s legal compliance. Deloitte reported that pay-parity laws had been enacted in 16 countries where \uc2 Google\uc2  was employing more than 350 temporary workers without providing equal treatment rates, including Argentina, Australia, Brazil, Canada, Israel, Switzerland and Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s underpayments to its temporary workers are substantial. One internal analysis estimated that \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s expenses for 1,200 temps in EMEA and APAC regions would need to increase by $17.3m to comply with pay parity laws, of which $12.65m would go to the temporary workers in increased wages and bonuses. (An additional $4.4m in costs would be the result of the estimated 35% markup that staffing agencies charge.)\par \par \uc2 But that analysis was based on a proposal to correct the rates for new hires only, without providing raises and back pay to existing and previous temp workers for the time they worked at the incorrect rates, suggesting that the total amount of lost wages to current and past workers is significantly higher.\par \par \uc2 Individual temps were paid between 12% and 50% less than they should have been, according to an internal memo also from February 2021.\par \par \uc2 Another document showed that temporary workers in \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s human resources department in the UK were paid between \u163\'00\'A34.10 and \u163\'00\'A38.25 less per hour than they should have been, with some temps earning \u163\'00\'A314.17 instead of \u163\'00\'A318.27 and others earning \u163\'00\'A321.56 instead of \u163\'00\'A329.80.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s delay in addressing the rates could hurt former temporary workers\u8217\'20\'19 ability to seek redress. Under the UK\u8217\'20\'19s Agency Worker Regulations 2010, temps can appeal to an employment tribunal if they believe their rights have been breached, but they must do so within three months. In Ireland, appeals to the rights commissioner are supposed to be made within six months.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese agency workers have been the victims of wage theft,\u8221\'20\'1D said Matthew Creagh, an employment rights policy officer for the Trades Union Council, who criticized the UK\u8217\'20\'19s lack of a state regulator to enforce worker protections. \u8220\'20\'1CHow will they be compensated? Often the discussion veers towards punishing employers, which is good, but getting the agency workers their money is overlooked.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Internal debate\par \par \uc2 Though executives in \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s xWS department were aware by May 2019 that the company was failing to comply with the law and underpaying workers, the company did not move quickly to correct the rates and provide back wages to those who were owed them.\par \par \uc2 Instead, it spent at least two years continuing to pay out-of-date rates while it debated internally how to come back into legal compliance without admitting what had happened, documents and emails show.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of the xWS team appeared keenly aware that admitting the problem would damage its reputation within \uc2 Google\uc2  by causing headaches for departments whose budgets would be effected, as well as with the staffing agencies that are liable for providing pay parity. They also expressed interest in preventing existing and former temp workers from knowing they had been underpaid, in order to prevent claims for back pay.\par \par \uc2 By October 2020, xWS executives, including Crowther and Deepak Negi, another director, and \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s employment counsels for EMEA and APAC had signed off on a plan to correct the rates, emails and documents show. The company would pursue a \u8220\'20\'1Cnatural correction\u8221\'20\'1D of the temp rates, whereby new joiners started at correct rates while existing temps remained at the lower rates, rather than an \u8220\'20\'1Cimmediate correction\u8221\'20\'1D, whereby all temps had their rates adjusted to the correct levels.\par \par \uc2 The plan had received support from executives and attorneys due to \u8220\'20\'1Cthe current low risk of a parity claim, the ability to correct rates and not make a great deal of noise, and the ability to correct rates without immediate substantial financial impact to [\uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s departments],\u8221\'20\'1D an xWS operations manager promoting it wrote in an email.\par \par \uc2 Some xWS staffers appear to have resisted the proposal, noting that it would result in groups of temps performing similar work earning significantly different wages. Some also worried that the staffing agencies, who are responsible for complying with pay parity laws, might ask questions about the changed rates, or even object.\par \par \uc2 But in an extraordinary December 2020 email, Barry, the attorney responsible for compliance in xWS, endorsed the \u8220\'20\'1Cnatural correction\u8221\'20\'1D despite acknowledging that it was insufficient.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe know that the impacted workers are not on the correct 2020 rates, nor were they in 2019,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CSo, strictly speaking, from the point that we put ourselves on notice as to the correct comparator rate, it should apply to both the incumbents as well as the new joiners. To do otherwise may place our staffing partners in a difficult position, legally and ethically.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn my view, while [an immediate correction] does appear to be the correct outcome from a compliance perspective, I recognise the barriers to this,\u8221\'20\'1D Barry wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CThe cost is significant and it would give rise to a flurry of noise/frustration on the part of the [\uc2 Google\uc2  departments] who have not accounted for the changed cost. I\u8217\'20\'19m also not keen to invite the charge that we\u8217\'20\'19ve allowed this situation to persist for so long that the correction required is significant \u8230\'20\'26 I am leaning towards favouring [the natural correction] \u8211\'20\'13 but with careful thought and consideration needed as to how we communicate it to the [staffing agencies].\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 By February 2021, Barry and others were considering a third option beyond the \u8220\'20\'1Cnatural\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cimmediate\u8221\'20\'1D corrections: applying the new rates to new joiners and existing temps and backdating the increases for the existing temps to their start date. (None of the options included providing back pay to temps who had been underpaid but no longer worked for \uc2 Google\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 The memo suggests that Barry and the xWS team\u8217\'20\'19s concerns remained primarily reputational. It includes guidance from \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s employment lawyers that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe threat of litigation is greater than the prospect of actual litigation\u8221\'20\'1D since actual litigation would require temps to know that they were being underpaid, and by how much. \u8220\'20\'1CIf and when they become aware they will seek recompense but will want to avoid litigation. Strategy should therefore be driven by an assessment of the internal PR/reputational risk and the risk of pay disparity becoming known within our temps population.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, Barry argued that despite the \u8220\'20\'1Csignificant\u8221\'20\'1D financial cost and potential for criticism of xWS from \uc2 Google\uc2  and staffing agencies, he now believed that this third option was the best course.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn my view \u8211\'20\'13 better to opt to for option three in a controlled fashion now, than in an uncontrolled fashion later,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210910eh9a002mq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
