\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Union seeks Hollywood ending for film industry\u8217\'20\'19s tale of exploitation\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,215 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2021\par 21:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Behind the glitz many of those working in movies and TV face low wages and conditions that strike at the industry\u8217\'20\'19s diversity\par \par \uc2 Working in Hollywood may sound glamorous but the vast majority of workers in entry-level, assistant and support staff positions are vulnerable to pervasive poor working conditions that ensure poor levels of pay and pose safety risks. Problems include low wages, gender pay disparities, long hours, increased workloads in regards to Covid-19 safety protocols without additional compensation, and bullying from managers who gatekeep the opportunities for advancement and success in the entertainment industry.\par \par \uc2 Marisa Shipley, vice-president of IATSE (International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees) Local 871 and an art department coordinator in the industry, explained workers in the industry in these positions, from script coordinators to writers and production assistants, are typically paid little more than the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wagesla.lacity.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 minimum wage}}}\uc2  of $15 an hour in Los Angeles, California.\par \par \uc2 The union is negotiating with the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/hollywood-living-wage-1234985472/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 raise}}}\uc2  the wage floor for members in the industry.\par \par \uc2 Recent trends in the industry have worsened the impact of low wages on workers, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theringer.com/tv/2017/8/4/16094348/inefficiency-week-mourning-the-lost-long-tv-season"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  shorter seasons}}}\uc2  for TV shows have limited opportunities for advancement and made it more difficult for workers to piece together jobs throughout the year to make a living.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s leaving people in these positions for much longer than people used to work in them and keeping them at very suppressed low wages,\u8221\'20\'1D said Shipley. \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s absurd that an industry of billion-dollar corporations continuing to make profits during a global pandemic based off of our work and our work contributions are not paying people wages to live on in Los Angeles.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ialocal871.org/Reel-Equity/Infographic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  commissioned by IATSE Local 871 found members in roles such as art department coordinators, art production coordinators, script supervisors, and writer\u8217\'20\'19s assistants, positions predominantly held by women, are typically paid just $16 to $17 an hour. Shipley cited as an example art department coordinators, who receive starting rates at $16 an hour while the position with the next lowest rate in these departments receive $44 an hour. In addition to low wages, significant gender pay gaps across positions throughout the entertainment industry have been reported, from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theconversation.com/exploring-the-data-on-hollywoods-gender-pay-gap-127414"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 famous actors }}}\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://iatse.com/_content/documents/public/IATSE_Local_891_Gender_Pay_Equity_Study_FINAL_REPORT_(003).pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 support staff}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe industry has done nothing to address it. They continue to ignore it,\u8221\'20\'1D added Shipley.\par \par \uc2 Noah Suarez-Sikes, a production assistant in the industry since 2018, helped create the Democratic Socialists of America-Los Angeles Hollywood Labor group, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dsa-la.org/event/hl_august/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 aimed}}}\uc2  at organizing workers in the industry around issues of low pay, equity and poor working conditions.\par \par \uc2 The group regularly holds {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dsa-la.org/event/hl_0625/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 events }}}\uc2  around Los Angeles, from social events to participating in protests in solidarity with other local unions, such as boycotting the Chateau Marmont hotel in Hollywood earlier this year as hotel workers represented by \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  Local 11 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.unitehere11.org/chateau-pledge/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported }}}\uc2  rampant mistreatment and discrimination on the job.\par \par \uc2 Suarez-Sikes described the culture within Hollywood that inhibits labor organizing, such as the wide range of job duties assigned to workers in entry-level and assistant positions, and the culture of fear and retaliation that reinforces poor labor practices, including workers being forced to do unpaid labor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is an industry-wide problem of making people work overtime they\u8217\'20\'19re not paid for,\u8221\'20\'1D said Suarez-Sikes. \u8220\'20\'1CYou kind of knuckle under and accept this condition that you\u8217\'20\'19re going to do unpaid work, you can get called to work on weekends, you can be asked to do things late into the evening and outside of work hours.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 During the pandemic, Suarez-Sikes noted many workers in assistant and entry-level positions lost their jobs, and while Hollywood operations have resumed, they are often being conducted with fewer workers, placing larger workloads and pressure on those still working.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEmployers are trying to concentrate more labor on fewer people. So overall, assistants are getting the full brunt of the pandemic,\u8221\'20\'1D added Suarez-Sikes. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to get worse for Hollywood workers unless assistants start recognizing the problems and we start working to fix it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lizalps.medium.com/the-2020-payuphollywood-survey-results-are-here-3e5c6be8744f'"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey}}}\uc2  of 1,014 assistants in the entertainment industry released in February 2021 by the #PayUpHollywood campaign found over 79% of respondents make $50,000 or less per year, with annual income in Los Angeles under $53,600 classifying an individual as rent burdened. More than 37% of respondents reported relying on income from family or friends.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve always had to have a lot of side hustles,\u8221\'20\'1D said Helen Silverstein, a writer\u8217\'20\'19s assistant and member of the DSA-LA Hollywood Labor group. She described the issue of having to navigate gaining experience through internships, often having to turn many down that are unpaid.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEveryone in Hollywood is trading on the currency that these are your dreams, that everyone wants to be in this industry, and this shows the bosses that if someone isn\u8217\'20\'19t willing to work for minimum wage, they can find someone else who\u8217\'20\'19s willing to work for minimum wage, and it leads to the quality of working conditions going down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lizalps.medium.com/payuphollywood-releases-survey-of-1-500-entertainment-industry-assistants-pay-working-conditions-df84e4432056"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey}}}\uc2  conducted in 2019 also found several workers have experienced abuse from employers, such as having objects thrown at them, and being forced to perform personal errands for their managers during and outside of work hours.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBecause of the glitz and glam image that people have of Hollywood, a lot of things are swept under the rug in service of preserving that image,\u8221\'20\'1D said Neda Davarpanah, a writer\u8217\'20\'19s assistant and leadership board member of DSA-LA Hollywood Labor. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a coercive nature of work in this industry, that we\u8217\'20\'19re all kind of living under this fear that if we cross the wrong person we will never work again, so we don\u8217\'20\'19t demand better.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 These working conditions and low wages, argued Alison Golub, a writer\u8217\'20\'19s assistant and Local 871 member, are severely impairing workers\u8217\'20\'19 ability to start families and afford expensive rent in the Los Angeles area, and limit the ability for many workers to sustain themselves in the industry. This contributes to the lack of diversity in Hollywood, Golub noted, because many workers cannot afford to work in the industry without relying on generational wealth to subsidize their cost of living.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGreat art is made when people aren\u8217\'20\'19t worried about how they\u8217\'20\'19re going to get their next meal on the table,\u8221\'20\'1D said Golub. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you want to keep making great art, then you have to pay people what they\u8217\'20\'19re owed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Alex Wolinetz, a screenwriter and co-founder of the Hollywood Labor group with DSA-LA, argued the Hollywood industry, along with the economy as a whole, is becoming increasingly divided along lines of wealth.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re living in an economic situation where we are making less and less money for more and more work. And I think one of the worst things about working in the film industry is that it\u8217\'20\'19s classically defined that you do a lot of unpaid work to prove yourself, improve your credit, everything,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wolinetz. \u8220\'20\'1CIt doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have to be this way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 8 September 2021. An earlier version misnamed Noah Suarez-Sikes as \u8220\'20\'1CNoah Van Sykes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210907eh97000mj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
