\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 America celebrates Labor Day \u8211\'20\'13 but are US workers winning?\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,314 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2021\par 15:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  and others increase wages, some economists say the balance is shifting in favor of workers \u8211\'20\'13 but will it last?\par \par \uc2 As the US celebrates Labor Day, many employers are still struggling to find enough workers. \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2 , Chipotle, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and many other companies have announced sizable wage increases to attract workers, and some economists argue American workers have the most bargaining power they\u8217\'20\'19ve had in years.\par \par \uc2 Many people, from low-wage workers to White House officials, are cheering this news, but there\u8217\'20\'19s a fierce debate about this increased bargaining power. On Friday, the US released {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/sep/03/us-jobs-report-august-2021-hiring-delta-variant-covid"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disappointing jobs figures }}}\uc2  that show the coronavirus is still affecting hiring. And while some argue this new-found worker power will be a longer-lasting phenomenon that yields years of better pay for workers, others believe it\u8217\'20\'19s just a temporary blip.\par \par \uc2 Already this year, Chipotle, CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  have raised their minimum pay to $15 an hour, while \uc2 Costco\uc2  has raised its minimum to $16. \uc2 Heidi Shierholz\uc2 , senior economist at the Economic Policy Institute, a progressive thinktank, applauds these increases, but sees them as largely a response to a temporary labor squeeze in specific industries, especially the hospitality industry. Shierholz fears the increase in worker bargaining power will be short-lived.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s nothing I see that gives me any feeling that it\u8217\'20\'19s going to be permanent,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe reasons we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen wage stagnation and declining economic leverage for workers is four decades of policy choices. A couple of months like this is not going to wipe that out. How can anyone seriously think this will be a lasting situation?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Arindrajit Dube, an economics professor at the University of Massachusetts, says increased worker bargaining power could last longer-term. Dube says that numerous factors have made many workers think twice about returning to their jobs \u8211\'20\'13 especially to low-wage jobs \u8211\'20\'13 and that hesitation has given them more bargaining power. Those hesitation-causing factors include protections against evictions, increased food stamps, the $300-a-month unemployment benefits supplement, and stimulus checks that fattened many workers\u8217\'20\'19 banks accounts.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, many workers hesitate to return to work because of difficulties finding childcare and fears about Covid.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople have become more discerning about which jobs they\u8217\'20\'19re willing to take,\u8221\'20\'1D Dube said. \u8220\'20\'1CA reasonable case can be made that we may be looking at a more extended period where workers have greater power.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Dube acknowledges that many of the factors that have let workers remain on the sidelines will dissipate: their savings may dwindle, childcare will improve, the $300 jobless supplement is ending. But when the pandemic recedes, Dube notes, the jobless rate is likely to fall, strengthening worker bargaining power. Dube says wage norms have been changing for the better; many employers raised pay pre-pandemic because of a low jobless rate. Moreover, public pressure, from Bernie Sanders to union protests, helped persuade some employers, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2018/oct/02/amazon-raises-minimum-wage-us-uk-employees"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most notably Amazon}}}\uc2 , to raise pay.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNorms on wage standards might start to get baked in,\u8221\'20\'1D Dube said. \u8220\'20\'1CFrom one perspective, you can have a replay of the 1980s, but backwards. In the 1980s, there were differing shocks, like the weakening of unions, that led to erosion of wage standards. We could be looking at a time where we rebuild some of those wage standards.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He added: \u8220\'20\'1CWe could actually perhaps bend the trajectory of the labor market\u8221\'20\'1D toward better pay and treatment. Economists point to a big factor helping to change that trajectory: the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s willingness to let the labor market run hot, paving the way for more wage increases.\par \par \uc2 Betsey Stevenson\uc2 , a University of Michigan economics professor, agrees that many workers are reluctant to return to their jobs. She noted that in-person service workers, like waiters, see their jobs as especially risky during the pandemic. \u8220\'20\'1CEconomists say the riskier the job, the more the pay should be,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve always had restaurant servers who face sexual harassment and hostility, but it was never as bad as it is now. It\u8217\'20\'19s given workers a lot more space to say, \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m not OK with being treated that way.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 With the federal government strengthening the social safety net because of Covid, Stevenson said, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe made it easier for people who live on the knife\u8217\'20\'19s edge of starvation to eat. We made it difficult to evict people. We made food stamps more available. We enabled people to catch their breath and ask, \u8216\'20\'18Is this something I want to do?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CComing out of a recession, we\u8217\'20\'19ve never seen so much movement by workers into new fields.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In her view, many businesses might adopt different norms during the pandemic, and that could result in more worker bargaining power. \u8220\'20\'1CSuccessful businesses are going to have to think harder about the humanity of the people they\u8217\'20\'19re hiring. The ones who adapt to that are going to be the first ones to get workers back.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe ruthless company that pays as little as possible, with all the profits going to managers and investors \u8211\'20\'13 that cutthroat approach was a successful US model. I think it\u8217\'20\'19s not a successful US model after the pandemic. That has the potential to reshape bargaining power.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 David Neumark\uc2 , an economics professor at the University of California, Irvine, says the troubled labor market in industries such as restaurants \u8220\'20\'1Cmay just be a temporary imbalance between supply and demand, and there may not be a fundamental shift in bargaining power.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe labor market is very hot right now,\u8221\'20\'1D he noted, saying it will of course cool off. He said some long-term trends, like retirements among older workers, might tighten the labor market and nudge wages upward. Concurring with Stevenson, he said some employers, having adopted a higher-pay strategy to retain and attract workers during the pandemic, may conclude, post-pandemic, that a high-road approach works best for their business.\par \par \uc2 Curtis Dubay, senior economist with the \uc2 US Chamber of Commerce\uc2 , said the $300-a-week unemployment supplement was one factor helping workers hesitate about taking jobs. \u8220\'20\'1CWorkers,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, \u8220\'20\'1Chave tremendous bargaining power because businesses are bargaining against government benefits, which are very generous right now. But they\u8217\'20\'19re winding down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Dubay said fears of returning to work because of Covid \u8220\'20\'1Cgive workers bargaining power. Employers have to pay more to overcome that fear.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The dynamic between workers and employers is always shifting, Dubay said, and that makes it unclear how long workers\u8217\'20\'19 beefed-up leverage will last. \u8220\'20\'1CThe one thing that will last is the pay increases,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, noting that employers are reluctant to roll back wages.\par \par \uc2 William Spriggs, the AFL-CIO\u8217\'20\'19s chief economist and a Howard University economics professor, said he thinks increased worker bargaining leverage is a \u8220\'20\'1Ctemporary blip\u8221\'20\'1D. To his mind, the so-called labor shortage is greatly exaggerated. His explanation: many people left their low-paying restaurant and retail jobs to work at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , and that created hiring difficulties, especially in restaurants, when customer traffic boomed in June and July.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo increase bargaining power, we really have to raise the minimum wage,\u8221\'20\'1D Spriggs said, asserting that many employers have been able to hold down wages because their workers feel little ability to move to other employers. \u8220\'20\'1CIncreasing the minimum wage to $15 will force them to do what they should have done on their own.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Shierholz of the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe most important things to do to increase worker bargaining power are increase the minimum wage and pass the Pro Act,\u8221\'20\'1D the Protecting the Right to Organize Act, which faces a Senate filibuster.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnother key thing is to always keep unemployment low,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf we look back in 10 years and there are no policy changes, I worry what will have happened to wages.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210906eh96000uo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
