\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Bosses turn to \u8216\'20\'18tattleware\u8217\'20\'19 to keep tabs on employees working from home\b0\par\par\uc2 Sandy Milne \par \uc2 1,316 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2021\par 09:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The pandemic prompted a surge in the use of workplace surveillance programs \u8211\'20\'13 and they\u8217\'20\'19re not going away any time soon\par \par \uc2 David, 23, admits that he felt a twinge of relief when the first wave of Covid-19 shut down his Arlington, Virginia, office. A recent college graduate, he was new to the job and struggled to click with his teammates. Maybe, he thought, this would be a nice break from \u8220\'20\'1Cthe face-to-face stuff\u8221\'20\'1D: the office politics and small talk. (His name has been changed for this story.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI couldn\u8217\'20\'19t have been more wrong,\u8221\'20\'1D David says.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s because, within their first week of remote work, David and his team were introduced to a digital surveillance platform called Sneek.\par \par \uc2 Every minute or so, the program would capture a live photo of David and his workmates via their company laptop webcams. The ever-changing headshots were splayed across the wall of a digital conference waiting room that everyone on the team could see. Clicking on a colleague\u8217\'20\'19s face would unilaterally pull them into a video call. If you were lucky enough to catch someone goofing off or picking their nose, you could forward the offending image to a team chat via Sneek\u8217\'20\'19s integration with the messaging platform Slack.\par \par \uc2 I signed up to manage their digital marketing, not to livestream my living room\par \par \uc2 According to the Sneek co-founder Del Currie, the software is meant to replicate the office. \u8220\'20\'1CWe know lots of people will find it an invasion of privacy, we 100% get that, and it\u8217\'20\'19s not the solution for those folks,\u8221\'20\'1D Currie says. \u8220\'20\'1CBut there\u8217\'20\'19s also lots of teams out there who are good friends and want to stay connected when they\u8217\'20\'19re working together.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For David, though, Sneek was a dealbreaker. He quit after less than three weeks on the job. \u8220\'20\'1CI signed up to manage their digital marketing,\u8221\'20\'1D he tells me, \u8220\'20\'1Cnot to livestream my living room.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Little did he realize that his experience was part of a wide-scale boom in worker surveillance\u8211\'20\'13 and one that\u8217\'20\'19s poised to become a standard feature of life on the job.\par \par \uc2 Rise of the spies\par \par \uc2 Remote surveillance software like Sneek, also known as \u8220\'20\'1Ctattleware\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Cbossware\u8221\'20\'1D, represented {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessofbusiness.com/articles/employee-monitoring-software-productivity-activtrak-hubstaff-covid/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 something of a niche market }}}\uc2  pre-Covid. But that all changed in March 2020, as employers scrambled to pull together work-from-home policies out of thin air. In April last year, \uc2 Google\uc2  queries for \u8220\'20\'1Cremote monitoring\u8221\'20\'1D were up {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://trends.google.com/trends/explore?date=2019-02-09%202021-09-02&q=remote%20monitoring"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 212% year-on-year}}}\uc2  ; by April this year, they\u8217\'20\'19d continued to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://trends.google.com/trends/explore?date=2019-02-09%202021-09-02&q=remote%20monitoring"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surge by another 243%}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One of the major players in the industry, ActivTrak, reports that during March 2020 alone, the firm scaled up from 50 client companies to 800. Over the course of the pandemic, the company has maintained that growth, today boasting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.activtrak.com/customers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 9,000 customers}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 or, as it claims, more than 250,000 individual users. Time Doctor, Teramind, and Hubstaff \u8211\'20\'13 which, together with ActivTrak, make up the bulk of the market \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessofbusiness.com/articles/employee-monitoring-software-productivity-activtrak-hubstaff-covid/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have all seen similar growth from prospective customers}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 These software programs give bosses a mix of options for monitoring workers\u8217\'20\'19 online activity and assessing their productivity: from screenshotting employees\u8217\'20\'19 screens to logging their keystrokes and tracking their browsing. But in the fast-growing bossware market, each platform potentially brings something new to the table. There\u8217\'20\'19s FlexiSpy, which offers call-tapping; Spytech, which is known for mobile device access; and NetVizor, which has a remote takeover feature.\par \par \uc2 Tattleware platforms are hardly the sole culprits of expanded workplace surveillance. Employers are reportedly drawing on in-house IT departments to monitor emails for flagged phrases at an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hbr.org/2020/05/how-to-monitor-your-employees-while-respecting-their-privacy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased rate}}}\uc2  compared with before the pandemic. By receiving alerts when certain employees are discussing \u8220\'20\'1Crecruiter\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Csalary\u8221\'20\'1D, for example, management hopes to know when employees are looking to up sticks for greener pastures.\par \par \uc2 Big-name tech companies have also dipped their toes into the spy game, with varying degrees of success. In April 2020, Zoom quickly backtracked on a short-lived \u8220\'20\'1Cattention tracking\u8221\'20\'1D setting, which alerted a call host when a participant was focused away from the meeting for more than 30 seconds. And in December, \uc2 Microsoft{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/blog/2020/12/01/our-commitment-to-privacy-in-microsoft-productivity-score/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bowed to tech experts\u8217\'20\'19 outcry}}}\uc2  over the release of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2020/nov/26/microsoft-productivity-score-feature-criticised-workplace-surveillance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cproductivity score\u8221\'20\'1D feature}}}\uc2  for its 365 suite, which rated individuals on criteria that included email use and network connectivity; the tool no longer identifies users by name.\par \par \uc2 Despite controversy, tattleware and remote monitoring are not going away any time soon, even as employees shift back to in-house and hybrid work models.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no real sign of this trend slowing down,\u8221\'20\'1D says Juan Carloz, a digital researcher and privacy advocate with the \uc2 University of Melbourne\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CNo sign of legislative change in any jurisdiction I can name, and no sign of pushback from employees, even when they\u8217\'20\'19re aware of it happening.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Many are all too content to let it slide\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Whether all of this amounts to corporate snooping, or just plain accountability, depends largely on which side of the fence you sit on. White-collar workers around the world have long taken it for granted that their emails are monitored on the job; warehouses, offices, and shops, meanwhile, are regularly monitored by CCTV.\par \par \uc2 The statistics seem to bear out that we are inured to the idea of some layer of surveillance built into our professional lives. In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://clutch.co/hr/resources/pros-cons-employee-monitoring"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent survey}}}\uc2 , nearly three-quarters of workers said their productivity wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be affected even if they knew their employer was monitoring them.\par \par \uc2 And while the jury\u8217\'20\'19s still out on whether there\u8217\'20\'19s any benefit to remote monitoring, Elizabeth Lyons, an associate professor of management at the University of California San Diego, is willing to play devil\u8217\'20\'19s advocate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/336784036_The_impact_of_job_training_on_temporary_worker_performance_Field_experimental_evidence_from_insurance_sales_agents"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  we conducted found people doing data collection work out of the office were more productive when they were made aware they were being monitored, compared to their colleagues who weren\u8217\'20\'19t told they were being tracked,\u8221\'20\'1D says Lyons. Surveillance even increased worker satisfaction, she adds, noting that remote employees appreciate signals that their performance is integral to the organization.\par \par \uc2 Yet Lyons acknowledges that when monitoring becomes overbearing, employee morale will take a hit.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn other studies we\u8217\'20\'19ve looked at, the workers were essentially saying, \u8216\'20\'18If the manager is going to watch everything I do, then I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to do anything above and beyond what they expect of me,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D says Lyons.\par \par \uc2 Then there\u8217\'20\'19s the question of privacy. Carloz, the digital researcher, is concerned that the boom in tattleware has tipped the scales too heavily in favor of the employer.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPrior to the pandemic, the line between work and play was [clearer] \u8211\'20\'13 surveillance, in other words, stopped at the door,\u8221\'20\'1D says Carloz.\par \par \uc2 But the rise of tattleware changes the game. If an employee uses a spy-enabled, work-sponsored computer outside of hours, their employer could easily access their personal data, down to internet banking passwords and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  messages.\par \par \uc2 Carloz concedes that most employers are probably not interested in collecting their workers\u8217\'20\'19 personal information. They want to know what websites employees are on, and what tasks they\u8217\'20\'19re dividing their time on, during the workday. However, if a boss does feel like snooping around off-hours, Carloz points out, there are \u8220\'20\'1Cessentially no legal protections afforded to [those employees] in most western nations\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut since, rightly or wrongly, [surveillance software] is being framed as a trade-off for remote work, many are all too content to let it slide,\u8221\'20\'1D says Carloz.\par \par \uc2 Which brings us back to David. In the weeks after boldly departing his first post-college job, the young digital marketer secured a post with a new, Sneek-free firm. He says he\u8217\'20\'19s much happier for it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut one of the first things they asked me to do was sign up for Hubstaff,\u8221\'20\'1D he laughs.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210905eh95000ru\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
