\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re peons to them\u8217\'20\'19: Nabisco factory workers on why they\u8217\'20\'19re striking\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,004 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2021\par 10:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 While the CEO of Nabisco\u8217\'20\'19s parent company is paid nearly $17m a year, plants are closing, jobs outsourced to Mexico, and older workers are unable to retire on weakened pension benefits\par \par \uc2 The pandemic drove many people to the cookie jar and helped Nabisco, maker of Oreos, Chips Ahoy!, Fig Newtons and other sweet treats weather the worst of the outbreak. But as the company\u8217\'20\'19s profits continue to recover, workers at its US plants are striking over the outsourcing of jobs to Mexico and concessions demanded by their employer in new union contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 On 10 August, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://katu.com/news/local/nabisco-workers-strike-at-portland-bakery-over-workweek"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about 200 workers }}}\uc2  in Portland, Oregon, represented by the \uc2 Bakery, Confectionery, Tobacco Workers and Grain Millers\uc2  (\uc2 BCTGM\uc2 ) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bctgm.org/2021/08/10/nabisco-workers-in-portland-ore-on-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 went on strike}}}\uc2 . Union workers in Aurora, Colorado, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bctgm.org/2021/08/12/nabisco-workers-in-aurora-colorado-on-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 began}}}\uc2  their strike on 12 August, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bctgm.org/2021/08/16/nabisco-workers-in-richmond-va-on-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 followed by}}}\uc2  those in Richmond, Virginia, on 16 August and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bctgm.org/2021/08/19/nabisco-workers-in-chicago-on-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Chicago, Illinois }}}\uc2  on 19 August .\par \par \uc2 Through the pandemic, Nabisco\u8217\'20\'19s parent company, \uc2 Mondelez International\uc2 , has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fortune.com/company/mondelez-international/fortune500/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recorded}}}\uc2  billions in profits; in the second quarter of 2021, the company {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2021/07/27/2269835/35903/en/Mondel%25C4%2593z-International-Reports-Q2-2021-Results.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported }}}\uc2  more than $5.5bn in profits and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fool.com/earnings/call-transcripts/2021/07/27/mondelez-international-inc-mdlz-q2-2021-earnings-c/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 spent $1.5bn}}}\uc2  on stock buybacks in the first half of 2021. The CEO of Mondelez International {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://seekingalpha.com/article/4430034-mondelez-international-inc-mdlz-ceo-dirk-van-de-put-on-2021-annual-meeting-of-shareholders"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 received }}}\uc2  $16.8m in total compensation in 2020, 544 times the company\u8217\'20\'19s median employee annual compensation of $31,000.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s greed. They don\u8217\'20\'19t have any respect for their workers that gave them the opportunity to make that kind of money. We\u8217\'20\'19re peons to them, and everyone is at the point where enough is enough,\u8221\'20\'1D said Darlene Carpenter, business agent of \uc2 BCTGM\uc2  local 358 in Richmond and a former employee at the plant. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re at the point where we\u8217\'20\'19re saying this is how the cookie is going to crumble now because we can\u8217\'20\'19t do this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 According to Keith Bragg, president of BCTGM local 358 who has worked at the Nabisco plant in Richmond for 45 years, during a discussion about contract negotiations with management, the company said that when the company does well, employees do well.\par \par \uc2 He took offence to this notion, citing his concerns about the treatment of workers over the past few years and the recent concessions being asked of them. During the pandemic, many workers had to work 12-hour shifts, six to seven days a week for several months and were praised as \u8220\'20\'1Cheroes\u8221\'20\'1D for their roles as essential workers. But now workers are being asked to give up overtime pay and concede to a two-tier healthcare system, Bragg said, which would downgrade benefits for new employees and cut overall wages.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re doing well, we\u8217\'20\'19re losing all the way around,\u8221\'20\'1D said Bragg. \u8220\'20\'1CThey shut down two plants this year, they\u8217\'20\'19re cutting overtime, they\u8217\'20\'19re making profits, but we lost half of our union membership. How is it that we\u8217\'20\'19re doing well?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In 2012, \uc2 Kraft Foods{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2012/05/24/business/mondelez-is-new-name-for-krafts-snack-foods-company.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 split}}}\uc2  into two companies, with \uc2 Mondelez International\uc2  formed as the parent company of Nabisco. Since the split, the union has been pressed to accept concessions during {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nwlaborpress.org/2019/08/nabisco-retirees-condemn-pension-cuts/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 drawn-out }}}\uc2  contract negotiations, such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nwlaborpress.org/2019/08/nabisco-retirees-condemn-pension-cuts/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliminating}}}\uc2  union pension contributions in May 2018 and switching to 401 retirement plans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of folks were very close to retirement, and were able to do so under the old plan, but when the company pulled out that basically meant that they had to continue working, they were no longer eligible to retire,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mike Burlingham, who has worked at the Nabisco plant in Portland since 2007 and serves as vice-president of local 364. \u8220\'20\'1CIt impacted all of us in a way that we can no longer count on this as being a place we can retire comfortably from.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mondelez International\uc2  has shuttered several Nabisco plants in the US over the past several years, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.doleta.gov/tradeact/petitioners/taa_search.cfm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 offshoring }}}\uc2  much of the work to Mexico. The plight of its workers briefly became a campaign issue during the 2016 election cycle, with both \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  attacking plans to shift jobs overseas. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not eating Oreos any more,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.c-span.org/video/?c4845726/user-clip-trump-oreo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump told voters in New Hampshire}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But despite the political heat the trend has continued. In 2021, Nabisco plants in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.northjersey.com/story/news/bergen/fair-lawn/2021/04/27/pascrell-congressmen-want-answers-mondelez-nabisco-closings/4862312001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fairlawn, New Jersey, }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fox5atlanta.com/news/bakery-plant-to-close-in-southwest-atlanta-after-nearly-40-years"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Atlanta}}}\uc2  were closed, resulting in the loss of about 1,000 jobs. \uc2 Mondelez International\uc2  denied that jobs from the two plants shut down in 2021 were offshored to Mexico, but a petition for trade adjustment assistance alleging outsourcing by the union at one of the plants is under review by the Department of Labor. In 2016, hundreds of workers were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.chicagotribune.com/business/ct-oreo-bakery-layoffs-1102-biz-20161102-story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  laid off}}}\uc2  at the Nabisco plant in Chicago and a plant in Philadelphia was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bctgm.org/PDFs/FaithFactSheet2018.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shut down}}}\uc2  in 2015.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe can\u8217\'20\'19t compete with the Mexican workers,\u8221\'20\'1D said Cameron Taylor, business agent at Local 364 in Portland. \u8220\'20\'1CThey just want to exploit cheap labor. If we were to accept all of what they want us to, accept all the working conditions and the two- tiered system of healthcare, this job would turn into a job not even worth fighting for.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In 2016, the union {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fightforamericanjobs.org/check-the-label/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 launched}}}\uc2  a \u8220\'20\'1Ccheck the label\u8221\'20\'1D boycott campaign that was endorsed by the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , asking consumers to refuse to buy Nabisco products that are made in Mexico. Workers have frequently reported finding Nabisco products for sale near their plants that were produced in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are disappointed by the decision of the local \uc2 BCTGM\uc2  unions in Portland (OR), Richmond (VA) and Aurora (CO) to go on strike,\u8221\'20\'1D said a spokesperson for Mondelez International, noting the company has a continuity plan in place at the facilities where workers are on strike. \u8220\'20\'1COur goal has been \u8211\'20\'13 and continues to be \u8211\'20\'13 to bargain in good faith with the \uc2 BCTGM\uc2  leadership across our US bakeries and sales distribution facilities to reach new contracts that continue to provide our employees with good wages and competitive benefits, including quality, affordable healthcare, and company-sponsored Enhanced Thrift Investment 401(k) Plan, while also taking steps to modernize some contract aspects which were written several decades ago.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210823eh8n001bb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
