\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Office politics: firms still grappling with home working puzzle\b0\par\par\uc2 Jasper Jolly \par \uc2 1,037 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 August 2021\par 15:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 38\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Whether seen as an obstacle to overcome or a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity, home working is proving hard to avoidWhat are workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights if they are asked to go back?\par \par \uc2 As the pandemic struck wealthy economies in early 2020, workers (in white-collar jobs, at least) found themselves carrying out their duties from home. Now that vaccination programmes are continuing apace, more companies are grappling with how \u8211\'20\'13 and whether \u8211\'20\'13 to end {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2020/jul/14/end-of-the-office-the-quiet-grinding-loneliness-of-working-from-home"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the great enforced experiment in home working}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The answer is far from settled, with Apple telling its global workforce on Friday that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/aug/20/apple-delays-return-to-corporate-offices-until-2022-as-covid-cases-rise"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 they will not return to its corporate offices until January at the earliest}}}\uc2 , amid concern over the spread of new coronavirus variants. The tech company\u8217\'20\'19s original plan to bring staff back in on Mondays, Tuesdays and Thursdays in a hybrid home-working pattern had already been delayed from early September to October.\par \par \uc2 Apple is far from alone in delaying the return, with health concerns unsurprisingly emerging as the governing factor. The \uc2 UK government\uc2  removed all guidance for people to work from home where possible on 19 July, but with infections remaining high, many companies have yet to bring all their workers back to the office.\par \par \uc2 In the US, prominent tech companies such as \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , Wall Street\u8217\'20\'19s Wells Fargo and \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  are among those who this month {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/business-58108457"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 delayed their return}}}\uc2  to the office until 2022 as cases of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/aug/10/delta-variant-renders-herd-immunity-from-covid-mythical"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more infectious Delta variant}}}\uc2  rise.\par \par \uc2 Yet many other US businesses have carried on as normal for months. Even within some companies there are inconsistencies: some \uc2 Facebook\uc2  contractors have complained that they have been forced back to offices even as permanent employees stay at home.\par \par \uc2 When businesses do return, one of the most significant frictions is proving to be vaccination status. The 2.1 million employees in US central government offices {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/jul/29/covid-federal-workers-vaccinated-testing-distancing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 must now disclose their vaccination status}}}\uc2  on forms brought in this month. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  personally announced that those who have not been jabbed will be made to wear masks and socially distance from colleagues, making their personal decisions very publicly visible.\par \par \uc2 However, individual states are taking wildly different approaches, reflecting political divisions. Democrat-controlled California last week mandated all teachers be vaccinated or be subjected to weekly testing. By contrast, Republican-dominated Florida, Texas, and Montana have all tried to ban vaccination mandates, with varying levels of success. The Texas governor, Greg Abbott, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/aug/17/texas-governor-greg-abbott-coronavirus-covid-tests-positive"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who himself caught Covid-19 this week}}}\uc2 , has appealed to the state\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court after lower courts ruled against his ban on government entities or private companies that receive state funds requiring the wearing of masks or proof of vaccination.\par \par \uc2 Yet many American companies are taking a harder line on employees being inoculated than elsewhere in the world, in part due to federal guidance, updated in May, that explicitly allows employers to demand it. Unvaccinated staff are banned from buildings of the investment banks \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , Jefferies and \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , while at \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  and \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  they must take regular Covid-19 tests.\par \par \uc2 The aviation industry is among the keenest to exude an aura of Covid safety: Australia\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Qantas\uc2  and Hong Kong\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Cathay Pacific\uc2  have both mandated vaccines for some staff.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, some employment lawyers believe vaccine mandates from individual employers are unlikely to be legal, although the UK and France have brought in vaccine requirements for care and healthcare workers.\par \par \uc2 I genuinely think this is a once-in-several-generations opportunity\par \par \uc2 Peter Cheese\par \par \uc2 France, Italy and Germany have all {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/aug/14/fifth-consecutive-weekend-of-protests-in-france-over-covid-pass"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 introduced controversial passes}}}\uc2 , which show vaccination or test status, that are required for entry to venues such as restaurants, cafes and cinemas. In Bordeaux, a trial is under way in tourist areas to issue bracelets for the vaccinated, so they can easily demonstrate their status to enter bars and restaurants. Some French employees will have to show their pass at work, as will Italian teachers.\par \par \uc2 For all the efforts to bring workers back safely, the longest battle may be over staff who want to continue working from home at least some of the time. The UK chancellor, \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 , has repeatedly said that working from home is damaging to younger workers\u8217\'20\'19 prospects.\par \par \uc2 The vacuum cleaner tycoon Sir \uc2 James Dyson\uc2  this week cast the return to the office as essential to the British economy\u8217\'20\'19s competitiveness. He wrote in the Telegraph that \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  should restore companies\u8217\'20\'19 right to determine where employees work \u8211\'20\'13 however unpopular.\par \par \uc2 Yet there are convincing signs that the pandemic has prompted a permanent shift to home or hybrid working. A survey of 120 British companies shared with \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  by the insurance broker Willis \uc2 Towers Watson\uc2  suggests 41% of employers expect to use hybrid working in two years\u8217\'20\'19 time, compared with 10% two years ago. In the US, a May survey of 500 top executives by McKinsey found that 48% expected workers to spend only two or three days in the office a week, compared with 8% before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Some employers are also enthusiastic, spying an opportunity to cut costs. In the UK an anonymous cabinet minister told the Daily Mail that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2021/aug/09/civil-servants-in-london-could-see-pay-cut-if-they-resist-office-return"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 civil servants should be forced to take pay cuts}}}\uc2  if they work from home. Removing the \u8220\'20\'1CLondon weighting\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 extra pay to cover the higher costs in the capital \u8211\'20\'13 was one mooted option.\par \par \uc2 However, the Cabinet Office minister, \uc2 Michael Gove\uc2 , called the comments \u8220\'20\'1Cunhelpful\u8221\'20\'1D and employment lawyers were quick to point out that UK employers would almost certainly face cases for constructive dismissal if they tried to unilaterally cut pay set in a contract. Melanie Stancliffe, an employment law partner at Cripps Pemberton Greenish, said she had not seen any employers trying to cut pay so far, even among those thinking about lowering costs.\par \par \uc2 Peter Cheese, the chief executive of the UK\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Chartered Institute of Personnel and Development\uc2 , suggested that employers should embrace new ways of working. Companies have the chance to shift their \u8220\'20\'1Cvery traditional paradigms of work\u8221\'20\'1D, while at the same time making themselves more attractive to potential employees, he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI genuinely think this is a once-in-several-generations opportunity to change working practices that haven\u8217\'20\'19t changed since the industrial era.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210820eh8k00233\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
