\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 American CEOs make 351 times more than workers. In 1965 it was 15 to one\b0\par\par\uc2 Indigo Olivier \par \uc2 1,122 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2021\par 10:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rather than address stagnant wages for hourly workers and yawning inequality, corporations are blaming a \u8216\'20\'18labor shortage\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 , a nonpartisan thinktank, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/ceo-pay-in-2020/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 released}}}\uc2  a report on the increasing pay gap between chief executives and workers. This research tells a familiar story with updated figures. When taking into account stocks, which now make up more than 80% of the average CEO\u8217\'20\'19s compensation package, the report found that chief-executive pay has risen by an astounding 1,322% since 1978. That\u8217\'20\'19s more than six times more than the top 0.1% of wage earners and more than 73 times higher than the growth of the typical worker\u8217\'20\'19s pay, which grew by only 18% in the same time period. Most remarkable, however, is the 18.9% increase in CEO compensation between 2019 and 2020 alone.\par \par \uc2 CEO compensation outpacing that of the 0.1% is a clear indication that this growth is not the product of a competitive race for skills or increased productivity, the EPI {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/ceo-pay-in-2020/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  explains, so much as the \u8220\'20\'1Cpower of CEOs to extract concessions. Consequently, if CEOs earned less or were taxed more, there would be no adverse impact on the economy\u8217\'20\'19s output or on employment,\u8221\'20\'1D the report concludes.\par \par \uc2 This report joins a slew of data sounding an alarm on a massive upward transfer of wealth to the top 1% over the course of the pandemic. One {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/billionaire-wealth-covid-pandemic-12-trillion-jeff-bezos-wealth-tax/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimate}}}\uc2  by the Institute for Policy Studies puts this figure as high as $4tn, or a 54% increase in fortunes for the world\u8217\'20\'19s 2,365 billionaires.\par \par \uc2 Today in the US, the CEO-to-worker pay gap stands at a staggering 351 to one, an unacceptable increase from 15 to one in 1965. In other words, the average CEO makes nearly nine times what the average person will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rd.com/article/how-much-money-average-person-earns-in-lifetime/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 earn}}}\uc2  over a lifetime in just one year.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s worth remembering that the federal minimum wage would be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theintercept.com/2021/03/05/minimum-wage-raise-15/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $24 an hour}}}\uc2  today had it kept pace with worker productivity, rather than $7.25, where it\u8217\'20\'19s been stuck {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://raisetheminimumwage.com/question-answer/when-was-the-last-minimum-wage-increased/#:~:text=The%20federal%20minimum%20wage%20was,approved%20by%20Congress%20in%202007."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 since}}}\uc2  2009. Additionally, inflation has resulted in a nearly 2% pay cut over the past year despite modest gains in hourly wages, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/opub/ted/2021/real-average-hourly-earnings-down-1-7-percent-over-the-12-months-ending-june-2021.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according}}}\uc2  to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This reality is unfolding against a narrative of a \u8220\'20\'1Clabor shortage,\u8221\'20\'1D with small businesses, retail giants and fast-food chains expressing difficulty in filling poorly paid positions \u8211\'20\'13 even though there are one million {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/08/10/the-labor-shortage-isnt-main-streets-biggest-problem.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more}}}\uc2  unemployed workers than there are open jobs. Clearly, something else is going on here.\par \par \uc2 The grim reality is that a huge section of the American labor force \u8211\'20\'13 between {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fool.com/the-ascent/personal-finance/articles/25-to-40-of-jobless-americans-are-making-more-on-unemployment-than-they-did-at-a-job/#:~:text=It's%20estimated%20that%2025%25%20to,to%20that%20%24300%20weekly%20boost."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 25% and 40%}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 made more on unemployment than they ever have working full-time at a minimum wage job. $7.25 an hour is $290 a week before taxes, compared with the $300 in weekly federal benefits that pandemic unemployment assistance provided. Nor does this account for the additional weekly state benefits that those on unemployment received.\par \par \uc2 In our current milieu, \u8220\'20\'1Clabor shortage\u8221\'20\'1D has become doublespeak for a stubborn reluctance on the part of politicians and businesses to address poverty wages, the remedy for which has been to let pandemic unemployment assistance expire so that workers are desperate enough to go back to the exploitative conditions that billion-dollar companies insist are necessary to keep the economy running.\par \par \uc2 Rather than succumb to mainstream accounts that our \u8220\'20\'1Clabor shortage\u8221\'20\'1D is the consequence of welfare-induced idleness, we should have an honest discussion about how we\u8217\'20\'19ve allowed the essential workers who uphold our standard of living to be abused for so long while the mega-wealthy billionaires whom they work for realize their infantile fantasies of starting colonies in space.\par \par \uc2 The EPI is correct. A wealth tax on the 1% would not hinder the economy nor employment, so much as rein in the excesses of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.jacobinmag.com/2021/07/billionaire-space-race-richard-branson-bezos-musk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 billionaire space race}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.re-thinkingthefuture.com/designing-for-typologies/a3311-11-luxury-doomsday-bunkers-around-the-world/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 luxury doomsday bunkers}}}\uc2  that stand in stark relief to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/interactive/2021/world-floods-climate/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 floods}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/08/01/us/us-western-wildfires-sunday/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fires}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.globaltimes.cn/page/202108/1230270.shtml"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 famine}}}\uc2  and pestilence that have currently taken hold.\par \par \uc2 Among the report\u8217\'20\'19s policy recommendations for reversing skyrocketing pay for CEOs are raising the marginal tax rate on the ultra rich to \u8220\'20\'1Climit rent-seeking behavior\u8221\'20\'1D and penalizing companies with unacceptable CEO-to-worker pay ratios with higher corporate taxes. Let\u8217\'20\'19s examine the feasibility of both under Joe Biden\u8217\'20\'19s administration.\par \par \uc2 When Biden came into office, Trump had cut corporate tax rates from 35% to 21% and lowered rates on the ultra-wealthy to such an extent that the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/sites/camilomaldonado/2019/10/10/trump-tax-cuts-helped-billionaires-pay-less-taxes-than-the-working-class-in-2018/?sh=2af3f3a13128"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 richest 400 people}}}\uc2  in the US paid a lower tax rate than any other group in the country \u8211\'20\'13 including the minimum wage workers who are rightly refusing to return to the same conditions they withstood before the pandemic. Investopedia called Trump\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.investopedia.com/taxes/trumps-tax-reform-plan-explained/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act}}}\uc2  the \u8220\'20\'1Clargest overhaul of the tax code in three decades\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Biden, seeking to undo some of this pillaging of the public sphere, has proposed raising corporate taxes to 28% \u8211\'20\'13 7% lower than what they had been when \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  left office \u8211\'20\'13 and raising the top rates for individuals back to 39.6% from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 37% as part of the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/08/09/democrats-release-budget-resolution-as-senate-works-on-infrastructure-bill.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $3.5tn}}}\uc2  budget proposal (though there was some suggestion that Biden would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/05/06/biden-says-corporate-tax-rate-should-be-between-25percent-and-28percent.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 concede}}}\uc2  to a 25% corporate tax rate if it would please congressional Republicans). Biden has also stated that he will not increase taxes on those making less than $400,000 \u8211\'20\'13 meaning less than the top 2% of wage earners.\par \par \uc2 In other words, popular slogans taking aim at the top 1% have resulted in an administration of corporate Democrats that will attempt to take aim at the 1%, but make clear to their base that the 1% is as far as they\u8217\'20\'19re willing to go.\par \par \uc2 This is a far cry from the popular policies Bernie Sanders proposed during the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://berniesanders.com/issues/corporate-accountability-and-democracy/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 presidential primaries}}}\uc2 , such as giving workers an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/2019/5/29/18643032/bernie-sanders-communist-manifesto-employee-ownership-jobs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ownership stake}}}\uc2  in the companies they\u8217\'20\'19re employed by, democratizing corporate boards through employee elections, passing a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2019/12/05/782135614/how-would-a-wealth-tax-work"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wealth tax}}}\uc2 , banning stock buybacks and more. And it falls short of what is by far the cheapest and easiest solution to stimulating the economy and vastly reducing income inequality while steering clear of Republican interference: full {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/07/02/opinion/student-loan-medical-debt-forgiveness.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 student debt cancellation}}}\uc2  through executive order.\par \par \uc2 With the stroke of a pen, Biden could provide life-changing financial relief for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nerdwallet.com/article/loans/student-loans/how-many-americans-have-student-loan-debt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one in eight people}}}\uc2  living in the US. Each day he chooses not to is further proof that Biden is keeping to his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.salon.com/2019/06/19/joe-biden-to-rich-donors-nothing-would-fundamentally-change-if-hes-elected/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 original promise}}}\uc2  to rich donors on the campaign trail: \u8220\'20\'1C\u8230\'20\'26 nobody has to be punished. No one\u8217\'20\'19s standard of living will change, nothing would fundamentally change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Indigo Olivier is an investigative reporting fellow at In These Times magazine\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210817eh8h001bi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
