\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We work non-stop\u8217\'20\'19: LA garment workers toil for top brands and earn paltry rate\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,036 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2021\par 06:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thousands of workers who make clothes for top fashion brands earn below minimum wage for 60-hour week in unsafe conditions\par \par \uc2 Thousands of garment workers in Los Angeles who make pants, shirts, blouses and other clothing for a variety of well-known fashion labels are paid less than minimum wage through a piece-rate payment system that compensates workers just a few cents per article of clothing.\par \par \uc2 Works say they typically work from 7am to 6pm Monday through Friday, and an additional five hours on Saturday \u8211\'20\'13 about 60 hours a week with no overtime pay, which results in overall wages at $5 an hour or less, far below California\u8217\'20\'19s statewide {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dir.ca.gov/dlse/faq_minimumwage.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 minimum wage}}}\uc2  of $14 an hour for companies with more than 26 employees.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe work non-stop. We don\u8217\'20\'19t take any breaks, but make anywhere from $250 to $300 per week,\u8221\'20\'1D said one worker who requested to remain anonymous for fear of retaliation and to protect her undocumented status.\par \par \uc2 During the pandemic, the worker explained that her employer and several other factories in the garment industry continued operating without enforcing Covid safety protocols, moving sewing machines into a windowless basement to hide production from regulatory authorities.\par \par \uc2 One factory \u8211\'20\'13 operated by Los Angeles Apparel \u8211\'20\'13 was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2020/07/13/style/los-angeles-apparel-dov-charney-coronavirus.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ordered}}}\uc2  to shut down in July last year after a Covid-19 outbreak among workers resulted in more than 300 positive cases and the deaths of four workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s very sad to live off of a salary that is $250 to $300 a week. It\u8217\'20\'19s not enough to survive on. I have a mom, dad, and daughters in Guatemala, and I\u8217\'20\'19m here paying rent with these wages. I can\u8217\'20\'19t afford shoes or choose to move anywhere else,\u8221\'20\'1D the worker added.\par \par \uc2 A second garment factory worker explained that workers have to spend their brief meal breaks eating in a kitchen infested with rats and cockroaches, and when workers get injured they are expected to take care of themselves.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSomething that happens very commonly is the sewing needle will actually break through and impale one of your fingers, and many factories don\u8217\'20\'19t even have a first aid kit,\u8221\'20\'1D said the worker.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s not even a Band-Aid for you, so it\u8217\'20\'19s up to you to pull the needle out of your finger. And there\u8217\'20\'19s no health insurance for us. It\u8217\'20\'19s really up to us to fix ourselves.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 During the pandemic, workers at her factory were laid off and not recalled to work until they were vaccinated several months ago, but because they are undocumented, they received no unemployment or stimulus benefits while out of work.\par \par \uc2 The majority of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://remake.world/films/debunking-aafa-and-ca-chamber-of-commerces-stance-on-sb62-heres-what-brands-really-think/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 46,000 workers}}}\uc2  in the Los Angeles garment industry {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://remake.world/stories/news/the-dirty-truth-behind-los-angeles-garment-sector/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 are undocumented immigrant women}}}\uc2  from Latin America and Asia who work long hours for very low wages with few or no breaks in conditions {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.labor.ucla.edu/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/DirtyThreads_FINAL_web_single.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 likened}}}\uc2  to sweatshops.\par \par \uc2 Though most clothing brands rely on outsourced garment workers abroad, part of the market is based in Los Angeles, so brands can quickly turn around immediate orders and tout their clothing as \u8220\'20\'1CMade in America\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2  conducted an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/projects/la-fi-forever-21-factory-workers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 investigation}}}\uc2  of the garment industry in Los Angeles, finding labor violations in 85% of the 77 factories randomly inspected during the investigation.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://remake.world/stories/news/the-dirty-truth-behind-los-angeles-garment-sector/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More than 80%}}}\uc2  of garment workers in Los Angeles have experienced wage theft. Cases of wage theft are so common that a restitution fund created for garment workers in California became insolvent from paying out so many claims when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://remake.world/stories/news/the-dirty-truth-behind-los-angeles-garment-sector/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 employers}}}\uc2  move, shut down or file for bankruptcy to avoid paying wage theft settlements. Many {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://calaborfed.org/honor-a-promise-made-to-californias-garment-workers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 workers who win }}}\uc2  their wage theft claims never recoup any of their lost wages or have to wait several years to receive restitution.\par \par \uc2 Fashion brands {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wclp.org/press-release-ca-garment-workers-continue-fight-against-wage-theft/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  including}}}\uc2  Forever 21, Fashion Nova, \uc2 Urban Outfitters\uc2 , Charlotte Russe, Los Angeles Apparel and several others have been linked to these factories in their supply chains.\par \par \uc2 Ayesha Barenblat, chief and founder of the nonprofit ethical fashion advocacy group Remake said: \u8220\'20\'1CBecause the fashion industry has long pandered and catered to fast fashion and discount brands, one of the focuses of the industry has been to keep prices really low. And that means \u8230\'20\'26 that manufacturing employers find ways to reduce the take home pay of garment workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe rock bottom prices brands pay result in factories cutting corners and essentially passing on all the risk to workers in terms of wages,\u8221\'20\'1D Barenblat said.\par \par \uc2 Worker groups are pushing for the passage of a California state bill, SB62, the Garment Worker Protection Act, which would end the piece-rate payment system employers use to pay workers below minimum wage, ensuring workers are paid at least the minimum wage per hour.\par \par \uc2 The legislation would also enforce joint liability for brands that benefit from the production of their clothing in these factories, but are not held accountable for rampant wage and labor violations. Last year, the legislation {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://calmatters.org/california-divide/2020/09/bills-defeat-keeps-undocumented-garment-workers-in-meager-wages/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cleared}}}\uc2  the California senate but failed to reach a vote in the assembly, where it is again {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billStatusClient.xhtml?bill_id=202120220SB62"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 waiting}}}\uc2  to be voted on.\par \par \uc2 Some employers and industry groups have aggressively {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://remake.world/films/debunking-aafa-and-ca-chamber-of-commerces-stance-on-sb62-heres-what-brands-really-think/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opposed}}}\uc2  the legislation, including the \uc2 California Chamber of Commerce\uc2 , which has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://l.facebook.com/l.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fsourcingjournal.com%2Ftopics%2Flabor%2Fsb62-california-chamber-commerce-garment-worker-protection-act-remake-reformation-293538%2F%3Ffbclid%3DIwAR264gFN9BXuWI_S-A7aBAeehpo2v6nHdeOuwMr9N-yI-baZ9cfN2sPNa7I&h=AT3LqVGTV80-5HBcrmXiDbzpNQ-75je_wPtIKCDlLMF04H_rJ6R3uhDyKXt-vR5Xowj4BlG98qD0FrgdxOuuVRDNdu2PFG_O1a2IDbNifrxsV2hZ0VuzwquPpfUBZhuXqKPjkN14iqNbcZUB&__tn__=-UK-R&c[0]=AT3W4GBbLr9pOMJQElRh6F71EUymXnzIcUMDn2Tag9RYlnI_DSkPBkEGqxoRPwiW_A58pzFDoDqAD5iHIf12pZTbWTZyJmSGfJCaZb-L_t_tQTVWbbiSIpYNeKMiHpfNrSQ1ef-GrpzqehlAa-rM8TnckjMd2GmeDZ8rE32plH1lyW8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 listed}}}\uc2  the legislation on its \u8220\'20\'1Cjob killer\u8221\'20\'1D list. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://payupfashion.com/brands-that-support-californias-garment-worker-protection-act/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 coalition}}}\uc2  of more than 140 brands and businesses has signed on in support of the bill in a push for more ethical and sustainable fashion.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe joint liability piece of SB62 is its superpower,\u8221\'20\'1D said Elizabeth Cline, advocacy and policy director at Remake and author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2013/02/10/books/review/overdressed-by-elizabeth-l-cline.html?.?mc=aud_dev&ad-keywords=auddevgate&gclid=CjwKCAjw3riIBhAwEiwAzD3Tie-zftUY8mt8OTQ0i8Jyt1BbBhDPzOAZvBWhZMx3e752vLrJnkjeohoCIgkQAvD_BwE&gclsrc=aw.ds"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Overdressed }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/599214/the-conscious-closet-by-elizabeth-l-cline/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Conscious Closet}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Cline explained that big brands at the top of clothing supply chains have hundreds of manufacturing factories competing for attention through a race to the bottom for who can offer the lowest prices.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s concentrated brand power combined with the lack of any sort of real accountability for workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights, and it just keeps driving wages down in factories and causing these sweatshop conditions,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mckinsey.com/industries/retail/our-insights/state-of-fashion#"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $2.5tn global industry}}}\uc2  that is the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fashionunited.uk/news/retail/the-soaring-profits-high-street-fashion-brands-make-every-second-of-the-day/2020020347325"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most profitable}}}\uc2  sector in retail, don\u8217\'20\'19t tell me that brands can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to be more accountable to the people in their supply chain.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210817eh8h000p2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
