\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Children born during pandemic have lower IQs, US study finds\b0\par\par\uc2 Natalie Grover Science correspondent \par \uc2 518 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 August 2021\par 18:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Researchers blame lack of stimulation as parents balanced childcare with working from homeCoronavirus \u8211\'20\'13 latest updatesSee all our coronavirus coverage\par \par \uc2 Children born during the coronavirus pandemic have significantly reduced verbal, motor and overall cognitive performance compared with children born before, a US study suggests.\par \par \uc2 The first few years of a child\u8217\'20\'19s life are critical to their cognitive development. But with Covid-19 triggering the closure of businesses, nurseries, schools and playgrounds, life for infants changed considerably, with parents stressed and stretched as they tried to balance work and childcare.\par \par \uc2 With limited stimulation at home and less interaction with the world outside, pandemic-era children appear to have scored shockingly low on tests designed to assess cognitive development, said lead study author Sean Deoni, associate professor of paediatrics (research) at \uc2 Brown University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the decade preceding the pandemic, the mean IQ score on standardised tests for children aged between three months and three years of age hovered around 100, but for children born during the pandemic that number tumbled to 78, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2021.08.10.21261846v1.full.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the analysis}}}\uc2 , which is yet to be peer-reviewed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not subtle by any stretch,\u8221\'20\'1D said Deoni. \u8220\'20\'1CYou don\u8217\'20\'19t typically see things like that, outside of major cognitive disorders.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The study included 672 children from the state of Rhode Island. Of these, 188 were born after July 2020 and 308 were born prior to January 2019, while 176 were born between January 2019 and March 2020. The children included in the study were born full-term, had no developmental disabilities and were mostly white.\par \par \uc2 Those from lower socioeconomic backgrounds fared worse in the tests, the researchers found.\par \par \uc2 The biggest reason behind the falling scores is likely the lack of stimulation and interaction at home, said Deoni. \u8220\'20\'1CParents are stressed and frazzled \u8230\'20\'26 that interaction the child would normally get has decreased substantially.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Whether these lower cognitive scores will have a long-term impact is unclear. In the first few years of life, the foundations for cognition are laid, much like building a house \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s easier to add rooms or flourishes when you\u8217\'20\'19re building the foundation, Deoni said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe ability to course-correct becomes smaller, the older that child gets.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Given this data comes from a relatively affluent part of the US, where social support and unemployment benefits are generous, the fear is that things could be worse in poorer parts of the country and the world, he added.\par \par \uc2 Sir Terence Stephenson, a Nuffield professor of child health at University College London, said the research was interesting given much has been written about the impact on the education of school-age children, but not much has come out on infants.\par \par \uc2 The key factor influencing these lower scores in infants has likely been stress on parents who faced challenges in both working and providing full-time attentive childcare, he said. \u8220\'20\'1CPerhaps not surprising that children from lower socioeconomic families have been most affected as this resonates with many of the other financial, employment and health impacts of the pandemic.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210812eh8c0030e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
