\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Wall Street hits record high after US economy added 943,000 new jobs in July \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Jasper Jolly and Graeme Wearden (now) \par \uc2 5,781 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2021\par 13:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rolling live coverage of business, economics and financial markets including July\u8217\'20\'19s US jobs reportLatest: US added 943k jobs in July, more than expectedJobless rate slides to 5.4%, wages roseLeisure and education lead jobs gainsEarlier:Asda boss resigns early after private equity-backed takeoverSurprise decline in German production amid chip shortageMillions of British homes face higher energy bills as cap liftedUK house price growth slows to 7.6% annually in July\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 S&P 500 hits record high after 'stellar' jobs report\par \par \uc2 And finally... the jobs report is going down well on Wall Street, where stocks keep going up.\par \par \uc2 The broad S&P 500 stock index, and the narrower Dow Jones industrial average, have both hit new all-time highs in early trading.\par \par \uc2 Investors are welcoming signs that the US recovery is continuing, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2021/aug/06/uk-house-prices-covid-pandemic-us-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-ftse-business-live?page=with:block-610d29bf8f089093df876247#block-610d29bf8f089093df876247"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 943,000 new jobs created last month}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Dow is currently up 159 points, or 0.5%, at 35,223, while the S&P 500 has gained 0.2% to 4,436.08 points.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe Dow and the S&P 500 opened at record highs Friday morning after the monthly jobs report came in better than expected. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/4uqCNWGyqa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/4uqCNWGyqa}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/SuLSo1pcFP"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/SuLSo1pcFP}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 MarketWatch\uc2  (@MarketWatch) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MarketWatch/status/1423641483379347462?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Janet Mui, investment director at wealth manager Brewin Dolphin, says it\u8217\'20\'19s hard to find fault in today\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cvery strong\u8217\'20\'19 strong labour market report.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA stellar set of US labour market data despite concerns on the Delta variant and difficulty in hiring. New jobs are created at a robust pace, working hours were up and the US unemployment rate fell notably from 5.9% to 5.4%.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWage growth picked up to +4.0% YoY despite the lower-income job seekers that may distort wages down, suggesting positive underlying pay increases. This is a positive backdrop for consumer spending and services sector activity.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith the supply shortage in labour repeated in other inputs we can expect to see the current extraordinary growth rate moderate but not reserve any time soon. On the positive side, participation rate rose modestly in July and we should expect to see better labour supply dynamics after extra unemployment benefits are curtailed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COverall, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  should feel pleased at the pace of the recovery in the labour market. Wages are rising and house prices are booming so it will become increasingly difficult to justify the very loose stance of monetary policy. Financial markets should be prepared for more communication in a gradual tapering in bond purchases in the fourth quarter.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On that note, it\u8217\'20\'19s time to wrap up. Our US Politics Live blog will be tracking reaction to the jobs report from president Biden.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2021/aug/06/joe-biden-coronavirus-covid-us-economy-us-politics-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joe Biden to tout strong July jobs numbers \u8211\'20\'13 US politics live}}}\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll be back on Monday....\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 Will jobs gains prompt Fed to ease stimulus?\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2021/aug/06/uk-house-prices-covid-pandemic-us-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-ftse-business-live?page=with:block-610d29bf8f089093df876247#block-610d29bf8f089093df876247"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July\u8217\'20\'19s robust jobs report}}}\uc2  will add to the debate on when America\u8217\'20\'19s central bank might start to ease back on its pandemic stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Ron Temple, Co-Head of Multi Asset and Head of US Equities at \uc2 Lazard Asset Management\uc2 , argues that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  should sit tight.\par \par \uc2 Finally - jobs data is hitting high expectations. The July data combined with significant positive revisions to prior months exceeded high projections and highlights the sharp economic recovery underway.\par \par \uc2 Still, with 5.7 million fewer Americans employed than before the pandemic, it\u8217\'20\'19s too early to contemplate tightening monetary policy or to pull back on the reins of fiscal policy. Instead, policymakers should fix their gaze on a full recovery and on raising US economic productivity for years to come through significant infrastructure investments.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Andrew Hunter\uc2  of \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  suggests Federal Reserve chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  might hint at slowing the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s bond-buying programme later this month, when he speaks at the Economic Policy Symposium in Jackson Hole, Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 The stronger 943,000 rise in non-farm payrolls in July and upward revision to previous months\u8217\'20\'19 gains indicates that employment growth has shifted into a higher gear and that the drag on hiring from labour shortages is easing.\par \par \uc2 That suggests economic growth may be holding up better than we had feared and leaves open the possibility of Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  dropping a stronger hint that tapering is on the way at Jackson Hole in three weeks\u8217\'20\'19 time.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg\u8217\'20\'19s Matthew Boes has pulled out some interesting charts from the US jobs report:\par \par \uc2 enltrIncredible chart from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/boes_?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @boes_}}}\uc2 . Leisure and hospitality workers seeing 6.6% annualized wage growth (on a 24-month basis) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/me9IRA2aE5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/me9IRA2aE5}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Joe Weisenthal\uc2  (@TheStalwart) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TheStalwart/status/1423627718093070339?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrTwo more from the blog: (1) average hourly pay at restaurants relative to average hourly earnings in all industries continues to set new records {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/7WE9xYyFK9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/7WE9xYyFK9}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Matthew B (@boes_) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/boes_/status/1423636237823496195?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 enltr(2) Black Americans continue to be left out of the employment recovery in the leisure and hospitality sector, with headcount among that cohort down 26% from two years ago (versus -10% for White Americans, -12% for Asian Americans and -9% for Hispanic or Latino Americans) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/wdLDPoR6vQ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/wdLDPoR6vQ}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Matthew B (@boes_) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/boes_/status/1423636930424672268?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 The US labor force participation rate, which measures how many people are either in jobs or looking for work, remains below its pre-pandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 It rose slightly to 61.7% in July, from 61.6% in June, still 1.6 percentage points lower than in February 2020.\par \par \uc2 enltrLabor force participation rate for prime working age population. Like so many other stats, it's better but still a long ways to go. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/3H05rzc3fL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/3H05rzc3fL}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Eddy Elfenbein (@EddyElfenbein) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/EddyElfenbein/status/1423636396883988482?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.27pm BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2021/aug/06/uk-house-prices-covid-pandemic-us-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-ftse-business-live?page=with:block-610d29bf8f089093df876247#block-610d29bf8f089093df876247"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 After July\u8217\'20\'19s payroll gains}}}\uc2 , the US has now added 16.7m jobs since employment levels plunged in April 2020 during the first lockdown.\par \par \uc2 But that still leave 5.7m jobs lost, compared to the pre-pandemic level in February 2020 - underlining that the recovery remains incomplete.\par \par \uc2 enltrStrong US jobs report for July shows continued strong rebound from depths of pandemic (and much faster than post-GFC recovery). But US economy still down 5.7 million jobs from pre-pandemic level of Feb 2020. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/eL4L0A2nZy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/eL4L0A2nZy}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jamie McGeever (@ReutersJamie) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ReutersJamie/status/1423634320158334983?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.15pm BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2021/aug/06/uk-house-prices-covid-pandemic-us-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-ftse-business-live?page=with:block-610d29bf8f089093df876247#block-610d29bf8f089093df876247"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The strong jobs gains over the last two months}}}\uc2  suggest that some of the worker shortages that have weighed on the US economic recovery have begun to ease.\par \par \uc2 But, the rise in the delta variant of Covid-19 could slow the jobs recovery in the coming months, cautions Richard Flynn, UK director at Charles Schwab :\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe employment data is starting to shine with rocketing payroll numbers. Today\u8217\'20\'19s positive numbers are an encouraging sign for markets that the economic reopening has kicked into a higher gear. This growth has been supported by robust hiring by small businesses which are the largest net U.S. job creators.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHowever, overall job numbers are yet to fully bounce back to pre-pandemic levels, and this gap is unlikely to close this year unless we see a string of million-plus job gains each month. While news of more delta variant coronavirus cases is likely to dampen sentiment somewhat, strong earnings and ample liquidity are likely to keep markets and the wider economy buoyant for now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Robert Frick\uc2 , corporate economist at Navy Federal Credit Union, is encouraged by the rise in educational jobs last month - suggesting it could help some parents return to work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis not only was a strong jobs report by nearly every measure, it also signals more good things to come. The 261,000 jobs added in local government and private education were particularly encouraging.\par \par \uc2 The lack of teachers has kept many parents, mainly mothers, at home to take care of their kids and supervise their education.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2 , former top economic adviser to President Obama, says today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report shows the US has made a lot of progress recovering from the economic shock of the pandemic (although there\u8217\'20\'19s much more still to do...):\par \par \uc2 enltrI have yet to find a blemish in this jobs report. I've never before seen such a wonderful set of economic data:\par \par \uc2 --Job gains in most sectors\par \par \uc2 --Big decline in unemployment rate, even bigger for Black & Hispanic/Latino\par \par \uc2 --Redn in long-term unemp\par \par \uc2 --Solid (nominal) wage gains\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2  (@jasonfurman) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/jasonfurman/status/1423624548419870723?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrSome of the details:\par \par \uc2 943,000 jobs in July w/ large upward revisions for May & June\par \par \uc2 0.5pp reduction in unemployment rate w/ a 1.0pp reduction for Black & 0.8pp reduction for Hispanic/Latino\par \par \uc2 Labor participation rose 0.1pp\par \par \uc2 Median duration unemployed fell from 19 to 15 weeks\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2  (@jasonfurman) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/jasonfurman/status/1423625640738234372?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrStill a long way to go: we're about 7.5 million jobs short of where we should have been right now absent the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 But we've made a lot of progress.\par \par \uc2 But is it "a lot" coming close to "substantial"? The Fed will be deciding that.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2  (@jasonfurman) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/jasonfurman/status/1423626427887407104?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Leisure, education and business services drive jobs gains\par \par \uc2 There were \u8220\'20\'1Cnotable job gains in leisure and hospitality, in local government education, and in professional and business services\u8221\'20\'1D last month, says the \uc2 U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Employment in leisure and hospitality surged by 380,000, with two-thirds (253k) of those gains at \u8216\'20\'18food services and drinking places\u8217\'20\'19 as restaurants and bars took on more staff.\par \par \uc2 Schools also saw a jump in hiring - with 221,000 new hires in local government education and by 40,000 in private education.\par \par \uc2 The report explains:\par \par \uc2 Staffing fluctuations in education due to the pandemic have distorted the normal seasonal buildup and layoff patterns, likely contributing to the job gains in July. Without the typical seasonal employment increases earlier, there were fewer layoffs at the end of the school year, resulting in job gains after seasonal adjustment.\par \par \uc2 enltrBig job gains across the board in July:\par \par \uc2 Restaurants: +253,000\par \par \uc2 Education (public): +221,000\par \par \uc2 Hotels: +74,000\par \par \uc2 Biz services: +60,000\par \par \uc2 Entertainment: +53,000\par \par \uc2 Warehouse/transport: +50,000 (19k in transit!)\par \par \uc2 Education (private) +40,000\par \par \uc2 Health: +37,000\par \par \uc2 Manufacture: +27,000\par \par \uc2 Laundry: 15,000\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Heather Long (@byHeatherLong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1423625738008342537?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 Pay also continued to pick up last month, suggesting that some US firms are raising wages to address labor shortages and record vacancies.\par \par \uc2 Average hourly earnings rose by 0.4% during the month, and were 4.0% higher than a year ago, July\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report shows.\par \par \uc2 enltrAverage hourly earnings +4% y/y vs. +3.9% est. & +3.7% in prior month {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/FZEDb6uE6h"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/FZEDb6uE6h}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Liz Ann Sonders (@LizAnnSonders) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LizAnnSonders/status/1423626208726618114?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe good news:\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22Nonfarm payrolls +943k vs. +870k expected\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22Net revisions to nonfarm payrolls +119k\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22Avg hourly earnings +0.4% m/m and +4.0% y/y, both above expectations\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22Unemployment rate 5.4% {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/iIfY3BSIhq"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/iIfY3BSIhq}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Liz Young (@LizYoungStrat) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LizYoungStrat/status/1423628393845829635?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 US economy adds 943k jobs in July\par \par \uc2 The US created 943,000 new jobs in July, a stronger performance than expected, suggesting that the economy continues to recover.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate has dropped sharply, to 5.4% from 5.9% in June.\par \par \uc2 June\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll has been revised up too, to show there were 938,000 new hires - up from 850k first estimated, in another boost.\par \par \uc2 enltrBREAKING: The US economy gained back 943,000 jobs in July, surpassing expectations. It\u8217\'20\'19s the biggest gain since last August.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment rate: 5.4% (down from 5.9% in June)\par \par \uc2 **The US has recovered ~75% of jobs lost during the pandemic. 5.7 million still out of work**\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Heather Long (@byHeatherLong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1423622841480761351?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrBetter-than-expected July nonfarm payrolls at +943k vs. +858k est. & +938k in prior month; great revision in June from +850k {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/m95D5lAqjd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/m95D5lAqjd}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Liz Ann Sonders (@LizAnnSonders) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LizAnnSonders/status/1423623515618652161?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 More details to follow...\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 A sign outside of American Legion Post 71 announces a job opening for a bartender. Photograph: Paul Weaver/SOPA Images/REX/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re approaching the US non-farm payrolls release (1:30pm BST, or 8.30am EDT) - one of the most closely followed data points in global economics because of its role as a bellwether for the US economy.\par \par \uc2 All indications are that this month\u8217\'20\'19s release - covering the number of jobs, excluding seasonal farm jobs, added in the US in July - will be a strong one.\par \par \uc2 Estimates vary, as ever, but the consensus is that payrolls increased by 870,000 last month, slightly above June\u8217\'20\'19s 850k jobs gains.\par \par \uc2 However, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/08/04/private-companies-added-330000-jobs-in-july-according-to-adp-far-short-of-the-653000-estimate.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s ADP payroll report}}}\uc2 , which tracks private sector jobs gains, came in lower than forecast (at just 330,000 new hires in July).\par \par \uc2 enltrJuly's non-farm payrolls report comes out in about twenty minutes. Gotta say, I don't think I can remember a period with so many mixed signals and this is a report that could be anywhere from huuuuge (>1m) to merely moderate (<400k). {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/0aRjlvnCCj"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/0aRjlvnCCj}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Justin Wolfers\uc2  (@JustinWolfers) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JustinWolfers/status/1423617880822779904?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrHigh-impact Events {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/ToWatch?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #ToWatch}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/AUD?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #AUD}}}\uc2  -> RBA Monetary Policy Statement {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/USD?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #USD}}}\uc2  -> NFP (Jul) -> expecting 870K jobs added to economy (up 20K from June) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/stocks?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #stocks}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/equities?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #equities}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/forexmarket?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #forexmarket}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/forextrading?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #forextrading}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/CFD?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #CFD}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/USD?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #USD}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/EUR?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #EUR}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/forex?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #forex}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/NFP?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #NFP}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/nonfarmpayroll?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #nonfarmpayroll}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/payroll?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #payroll}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/USEconomy?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #USEconomy}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/australialockdown?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #australialockdown}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/vrp6fBrfEZ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/vrp6fBrfEZ}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 BlackBull Markets (@blackbullforex) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/blackbullforex/status/1423376806501965824?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrNFP Friday Notes:\par \par \uc2 ??NFP {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/business?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @business}}}\uc2  Survey +870K seems reasonable. Any deviation will drive volatility and rumors about Jackson Hole\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\uc2 Baker Hughes\uc2  Rigs\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Brent aka Blacklion (@BlacklionCTA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BlacklionCTA/status/1423582871978078209?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 As we approach lunchtime in London there isn\u8217\'20\'19t much moving out there.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 is very flat indeed - the index has moved up by less than half a point - a lesser-spotted 0% move.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 was completely flat on Friday. Photograph: Refinitiv\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 London Stock Exchange Group\uc2  has held on to the top riser spot, up by 4.4%.\par \par \uc2 Hikma Pharmaceuticals\uc2  is the biggest faller on the FTSE 100, down by 7% despite beating revenue expectations.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Morrisons shares have risen by 2.8% after the latest bid from the Fortress-led private equity consortium.\par \par \uc2 You can see the jump after it was announced in the below graph (although there must have been an error with the feed during that spike downwards).\par \par \uc2 Morrisons shares jumped in value after a renewed bid from a group led by private equity firm Fortress. Photograph: Refinitiv\par \par \uc2 Interestingly, however, they are now trading at about 279p per share. That suggests that investors believe the latest offer could still be bettered.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.57am BST\par \par \uc2 The new bid for Morrisons will also include extra financial firepower from Cambourne Life Investment, a subsidiary of the Singaporean sovereign wealth fund, GIC.\par \par \uc2 The investors\u8217\'20\'19 shares of the company after the takeover would be: Fortress - 65%; Koch Real Estate Investments - 22%; and Cambourne - 13%.\par \par \uc2 Canada Pension Plan Investments will put up 29% of the funds for the deal, but will not have any equity in the supermarket.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.43am BST\par \par \uc2 The latest bid will put the ball very firmly back in \uc2 Clayton, Dubilier and Rice\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s court.\par \par \uc2 It came after pressure from Morrisons\u8217\'20\'19 biggest shareholder for the board to talk to investors other than the Fortress group.\par \par \uc2 Silchester Asset Management, which owns a 15% stake in the UK\u8217\'20\'19s fourth largest supermarket, said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/jul/27/morrisons-biggest-shareholder-inclined-not-to-back-takeover-bid"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Morrisons should \u8220\'20\'1Callow more time to respond to other parties}}}\uc2  who might offer better value to Morrison\u8217\'20\'19s public shareholders\u8221\'20\'1D - essentially saying they should hold out for more money.\par \par \uc2 enltrFortress consortium raises bid for Morrisons to 270p a share, plus the 2p divi, from 252p\par \par \uc2 Scene in \uc2 Clayton, Dubilier & Rice\uc2  offices right now... {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/dpgX1IuNED"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/dpgX1IuNED}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jonathan Eley (@JonathanEley) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JonathanEley/status/1423595072659021828?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.35am BST\par \par \uc2 Morrisons\u8217\'20\'19 board has unanimously backed the renewed offer from the Fortress consortium - perhaps unsurprising considering the fact that they backed a bid that was worth \u163\'00\'A3400m less.\par \par \uc2 The consortium also includes the \uc2 Canada Pension Plan Investment Board\uc2  and an investment company controlled by the US billionaire Koch family (who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/jul/03/who-are-the-american-investors-making-a-move-on-morrisons"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 might also prove controversial stewards}}}\uc2  for a major British employer).\par \par \uc2 The group (operating through a \u8220\'20\'1Cbidco\u8221\'20\'1D) made clear in their renewed offer that it was interest from another private equity firm that prompted its new-found beneficence: \uc2 Clayton, Dubilier and Rice\uc2  (\uc2 CD&R\uc2 ) is reportedly considering making another offer after its first \u163\'00\'A35.5bn bid was rejected.\par \par \uc2 In its statement the investors said:\par \par \uc2 Bidco notes the speculation regarding a possible counter-offer by \uc2 CD&R\uc2 . Bidco remains committed to becoming the new owner of Morrisons and to being a responsible long-term steward of this great British company through the next stage of its evolution. Accordingly, Bidco has engaged with the Morrisons board and its advisers in relation to the value of the original Fortress offer.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.18am BST\par \par \uc2 Morrisons receives new \u163\'00\'A36.7bn bid from Fortress\par \par \uc2 And more breaking retail news: Morrisons has announced that it has received a fresh offer from a group led by US private equity Fortress that values the supermarket at \u163\'00\'A36.7bn.\par \par \uc2 The 270p-per-share bid, plus a 2p-per-share dividend, improves on Fortress\u8217\'20\'19s previous offer, which valued the supermarket chain at \u163\'00\'A36.3bn.\par \par \uc2 Major shareholders had objected to the previous offer, arguing that Morrisons should give more time to talk to other potential suitors.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.09am BST\par \par \uc2 The Blackburn-based Issa brothers became billionaires by building up a petrol stations empire, \uc2 EG Group\uc2 . It appears they are keen to stamp their authority on Asda.\par \par \uc2 They will work directly with executives at the supermarket chain after the abrupt departure of Burnley. That suggests that a new chief executive, whoever it may be, will be taking her or his lead from the Issas.\par \par \uc2 enltr"Mutually agreed" that \uc2 Roger Burnley\uc2  will leave Asda now rather than stay on through new CEO appointment and handover\par \par \uc2 Follows exit of interim CFO and head of George\par \par \uc2 Issas "will work closely with the Asda team"\par \par \uc2 No doubt who is running the show now\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jonathan Eley (@JonathanEley) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JonathanEley/status/1423579565973295112?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrRoger Burnley has left Asda much sooner than the one year he said he would be staying for when he announced his departure. His premature exit marks one month since the CMA cleared the takeover and the Issas were able to come in and run the supermarket...No CEO successor yet.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Ashley Armstrong (@AArmstrong_says) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AArmstrong_says/status/1423578752307634178?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.51am BST\par \par \uc2 Asda\uc2  boss \uc2 Roger Burnley\uc2  resigns early after takeover \uc2 Roger Burnley\uc2  was formerly chief executive of Asda. Photograph: Peter Nicholls/Reuters\par \par \uc2 Roger Burnley\uc2  has stepped down from Asda at least four months earlier than initially planned, in a move that will leave Britain\u8217\'20\'19s third-largest supermarket without a boss as it adjusts to new owners.\par \par \uc2 Asda\uc2  was taken over by the billionaire Issa brothers, Mohsin and \uc2 Zuber Issa\uc2 , in May, after their offer was initially accepted in October 2020. The deal was backed by \uc2 TDR Capital\uc2 , a private equity firm.\par \par \uc2 In March Asda had announced that Burnley would leave at some point in 2022. \uc2 Asda\uc2  did not say why his departure had been brought forward by at least four months, but the new owners said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cmutually agreed\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Asda\uc2  said the process to recruit Burnley\u8217\'20\'19s replacement was \u8220\'20\'1Congoing\u8221\'20\'1D. It said that the Issas will \u8220\'20\'1Cwork closely with the Asda team on the execution of Asda\u8217\'20\'19s strategic vision\u8221\'20\'1D until a new boss is appointed.\par \par \uc2 In a joint statement, the Issas and \uc2 TDR Capital\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 The Asda business has proven its resilience over the last 18 months and has a strong platform in place for further innovation and growth. We have mutually agreed with Roger that now is the right time for him to step down from the business following a transition period under our ownership.\par \par \uc2 We would like to thank Roger for his leadership and contribution during his time with the business, particularly during the last year. We have a great team of more than 140,000 colleagues at Asda, and we look forward to supporting all of them to deliver for our customers in the second half of the year.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.01am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.31am BST\par \par \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  will repay another $400m to investors into supply chain finance funds linked to \uc2 Greensill Capital\uc2 , the investment company that collapsed in March.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss bank has suffered an annus horribilis. As well as having to pay for the Greensill debacle, its bankers backed Archegos, a hedge fund that blew up, and it is looking at a big restructuring.\par \par \uc2 It is the fourth Greensill payout so far and means there have been payments of about $5.9bn, Reuters reported.\par \par \uc2 Together with the cash that has already been distributed and cash remaining in the funds, the cash position is equivalent to approximately $6.6bn or 66% of the funds\u8217\'20\'19 assets under management at the time of their suspension.\par \par \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s\u8217\'20\'19 asset management arm said on Friday:\par \par \uc2 Liquidation proceeds will be distributed as soon as feasible until the investors receive the funds\u8217\'20\'19 total net collected liquidation proceeds.\par \par \uc2 Investors will receive notification of these payments. Management fees are waived with immediate effect.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.06am BST\par \par \uc2 An employee works on reconditioning Apple iPhones in France. Photograph: Lo\u239\'00\'EFc Venance/AFP/Getty Images\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s an interesting story from overnight: Apple has said it will scan iPhones in the US for images of child sexual abuse.\par \par \uc2 That sounds good - and child protection groups are delighted - but some security researchers that the system could be misused, including by governments looking to spy on their citizens.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press reports:\par \par \uc2 The tool designed to detected known images of child sexual abuse, called neuralMatch, will scan images before they are uploaded to iCloud. If it finds a match, the image will be reviewed by a human. If child abuse is confirmed, the user\u8217\'20\'19s account will be disabled and the US National Center for Missing and Exploited Children notified.\par \par \uc2 Separately, Apple plans to scan users\u8217\'20\'19 encrypted messages for sexually explicit content as a child safety measure, which also alarmed privacy advocates. The detection system will only flag images that are already in the centre\u8217\'20\'19s database of known child abuse images. Parents taking photos of a child in the bath presumably need not worry.\par \par \uc2 You can read the full report here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/aug/06/apple-plans-to-scan-us-iphones-for-child-sexual-abuse-images"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Apple plans to scan US iPhones for child sexual abuse images}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.39am BST\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 has sagged a little bit more - it\u8217\'20\'19s now down 0.2% today.\par \par \uc2 But at the top end of the \uc2 London Stock Exchange\uc2  this morning is... the \uc2 London Stock Exchange\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The share price of the owner of London\u8217\'20\'19s stock market is up by 3.9%, after it reported \u163\'00\'A31.2bn in operating profits in the first half of 2021. Revenues rose by 4.6% year-on-year.\par \par \uc2 The update reassured investors worried about the costs of its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2019/aug/01/london-stock-exchange-agrees-22bn-deal-to-buy-refinitiv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 takeover of Refinitiv (which was formerly the data side of Thomson Reuters)}}}\uc2 . The (now completely separate) Reuters news agency explains:\par \par \uc2 The 300-year-old bourse is trying to transform into a one-stop shop for data, trading and analytics with its takeover of Refinitiv. However the costs of absorbing the data provider have worried some investors, sending its shares down 20% since early March when it gave more details on the integration.\par \par \uc2 The group said on Friday that about \u163\'00\'A377m of cost synergies from the Refinitiv takeover have been realised on a run-rate basis. It expects that to hit \u163\'00\'A3125m by the end of the year, up from its previous guidance of \u163\'00\'A388m.\par \par \uc2 And there was some good news for long-suffering traders (and, ahem, markets reporters): London Stock Exchange chief executive {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/shortcuts/2018/apr/13/david-schwimmer-could-he-be-any-more-in-charge-of-the-stock-exchange"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 David Schwimmer}}}\uc2  said the company has identified an issue that has caused repeated outages on its Eikon data and trading terminal.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.17am BST\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBy any conventional yardstick, this is still a very brisk market,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Jonathan Hopper\uc2 , CEO of Garrington Property Finders - but there are some signs of a bit more scrutiny from buyers.\par \par \uc2 He said:\par \par \uc2 Astute buyers have begun to ask much tougher questions on price. After six months of having things almost entirely their own way as prices rose almost by the week, sellers are finally being forced into a reality check.\par \par \uc2 On the front line we\u8217\'20\'19re starting to see buyers adopt a more pragmatic view on pricing, and a growing number of sellers are having to rein in their price aspirations.\par \par \uc2 Could there be a correction (jargon for a 10% decline from a peak) coming in the next year? Guy Harrington, chief executive of Glenhawk, a residential lender, said it might be possible:\par \par \uc2 Despite flickering, and frankly welcome signs, that the fuel igniting the market may be running low, house prices continue to demonstrate a complete disconnect from economic reality.\par \par \uc2 Only once the pandemic fallout picture becomes clear, with government stimulus fully withdrawn, and people start returning to the office en masse, will the read across to the housing market become meaningful. 2022 still looks more likely to see a correction of sorts than not.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.08am BST\par \par \uc2 Let\u8217\'20\'19s get some analysis of Halifax\u8217\'20\'19s housing data from this morning. It showed that UK house prices are still rising rapidly, but the pace of growth appears to be slowing.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crosby, UK head of infrastructure, building and construction at \uc2 KPMG\uc2 , an accountancy firm, said:\par \par \uc2 While the market is buoyant, overall annual growth is slowing and we are seeing much higher demand than supply, with owners nervous about putting their properties up for sale in case they can\u8217\'20\'19t find the right home to buy, leading to low stock for estate agents.\par \par \uc2 Then again, higher demand than supply usually means one thing: price rises! That could mean average selling prices stay the same, even if the volume of sales falls away somewhat.\par \par \uc2 Martin Beck\uc2 , senior economic advisor to the EY Item Club, an accountant-backed forecaster, argued that the Halifax data add to good reasons to think house prices will stay elevated. He said:\par \par \uc2 July\u8217\'20\'19s rise in prices on the Halifax measure contrasted with a fall in Nationwide\u8217\'20\'19s index. But it offers some tentative evidence that the tapering of the stamp duty holiday on 30 June has not affected the housing market too much and that other factors have continued to support house prices. These include government support to household incomes and ultra-low mortgage rates. While the former, notably the furlough scheme, will finish in the autumn, there seems little prospect of interest rates rising until well into next year, even after August\u8217\'20\'19s MPC meeting delivered a more hawkish tone.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic has also had potentially long-lasting effects on property preferences, including a \u8216\'20\'18race for space\u8217\'20\'19 as people seek larger homes in a world of more home working. There is plenty of fuel for property deposits provided by the substantial savings accumulated by households during lockdowns. Meanwhile job and incomes losses during the pandemic have disproportionately affected younger, lower-paid, people who are generally not in the market to buy a property.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.36am BST\par \par \uc2 Gas prices will rise after \uc2 Ofgem\uc2  raised its cap. Photograph: Lauren Hurley/PA\par \par \uc2 Millions of homes will be forced to pay some of the highest energy bills for the last decade after the industry regulator gave suppliers the green light to raise their prices by up to \u163\'00\'A3153 a year.\par \par \uc2 Households across Great Britain that use a default energy tariff to buy their gas and electricity can expect a sharp increase in their bills from October this year after the regulator lifted its energy price cap for the winter.\par \par \uc2 Ofgem\uc2  said gas prices have risen to a record high in Europe \u8220\'20\'1Cdue to a recovery in global demand and tighter supplies\u8221\'20\'1D, which is increasing the cost of heating homes and pushing up electricity prices.\par \par \uc2 You can read the full report here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/aug/06/millions-of-great-britain-homes-face-highest-energy-bills-in-a-decade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Millions of Great Britain homes face highest energy bills in a decade as cap lifted}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.32am BST\par \par \uc2 Surprise decline in German industrial production A robot adjusts a windscreen in a fully automated process on a model of the A-class production line of German car manufacturer \uc2 Mercedes Benz\uc2  at the Daimler factory in Rastatt. Photograph: Kai Pfaffenbach/Reuters\par \par \uc2 Output from Germany\u8217\'20\'19s mighty manufacturers fell in June, likely in part due to the global shortage of computer chips, according to official data that took economists by surprise.\par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters had expected production to increase by 0.5% in June, but instead it fell by 1.3%, the Federal Statistics Office reported.\par \par \uc2 The fall was driven by a drop in the production of capital goods, such as machinery and vehicles, which fell 2.9%. Consumer goods output continued to grow, rising by 3.4%.\par \par \uc2 The shortage of semiconductor computer chips has acted as a brake on the car industry around the world after the pandemic disrupted... well, everything. Car companies cut their chip orders at the start of the pandemic, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/apr/22/jaguar-land-rover-to-suspend-work-at-major-uk-car-plants-amid-computer-chip-shortage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 did not anticipate a strong recovery in demand}}}\uc2 , while rising home computing has caused an increased need for semiconductors from consumer electronics companies.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Kenningham, chief Europe economist at Capital Economics, a consultancy, said there were \u8220\'20\'1Csevere problems\u8221\'20\'1D with German manufacturing. He said:\par \par \uc2 Auto production was 29% below its pre-pandemic level, reflecting the impact of component shortages, notably of semiconductors. These are now expected to continue throughout the second half of the year and will hold back an otherwise strong economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Media reports suggest that auto manufacturers themselves think the component shortages are likely to continue into next year although they expect (or hope) the problems will be less severe. So a full recovery for Germany remains some way off.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.11am BST\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s looking very much like a Friday in August on Europe\u8217\'20\'19s stock markets. The FTSE 100 has eased to a gentle decline in early trades, down eight points, or 0.1%, to 7,111 points.\par \par \uc2 Here are the opening snaps from Europe\u8217\'20\'19s main markets:\par \par \uc2 * FRANCE\u8217\'20\'19S CAC 40 DOWN 0.1%\par \par \uc2 * SPAIN\u8217\'20\'19S IBEX FLAT\par \par \uc2 * EURO STOXX 600 INDEX DOWN 0.1%; EURO ZONE BLUE CHIPS DOWN 0.1%\par \par \uc2 * GERMANY\u8217\'20\'19S DAX UP 0.2%\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.44am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.02am BST\par \par \uc2 UK house price growth slows to 7.6% annually - Halifax\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our live coverage of business, economics, and financial markets.\par \par \uc2 UK house prices rose by 7.6% in the year to July, but Halifax, the lender which reported the data, suggests this might be a sign of a \u8220\'20\'1Ccooling market\u8221\'20\'1D after months of pumped-up activity.\par \par \uc2 The average UK house sold for \u163\'00\'A3261,221 in July - still a cool \u163\'00\'A318,500 more expensive than a year ago. Yet the rate of annual house price inflation has come back from 8.7% in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2021/jul/19/frenzied-buyer-activity-drives-house-prices-to-new-high"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a \u8220\'20\'1Cfrenzied\u8221\'20\'1D June}}}\uc2  and 9.6% in May - see Halifax\u8217\'20\'19s fetching blue graph for signs of a possible peak in growth.\par \par \uc2 Halifax data suggested house price growth may be on a downward trajectory. Photograph: Halifax\par \par \uc2 Of course, annual growth of 7.6% is still striking - and it far outstrips average wage growth at any point in the last decade (with the possible exception of May because of comparison with the drop in wages during the first UK lockdowns).\par \par \uc2 The reasons for soaring house prices (amid a pandemic that shut down large parts of the economy for months) are by now familiar: a combination of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/may/02/soaring-house-prices-and-cheap-credit-are-creating-the-wrong-sort-of-growth"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 historically low borrowing costs}}}\uc2 , pent-up demand following the temporary market freeze, a desire for more space prompted by lockdowns, and a hefty chunk of government subsidies.\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1Ccooling\u8221\'20\'1D foreseen by Halifax in the next few months will be in part caused by the last of these: the 0% stamp duty rate now only applies to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2021/jun/28/homebuyers-rush-to-complete-before-stamp-duty-holiday-deadline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the first \u163\'00\'A3250,000 of the purchase price (rather than the \u163\'00\'A3500,000 pandemic threshold)}}}\uc2  and it will revert back to standard rates starting from \u163\'00\'A3125,000 from October.\par \par \uc2 Russell Galley, a managing director at Halifax, said:\par \par \uc2 Recent months have been characterised by historically high volumes of buyer activity, with June the busiest month for mortgage completions since 2008. This has been fueled both by the \u8216\'20\'18race for space\u8217\'20\'19 and the time-limited stamp duty break. With the latter now entering its final stages, buyer activity should continue to ease over the coming months, and a steadier period for the market may lie ahead.\par \par \uc2 Latest industry figures show instructions for sale are falling and estate agents are experiencing a drop in their available stock. This general lack of supply should help to support prices in the near-term, as will the exceptionally low cost of borrowing and continued strong customer demand.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 * 1:30pm BST: US non-farm payrolls (July)\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.04am BST\par \par \uc2 2021-08-06T07:02:21Z 2021-08-06T14:16:01Z\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://media.guim.co.uk/811ae12df486e86275dfca89dd0b6f3902bb0bda/0_49_5017_3010/500.jpg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://media.guim.co.uk/811ae12df486e86275dfca89dd0b6f3902bb0bda/0_49_5017_3010/500.jpg}}}\uc2  A now hiring sign at a \uc2 7-Eleven\uc2  convenience store. A sign outside of American Legion Post 71 announces a job opening for a bartender. Morrisons shares jumped in value after a renewed bid from a group led by private equity firm Fortress. \uc2 Roger Burnley\uc2  was formerly chief executive of Asda. An employee works on reconditioning Apple iPhones in France. Gas prices will rise after \uc2 Ofgem\uc2  raised its cap. A robot adjusts a windscreen in a fully automated process on a model of the A-class production line of German car manufacturer \uc2 Mercedes Benz\uc2  at the Daimler factory in Rastatt. Halifax data suggested house price growth may be on a downward trajectory.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210806eh8600106\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
