\par\par \uc2 Society\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We went from heroes to zeroes\u8217\'20\'19: US nurses strike over work conditions\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,067 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 July 2021\par 10:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nurses across the US are picketing over severe understaffing issues and inadequate equipment amid the pandemic\par \par \uc2 Last April people across America came out of quarantine each night to cheer the healthcare workers fighting to save lives a the height of the coronavirus pandemic. Sixteen months on, nurses around the US are holding strikes and picket actions amid claims of deteriorating working conditions and severe understaffing issues.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost of us felt like we went from heroes to zeroes quickly,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dominique Muldoon, a nurse for more than 20 years at Saint Vincent\u8217\'20\'19s hospital in Worcester, Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 For over four months, more than 700 nurses at the Tenet Healthcare-owned Saint Vincent hospital have been on strike, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.boston25news.com/news/local/st-vincent-nurses-strike-worcester-becomes-second-longest-strike-state-history/I3KF2U44P5E2LB3HS4TDD3S374/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the second longest nurses\u8217\'20\'19 strike}}}\uc2  in Massachusetts\u8217\'20\'19 history. The hospital has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcboston.com/news/local/saint-vincent-hospital-plans-to-hire-permanent-replacement-nurses-as-strike-continues/2378798/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brought}}}\uc2  in replacement workers throughout the strike and have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.telegram.com/story/news/2021/03/26/strike-rich-city-billing-st-vincent-hospital-32-000-day-police-detail/7009189002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 spent}}}\uc2  more than $30,000 a day on police coverage during the strike.\par \par \uc2 Muldoon, co-chair of the local bargaining unit, said understaffing worsened during the pandemic, with more staffing cuts and furloughs, while nurses worked through breaks and past scheduled shifts to try to keep up with the demand for patient care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNurses were going home at night in their cars crying,\u8221\'20\'1D said Muldoon. \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19ll end up staying late or working through your break trying to fit the workload all in, but ultimately become so frustrated, because eventually you keep trying to overcompensate and cannot keep up with it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even through coronavirus surges, Muldoon affirmed understaffing and cuts were the \u8220\'20\'1Cnew normal\u8221\'20\'1D at the hospital, despite nurses going above and beyond during an emergency situation to take care of patients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve done our jobs long enough to know what standard we should need for patients,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Marlena Pellegrino, a nurse at Saint Vincent hospital, said nurses and the union tried to negotiate with the hospital administration to enact safe staffing ratios since before the pandemic, but their concerns were repeatedly brushed aside.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe respect for our profession was not evident by this employer and it\u8217\'20\'19s been going on for a long time. I think the pandemic shined a spotlight on that. We worked through some very tumultuous times where our employer could have stepped up to assist us instead of being an obstacle in our way of trying to care for our patients,\u8221\'20\'1D said Pellegrino. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen there aren\u8217\'20\'19t enough nurses at the bedside, bad things can happen to patients so we were forced to take this step until they\u8217\'20\'19re resolved.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Tenet Healthcare\uc2  did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 In Chicago, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nationalnursesunited.org/press/overwhelming-victory-nurses-community-first-medical-center-chicago"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about 300 nurses }}}\uc2  at Community First medical center held a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nationalnursesunited.org/press/registered-nurses-at-community-first-issue-10-day-strike-notice"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one-day strike}}}\uc2  on 26 July over hospital failures during the pandemic and new contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Kathy Haff, an emergency room nurse at Community First medical center for 29 years, explained the hospital lost a significant number of nurses on staff during the pandemic, including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://chicago.suntimes.com/2021/2/24/22299773/national-nurses-united-death-community-first-medical-center-our-lady-resurrection-presence-health"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three nurses}}}\uc2  who died from the virus, and now nurses are working severely understaffed and with inadequate equipment to perform their duties.\par \par \uc2 All those \u8216\'20\'18healthcare heroes\u8217\'20\'19 signs were garbage... We\u8217\'20\'19re like healthcare suckers because they didn\u8217\'20\'19t protect us\par \par \uc2 Kathy Haff\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey don\u8217\'20\'19t appreciate us. They claim to, but they don\u8217\'20\'19t. They just take advantage of us left and right,\u8221\'20\'1D said Haff. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re working at half staff basically. They don\u8217\'20\'19t care that we\u8217\'20\'19re short. They just keep loading us up and keep criticizing if you\u8217\'20\'19re not moving fast enough. There is no appreciation. All those \u8216\'20\'18healthcare heroes\u8217\'20\'19 signs were garbage. We didn\u8217\'20\'19t believe one bit of them. We\u8217\'20\'19re like, yeah whatever. We\u8217\'20\'19re like healthcare suckers because they didn\u8217\'20\'19t protect us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Community First medical center denied staffing issues. \u8220\'20\'1CCommunity First has policies and protocols to evaluate daily volumes and acuity by department by each shift or more frequently, as needed,\u8221\'20\'1D said the interim CEO for the hospital in a statement.\par \par \uc2 About 1,400 nurses at USC Keck hospital and USC Norris Cancer hospital in Los Angeles {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nationalnursesunited.org/press/usc-nurses-hold-two-day-strike-for-patient-safety"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 held a two-day strike }}}\uc2  on 13 and 14 July over understaffing and patient safety concerns.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of nurses represented by \uc2 National Nurses United\uc2  at hospitals throughout California and Texas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nationalnursesunited.org/july-21-national-day-of-action"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 held a day of action}}}\uc2  on 21 July to call attention to workplace issues highlighted by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Juan Anchondo, a nurse for nearly 18 years at Las Palmas medical center in El Paso, Texas, explained staffing issues at his hospital have worsened throughout the pandemic as nearby hospitals have lured workers away with bonuses and better pay, and support nurses from the \uc2 Federal Emergency Management Agency\uc2  (Fema) left several months ago after assisting with Covid-19 surges in the region.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople don\u8217\'20\'19t take breaks,\u8221\'20\'1D said Anchondo. \u8220\'20\'1COne of the things we\u8217\'20\'19re trying to negotiate is a relief break nurse, so nurses can properly decompress and take a break without interruptions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kimberly Smith, an ICU nurse for 12 years at the Corpus Christi medical center in Texas, said unsafe staffing was a prevailing issue in new union contract negotiations but that these important issues to nurses have fallen by the wayside for the sake of profits and public relations campaigns asserting nurses are heroes for working on the frontlines during the pandemic and empty thank you events where nurses were given free hotdogs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI just want to be safe at work. I don\u8217\'20\'19t need a hotdog. You\u8217\'20\'19re telling me I\u8217\'20\'19m a hero and how wonderful I am. Just make the working conditions safe. That\u8217\'20\'19s all nurses want. We want to feel like we\u8217\'20\'19re able to give the best care we can and have enough resources to do it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Smith, who added that nurses regularly skip breaks because there is no staff to relieve them. \u8216\'20\'18\u8216\'20\'18Even before the pandemic the staffing wasn\u8217\'20\'19t this bad. It\u8217\'20\'19s been a horrible year. Nurses have passed away, are getting out of the profession, they\u8217\'20\'19re retiring.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A Corpus Christi Medical Center spokesperson denied staffing shortages at the hospital. \u8220\'20\'1COur goal since the onset of the Covid-19 pandemic has been to protect our frontline clinicians and caregivers so they are able to continue to care for our patients and our communities,\u8221\'20\'1D they said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have worked to procure the much-needed PPE, supplies and staffing resources needed to combat this pandemic.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210730eh7u0018j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
