\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Union advocates rally in New York to support striking Alabama coalminers\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani in New York \par \uc2 703 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 July 2021\par 20:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 1,100 workers from two \uc2 Warrior Met Coal\uc2  mines in Brookwood have been on strike since April amid contract negotiations\par \par \uc2 Coal miners and union advocates from across the country rallied in New York on Wednesday morning in support of Alabama miners who are four months into a strike against their employer, \uc2 Warrior Met Coal\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dressed in camouflage T-shirts with the slogan \u8220\'20\'1CWe Are Everywhere\u8221\'20\'1D, members of the United Mine Workers Association (UMWA) gathered in midtown Manhattan in front of the headquarters of BlackRock, a hedge fund that is \uc2 Warrior Met Coal\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s largest investor.\par \par \uc2 Over 100 protesters chanted \u8220\'20\'1CNo contract, no coal!\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Warrior Met Coal\uc2  ain\u8217\'20\'19t got no soul!\u8221\'20\'1D as trucks and cars driving between the barricades enclosing the protesters honked in support.\par \par \uc2 Over 1,100 workers from two \uc2 Warrior Met Coal\uc2  mines in Brookwood, Alabama have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jun/01/striking-coalminers-alabama"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on strike}}}\uc2  since April amid union contract negotiations with the company. It has become one of the largest labor demonstrations seen in the deep south, a region that is typically hostile to labor disputes.\par \par \uc2 Workers say they took on a $6-an-hour cut in wages and reduced benefits in their 2016 union contract after \uc2 Walter Energy\uc2 , which eventually became \uc2 Warrior Met Coal\uc2 , declared bankruptcy in 2015.\par \par \uc2 UMWA rejected a contract offered up by the company in April, just a few weeks after the strike began, which would have given workers a $1.50 pay increase over five years. Workers say they want pay and benefits to match what they were receiving before the contract that was signed in 2016.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s about time to get what we deserve. We brought this company out of bankruptcy,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mike Wright, 45, a Brookwood miner on strike \u8211\'20\'13 one of the dozens who were in attendance at the protest. \u8220\'20\'1CWe deserve to get back what we had.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Miners said that in addition to a cut in pay and benefits, the company has also gotten stricter on attendance. Workers could be expected to work seven days a week, up to 16 hours a day, and termination for missing more than four days of work except in the case of a family death.\par \par \uc2 Coal mining is already a dangerous job, with miners going hundreds of feet underground and being exposed to toxic fumes, like methane gas.\par \par \uc2 Brian Kelly, 50, a third-generation miner and president UMWA Local 2245 who attended the protest with his wife, two daughters and grandson, said that many of his coworkers struggle with paying medical bills after healthcare became more expensive for workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have debt collectors all over our people because we can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to pay these bills,\u8221\'20\'1D Kelly said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere used to be a little respect for people who go down there\u8230\'20\'26 and this company just lost all respect for us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The company brought in contract replacement workers to keep the mines running, though workers say safety conditions have worsened and the company is running below capacity with the replacement workers.\par \par \uc2 Fellow coal miners from Ohio, Kentucky and West Virginia were in attendance at the rally, along with representatives from other labor unions like the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  and the Retail, Wholesale and Department Store Union.\par \par \uc2 Actress Susan Sarandon also showed up in support of the strikers, telling the crowd that BlackRock \u8220\'20\'1Cis a horrible smear on the vision of the United States\u8221\'20\'1D and that she admires the strength of the miners for continuing their strike.\par \par \uc2 The protest in New York is one of multiple protests UMWA has held for the current strike. Nearly a dozen workers were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/al-state-wire-alabama-strikes-arrests-health-fe0d54737ba5b090afdeb48e0e1ea8d6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 arrested}}}\uc2  and charged with trespassing during a protest that was held at a \uc2 Warrior Met Coal\uc2  mine in Tuscaloosa, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 Carl White, 35, president of UMWA Local 2397, said that BlackRock likely sees \u8220\'20\'1Cthat this company is not performing like it did when the union workers were in these coal mines\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re not asking for much here,\u8221\'20\'1D White said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s time to come to the table and give us a fair and decent contract.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 BlackRock declined to comment on the strike and protest. \uc2 Warrior Met Coal\uc2  did not immediately respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210728eh7s0035y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
