\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Why are Frito-Lays workers working \u8216\'20\'18suicide shifts\u8217\'20\'19 on the job?\b0\par\par\uc2 Indigo Olivier \par \uc2 774 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 July 2021\par 10:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers are now entering their third week on strike to demand better conditions\par \par \uc2 Since 5 July, hundreds of striking workers at a \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  plant in Topeka, Kansas, have drawn attention to the Dickensian conditions workers have been subjected to in order to produce some of the biggest brand name chips in the United States, including Fritos, Cheetos, Doritos, Lay\u8217\'20\'19s, Ruffles, Funyuns and Tostitos.\par \par \uc2 Workers have publicly aired a list of grievances ranging from stagnant wages, high turnover rates and a lack of hazard pay during the pandemic to 84-hour workweeks, warehouses in triple-digit heat with no air conditioning, months on end without a day off, and so-called \u8220\'20\'1Csuicide shifts\u8221\'20\'1D where workers are only off the clock for eight hours before having to come back in.\par \par \uc2 As one \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  worker {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cjonline.com/story/opinion/2021/07/02/frito-lay-employee-topeka-shares-complaints-working-conditions/7838411002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  in an op-ed for the Topeka Capital-Journal, \u8220\'20\'1CThis storm has been brewing for years.\u8221\'20\'1D The worker describes \u8220\'20\'1Ciron-fisted management\u8221\'20\'1D that has forced employees to continue working through the smoke and fumes of a fire, the loss of a father, a \u8220\'20\'1Cdeep freeze\u8221\'20\'1D cold front, years of inflation with cost-of-living increases as low as 20 cents, and the on-the-job death of a co-worker.\par \par \uc2 In an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/pkbmwy/im-a-frito-lay-factory-worker-i-work-12-hour-days-7-days-a-week"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interview}}}\uc2  with Vice, Mark McCarter, a 59-year-old palletizer and union steward who has been working at the facility since he was 19, described one instance a few years ago where a co-worker died on the line and the company had his body moved to the side without stopping production. \u8220\'20\'1CIt seems like I go to one funeral a year for someone who\u8217\'20\'19s had a heart attack at work or someone who went home to their barn and shot themselves in the head or hung themselves,\u8221\'20\'1D said McCarter.\par \par \uc2 Workers are now entering their third week on strike without pay as a last resort and have asked consumers to stop buying \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  products until an agreement with the company is reached. \u8220\'20\'1CWe would rather nobody buy any \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  products, Fritos, Doritos, Tostitos, Funyuns, Cheetos, all those, while we\u8217\'20\'19re on strike,\u8221\'20\'1D McCarter told Vice. \u8220\'20\'1CWe make all of those in Topeka, Kansas.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While these shocking conditions (more fitting for an Upton Sinclair novel than the facility of a Fortune 500 company) have been given a human face by the workers organizing against them, they point to an imbalance of power that transcends \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The movement for an eight-hour workday in the US has been waged by workers since the late 1800s. And wasn\u8217\'20\'19t legally guaranteed until 1938. In the decades that followed, all major federal legislation for workplace health and safety was won through the concerted efforts of organized labor and organized labor alone.\par \par \uc2 In a country where labor history is all but forgotten, it is all too easy for people to understand the modern workplace as the result of a progressive advancement in thinking over what people consider acceptable terms of employment in a developed nation. But \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  and the countless other workers that have organized against low wages and poor working conditions under the pandemic have debunked this narrative, revealing a constant tug of war between workers and the boss.\par \par \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  is a subsidiary of \uc2 Pepsico\uc2 , a multinational conglomerate that owns Pepsi, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , Aquafina, Mountain Dew, Tropicana, Gatorade, Aunt Jemima and a number of other brand-name products we see on supermarket shelves across the country. The company\u8217\'20\'19s tens of billions of dollars in yearly revenue across 23 brands is the outcome of decades of unchecked corporate consolidation hiding behind the appearance of consumer choice through clever marketing tactics and an absence of organized opposition.\par \par \uc2 If Covid-19 has taught us anything it is that the conditions we work under will be pushed as far as a company can take them while still maximizing its bottom line. That is until workers reach a breaking point.\par \par \uc2 When bargaining with the ??Bakery, Confectionery, Tobacco Workers, and Grain Millers Local 218, the union representing \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  workers, a company negotiator said the quiet part out loud.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYour negotiator told us that it isn\u8217\'20\'19t that \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to give us raises, it\u8217\'20\'19s that he is there to protect the stockholder investments,\u8221\'20\'1D said Cheri Renfro in an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cjonline.com/story/opinion/2021/07/02/frito-lay-employee-topeka-shares-complaints-working-conditions/7838411002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 open letter}}}\uc2  to her employer. \u8220\'20\'1CYour threats and bully tactics only fuel our fire. You have pushed us into a corner and we came out swinging. And now you\u8217\'20\'19re \u8216\'20\'18shocked\u8217\'20\'19?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Indigo Olivier is an investigative reporting fellow at In These Times magazine\par \par \uc2 Document 