\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers join day-long strike over working conditions\b0\par\par\uc2 Kari Paul in San Francisco \par \uc2 917 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 July 2021\par 23:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers for app companies call for better wages and protections for those seeking to unionize\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers have joined other app-based workers across the US for a day-long strike to protest against poor working conditions and demand the right to organize.\par \par \uc2 The workers are calling for better wages and congressional support of the Pro Act, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2021/03/09/975259434/house-democrats-pass-bill-that-would-protect-worker-organizing-efforts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bill that would}}}\uc2  provide protections for workers who attempt to unionize, including members of the gig economy. The bill has stalled indefinitely after passing in the US House in March.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CApp-based workers are fed up with exploitation from big tech companies,\u8221\'20\'1D said Eve Aruguete a driver from Oakland and member of organizing group Rideshare Drivers United. \u8220\'20\'1CMisclassification is like concrete, keeping us underground. The Pro Act is the jackhammer that will break that concrete apart, allowing app-based workers to organize.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The strike began at midnight on Wednesday with workers in California, Boston, Las Vegas, Denver and Austin refusing to take orders. Rallies took place across several cities.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of workers rallied outside of Los Angeles international airport and at Uber\u8217\'20\'19s headquarters in San Francisco, where drivers blocked the street with cars emblazoned with slogans such as \u8220\'20\'1Cstrike for dignity\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1CUber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  are driving us into poverty\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 On the ground below Uber\u8217\'20\'19s towering headquarters in San Francisco\u8217\'20\'19s South Beach neighborhood, speakers at the rally underscored how the pandemic benefited white-collar Uber employees while thousands of drivers were left without work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWithout drivers, there is no Uber \u8211\'20\'13 without drivers, there is no \uc2 Lyft\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Eddy Hernandez, formerly a senior software engineer at Uber who quit because he disagreed with how the company treated drivers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTech workers and drivers need to come together and demand the end to the second-class employment status that restricts workers from having the fair pay and dignity only some are afforded,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 Erica Mighetto, who has driven for \uc2 Lyft\uc2  for four years and for Uber since 2019, said at the protest in San Francisco that workers fear for their livelihoods as some pandemic-related unemployment benefits are set to run out in September.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe want to get out ahead of that devastation and let our voices be heard,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need protections \u8211\'20\'13 we need the right to organize.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I say worker, you say power\u8221\'20\'1D chants at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/_drivers_united?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @_drivers_united}}}\uc2  protest at LAX today {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/JVtVldE8IU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/JVtVldE8IU}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 Carly Olson (@CarlyOlson_) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CarlyOlson_/status/1417946567311446017?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 21, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The strike comes as Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  hike prices amid a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2021/06/09/uber-lyft-drivers-price-hike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 record driver shortage}}}\uc2 . That shortage has been driven by a \u8220\'20\'1Csilent strike\u8221\'20\'1D, said Brian Dolber, an organizer and communications professor, as drivers refuse to return to a job they see as exploitative.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is drivers fighting back and saying they are not going to be second-class workers,\u8221\'20\'1D Dolber said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are saying they cannot continue to work under the forms of inequality we have seen during the pandemic.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In 2020, the number of Uber rides decreased by 80% in some areas, leaving hundreds of thousands of drivers without work, according to a survey from the \uc2 University of California, Santa Cruz\uc2 , and Jobs With Justice San Francisco. Some 37% of respondents said they had lost 100% of their income, while another 19% had lost more than 75% of their income.\par \par \uc2 But as vaccinations increased and demand bounced back, many drivers refused to return to their work behind the wheel, said Daniel Russell, a driver for Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  for the past four years and an organizer with Rideshare Drivers United.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe pandemic really underscored for us our vulnerability when the market dried up,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CNow is the time to take action.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A spokesman from \uc2 Lyft\uc2  told \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  that as vaccines had rolled out, it had begun to see the demand for rides outpace drivers but had been adding more drivers in recent weeks. It declined to provide any additional comment on the protests.\par \par \uc2 The strike originally focused on workers in California, where an industry-backed bill {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/feb/18/uber-lyft-doordash-prop-22-drivers-california"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called Proposition 22}}}\uc2  went into effect in early 2021, exempting some major tech firms from fully complying with labor laws. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/nov/11/california-proposition-22-uber-lyft-doordash-labor-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Under Prop 22}}}\uc2 , gig companies can continue to be classify workers as contractors, without access to employee rights such as minimum wage, unemployment benefits, health insurance and collective bargaining.\par \par \uc2 Organizers say in the months since Prop 22 passed, Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  have raised prices for riders while decreasing the portion of the fare drivers receive. Uber did not immediately respond to request for comment. \uc2 Lyft\uc2  denied that claim.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey promised us flexibility, greater control and greater transparency,\u8221\'20\'1D said driver Carlos Pelayo. \u8220\'20\'1CBut since Prop 22 passed, I have less control over where I drive, who I pick up, and how much I make. Prop 22 was the most expensive lie ever told to California voters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Organizers say the Pro Act can right some of the failures of Prop 22 but requires more support from Senate Democrats. If passed, it would make it more difficult for gig economy firms to classify workers as independent contractors and allow Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers to join together to collectively bargain.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDrivers need the Pro Act because it allows us to form a union and organization that looks out on our behalf and ensures our safety and fair pay,\u8221\'20\'1D said Russell, who drives in the Los Angeles area. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to be able to have a say.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210721eh7l001jo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
