\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Overworked, underpaid: workers rail against hotel chains\u8217\'20\'19 cost-cutting\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 843 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 July 2021\par 09:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Housekeepers say plans to cut daily cleaning and save money means they\u8217\'20\'19ll have longer hours and more dangerous work\par \par \uc2 The hotel industry is rebounding from the pandemic, but workers now fear planned labor cuts could cost tens of thousands of jobs and increased workloads for those who remain.\par \par \uc2 Several of the largest US hotel chains have outlined different ways of working, which labor groups say amount to a slide in standards that could have a profound impact on workers\u8217\'20\'19 lives.\par \par \uc2 Nuris Veras Merlos, a housekeeper at a Hilton in downtown Seattle, was recently recalled to her job, but is only scheduled one or two days a week because her hotel eliminated daily room cleaning. She is concerned her job may disappear.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBefore the pandemic it was relatively simple touch-up cleaning. All of the surfaces, tiles, mirrors, glass, bathroom \u8211\'20\'13 it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t such a big deal to clean them. Now when we work, the guests build up towels, wet floors, dirty linens, and it\u8217\'20\'19s very difficult to scrub the tiles and all the surfaces. Sometimes they\u8217\'20\'19re so dirty they\u8217\'20\'19re black. And we have to use stronger chemicals to get them clean again and scrub with much more intensity,\u8221\'20\'1D said Veras Merlos.\par \par \uc2 It takes Veras Merlos and her co-workers nearly twice as long now to clean rooms after long stays, with much of the time spent picking up days\u8217\'20\'19 worth of garbage and hauling a lot of dirty linens, trash, and cleaning equipment. She has to take ibuprofen to get through the more intense physical exertion.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels like I\u8217\'20\'19ve been run over by a train,\u8221\'20\'1D said Veras Merlos. \u8220\'20\'1CHotels aren\u8217\'20\'19t talking about the sacrifice and quantity of demanding work the housekeepers who worked during the pandemic have gone through. It\u8217\'20\'19s scary seeing older, more senior workers getting injured. I have a co-worker whose shoulder is failing her and has been out of work for months. It makes me worry about my future.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Several of the largest hotel corporations in the US have proposed to permanently cut housekeeping and staffing throughout their portfolios as part of hotel industry recovery.\par \par \uc2 In a November {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://seekingalpha.com/article/4385518-host-hotels-and-resorts-hst-ceo-jim-risoleo-on-q3-2020-results-earnings-call-transcript"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quarterly earnings call}}}\uc2 , Host Hotels and Resorts chief executive Jim Rosoleo noted hotels will transition to make daily housekeeping services an opt-in amenity for guests.\par \par \uc2 Park Hotels and Resorts presented a plan to investors in November to reduce full-time staffing with a projected $70m annual savings in cuts, including cuts to housekeeping services.\par \par \uc2 Pebblebrook chief \uc2 Jon Bortz{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://annual-virtual-conference-slideshow"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 explained}}}\uc2  plans to continuously cut labor as part of the hotel industry recovery, and Marriott boss \uc2 Leeny Oberg\uc2  told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://seekingalpha.com/article/4426889-marriott-internationals-mar-ceo-tony-capuano-on-q1-2021-results-"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 investors}}}\uc2  the corporation plans to solidify savings on labor costs from Covid restrictions in areas such as housekeeping.\par \par \uc2 The Hilton chief executive also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://seekingalpha.com/article/4406752-hilton-worldwide-holdings-hlt-ceo-chris-nassetta-on-q4-2020-results-earnings-call-transcript"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 affirmed}}}\uc2  to investors cost-cutting reductions to housekeeping would be made permanent, even as his compensation {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.asyousow.org/our-work/ceo-pay/blog/2021/5/14/hilton-hotels"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased by 161%}}}\uc2  from 2019 to over $55m in 2020, while median pay for Hilton employees declined by 34% from 2019 to 2020.\par \par \uc2 A recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://unitehere.org/wp-content/uploads/Playing-Dirty-Report-FINAL.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  from \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 , a labor union representing hotel workers, found plans to end daily housekeeping would eliminate over 180,000 positions around the US \u8211\'20\'13 39% of all hotel housekeeping jobs.\par \par \uc2 The cuts would result in $4.8bn in lost wages for workers in an industry who are predominantly women of color. According to \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 , about 65% of their 300,000 union members in the US and Canada remain unemployed.\par \par \uc2 Julie Gabot has worked as a housekeeper at the Marriott-operated Sheraton Waikiki for over 30 years. She was recalled to work in November, but said many of her co-workers have yet to be recalled even as the hotel occupancy rates have recovered, and she\u8217\'20\'19s been faced with higher workloads.\par \par \uc2 She explained the higher workloads have caused her constant shoulder and back pain, and she\u8217\'20\'19s banged her fingers and arms on cleaning equipment and furniture while trying to rush to complete rooms. Workers often skip lunch breaks to complete workloads, she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COnce you finish working, you can really feel the pain in your body,\u8221\'20\'1D Gabot said.\par \par \uc2 Gabot and her co-workers, who are organized with \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 , have been handing out leaflets to hotel guests encouraging them to request daily room services from housekeepers to restore previous working conditions and so housekeepers who are still furloughed will be recalled.\par \par \uc2 Marriott did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Andee Huang, a housekeeper at Westin Seaport in Boston for 13 years, returned to work in September while many of her coworkers were still waiting to be recalled.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I came home, I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t cook or take care of my kids, and needed to take pills to be able to sleep due to the pain,\u8221\'20\'1D said Huang. \u8220\'20\'1CWe were running around all day, and while rushing, you knock into tables and corners of furniture, so it\u8217\'20\'19s dangerous. And it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t fair for our co-workers who are stuck at home without work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210705eh75001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
