\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  fares surge as pandemic recedes \u8211\'20\'13 but drivers don\u8217\'20\'19t get \u8216\'20\'18piece of pie\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,134 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 June 2021\par 14:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Recent changes have driven down pay for drivers, including a reduction in minimum pay for long-distance trips\par \par \uc2 Socializing, parties and late night trips are all coming back as the coronavirus pandemic recedes in the US and along with them has come a surge in the price of calling a cab. It\u8217\'20\'19s good news for Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , but for the rideshare drivers who were hit so hard by the pandemic? Not so much.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re making less than normal,\u8221\'20\'1D said Robert Eaton of Reno, Nevada, a full-time Uber driver for over two years. He tries to work as many hours as possible every week to provide for his family, citing his most common shift is 16 hours, noon to 4am. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile fares have skyrocketed in this market, the drivers\u8217\'20\'19 pay has not been raised at all.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Eaton said several recent changes have driven down pay for drivers, including an increased cancellation waiting time, a reduction in minimum pay for long distance trips.\par \par \uc2 Also, with significantly increased fares, Eaton said there have been no raises to compensate for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rgj.com/story/news/money/business/2021/03/29/reno-among-least-affordable-cities-country-housing-analysis/7050713002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased costs }}}\uc2  of living in his area or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gasprices.aaa.com/state-gas-price-averages/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rising gas prices}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs there has been a driver\u8217\'20\'19s shortage in my area, Uber continues to pay lower cost per mile and offer more unattainable so-called bonuses. They\u8217\'20\'19re chewing their way through a never-ending supply of drivers who think they can get bonuses only to find out that it\u8217\'20\'19s nearly impossible,\u8221\'20\'1D added Eaton.\par \par \uc2 Giovanny Tarrago, a full-time Uber driver for four years in the Chicago, Illinois area, said rideshare drivers used to receive significantly higher portions of fares, but the percentage Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  take from fares and distribute to drivers for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/uber-and-the-labor-market-uber-drivers-compensation-wages-and-the-scale-of-uber-and-the-gig-economy/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  compensation}}}\uc2  has reduced over the years from around 60% to 75% to a fraction of the fare in many cases. Some Uber drivers have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/06/16/tech/uber-software-bug-drivers-charged/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  even being charged for providing passengers with rides, cases that have been attributed to software glitches on the app.\par \par \uc2 Drivers\u8217\'20\'19 wages are not directly tied to surge pricing, so we end up getting the short end of the stick\par \par \uc2 Ken Parks, Uber driver\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat\u8217\'20\'19s happening now is Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  are charging high in demand prices to the passengers charging them surge rates and most of the time the rates aren\u8217\'20\'19t passed off evenly or fairly to drivers,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tarrago. \u8220\'20\'1CDrivers are not getting their rightful piece of the pie when riders are charged very high fares, and since fares are already so high the riders are less likely to tip on the app.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers in California have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/feb/18/uber-lyft-doordash-prop-22-drivers-california"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  rollback of features and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/may/16/uber-lyft-drivers-california-prop-22"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pay cuts }}}\uc2  over the past several months after Proposition 22 \u8211\'20\'13 a ballot measure that allowed ridesharing companies to classify their drivers as \u8220\'20\'1Cindependent contractors\u8221\'20\'1D, rather than \u8220\'20\'1Cemployees\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/nov/11/california-proposition-22-uber-lyft-doordash-labor-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 went into effect}}}\uc2  last November after an expensive lobbying effort by gig companies.\par \par \uc2 The companies are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://themarkup.org/news/2021/06/17/uber-and-lyft-donated-to-community-groups-who-then-pushed-the-companies-agenda"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 currently spending millions of dollars}}}\uc2  on public relations efforts around the US, including donating to community groups through political action committees, to publish opinion stories supporting Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and oppose efforts to classify gig workers as employees.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Moore, an organizer with Rideshare Drivers United in Los Angeles, California, noted Uber {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/may/16/uber-lyft-drivers-california-prop-22"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reduced }}}\uc2  mileage rates from Los Angeles International Airport earlier this year, cutting out an important source of revenue for drivers in the area as riders in cities around the US, particularly airports, have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usatoday.com/story/travel/news/2021/05/28/uber-lyft-shortage-airports-travelers-long-wait-time/7465600002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  long wait times and significantly higher fares.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe companies found a great solution to the problem, drivers can no longer see what passengers pay most of the time,\u8221\'20\'1D said Moore in regards to Uber {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2021/06/09/uber-lyft-drivers-price-hike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shifting}}}\uc2  back to a model in California used in the rest of the US where drivers\u8217\'20\'19 compensation is based on time and distance driving, with added surge bonuses. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have always been their piggy bank. They squeeze drivers when they need cash. They need a lot of cash now so they are squeezing drivers and passengers right now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ken Parks, an Uber driver in the Memphis, Tennessee, area for over two years explained that based on his experience if riders are charged surge pricing, for example a ride that normally costs $10 is raised to $30, drivers only receive an extra two or three dollars from that surge.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDrivers\u8217\'20\'19 wages are not directly tied to surge pricing, so we end up getting the short end of the stick,\u8221\'20\'1D said Parks. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are charging surge rates to riders and drivers see Uber take over 50% of the fare. At the end of the day we are trading wear and tear on our vehicles for exactly $.06 cents over the federal mileage write off and that\u8217\'20\'19s after three years of consistent rate cuts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Uber CEO \uc2 Dara Khosrowshahi\uc2 , whose {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marketwatch.com/story/uber-ceo-other-execs-saw-pay-cuts-in-2020-but-still-raked-in-millions-11615593162#:~:text=Uber%20UBER%2C%20-2.90%25%20Chief,the%20company%20proxy%20released%20Friday."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 total compensation}}}\uc2  from the company decreased from $42.4m in 2019 to $12.2m in 2020, recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/dkhos/status/1403465863320113155"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 argued}}}\uc2  on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that claims that drivers were receiving decreased shares of fare increases were false, citing proportional statistics to data Uber has refused to publicly disclose on drivers\u8217\'20\'19 earnings. Uber {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/02/10/uber-earnings-q4-2020-.html#:~:text=The%20company%20reported%20mixed%20Q4,%248.51%20billion%20loss%20in%202019."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  losses of $6.77bn in 2020 and $8.51bn in 2019.\par \par \uc2 His comments were in response to a Washington Post {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2021/06/09/uber-lyft-drivers-price-hike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 article}}}\uc2  where drivers in California argued their loss of control over rides and pricing has essentially resulted in pay cuts.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Uber declined to provide further details on the data cited by the Uber CEO, but added in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CSurge pricing is designed to balance supply and demand in our marketplace. It goes into effect automatically when there are more riders in a given area than available drivers. For riders, it\u8217\'20\'19s a premium: demand decreases as some riders wait for more drivers to become available, and the marketplace rebalances. For drivers, it\u8217\'20\'19s an incentive to serve busier areas. So while rider pricing and driver pay are calculated separately in the US, more surge leads to more earnings by design.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s not how Lisa Zambri Hull, a full-time Uber driver in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, sees it. She said support services for drivers, such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dallasnews.com/business/technology/2020/05/06/uber-cutting-3700-jobs-closing-driver-centers-in-first-round-of-cost-saving-moves/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hub centers }}}\uc2  and support lines, have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/uber-cuts-3-000-more-jobs-shuts-45-offices-in-coronavirus-crunch-11589814608"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cut}}}\uc2 , and drivers aren\u8217\'20\'19t paid for tasks such as returning lost items left in their cars to riders or fully compensated for cleaning fees or at all for car maintenance.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m making less now than during the middle of the pandemic. I have driven this whole pandemic and wages were better from last June to March. Since this May too many drivers are returning to work and there are not enough passengers to merit it,\u8221\'20\'1Dsaid Zambri Hull. \u8216\'20\'18\u8216\'20\'18Surges are down for drivers, while up for passengers. Where is all the extra money going? Who knows, not to our pockets.\u8220\'20\'1C\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210621eh6l001e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
