\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Can direct elections restore \uc2 UAW\uc2  as America\u8217\'20\'19s most powerful labor union?\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,199 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 May 2021\par 09:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 After a corruption scandal saw 12 union officials convicted of crimes, \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  members hope direct elections will bring inspiring leaders\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , once America\u8217\'20\'19s most powerful labor union, a union that led the way in building America\u8217\'20\'19s middle class, has sunk into hard times, with a huge corruption scandal in which 12 union officials, including two former \uc2 UAW\uc2  presidents, have been convicted of crimes.\par \par \uc2 But now many rank-and-filers are hoping the union will regain its moral stature and some of its old swagger through a major push to inject more democracy into the union.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of members are backing a campaign to have the \uc2 UAW\uc2  hold direct elections for union president, a move they believe will bring more inspiring union leaders, ones they say will fight harder for the union\u8217\'20\'19s rank-and-file.\par \par \uc2 In recent decades, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has been run by a single party or caucus, with each union president having a powerful say in choosing his successor, and that, dissidents say, led to internal rot \u8211\'20\'13 and the wide-ranging scandal in which top \uc2 UAW\uc2  officials embezzled more than $1.5m in union money and accepted more than $5m in kickbacks from auto company contractors and \uc2 Fiat-Chrysler\uc2  executives.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, a federal judge appointed an independent monitor to oversee the union\u8217\'20\'19s finances and internal elections to help prevent further corruption. The monitor will also oversee a referendum on whether to allow direct elections of the UAW\u8217\'20\'19s president and executive board.\par \par \uc2 For 70 years, the union\u8217\'20\'19s president has been chosen by convention delegates who have overwhelmingly been from a single group, the Administration Caucus, which was founded by the \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s legendary president, Walter Reuther. Reuther led the union during its golden years, when it was celebrated for staging huge strikes and reaching landmark contracts with \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Ford\uc2  during the 1950s that included generous raises and health and pension benefits that catapulted many workers into the middle class and became a model for unions across the US.\par \par \uc2 Scott Houldieson, an electrician at a \uc2 Ford\uc2  assembly plant in Chicago, says the Administration Caucus has lost its way. Houldieson is president of a new caucus, United All Workers for Democracy (UAWD), which is leading the fight to hold direct elections.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Administration Caucus has had control of our union for 70 years. Over the last several decades, it\u8217\'20\'19s gone downhill,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, noting that the \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s membership has plunged from 1.5 million in 1979 to 400,000 today, because of plant closings, automation and competition from imports and transplants in the south.\par \par \uc2 Houldieson said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe corruption scandal is just a symptom of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s one-party state. We\u8217\'20\'19re hoping that with direct elections, we can break up the one-party state and have checks and balances to not only prevent future corruption, but to get back to our relationship of fighting for the betterment of our members, rather than collaborating with the companies. I think that when members have the ability to directly elect the top leaders who represent them, it\u8217\'20\'19s going to make a big difference.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 No date has been set for the referendum on whether to hold a direct election, although under a consent decree the \uc2 UAW\uc2  reached with federal prosecutors, the referendum is to be held in the next six months.\par \par \uc2 On 12 May, federal district court judge David Lawson in Detroit appointed Neil Barofsky as monitor; Barofsky is a lawyer with Jenner and Block who served as inspector general for the $700bn Troubled Asset Relief Program. The day before, \uc2 Dennis Williams\uc2 , a former UAW president, was sentenced to 21 months in prison for misappropriating union funds for personal expenses including private villas in Palm Springs, cigars, high-end liquor and golfing fees and apparel.\par \par \uc2 Erik Gordon\uc2 , a professor at the University of Michigan Ross School of Business, criticized the consent decree for not requiring direct elections and for instead calling for a referendum.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt gives the entrenched leadership the opportunity to stay entrenched,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, noting that the rank-and-file is very angry at the union\u8217\'20\'19s leaders. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s real important that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  regain its credibility and moral standing with the rank-and-file, people who get up in the cold, drive in the midwest winter and go to work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rory Gamble\uc2 , the union\u8217\'20\'19s current president, hasn\u8217\'20\'19t endorsed direct elections, noting that many \uc2 UAW\uc2  board members believe it would let outsiders and outside donors interfere with the union\u8217\'20\'19s politics and policymaking. When the union signed the consent decree in January, Gamble said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are committed to making the Monitor\u8217\'20\'19s job a boring one, by doing everything we can to make sure there are no financial or ethical misconduct issues to monitor.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nelson Lichtenstein, a labor historian and author of the leading biography of Walter Reuther, vigorously {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://labornotes.org/2021/01/opening-door-more-democratic-uaw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supports direct elections}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think direct elections are a panacea,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut given the actual nature of the beast \u8211\'20\'13 the endemic corruption, the collaboration with management, the lethargy of some \uc2 UAW\uc2  leaders \u8211\'20\'13 given that, direct elections is something that would shake things up. It would release new social forces and energy.\u8221\'20\'1D Pointing to the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , which agreed to direct elections in 1989 as part of an anti-corruption consent decree, Lichtenstein said direct elections create pressure on union leaders to police themselves.\par \par \uc2 The referendum has not yet been scheduled, and opponents of direct elections have not yet geared up. But the Unite All Workers for Democracy caucus has mobilized, scheduling a week of action the week of 24 May to promote direct elections. Eric Truss, a UAWD activist and a worker at \uc2 Ford\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Rouge plant in Dearborn, Michigan, has been distributing flyers at several plants. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are excited about being able to have them make the choice themselves,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of direct elections assert that such elections would lead to more rank-and-file involvement and more responsive union leadership, and that, they say, would ultimately help the \uc2 UAW\uc2  win more unionization drives. The union suffered stinging losses in unionization elections at \uc2 Nissan\uc2  in Mississippi in 2017 and at \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  in Chattanooga in 2019 and 2014.\par \par \uc2 Rick Isaacson, a longtime auto worker who became president of his local in Michigan, opposes direct elections, warning that it would hurt small union locals.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI came from a local with only 1,800 members \u8211\'20\'13 why would anyone pay attention to my union any more with one member, one vote?\u8221\'20\'1D he said. Isaacson, who once served as assistant to the UAW president on union constitutional matters, said the current delegates\u8217\'20\'19 system makes it easier to assure diversity on the union\u8217\'20\'19s 13-member executive board, which has four Black people, one Latina and two women.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith one member, one vote,\u8221\'20\'1D Isaacson added, \u8220\'20\'1Coutside money is going to become an influence in these elections, and that\u8217\'20\'19s a problem. And then there\u8217\'20\'19s dark money. We see it in our national elections all the time. Why would we think it would be different in our union elections?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210525eh5p0015s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
