\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Americans are more pro-union \u8211\'20\'13 and anti-big business \u8211\'20\'13 than at any time in decades\b0\par\par\uc2 Emily DiVito and Aaron Sojourner \par \uc2 1,020 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2021\par 12:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Support for organized labor has even gone up among Republican voters - perhaps Trump\u8217\'20\'19s faux-populism had an unintended effect\par \par \uc2 Today, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/aaronsojourner/status/1375443938396409861"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 public feeling}}}\uc2  toward labor is more positive , and public feeling toward big business more negative, than at any time in five decades. What\u8217\'20\'19s more, workers increasingly want to be in unions: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0019793918806250"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 over half}}}\uc2  of Americans say they would vote for a union at work, while only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/union2.nr0.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 11 percent}}}\uc2  of US employees currently belong to one \u8211\'20\'13 largely because labor laws remain {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com/amazon-union-vote-bessemer-alabama-labor-law-experts-takeaways-2021-4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stacked}}}\uc2  in favor of big business.\par \par \uc2 Americans\u8217\'20\'19 rising affinity for organized labor and antipathy toward big business opens up new possibilities for a more balanced economy and society \u8211\'20\'13 but not without reform to the labor laws that hold workers back. For instance, because penalties are negligible, Amazon management has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/04/05/labor-board-reportedly-finds-amazon-illegally-fired-activist-workers.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 repeatedly violated workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights}}}\uc2  when workers acted collectively to improve their working conditions. When workers at an Amazon warehouse in Bessemer, Alabama, recently started to unionize, management {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/daily-labor-report/amazon-union-election-fails-now-what-nlrb-process-explained"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seems to have acted illegally}}}\uc2  again and the unionization effort failed. By making an example of one person, bank robbers can control a whole crowd; too many managers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s12122-001-1019-6.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have felt free}}}\uc2  to follow that logic with impunity.\par \par \uc2 Between 1964, when the American National Election Studies (ANES) began collecting data, and 2012, Americans\u8217\'20\'19 sentiment toward labor unions and big business trended together, each suffering public opinion surges and dips in tandem. But by 2016, these sentiments had unlinked: Americans\u8217\'20\'19 feelings toward big business chilled, while feelings toward labor unions warmed. ANES just released data from late 2020, and it reveals that this post-2012 trend continued into the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Today, all {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/aaronsojourner/status/1375812781694328837"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 political}}}\uc2  and all {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/aaronsojourner/status/1376397802427719680"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 age cohorts}}}\uc2  hold record or near-record positive views favoring labor over big business. Looking across generations, Americans born after 1975 have particularly strong positive feelings toward labor unions over big business. Democrats and independents have always felt more positively toward labor unions and less positively toward big business than Republicans, and that pro-union bent has risen to record heights since 2012. But even among Republicans, the union versus big business sentiment gap {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/aaronsojourner/status/1375812779517505536"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rose quickly}}}\uc2  between 2012 and 2016, and hit a record high in 2020.\par \par \uc2 So, why the recent public sentiment uptick? Since 2012, workers have organized and engaged in highly publicized {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fightfor15.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 minimum wage}}}\uc2  and union fights, which helped populist wings ascend and anti-labor, pro-business wings weaken within each party. Since 2016, Senator Bernie Sanders, long a vocal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jacobinmag.com/2020/03/bernie-sanders-unions-vermont-labor-movement-cwa-ibew/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 advocate}}}\uc2  of labor and antagonist of big business, has helped solidify a strong pro-labor constituency in the Democratic party and within the broader political landscape. Sanders\u8217\'20\'19s prominent presidential campaigns served as an organizing vehicle for a pro-labor left, and he continues to help {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BernieSanders/status/1376195621120569346?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 garner}}}\uc2  public support for union efforts. As president, Biden has taken unprecedented {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/POTUS/status/1369341211039961095"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pro-labor}}}\uc2  stances and helped consolidate liberal support for unions.\par \par \uc2 The left isn\u8217\'20\'19t the only reason why union support is up nationwide. Ironically, for all of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s atrocious {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/the-trump-administrations-attacks-on-workplace-union-voting-rights-forewarned-of-the-broader-threats-to-voting-rights-in-the-upcoming-election/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 anti-labor}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorker.com/news/john-cassidy/the-real-trump-agenda-helping-big-business"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pro-corporate}}}\uc2  policies, his phony {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2017/04/donald-trump-anti-union-president-acosta-gorsuch/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 populism}}}\uc2  may have realigned labor-business opinion among Republican voters. His red-herring bloviating {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2020/09/22/donald-trump-union-support-snub-joe-biden-418329"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in support of unions}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2021/04/06/why-half-hearted-conservative-boycotts-rarely-take-root/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 against corporations}}}\uc2  could be partly responsible for broadening labor and declining big business sentiment, especially among some Americans on the right.\par \par \uc2 The shift of public opinion in favor of organized labor comes against a backdrop of decades of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://econofact.org/what-is-at-stake-if-unions-wield-greater-clout"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declining}}}\uc2  union membership rates but rising union interest among workers. As such, the representation gap \u8211\'20\'13 the difference between the share of workers who\u8217\'20\'19d like to be in a union and those who are \u8211\'20\'13 is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/TcuuwQKva6?amp=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wider than it\u8217\'20\'19s been in decades}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Beyond the impact unions have on their members \u8211\'20\'13 including higher {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nber.org/papers/w9973"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wages}}}\uc2 , better health, retirement and other {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/001979398103400401"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fringe benefits}}}\uc2 , and reduced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/epdf/10.1111/ajps.12537"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 racial resentment}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 unions benefit non-member workers, too. Higher pay in union firms can increase competition for labor such that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/union-decline-lowers-wages-of-nonunion-workers-the-overlooked-reason-why-wages-are-stuck-and-inequality-is-growing/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nonunion firms}}}\uc2  raise wages. Strong unions {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w24587/w24587.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reduce inequality}}}\uc2 , and they increase {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ideas.repec.org/p/zbw/glodps/725.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voter turnout}}}\uc2 , the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/001979391306600207"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 election}}}\uc2  of working- and middle-class Americans to public office, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0019793916677595"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 charitable giving}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Unions {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/why-unions-are-good-for-workers-especially-in-a-crisis-like-covid-19-12-policies-that-would-boost-worker-rights-safety-and-wages/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 also help workers}}}\uc2  establish and leverage a collective voice to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theconversation.com/unionized-workers-are-more-likely-to-assert-their-right-to-a-safe-and-healthy-workplace-144718"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 raise concerns and demand better protections}}}\uc2 , making them especially valuable to workers when the Covid-19 pandemic has upended long-held expectations of workplace norms. Indeed, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rooseveltinstitute.org/publications/understanding-the-covid-19-workplace-evidence-from-a-survey-of-essential-workers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 evidence from the pandemic}}}\uc2  suggests that union members had safer workplace practices and conditions than nonunion members, and risk of Covid led to greater interest in unionization and greater willingness to engage in workplace collective actions, like going on strike or joining a protest.\par \par \uc2 The combination of the public\u8217\'20\'19s heightened sympathy for unions and the widening representation gap underscores how biased our current labor laws are against workers. Because unionization pulls power toward workers and away from managers and owners, employers go to extreme lengths \u8211\'20\'13 from outright {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/16/technology/amazon-unions-virginia.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 intimidation}}}\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/unlawful-employer-opposition-to-union-election-campaigns/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 illegally firing leaders}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 to suppress unionization. Penalties for violating workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights to organize are negligible, letting managers violate workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights with impunity.\par \par \uc2 New legislation \u8211\'20\'13 like the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/842"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Protecting the Right to Organize (PRO) Act}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 can help rebalance bargaining power in the labor market, supporting workers\u8217\'20\'19 ability to bargain on more-equal terms with representatives of concentrated wealth. If passed, the PRO Act would repeal state laws that undermine unions, institute tougher penalties for employers who violate workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights to organize, and recognize gig workers\u8217\'20\'19 right to collectively bargain.\par \par \uc2 Independent of the PRO Act\u8217\'20\'19s legislative future \u8211\'20\'13 a future made uncertain by the Senate\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rooseveltinstitute.org/publications/how-the-filibuster-has-hurt-workers-and-protected-corporate-influence/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 long history}}}\uc2  of obstructing progressive labor reform \u8211\'20\'13 the swelling public support for labor and simultaneous declining support for big business suggests a transformational shift in terrain. While previously, public perception of organized labor and organized capital was balanced, that no longer holds. These days, Americans worry far more about organized capital than organized labor.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Emily DiVito is program manager for the corporate power program at the Roosevelt Institute\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Aaron Sojourner is a labor economist at the University of Minnesota, a Roosevelt Institute fellow, and a former senior economist for labor at the US President\u8217\'20\'19s Council of Economic Advisers under Presidents Obama and Trump\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210513eh5d0019v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
