\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18No one wants to work anymore\u8217\'20\'19: the truth behind this unemployment benefits myth\b0\par\par\uc2 Amanda Holpuch in New York \par \uc2 875 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2021\par 12:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US employees are concerned about safety, others have caregiving responsibilities and some are using their job loss as an opportunity to find other work\par \par \uc2 At restaurants across the country \u8211\'20\'13 from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/KOBPatrickHayes/status/1382415576006496264"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Albuquerque}}}\uc2 , New Mexico, to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/bernrome/status/1382532094715305986"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fort Worth}}}\uc2 , Texas \u8211\'20\'13 the same sign is popping up: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are short staffed. Please be patient with the staff that did show up. No one wants to work any more.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The implication is that the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s expanded unemployment benefits of $300 each week are keeping people at home instead of behind cash registers and in fast-food kitchens.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a concern shared by independent business owners in interviews with local and national media, worried that their efforts to bump wages and increase benefits aren\u8217\'20\'19t luring in the workers they need as Covid-19 restrictions fall and consumer spending soars.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately for them, what\u8217\'20\'19s happening is a feature, not a bug, of the US economic system and the blame can\u8217\'20\'19t entirely be placed on a $300 weekly check.\par \par \uc2 The University of Pennsylvania economist {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mioana/status/1369698428847067143"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ioana Marinescu}}}\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CIn the absence of the benefits there would probably be a little bit more applications and hiring would be a little bit easier, but the main drive of the recent change in sentiment is that hiring is accelerating.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Job openings rose to a two-year high in February, according to the \uc2 US Labor Department\u8217\'20\'19s{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/jolts.nr0.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 job openings and labor turnover survey}}}\uc2  published last month. And in March, employers added nearly 1 million new jobs, with many economists expecting similar or better gains in the April jobs report on Friday.\par \par \uc2 If job openings accelerate faster than people apply for work, there will be pain for business owners. The pandemic has added some quirks to this economic reality.\par \par \uc2 It is true that a sliver of people would rather stay home for a few months making as much, or more, from unemployment than they would defrosting meat patties or answering phones.\par \par \uc2 But would-be employees are also concerned about safety - {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://covid.cdc.gov/covid-data-tracker/#vaccinations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 46% of the population}}}\uc2  hasn\u8217\'20\'19t received a single vaccine dose and the spread of Covid-19 is uncontrolled in the US. Potential employees also have caregiving responsibilities: this recession has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/apr/24/us-hiring-jobs-women-shecession"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disproportionately affected women}}}\uc2 , who largely take up these duties and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/schools-reopen-data/2021/03/23/a7d10b42-8bed-11eb-9423-04079921c915_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in late March}}}\uc2  more than half of schools were still doing remote learning or a combination of remote and in-person classes.\par \par \uc2 Others, like LaShanta Knowles in Cleveland, Ohio, are using their job loss as an opportunity to do something different.\par \par \uc2 Until September, Knowles had worked the same job at a relocation services company for seven years, unhappy and unsupported, only ever receiving attention for doing things wrong.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI stayed out of comfort and out of fear if this job didn\u8217\'20\'19t want me, I didn\u8217\'20\'19t think anyone else would,\u8221\'20\'1D Knowles said. \u8220\'20\'1CLuckily, I don\u8217\'20\'19t feel that way any more since I went back to school.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This month, Knowles will receive a certification in web programming and development. Even with the unemployment, the financial situation has been tough, in part because Ohio has been inconsistent with unemployment payments.\par \par \uc2 This is a problem facing millions across the country because the benefit system is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/mar/02/us-unemployed-millions-without-benefits"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unequipped to respond to this recession}}}\uc2 . It\u8217\'20\'19s been such an issue that the not-for-profit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ohorganizing.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ohio Organizing Collaborative}}}\uc2  stepped in to help people like Knowles navigate the system and invite them to take action to call for something better. And momentum is picking up to consider a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/04/14/senate-bill-would-expand-unemployment-and-pay-250-a-week-to-gig-workers.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 national unemployment reform}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, though, Knowles feels like luck is on her side. \u8220\'20\'1CUnemployment actually helped direct me on the path that I should have been on,\u8221\'20\'1D Knowles said.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment benefits have also allowed out of work people to help support the economy.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bfi.uchicago.edu/working-paper/spending-and-job-search-impacts-of-expanded-ui/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 University of Chicago researchers found}}}\uc2  that the unemployment expansion of $600 a week in 2020 allowed people to spend money in a way they wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t without it. That means some of the same businesses complaining about hiring might not earn as much money without unemployment.\par \par \uc2 The expansion is set to expire in early September and economists are watching to see what that means for business owners and low-wage workers, who are still struggling while the economy recovers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tracktherecovery.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more quickly for high-wage workers}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The University of Massachusetts Amherst economist {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/arindube/status/1382388433771958273"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arindrajit Dube }}}\uc2  said the fiscal stimulus, including unemployment benefits, could lead to a once in a generation or two generations increase in wages and reduced unemployment rates.\par \par \uc2 The last time this type of wage growth happened was in the late 1990s when the labor market tightened, with many employers chasing fewer workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou had a tight enough labor market which led to broad-based wage growth of the sort we hadn\u8217\'20\'19t really seen since maybe the 70s,\u8221\'20\'1D Dube said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd that was unusual and yes, employers had a hard time filling vacancies and they had to raise wages a lot and that\u8217\'20\'19s OK.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Cleveland, Knowles is most concerned about finding work with her new certificate. She didn\u8217\'20\'19t know when the unemployment expansion ended, and when the Guardian informed her it was September, she exclaimed: \u8220\'20\'1CI hope I find a job before then!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210507eh570015p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
