\par\par \uc2 Environment\par \b \uc2 The hunger industry: does charity put a Band-Aid on American inequality?\b0\par\par\uc2 Nina Lakhani in Houston. Graphics by Aliya Uteuova \par \uc2 2,443 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2021\par 18:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The pandemic has raised awareness of the US food insecurity crisis and highlighted the reliance on corporate food philanthropy\par \par \uc2 Alyson Graham raised three children by juggling multiple jobs and making tough choices about what they should go without. For more than 20 years, she made weekly visits to a food pantry, before going to the store to supplement the free groceries using food stamps and whatever money she had left after paying rent and bills.\par \par \uc2 It was a struggle to put nutritious food on the table.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI always had two jobs when my kids were growing up, but still couldn\u8217\'20\'19t make ends meet,\u8221\'20\'1D said Graham, 51, who worked minimum-wage jobs in call centres, bars and restaurants in Houston. \u8220\'20\'1CI couldn\u8217\'20\'19t let the lights or water go off, and there were always other expenses like shoes and books, so I relied on food pantries and frozen food like chicken nuggets to fill them up. The system is so skewed, it\u8217\'20\'19s almost impossible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Graham\u8217\'20\'19s story is a typical American story, and one that predates the unprecedented economic crisis caused by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Every month, millions of working folks are forced to choose between rent, bills, healthcare, childcare and food because they are not paid a living wage. According {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2019/06/20/joe-bidens-claim-that-almost-half-americans-live-poverty/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to one measure}}}\uc2 , 43.5% of Americans were living in poverty or low-income households in 2017, with the latter often just one emergency or missed paycheck away from falling below the poverty line.\par \par \uc2 Food philanthropy is focused on mitigating rather than ending hunger\par \par \uc2 Raj Patel, author and academic\par \par \uc2 Even when the economy is booming, at least one in eight families with children in the world\u8217\'20\'19s so-called richest country do not have reliable access to sufficient nutritious food needed for a healthy active life, according to \uc2 USDA\uc2  data collected since the mid-1990s. In times of recession, a fifth or more families have experienced food insecurity, which research shows can cause lifelong damage to a child\u8217\'20\'19s health, education and employment potential.\par \par \uc2 No matter what the state of the economy, the need for food aid has continued to rise as wages and government assistance have failed to keep up with the cost of living. A third of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hamiltonproject.org/assets/files/twelve_facts_about_food_insecurity_and_snap.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 food-insecure people }}}\uc2  are not considered poor enough to qualify for government food assistance.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFood charity has become so normalized, it\u8217\'20\'19s deeply embedded in our cultural and social values. But charity doesn\u8217\'20\'19t address the root causes of food insecurity, rather it perpetuates it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Alison Cohen, senior director of programs at WhyHunger, a global nonprofit working to end hunger and advance the human right to nutritious food.\par \par \uc2 Advocates argue that low wages are key to understanding why so many Americans struggle to afford adequate nutrition, which can only be fixed through major economic and political reforms. The federal minimum wage has been $7.25 since 2009, which progressive lawmakers such as Senator Bernie Sanders have lambasted as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/feb/25/bernie-sanders-mcdonalds-walmart-workers-congress-minimum-wage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 starvation wages}}}\uc2  that boost the bank balances of billionaire industrialists while costing the taxpayer billions in welfare programs such as food stamps (Snap) and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 Before the pandemic, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2020/07/most-families-that-received-snap-benefits-in-2018-had-at-least-one-person-working.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost 80% of households}}}\uc2  receiving food stamps had at least one worker, and about 31% included two or more workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFood stamps are fundamentally a subsidy to low-wage work which suits big corporations that campaign against a living minimum wage like \uc2 Walmart\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Charlotte Spring, a researcher in food and housing insecurity at the \uc2 University of Calgary\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Not everyone agrees that radical reforms are needed. \u8220\'20\'1CWe could eliminate hunger in the US by tuning up the tools at our disposal like Snap and tax credits, while we have a conversation about systemic issues and without fundamentally redoing capitalism,\u8221\'20\'1D said Diane Whitmore Schanzenbach, director of the Institute of Policy Research at Northwestern University.\par \par \uc2 The campaign for a federal $15 minimum wage suffered a major setback after it was removed from Joe Biden\u8217\'20\'19s Covid relief bill. Yet many campaigners argue that even $15 would not be enough in some places to cover all basic costs including food, utilities, rent, childcare and healthcare. Research by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reports.nlihc.org/oor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Low Income Housing Coalition}}}\uc2  last year showed that full-time workers earning $15 an hour could afford a two-bedroom apartment in only four states: Arkansas, Kentucky, Mississippi and West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Graham covered the rent because the family lived in subsidized housing. But she sacrificed her own health to make sure the children got what they needed. Apart from while being pregnant, she rarely saw a doctor because she didn\u8217\'20\'19t have insurance. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/31/us-affordable-healthcare-uninsured-underinsured"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A recent survey found}}}\uc2  that one in eight Americans has reduced food spending to pay for healthcare.\par \par \uc2 Graham was diagnosed with Grave\u8217\'20\'19s disease \u8211\'20\'13 an autoimmune disorder that causes the overproduction of thyroid hormones \u8211\'20\'13 after a visit to the emergency room. In 2019, three years after she started her current job in customer services, she was made full time and therefore finally eligible for employee healthcare. Her youngest son had just moved out, so she could afford the $200 monthly plan, and last year, doctors advised surgery to remove her thyroid gland. Graham survived financially thanks to the food pantry and help from relatives, as she hadn\u8217\'20\'19t accrued sufficient paid time off to cover her sick leave.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was undiagnosed for years because I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t access preventive healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D said Graham, who now earns $15.50 per hour and grows her own fruit and vegetables at the Westbury community garden. \u8220\'20\'1CI can finally survive without the pantry because my kids have gone, but I still don\u8217\'20\'19t have car insurance, that would be a luxury.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The neighbourhood is classified as a \u8216\'20\'18food desert\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 a low-income area with limited access to nutritious food. The nearest grocery store is just over a mile away along a road with a discontinuous sidewalk. Much closer is a gas station where food stamps can buy processed snacks, and the food pantry Graham\u8217\'20\'19s family relied on.\par \par \uc2 Food charity\par \par \uc2 The country\u8217\'20\'19s first food bank was established in Phoenix, Arizona, in the late 1960s by a retired businessman, who went on to create a national organization that became \uc2 Feeding America\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The rollout of neoliberalism in the 1980s by Ronald Reagan (and Margaret Thatcher in the UK) saw a shift away from social policies to tackle systemic problems such as poverty and hunger to an era of promoting deregulation, individual responsibility and global markets as the answer. The role of food charity was promoted as an alternative to government interventions, and food banks proliferated in the 1990s after another wave of austerity measures weakened America\u8217\'20\'19s already weak safety net.\par \par \uc2 Today, \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  is a network of 200 food banks which provides food to about 600,000 pantries, soup kitchens, shelters, and schools. It is the second largest US charity, according to Forbes, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.feedingamerica.org/sites/default/files/2021-03/FA_2020AnnReport_FINAL_updated0309_v2.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a revenue of $3.64bn}}}\uc2  in the last fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 Amid the sector\u8217\'20\'19s exponential growth, food banking has come under criticism for failing to tackle the root causes of food hardship. The level of food insecurity for households with children has never fallen below 14% since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ers.usda.gov/topics/food-nutrition-assistance/food-security-in-the-us/interactive-charts-and-highlights/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 records began in}}}\uc2  the mid 1990s.\par \par \uc2 Critics say the food charity industry has become a key part of the capitalist economy, which is why it shies away from advocating for systemic anti-poverty solutions such as a livable minimum wage and universal healthcare which some corporate donors lobby against.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFood philanthropy is focused on mitigating rather than ending hunger, because it is connected to capitalism by the hip,\u8221\'20\'1D said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rajpatel.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Raj Patel}}}\uc2 , author of Stuffed and Starved. \u8220\'20\'1CThere is so much money to be made in food aid through tax breaks, free publicity, salaried executives, electronic Snap cards \u8230\'20\'26 Food banks are expanding rather than trying to put themselves out of business.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  members distributed 3.5bn pounds of unsold excess food in 2019, of which 70% was classified as healthy. A third came from retailers such as \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , Krogers, HEB, \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  and Target, while 29% was donated by farmers and manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usda.gov/foodlossandwaste/donating"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 government incentivises}}}\uc2  food donations by offering businesses generous tax breaks and liability protection, claiming that this diverts food waste destined for landfills to needy families.\par \par \uc2 Critics say this allows \u8211\'20\'13 even encourages \u8211\'20\'13 the food industry to continue overproducing food which contributes to harmful greenhouse gases. \u8220\'20\'1CFood charity feels like a rational response to hunger and food waste, resolving the paradox of scarcity with excess production. It\u8217\'20\'19s the win-win narrative, but ultimately they are two different systemic problems which require distinct systemic responses,\u8221\'20\'1D Spring said.\par \par \uc2 Corporate gains\par \par \uc2 In the past, food banks were criticized for distributing mostly unhealthy processed food. The quality of food given to struggling Americans has significantly improved over the years, but stale bakery goods, fruits and vegetables past their best and sugary sodas are still found in grocery boxes, \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  has found.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFood banks put a Band-Aid on the problem, so yes, it would be worse if they stopped, and yes, they provide people with more nutritious and fresh food than 20 years ago, but they remain downstream appendages of the food industry and poor people are still being treated as garbage disposal,\u8221\'20\'1D said Andy Fisher, author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bighunger.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Big Hunger}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Big business doesn\u8217\'20\'19t just donate to food banks, it plays a critical role in the work they do and don\u8217\'20\'19t do.\par \par \uc2 Food bank boards are mostly white, and disproportionately made up of representatives of corporate America, while there are few people from labor, anti-poverty groups or the communities they serve, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thecounter.org/food-banks-hunger-board-members/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  research by Fisher.}}}\uc2  No matter what the state of the economy, a huge racial pay and wealth gap means food insecurity is always worse for Black, brown and Indigenous Americans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhite supremacy is financed by capitalism which created and enshrined charity as our default response to food insecurity,\u8221\'20\'1D said Paul Taylor from FoodShare.\par \par \uc2 Fisher added: \u8220\'20\'1CWho\u8217\'20\'19s at the boardroom table dictates what issues are prioritized, and with mostly white middle-class corporate America represented, you\u8217\'20\'19re not going to find discussions on economic justice and structural inequalities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The salaries of food banking executives have also come under scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  reviewed the most recent tax filings of 29 of the biggest food banks and found six figure CEO/director compensation packages ranging from $126,000 to $657,000. The total amount for executive salaries at \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  topped $6m, a level reasonable for market norms, according to its advisers. Meanwhile food banks rely on thousands of volunteers and low wage workers.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson said \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Cdeeply committed\u8221\'20\'1D to diversifying its board and last year gave $100m to food banks to increase access to people of color and rural communities. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no way that the charitable food system can solve hunger alone. This is an all-in fight that requires policy interventions, regulatory interventions and public-private partnerships. For this reason, we advocate for policies that increase food access with a keen focus on Snap \u8230\'20\'26 \uc2 Walmart\uc2  has been a longstanding partner in hunger relief.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest retailer, has deep ties to food assistance.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  is one of \uc2 Feeding America\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s so-called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.feedingamerica.org/about-us/partners/food-and-fund-partners"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 visionary partners }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the highest accolade reserved for the most generous donors (others include \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , Conagra, \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  and \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2 ). \uc2 Feeding America\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s CEO since 2018, Claire Babineaux-Fontenot, joined the organization after 13 years as a \uc2 Walmart\uc2  executive.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  helps fund \uc2 Feeding America\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Snap application assistance program, helping food banks get eligible people signed up. The retailer directly benefits from this generous support as it is one of the top \u8211\'20\'13 if not the number one beneficiary of Snap \u8211\'20\'13 the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s biggest food aid program, worth $55bn in 2019. The \uc2 USDA\uc2  does not release retailer-specific Snap sales figures but in 2019 analysts estimated \uc2 Walmart\uc2  derived about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pymnts.com/news/retail/2019/online-grocery-food-stamps-snap"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 4%}}}\uc2  of its US revenue from Snap dollars. (This doesn\u8217\'20\'19t include non-food items purchased by Snap customers during the same shopping trip.)\par \par \uc2 Last year, revenue for Walmart US was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://corporate.walmart.com/media-library/document/2020-walmart-annual-report/_proxyDocument?id=00000171-a3ea-dfc0-af71-b3fea8490000#:~:text=Walmart%20U.S.%20had%20net%20sales,fiscal%202019%20and%202018%2C%20respectively"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $341bn}}}\uc2 . The proportion from Snap may have been even higher given the 40% expansion of Snap during the pandemic, and the online shopping rollout which was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thecounter.org/amazon-walmart-online-snap-sales-explode-instacart/#:~:text=Though%20USDA%20does%20not%20release,U.S.%20sales%20from%20SNAP%20dollars."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 initially restricted to Walmart and Amazon}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some Snap dollars come from its own employees. The company, which recently announced that it would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marketwatch.com/story/some-730-000-walmart-employees-wont-be-getting-15-an-hour-pay-theyre-hardly-alone-11613765154"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 start paying about half of its employees $15 an hour}}}\uc2 , is one of the top four employers of food stamps and Medicaid recipients in every state, according to a 2020 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gao.gov/assets/720/710203.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  by a non-partisan government watchdog.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a certain amount of public relations in what corporate America does. They [grocery retailers] are key partners who are part of our solution, but also part of the problem \u8230\'20\'26 I\u8217\'20\'19m aware of the irony and it\u8217\'20\'19s uncomfortable,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mark Brown, CEO of West Houston Assistance Ministries (Wham) food pantry.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Walmart said the company offers education and training opportunities which allows low wage workers to move up the corporate ladder, with more than 75% of store management having started on hourly rates.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn addition to providing access to employment, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  has long provided access to affordable, nutritious food for our customers as well as for our communities through our philanthropic investments. In FY2020, Walmart US donated more than 585m pounds of food, 65% of which was fruits, vegetables, dairy and meats.\u8221\'20\'1D The company did not provide details on the tax savings gained.\par \par \uc2 Some food banks are trying to change.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.houstonfoodbank.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/04/finance_annualreport_fy20_web0419.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Houston food bank is}}}\uc2  the nation\u8217\'20\'19s largest, serving a region with a million food-insecure residents. It has one of the most racially diverse workforces and boardroom\u8217\'20\'19s in the industry, and last year introduced a $15 minimum wage for permanent staff. The main facility in east Houston is a round-the-clock 308,000 square foot logistical operation, where every day 1,100 or so volunteers work in shifts to sort and repack the food. The food bank distributes more food every year, yet still only meets a fraction of the need, according to Regi Young, chief strategy officer.\par \par \uc2 Young says the food bank is slowly shifting its model and mindset from measuring success by pounds of food distributed to imagining a world without food banks, which means addressing structural racism and the root causes of poverty. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a benefit to being Switzerland in our messaging, but we have to address the root causes and we\u8217\'20\'19re still grappling with that concept. The organization is willing to change, but we\u8217\'20\'19re not even close yet.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Alyson Graham\u8217\'20\'19s surname has been changed\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210428eh4s001e6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
