\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  raises minimum wage to $15 an hour for federal contractors\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff and agencies \par \uc2 550 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 April 2021\par 20:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 President signed an executive order that will provide a pay bump to thousands of workers, some who make as little as $10.95 an hour\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has signed an executive order that will raise the minimum wage to $15 an hour for federal contractors, providing a pay bump to hundreds of thousands of workers.\par \par \uc2 Biden administration officials said that the higher wages would lead to greater worker productivity, offsetting any additional costs to taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis executive order will promote economy and efficiency in federal contracting, providing value for taxpayers by enhancing worker productivity and generating higher-quality work by boosting workers\u8217\'20\'19 health, morale and effort,\u8221\'20\'1D the White House said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Biden has pushed to establish a $15 hourly minimum wage nationwide for all workers, making it a part of his coronavirus relief package. But the Senate parliamentarian said the wage hike did not follow the budgetary rules that allowed the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/feb/26/joe-biden-coronavirus-stimulus-bill-aid-package"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $1.9tn plan }}}\uc2  to pass with a simple majority, so it was not included in the bill that became law in March.\par \par \uc2 The liberal \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2  estimates that as many as 390,000 low-wage federal contractors would receive a raise, with roughly half of the beneficiaries being Black or Hispanic workers. There are an estimated 5 million contract workers in the federal government, according to a posting last year for the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  by Paul Light, a public policy professor at New York University.\par \par \uc2 Sylvia Walker, a federal contract worker at Maximus, which operates call centers for the \uc2 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services\uc2 , called the executive order an \u8220\'20\'1Cimportant first step\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve fought hard for fair pay and better working conditions, and we are thankful Biden\u8217\'20\'19s administration has heard our call to action,\u8221\'20\'1D Walker said.\par \par \uc2 The increase could be dramatic for workers who earn the current minimum of $10.95 an hour. Those workers would receive a 37% pay hike, though the increase would be introduced gradually, according to the terms of the order.\par \par \uc2 The White House said the workers would include cleaning professionals and maintenance workers, nursing assistants who care for veterans, cafeteria workers providing for the military and laborers who build and repair federal infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 All federal agencies would need to include the higher wage in new contract offerings by 30 January of next year. By 30 March, agencies would need to implement the higher wage into new contracts. The increase would also be in existing contracts that are extended.\par \par \uc2 The wage would be indexed to inflation, so it would automatically increase with each year to reflect changes in prices. The tipped minimum wage of $7.65 an hour for federal contractors would be replaced by the standard minimum by 2024.\par \par \uc2 Congress has not raised the federal minimum wage for all workers \u8211\'20\'13 $7.25 an hour \u8211\'20\'13 since 2007, despite opinion polls showing Americans overwhelmingly favor an increase.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CChange is possible. We urge Congress to follow President Biden\u8217\'20\'19s courageous leadership and make sure all workers \u8211\'20\'13 not just federally contracted workers \u8211\'20\'13 are given the same opportunity to thrive by passing the Raise the Wage Act,\u8221\'20\'1D said Saru Jayaraman, the president of One Fair Wage, a national non-profit that advocates on behalf of tipped workers.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210428eh4s0030e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
