\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Twelve months of trauma: more than 3,600 US health workers died in Covid\u8217\'20\'19s first year\b0\par\par\uc2 Jane Spencer and Christina Jewett \par \uc2 1,378 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2021\par 13:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lost on the Frontline, a year-long investigation by the Guardian and KHN to count healthcare worker deaths, ends today. This is what we learned in a year of tracing the lives of those who made the ultimate sacrifice3,607 healthcare workers have died in the first year of the pandemic. Explore our interactive database\par \par \uc2 More than 3,600 US healthcare workers died in the first year of the pandemic according to Lost on the Frontline, a 12-month investigation by the Guardian and Kaiser Health News (KHN) to track such deaths.\par \par \uc2 Lost on the Frontline is the most complete accounting of US healthcare worker deaths. The federal government has not comprehensively tracked this data. But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theguardian.com/us-news/2021/apr/07/biden-track-us-healthcare-worker-covid-deaths"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 calls are mounting for the Biden administration to undertake a count}}}\uc2  as the Guardian/KHN project comes to a close today. The project, which tracked who died and why, provides a window into the workings \u8211\'20\'13 and failings \u8211\'20\'13 of the US healthcare system during the pandemic. One key finding: two thirds of deceased healthcare workers for whom we have data identified as people of color, revealing the deep inequities tied to race, ethnicity and economic status in America\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare workforce. Lower-paid workers who handled everyday patient care, including nurses, support staff, and nursing home employees, were far more likely to die in the pandemic than physicians.\par \par \uc2 The year-long series of investigative reports found that many of these deaths could have been prevented. Widespread PPE and mask shortages, a lack of Covid testing, weak contact tracing, inconsistent mask guidance by politicians, missteps by employers, and lax enforcement of workplace safety rules by government regulators all contributed to the increased risk faced by healthcare workers. Studies show that healthcare workers were more than 3 times as likely to contract Covid as the general public.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe rightfully refer to these people without hyperbole \u8211\'20\'13 that they are true heroes and heroines,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Dr Anthony Fauci\uc2  in an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theguardian.com/us-news/2021/apr/08/anthony-fauci-thanks-us-healthcare-workers-true-heroes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 exclusive interview with the Guardian and KHN}}}\uc2 . The Covid deaths of so many health workers are \u8220\'20\'1Ca reflection of what healthcare workers have done historically, putting themselves in harm\u8217\'20\'19s way, by living up to the oath they take when they become physicians and nurses,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Lost on the Frontline launched last April with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/apr/09/first-doctor-die-coronavirus-us-frank-gabrin-er"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 story of Frank Gabrin}}}\uc2 , the first known American emergency room doctor to die of Covid-19. In the early days of the pandemic, Gabrin, 60, was on the frontlines of the surge, treating Covid patients in New York and New Jersey. Yet, like so many others, he was working without proper personal protective equipment, known as PPE. \u8220\'20\'1CDon\u8217\'20\'19t have any PPE that has not been used,\u8221\'20\'1D he texted a friend. \u8220\'20\'1CNo N95 masks \u8211\'20\'13 my own goggles \u8211\'20\'13 my own face shield.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gabrin\u8217\'20\'19s untimely death was the first fatality entered into the Lost on the Frontline database. His story of working through a crisis to save lives shared similarities with the thousands that followed.\par \par \uc2 Maritza Beniquez, an emergency room nurse at Newark\u8217\'20\'19s University hospital in New Jersey, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/dec/30/covid-ravages-minority-healthcare-workers-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 watched 11 colleagues die}}}\uc2  in the early months of the pandemic. Like the patients they had been treating, most were Black and Latino. \u8220\'20\'1CIt literally decimated our staff,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Her hospital has placed 11 trees in the lobby, one for each employee who has died of Covid-19; they have been adorned with remembrances and gifts from their colleagues.\par \par \uc2 More than 100 journalists contributed to the project in an effort to record every death and memorialize those who died. The project\u8217\'20\'19s journalists filed public records requests, cross-connected governmental and private data sources, scoured obituaries and social media posts, and confirmed deaths through family members, workplaces, and colleagues.\par \par \uc2 Among its key findings on those fatalities for which we have detailed information:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 More than half of those who died were younger than 60. In the general population, the median age of death from Covid-19 is 78. Yet among healthcare workers in our database, it is only 59.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 More than a third of the healthcare workers who died were born outside the United States. Those from the Philippines accounted for a disproportionate number of deaths.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Nurses and support staff died in far higher numbers than physicians.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Twice as many workers died in nursing homes as hospitals. Only 30% of deaths were among hospital workers, and relatively few were employed by well-funded academic medical centers. The rest worked in less-prestigious residential facilities, outpatient clinics, hospices and prisons, among other places.\par \par \uc2 The death rate among healthcare workers has slowed dramatically since the vaccine was made available to them last December. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://link.axios.com/click/23307459.49616/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cubmVqbS5vcmcvZG9pL2Z1bGwvMTAuMTA1Ni9ORUpNYzIxMDIxNTM_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3N2aXRhbHMmc3RyZWFtPXRvcA/5d667f6cf7304309f721b0b1Bd921bd6b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  published in late March found that only four of 8,121 fully vaccinated employees at the University of Texas Southwestern medical center in Dallas became infected. But deaths lag behind infections and KHN and the Guardian have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/feb/25/us-healthcare-workers-died-covid-since-december-vaccine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tracked more than 400 healthcare worker deaths}}}\uc2  since the vaccine rollout began.\par \par \uc2 Many factors contributed to the high toll \u8211\'20\'13 but our investigations uncovered some consistent problems that heightened the risks faced by healthcare workers.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://khn.org/news/widely-used-surgical-masks-are-putting-health-care-workers-at-serious-risk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Our reporting}}}\uc2  found that \uc2 CDC\uc2  guidance on masks \u8211\'20\'13 which encouraged hospitals to reserve high performance N95 masks for intubation procedures and initially suggested surgical masks were adequate for everyday patient care \u8211\'20\'13 may have put thousands of health workers at risk.\par \par \uc2 We exposed how the labor department, run by Trump appointee \uc2 Eugene Scalia\uc2  in the early part of the pandemic, took a hands-off approach to workplace safety. We identified {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://khn.org/news/osha-investigations-workers-filed-nearly-4000-complaints-about-protective-gear-some-still-died/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 4,100 safety complaints}}}\uc2  filed by healthcare workers to OSHA, the Labor Department\u8217\'20\'19s workplace safety agency. Most were about PPE shortages, yet even after some complaints were investigated and closed by regulators, workers continued to die at the facilities in question.\par \par \uc2 We also found that healthcare employers were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://khn.org/news/article/osha-let-employers-decide-whether-to-report-health-care-worker-deaths-many-didnt/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 failing to report worker deaths}}}\uc2  to OSHA. Our data analysis found that more than a third of workplace Covid deaths were not reported to regulators.\par \par \uc2 Among the most visceral findings of Lost on the Frontline was the devastating impact of PPE shortages. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/oct/07/texas-doctor-adeline-fagan-covid-coronavirus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Adeline Fagan, a 28-year-old OB-GYN resident in Texas}}}\uc2 , suffered from asthma and had a long history of respiratory ailments.\par \par \uc2 Months into the pandemic, her family says she was using the same N95 mask over and over, even during a high-risk rotation in the emergency room.\par \par \uc2 Her parents blame both the hospital administration and government missteps for the PPE shortages that may have contributed to Adeline\u8217\'20\'19s death last October. Her mother, Mary Jane Abt-Fagan, said Adeline\u8217\'20\'19s N95 had been re-used so many times the fibers were beginning to disintegrate.\par \par \uc2 Not long before she fell ill \u8211\'20\'13 and after she\u8217\'20\'19d been assigned to a high risk ER rotation \u8211\'20\'13 Adeline talked to her parents about whether she should spend her own money on an expensive N95 with a filter that could be changed daily. The $79 mask was a significant expense on her $52,000 resident\u8217\'20\'19s salary.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe said you buy this mask, you buy the filters, your father and I will pay for it. We didn\u8217\'20\'19t care what it cost,\u8221\'20\'1D said her mother, Abt-Fagan.\par \par \uc2 She never had the opportunity to use it. By the time the mask arrived, Adeline was already on a ventilator in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 Fagan\u8217\'20\'19s family feels let down by the \uc2 US government\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s response to the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNobody chooses to go to work and die,\u8221\'20\'1D said Abt-Fagan. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to be more prepared, and the government needs to be more responsible in terms of keeping healthcare workers safe.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Adeline\u8217\'20\'19s father, Brant Fagan, wants the government to begin tracking healthcare worker deaths and examining the data to understand what went wrong. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s how we\u8217\'20\'19re going to prevent this in the future, he says. \u8220\'20\'1CKnow the data, follow where the science leads.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Adeline\u8217\'20\'19s parents say her death has been particularly painful because of her youth \u8211\'20\'13and all the life milestones she never had the chance to experience. \u8220\'20\'1CFalling in love, buying a home, sharing your family and your life with your siblings,\u8221\'20\'1D said her mother. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s all those things she missed that break a parent\u8217\'20\'19s heart.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210408eh48000p3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
