\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 In the US, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is backing the unions. Britain can only look on in envy\b0\par\par\uc2 Martin Kettle \par \uc2 796 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2021\par 16:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The UK needs a political leader who will follow the US president \u8211\'20\'13 and put dignified work at the heart of our post-Covid recovery\par \par \uc2 Last week, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  unveiled a $2tn {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/31/biden-infrastructure-plan-address-climate-crisis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 infrastructure renewal plan}}}\uc2  whose boldness and scale caught the attention of the world. He began his launch speech in Pittsburgh with a particularly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/speeches-remarks/2021/03/31/remarks-by-president-biden-on-the-american-jobs-plan/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 striking affirmation}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m a union guy,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said. \u8220\'20\'1CI support unions. Unions built the middle class. It\u8217\'20\'19s about time they start to get a piece of the action.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2021/03/31/fact-sheet-the-american-jobs-plan/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 American Jobs Plan}}}\uc2  consists of many more substantial things than this warm rhetorical embrace of America\u8217\'20\'19s trade unions. The package involves massive federal investment in transport, housing, green jobs, electric cars, social care and much else besides. And Biden has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/01/22/business/economy/biden-trump-tax-law.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vowed to reverse}}}\uc2  much of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 2017 tax cuts on companies and the wealthy in order to pay for it.\par \par \uc2 But the warm words about unions are important too. Unions were not incidental to the US jobs plan. They were integral to it. Biden promised the plan would create as many as 18 million new jobs over four years. They would, he promised, be \u8220\'20\'1Cgood-paying jobs [...] jobs that you can raise a family on, and ensure free and fair choice to organise and bargain collectively.\u8221\'20\'1D A new law protecting union rights is part of the package.\par \par \uc2 The proportion of US workers in unions is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/union2.nr0.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 only around 11%}}}\uc2 . So Biden was not bowing to irresistible pressure when he framed his speech in pro-union language. Instead, he was placing a bet on economic security and respect as winning issues. He was betting that the dignity of work and workplace that unions provide, at their best, remains an essential part of a productive labour market, even in the gig economy era.\par \par \uc2 Contrast this with our own country. Britain remains significantly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tuc.org.uk/blogs/union-membership-rises-third-year-running-64-million#:~:text=There%20are%20now%206.44%20million%20union%20members%20in%20the%20UK.&text=Union%20membership%20increased%20in%20both,increased%20to%2023.5%20per%20cent."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more unionised}}}\uc2  today than the US, 24% compared with America\u8217\'20\'19s 11%. Yet it is hard \u8211\'20\'13 indeed inconceivable \u8211\'20\'13 to imagine any British politician, let alone a prime minister, announcing a post-Covid recovery programme with the words: \u8220\'20\'1CI support unions.\u8221\'20\'1D Britain is the poorer for that absence.\par \par \uc2 Some of this transatlantic disjunction can be explained by temperamental and ideological differences between individual political leaders. But a deeper part of the answer lies in enduring contrasts, across Europe as well as Britain and America, between the evolution of the different countries\u8217\'20\'19 union movements and political parties. Here is where Britain\u8217\'20\'19s comparative weakness now lies.\par \par \uc2 Emergence from the pandemic ought to be a fertile moment for Britain to be given the same kind of leadership that Biden is offering America. There is mounting concern here about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/mar/23/uk-furlough-scheme-covid-job-losses-unemployment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 job losses}}}\uc2  after the furlough ends, new post-Covid workplace issues, the rise and rise of the online giants and real wage decline for the many, plus a slew of legal cases and disputes in the gig economy, of which a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/apr/07/deliveroo-workers-strike-as-shares-rise-on-first-day-of-open-trading"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Deliveroo strike}}}\uc2  is the latest.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Trades Union Congress (TUC)\uc2 , led by \uc2 Frances O\u8217\'20\'19Grady\uc2 , includes many of Britain\u8217\'20\'19s unions, and has kept workplace issues on the government\u8217\'20\'19s agenda during lockdown. The Labour MP Jon Cruddas is also about to publish The Dignity of Labour, a trenchant reassertion of the centrality of work in the politics of the common good, with an endorsement by Keir Starmer on its cover.\par \par \uc2 Individually, unions have launched campaigns during the pandemic to fight for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.unitetheunion.org/campaigns/key-workers-deserve-a-pay-rise/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pay rises for key workers}}}\uc2 . But some, notably \uc2 Len McCluskey\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Unite, have also spent their efforts fighting to control the Labour party. The new Unison leader, Christina McAnea, rightly accused McCluskey of \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://inews.co.uk/news/uk/unite-leader-len-mccluskey-keir-starmer-battles-942078"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 indulgence}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D this week after his criticism of Starmer. But Unison, like Unite, does not often have much to say about how unions can improve wealth creation either. Too many unions remain too self-satisfied, too stuck in the past and too unresponsive to the workplace co-determination agenda that their predecessors tragically killed off in the 1970s.\par \par \uc2 Imagine, post-pandemic, if a British political leader was to make the world of dignified work the backbone of an ethical appeal for a national fair deal crossing economic class and geographic divides. It could even happen under the Conservatives. Indeed it almost did so under \uc2 Theresa May\uc2 , when she began highlighting the failures of management and the case for employee empowerment in 2017.\par \par \uc2 May\u8217\'20\'19s subsequent failure should not make Labour politicians complacent. If Starmer was to make good on his Cruddas endorsement and, to use his own words, aim to \u8220\'20\'1Cre-establish Labour as the party of work\u8221\'20\'1D he might put himself in pole position to own the politics of the post-pandemic era.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Martin Kettle is a Guardian columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210407eh470028r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
