\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US economy adds 916,000 March jobs as vaccine rollout fuels hiring boom\b0\par\par\uc2 Dominic Rushe in New York \par \uc2 703 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2021\par 14:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unemployment at 6% as businesses reopen and hire workersData shows 30% of Americans have had at least one vaccine shot\par \par \uc2 The US labor market went on a hiring spree in March, adding 916,000 jobs amid the fast-paced coronavirus vaccine rollout.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate edged down to 6% as businesses across the country started opening up and rehiring.\par \par \uc2 The surge in hiring was the highest since August last year and was fueled by \u8220\'20\'1Cthe continued resumption of economic activity that had been curtailed due to the coronavirus pandemic,\u8221\'20\'1D the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics}}}\uc2  (\uc2 BLS\uc2 ) said.\par \par \uc2 Thirty percent of the US population has now received one vaccination against the coronavirus and 16.9% is fully vaccinated, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://covid.cdc.gov/covid-data-tracker/#vaccinations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention}}}\uc2 . Covid-19 cases are still rising in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/24/michigan-covid-coronavirus-cases-hospitalizations-surge-vaccine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 many states}}}\uc2 , and over 549,000 people have died, but economic confidence appears to be rising.\par \par \uc2 In March, consumer confidence hit its highest level since the start of the pandemic, according to the latest poll by the \uc2 Conference Board\uc2 , and US stock markets have been setting new highs.\par \par \uc2 The gain in employment and confidence follows the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s passage of a $1.9tn stimulus package that will hand $1,400 checks to the majority of Americans.\par \par \uc2 One year after the pandemic burned a hole in the jobs market unemployment remains high. The slump hit low-wage workers, people of color and women the hardest.\par \par \uc2 March\u8217\'20\'19s largest gains were in leisure and hospitality, areas where low wage workers, women and people of color are over represented. The sector, hit hardest by the pandemic, added 280,000 jobs over the month. The industry is still down by 3.1m jobs from the pre-pandemic peak.\par \par \uc2 But there were also strong gains in construction, which added 100,000 jobs, and public and private education, which added 190,000 positions in total after months of heavy losses.\par \par \uc2 A new measure of the number of people working from home also dipped slightly. In March, 21% of employed people teleworked because of the coronavirus, according to the \uc2 BLS\uc2 , down from 22.7% in the prior month.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith the vaccination program likely to reach critical mass within the next couple of months and the next round of fiscal stimulus providing a big boost, there is finally real light at the end of the tunnel,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 The latest gains suggest that the worst of the pandemic-related jobs slump may be over \u8211\'20\'13 but big issues remain.\par \par \uc2 While the unemployment rate has fallen sharply from a dizzying high of 14.8% last April the US still has 8.4m jobs less than it did before the pandemic and layoffs remain high. Last week, 719,000 people filed claims for unemployment benefits, the labor department announced on Thursday. The figure was up from 658,000 the week before. Although the number of claims is trending down from record highs, it is still more than three times as high as it was before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1Creal\u8221\'20\'1D unemployment rate may be closer to 9%, according to a broader measure of the unemployment rate that is closely watched by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and includes people on the sidelines of the job market who are not actively looking for work.\par \par \uc2 Economists surveyed by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/graphics/econsurvey/?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Wall Street Journal}}}\uc2  are predicting the US will add an average of 514,000 jobs each month over the next year, for a total of more than 6 million. While such a gain would represent the best 12-month stretch of job creation in decades, the US is not expected to regain all the jobs lost to the pandemic until later in 2022.\par \par \uc2 This week, ADP, the US\u8217\'20\'19s largest payroll supplier, said the private sector had added 517,000 jobs in March, its strongest gain since September.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe saw marked improvement in March\u8217\'20\'19s labor market data, reporting the strongest gain since September,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Nela Richardson\uc2 , chief economist at ADP. \u8220\'20\'1CJob growth in the service sector significantly outpaced its recent monthly average, led with notable increase by the leisure and hospitality industry.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210402eh42001jn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
