\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 New Yorker staffers vote to authorize strike amid tensions with Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast\b0\par\par\uc2 Adam Gabbatt \par \uc2 540 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 March 2021\par 16:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 New Yorker, Ars Technica and Pitchfork unions said they are in their third year of bargaining with the media company\par \par \uc2 Staff at the New Yorker have voted overwhelmingly to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorkerunion.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 authorize a strike}}}\uc2 , after years of what they say has been fruitless negotiation with the magazine\u8217\'20\'19s publisher, the media empire Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast.\par \par \uc2 Members of the New Yorker union were joined by the Ars Technica union and Pitchfork union, which are also owned by Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast, in a vote which could see writers and other workers walk off the job.\par \par \uc2 Across the three unions 98% of members voted in favor of authorizing a strike, the New Yorker union said. The unions have not set a date for any strike action, but aim to stir Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast, which also publishes GQ, Vanity Fair and Vogue, into action.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are not on strike \u8211\'20\'13 yet,\u8221\'20\'1D the New Yorker union said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/newyorkerunion/status/1375465146005475328"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on Twitter}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe hope that management will meet us at the table and bargain in good faith. Otherwise, our members are ready to do whatever it takes to secure a fair contract.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The unions said they are in their third year of bargaining with Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast. The New Yorker union added: \u8220\'20\'1CIf and when our bargaining committees call for it, we will walk off the job to demand the contracts we deserve.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast has long profited off the exploitation of its workers, but that exploitation ends now,\u8221\'20\'1D the New Yorker union said in its statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe demand fair and equitable pay \u8211\'20\'13 including wage minimums that are in line with industry standards, a humane salary floor for current and future positions, and regular annual increases that keep pace with the ever-rising cost of living.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The unions {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/newyorkerunion/status/1375835613170180096"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 held a rally}}}\uc2  with NewsGuild of New York members outside One World Trade Center, in downtown Manhattan, on Saturday, in a bid to draw attention to the strike authorization. The Guardian US is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nyguild.org/front-page-details/the-guardian-us-writers-vote-unanimously-to-join-newsguild-cwa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 also a member}}}\uc2  of the NewsGuild of New York.\par \par \uc2 The New Yorker union said Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast \u8220\'20\'1Chas not negotiated in good faith\u8221\'20\'1D during the bargaining process, and had \u8220\'20\'1Cdelayed and undermined the bargaining process by refusing to respond to proposals and information requests for months at a time (or at all)\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In a statement provided to the press Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast said it had \u8220\'20\'1Creached agreement\u8221\'20\'1D with the New Yorker, Pitchfork and Ars Technica unions on some issues including just cause, additional paid time off and training.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COn wages and economics, management has proposed giving raises to everyone in these bargaining units; increasing minimum salaries for entry-level employees by nearly 20%; and providing guaranteed annual raises for all members, among other enhancements,\u8221\'20\'1D Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll of this has been accomplished in just two rounds of bargaining, as we first received the unions\u8217\'20\'19 economic proposals at the end of last year. We look forward to seeing this process through at the bargaining table.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The New Yorker union disagreed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn some cases, management\u8217\'20\'19s proposals would make the terms and conditions of our employment even worse; currently, they are proposing annual wage increases so small that salaries would not even keep up with the rate of inflation,\u8221\'20\'1D the union said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210329eh3t002gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
