\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Get ready for the return of the office\b0\par\par\uc2 Gene Marks \par \uc2 845 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 March 2021\par 08:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Companies are snapping up office space because despite Covid-induced working from home, bustling cities will be back\par \par \uc2 A strange thing is happening, thanks to the pandemic. While everyone is stuck working from home, some of the country\u8217\'20\'19s largest tech companies are snapping up real estate like nobody\u8217\'20\'19s business.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 , for example, announced just last week that it was investing more than $7bn to open new offices and expand data centers across the country. The tech giant is opening offices and data centers in Atlanta, Pittsburgh and Cambridge, Massachusetts, as well as Portland, Oregon, Houston, Reston, Virginia, and South Haven, Mississippi. It will expand data centers and workspaces in Nevada and Nebraska, Washington state, Detroit, Chicago, Ann Arbor and Austin.\par \par \uc2 The tech giant will \u8220\'20\'1Ckeep growing our offices across the US\u8221\'20\'1D, a spokesman for the company said in the Fox Business report. And \uc2 Google\uc2  is not alone in this real estate expansion.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/03/nyregion/facebook-nyc-office-farley-building.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times report}}}\uc2 , committed to a \u8220\'20\'1Cmammoth\u8221\'20\'1D 730,000 sq ft lease in the middle of New York last August, which brings its presence up to more than 2.2m sq ft of office space in the city. Throughout 2020 \uc2 Microsoft{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.costar.com/article/952974951/microsoft-signs-three-of-the-five-biggest-new-us-office-leases-in-pandemic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 snapped up}}}\uc2  office leases across the country and Apple also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com/apple-reportedly-signs-lease-for-new-office-space-new-york-2020-2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 added to}}}\uc2  its square footage in New York City. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is planning a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://therealdeal.com/2020/09/04/amazon-nabs-2m-sf-of-new-office-space-near-seattle/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 major expansion}}}\uc2  of nearly 2m sq ft of office space near its headquarters in Bellevue, Washington, and continues to lease and buy millions more square feet of warehouse and office space across the country and in places like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.costar.com/article/2119269614/amazon-extends-new-jersey-industrial-leasing-spree"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New Jersey}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bizjournals.com/philadelphia/news/2021/01/10/amazon-takes-another-warehouse-in-phila.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Philadelphia}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not just the tech giants that are snapping up real estate. Leases are quietly being signed by companies \u8211\'20\'13 big and small \u8211\'20\'13 in downtown and suburban areas across the country like Denver, Phoenix and even San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Of course the real estate market took a major hit during the pandemic and, yes, some areas will probably take years to recover. Many companies are looking into reducing their square footage and one year into the crisis many urban areas are still ghost towns compared with their pre-pandemic activity. Rents are significantly lower than pre-pandemic levels and deals are way, way down. The real estate industry is gloomy and looking at a gloomy 2021.\par \par \uc2 But the leaders at \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , Apple, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  aren\u8217\'20\'19t thinking about 2021, or even 2022. Neither are their counterparts at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://therealdeal.com/la/2020/12/22/here-are-las-biggest-office-leases-of-2020/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Disney, Netflix}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://commercialobserver.com/2021/03/morgan-stanley-planning-full-return-of-its-employees-to-midtown-headquarters/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Morgan Stanley}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.miamitodaynews.com/2021/03/16/four-national-global-firms-sign-downtown-doral-office-leases/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kia Motors}}}\uc2  and countless other firms you haven\u8217\'20\'19t heard of. While some companies are retreating from the real estate market, others are jumping in. Why?\par \par \uc2 Because they see a not-too-distant-future where cities return to their normal levels of activity. And they\u8217\'20\'19re snapping up deals when prices are low. This is not a bet. This is a certainty. Do you really think New York will not be New York? Or that downtown San Francisco will remain empty? C\u8217\'20\'19mon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are believers in offices, and I think there will continue to be good opportunities, particularly in the markets oriented around innovation, research, and content development,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Kathleen McCarthy\uc2 , the senior managing director and global co-head of real estate for the investment giant \uc2 Blackstone\uc2 , recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fortune.com/2020/11/06/blackstones-co-head-of-real-estate-describes-the-post-pandemic-office/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told Fortune}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CI think New York and San Francisco are experiencing a lot of pressure right now, but long-term, we think urban areas will get back to thriving.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Considering that these tech companies built the platforms that have enabled millions of workers to work remotely you would think that they would be the biggest champions of reduced office spaces. But no, that\u8217\'20\'19s not the case.\par \par \uc2 They get that a company\u8217\'20\'19s culture of innovation can\u8217\'20\'19t be built from people\u8217\'20\'19s homes, but from a place where people congregate\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s because the people running these tech companies know full well that their products have limitations. They know that technology can provide mobility, flexibility, independence and a better quality of life. They understand that their software, hardware and e-commerce platforms have enabled millions of business people to operate their businesses even when they\u8217\'20\'19re not in the office.\par \par \uc2 But they also know that an office, a workspace, a shared environment where humans interact face to face over coffee and donuts is a crucial part of any business. They understand that people working remotely do not connect, innovate, share ideas, debate, argue and brainstorm like they do when they\u8217\'20\'19re together. They get that a company\u8217\'20\'19s culture of innovation can\u8217\'20\'19t be built from people\u8217\'20\'19s homes, but from a place where people congregate.\par \par \uc2 Sure, they\u8217\'20\'19ll all have \u8211\'20\'13 like many forward-thinking organizations \u8211\'20\'13 generous work from home policies, because that\u8217\'20\'19s part of the work/life balance available to us and these benefits will be demanded by their employees, thanks to the pandemic. But they\u8217\'20\'19re investing in office spaces because they have every intention of filling those offices with people.\par \par \uc2 So get ready folks: someday, some way, you\u8217\'20\'19re coming back to the office.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210325eh3p0018m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
