\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 House will vote Wednesday morning on $1.9tn Covid relief bill \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Maanvi Singh (now), Joan E Greve and Martin Belam (earlier) \par \uc2 12,327 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2021\par 02:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Steny Hoyer confirms House to take up bill at 9am ET WednesdayFBI releases video of suspect planting pipe bombs in DC on 5 JanuaryOutrage as Georgia Republicans advance bill to restrict voting accessBiden pledges to tackle \u8216\'20\'18scourge\u8217\'20\'19 of sexual assault in US militaryJury selection gets under way in Derek Chauvin murder trialSign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.01am GMT\par \par \uc2 Summary\par \par \uc2 * The House will hold its final vote on the $1.9tn coronavirus relief bill tomorrow morning, majority leader \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s office said. The House will convene at 9am ET tomorrow, and Democrats are expected to quickly pass the bill in a party-line vote. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has already said he will sign the legislation as soon as it reaches his desk.\par \par \uc2 * The White House said Biden\u8217\'20\'19s name would not appear on the $1,400 stimulus checks, which will be distributed as part of the relief package . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s name appeared on the first round of stimulus checks last year, an unprecedented move that sparked intense criticism among Democrats.\par \par \uc2 * The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has released a video of the suspect who planted pipe bombs in Washington on 5 January, the day before the Capitol insurrection. The bureau is offering a $100,000 reward for information leading to the arrest of the person who placed the pipe bombs near the headquarters of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  and the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * A coalition of American environmental groups has urged the US to cut 50% of emissions by 2030, in order to address the climate crisis and inspire similar action from US allies.\par \par \uc2 * Biden\u8217\'20\'19s promise of a more humane US immigration system is facing its first big test. The White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , was grilled today on the recent surge in unaccompanied migrant children trying to enter the country. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are still digging our way out of a dismantled and immoral and ineffective immigration policy that was being implemented by the last administration,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki told reporters. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to take us some time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.21am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 Senate minimum wage battle could play out in midterm elections\par \par \uc2 By Jessica Goodheart for Capital and Main:\par \par \uc2 Sara Fearrington, a North Carolina waitress, joined the Fight for $15 campaign two years ago. A server at a Durham Waffle House, her take-home pay fluctuates between $350 and $450 a week, leaving her struggling to pay bills every month. She voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who had pledged to increase the federal minimum wage to $15 an hour. It was the first time Fearrington, who is 44, had ever voted in a presidential election.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt would mean everything. It would create stability for my household,\u8221\'20\'1D she said of the impact that a higher wage could have on her and her family of five, which includes her husband, who suffers from a rare lung condition, and a granddaughter who has asthma.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats}}}\uc2  will need her support for their \uc2 US Senate\uc2  nominee next year if they are to maintain and strengthen their tenuous hold on the upper chamber. Some of 2022\u8217\'20\'19s hotly contested Senate races are expected to play out in low-wage regions like Fearrington\u8217\'20\'19s home state.\par \par \uc2 The purple states of North Carolina, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin have rock-bottom minimum wages of just $7.25 per hour \u8211\'20\'13 the current federal minimum. Georgia, where the Democrat \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  will fight to hold on to the Senate seat he wrested from the Republican senator Kelly Loeffler in November, abides by the federal minimum wage, even though the one it has on the books is $5.15. Recent polling suggests {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans}}}\uc2  could gain a seat in New Hampshire, another low-minimum-wage state, where the Democratic senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  is facing a potential challenge from the state\u8217\'20\'19s Republican governor, \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The federal minimum wage has not increased from $7.25 since 2009, and for 21 states in the country the minimum wage law that governs employers is no higher than the federal minimum. Fearrington earns an hourly wage of just $3.10 an hour as a tipped worker, making her income unpredictable.\par \par \uc2 Biden had hoped to include a $15-an-hour minimum wage increase in his $1.9tn economic stimulus package, which is expected to pass this week, and would also gradually phase out the sub-minimum wages for tipped workers like Fearrington. But prospects for the minimum wage provision evaporated after the Senate parliamentarian ruled that the wage hike could not remain in the budget reconciliation bill where Democrats had placed it in order to avoid a Republican-led filibuster they lacked the votes to override.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThen, at least, it\u8217\'20\'19s a public conversation, where people are fighting for what they said they were going to fight for, for the poor and low-income people who turned out in record numbers in this past election,\u8221\'20\'1D said the Rev Liz Theoharis, co-chair of the National Poor People\u8217\'20\'19s Campaign.\par \par \uc2 Whether such a conversation ever takes place is a growing concern to progressives and a source of discord within the coalition that brought Biden to the White House \u8211\'20\'13 at a moment when the battered US economy stands at a crossroads. On Friday, the Vermont senator Bernie Sanders made an 11th-hour effort to reinsert the provision into the stimulus package. Eight Democrats crossed over to vote it down, including two senators from his neighboring state, New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/09/senate-minimum-wage-battle-midterm-elections-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Senate minimum wage battle could play out in midterm elections}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The House is currently considering the Protecting the Right to Organize Act, a bill that would expand labor rights and give new protections to workers.\par \par \uc2 Representatives approved a version of the bill last year, and it\u8217\'20\'19s poised to pass again. But it\u8217\'20\'19s unlikely to pass through the Senate, where Republicans have unanimously expressed opposition. Although Democrats have a slight majority in the Senate, they don\u8217\'20\'19t have the 60 votes needed to overcome a filibuster.\par \par \uc2 The law would allow contractors to be given employee status for union organization purposes, which would mean that gig workers could more easily unionize. Democrats have made it a priority to boost labor rights have tied the issue to the pandemic\u8217\'20\'19s economic toll, and promoted the reforms as a way to help hard-hit workers out of the current crisis\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs America works to recover from the devastating challenges of a deadly pandemic, an economic crisis, and reckoning on race that reveals deep disparities, we need to summon a new wave of worker power to create an economy that works for everyone,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in a statement.\par \par \uc2 In an impassioned speech, democratic Representative \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  of Ohio implored Republican colleagues to get on board: \u8220\'20\'1CHeaven forbid we pass something that\u8217\'20\'19s going to help the damn workers in the United States of America! Heaven forbid!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltr. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RepTimRyan?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @RepTimRyan}}}\uc2  : "Heaven forbid we pass something that's going to help the damn workers in the United States of America! Heaven forbid!" He concludes, "Now, stop talking about Dr. Seuss and start working with us on behalf of the American workers." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/aMR5hPssYI"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/aMR5hPssYI}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 CSPAN (@cspan) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/cspan/status/1369378736047616008?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 9, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.07pm GMT\par \par \uc2 One in six jobs lost: the effect of the pandemic on childcare providers\par \par \uc2 Carolyn Todd and her two sisters didn\u8217\'20\'19t enter the childcare business for the money \u8211\'20\'13 when they opened in 1995 their hope was to give low-income parents in Senatobia, Mississippi, a place to get high-quality, affordable care.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s never been easy \u8211\'20\'13 but 2020 brought new challenges as attendance numbers dropped, strict new safety requirements were introduced and the families they served bore the brunt of the economic recession\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou have to rethink and reorganize everything to make it work,\u8221\'20\'1D said Todd, who added that Enchanted Days Learning Center has only survived because of a temporary change in state funding.\par \par \uc2 Others have been hit even harder. Since the pandemic struck, thousands of childcare providers across the country have collapsed under the strain. One in six childcare jobs has been lost since the start of the pandemic, according to a January report by the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The impact has been felt across the country as parents have missed, or left, work for childcare demands or are unable to return to work after being laid off because of childcare responsibilities. In the past year, nearly 700,000 parents of children under five \u8211\'20\'13 over half of them women \u8211\'20\'13 have dropped out of the workforce, according to an analysis by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.americanprogress.org/issues/early-childhood/news/2021/01/13/494450/saving-child-care-means-preserving-jobs-supporting-working-families-small-businesses/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Center for American Progress}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Melissa Boteach, vice-president of income security and childcare at the \uc2 National Women\u8217\'20\'19s Law Center\uc2  (NWLC), said: \u8220\'20\'1CWhen the economy reopens, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to have lost a big chunk of our childcare supply. People are going to be trying to go back to work and there will be nowhere to put their children.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2021/mar/09/childcare-providers-coronavirus-jobs-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 One in six jobs lost: the effect of the pandemic on childcare providers}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A sixth woman has come forward with allegations of sexual harassment against the New York governor, \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Times-Union {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.timesunion.com/news/article/Cuomo-faces-new-allegation-of-sexual-harassment-16011424.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reports}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 An official close to the matter on Tuesday confirmed to the Times Union that the new allegation had been made. The governor\u8217\'20\'19s office learned of the matter on Monday, an aide said.\par \par \uc2 In response to questions about the handling of the revelation of the woman\u8217\'20\'19s allegations, Beth Garvey, acting counsel to the governor, said: \u8220\'20\'1CAll allegations that we learn of directly or indirectly are going promptly to the investigators appointed by the attorney general.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cuomo, in a conference call with reporters on Tuesday afternoon, claimed he was unaware of the woman\u8217\'20\'19s allegations. He also has not denied touching women, but has said if he did it was not \u8220\'20\'1Cinappropriate\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFirst, I\u8217\'20\'19m not aware of any other claim,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CAs I said last week, this is very simple, I never touched anyone inappropriately \u8230\'20\'26 I never made any inappropriate advances... [and] no one ever told me at the time that I made them feel uncomfortable. Obviously, there are people who said after the fact they felt uncomfortable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The woman has not filed a formal complaint with the office of the governor.\par \par \uc2 Her allegations were reported to the governor\u8217\'20\'19s counsel by other employees in the executive chamber. The information also was relayed by the governor\u8217\'20\'19s office to the attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s office, which is coordinating an investigation into multiple allegations of sexual harassment that have been made against the governor.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 70% of Americans say they they favor the $1.9tn economic relief package\par \par \uc2 A hefty majority \u8211\'20\'13 70% \u8211\'20\'13 say they are in favor of the coronavirus relief package that the House is poised to approve. Only a third of Americans said the legislation is too costly, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2021/03/09/broad-public-support-for-coronavirus-aid-package-just-a-third-say-it-spends-too-much/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a poll}}}\uc2  from Pew research.\par \par \uc2 Some key figures:\par \par \uc2 * About 40% of Republicans and Republican-leaning independents support the package.\par \par \uc2 * A whopping 94% of Democrats and Democratic leaners favor it.\par \par \uc2 * A majority of those polled (57%) said the Biden administration made a good-faith effort to work with Republicans, whereas only 42% said Republicans did the same.\par \par \uc2 See the full poll {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2021/03/09/broad-public-support-for-coronavirus-aid-package-just-a-third-say-it-spends-too-much/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A day after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  announced the nomination of two female military officers, Gen Jacqueline Van Ovost and Lt Gen Laura Richardson, to become four-star commanders, on International Women\u8217\'20\'19s Day, the US president tweeted out a cool Oval Office pic and caption.\par \par \uc2 enltrI want every child to know that this is what vice presidents and generals in the United States Armed Forces look like. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/y2OFL1z29r"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/y2OFL1z29r}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 President Biden (@POTUS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/POTUS/status/1369386260964532225?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 9, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 On the left is air force officer Van Ovost, then army officer Richardson, no compulsory skirts or silly little hats, and finally of course the US vice-president, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/kamala-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kamala Harris}}}\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.18pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.40pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Today so far\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s it from me today. My west coast colleague Maanvi Singh will take over the blog for the next few hours.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s where the day stands so far:\par \par \uc2 * The House will hold its final vote on the $1.9tn coronavirus relief bill tomorrow morning, majority leader \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s office said. The House will convene at 9am ET tomorrow, and Democrats are expected to quickly pass the bill in a party-line vote. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has already said he will sign the legislation as soon as it reaches his desk.\par \par \uc2 * The White House said Biden\u8217\'20\'19s name would not appear on the $1,400 stimulus checks, which will be distributed as part of the relief package . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s name appeared on the first round of stimulus checks last year, an unprecedented move that sparked intense criticism among Democrats.\par \par \uc2 * The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has released a video of the suspect who planted pipe bombs in Washington on 5 January, the day before the Capitol insurrection. The bureau is offering a $100,000 reward for information leading to the arrest of the person who placed the pipe bombs near the headquarters of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  and the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * A coalition of American environmental groups has urged the US to cut 50% of emissions by 2030, in order to address the climate crisis and inspire similar action from America\u8217\'20\'19s allies.\par \par \uc2 * Biden\u8217\'20\'19s promise of a more humane US immigration system is facing its first big test. The White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , was grilled today on the recent surge in unaccompanied migrant children trying to enter the country. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are still digging our way out of a dismantled and immoral and ineffective immigration policy that was being implemented by the last administration,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki told reporters. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to take us some time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Maanvi will have more coming up, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Queen has expressed her \u8220\'20\'1Cconcern\u8221\'20\'1D over race allegations made by the Duke and Duchess of Sussex, as well as her sadness on learning exactly how challenging the couple had found life as working royals.\par \par \uc2 Buckingham Palace finally broke its silence on Tuesday evening over {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2021/mar/09/no-10-silent-goldsmith-says-prince-harry-blowing-up-royal-family-meghan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Harry and Meghan\u8217\'20\'19s explosive claims}}}\uc2 , made in an interview with \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2 , saying the issues raised would be taken \u8220\'20\'1Cseriously\u8221\'20\'1D and dealt with \u8220\'20\'1Cprivately\u8221\'20\'1D by the royal family.\par \par \uc2 A short statement issued on behalf of the Queen read: \u8220\'20\'1CThe whole family is saddened to learn the full extent of how challenging the last few years have been for Harry and Meghan.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe issues raised, particularly that of race, are concerning. While some recollections may vary, they are taken very seriously and will be addressed by the family privately.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHarry, Meghan and Archie will always be much loved family members.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The statement followed two days of crisis talks among senior royals and palace aides over how best to handle the very public fallout from the damaging interview. Buckingham Palace had been given no advance warning before broadcast of what the couple had said.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2021/mar/09/buckingham-palace-breaks-silence-on-meghan-and-harry-oprah-claims"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Buckingham Palace breaks silence on Meghan and Harry Oprah claims}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.10pm GMT\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2  releases video of suspect planting pipe bombs in DC on 5 January\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has released a video of the suspect who planted pipe bombs near the headquarters of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  and the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  on 5 January, the day before the Capitol insurrection.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Washington field office is asking anyone with potential information about the suspect to immediately contact the bureau.\par \par \uc2 enltrTwo pipe bombs were placed near the DNC and \uc2 RNC\uc2  between 7:30 and 8:30 on the evening of Jan. 5th. The devices found were viable devices. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/mimYfgymGb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/mimYfgymGb}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 FBI Washington Field (@FBIWFO) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/FBIWFO/status/1369389651711168512?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 9, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese pipe bombs were viable devices that could have been detonated, resulting in serious injury or death. We need the public\u8217\'20\'19s help to identify the individual responsible for placing these pipe bombs to ensure they will not harm themselves or anyone else,\u8221\'20\'1D the FBI\u8217\'20\'19s assistant director in charge of the Washington field office, Steven D\u8217\'20\'19Antuono, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is offering a reward of up to $100,000 for information leading to the location, arrest and conviction of the person responsible for the pipe bombs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe still believe there is someone out there who has information they may not have realized was significant until now,\u8221\'20\'1D D\u8217\'20\'19Antuono said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe know it can be a difficult decision to report information about family or friends \u8211\'20\'13 but this is about protecting human life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.17pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Senate majority leader, Chuck Schumer, said he will \u8220\'20\'1Cimmediately\u8221\'20\'1D file cloture on Deb Haaland \u8217\'20\'19s nomination to lead the interior department.\par \par \uc2 Procedural votes on Haaland\u8217\'20\'19s nomination will then occur later this week.\par \par \uc2 Schumer\u8217\'20\'19s announcement comes hours after two Republican senators, Steve Daines and \uc2 Cynthia Lummis\uc2 , put a hold on Haaland\u8217\'20\'19s nomination because of her views on fossil fuel development.\par \par \uc2 The hold can delay Haaland\u8217\'20\'19s confirmation, but she is still expected to be confirmed, given that Republicans \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and Lisa Murkowski have already signaled they will support her nomination.\par \par \uc2 If confirmed, Haaland will be the first Native American to serve as a cabinet secretary.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/feb/23/deb-haaland-confirmation-hearing-interior-secretary"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Deb Haaland faces hostile Republican questioning in confirmation hearing}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.32pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , the Democratic majority whip, recalled that only two Democrats voted against the earlier version of the coronavirus relief bill. \u8220\'20\'1CI think we\u8217\'20\'19ll cut that at least in half,\u8221\'20\'1D he added jokingly at Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 press conference.\par \par \uc2 Though many of the provisions in the bill are temporary, the ways and means committee chairman, \uc2 Richard Neal\uc2 , a Democrat of Massachusetts, said there were plans underway to make the child tax credit permanent. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we did is unlikely to go away,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats warned that Republicans would try to take credit for the bill in their districts, even if not a single one ultimately votes for the measure.\par \par \uc2 The House budget committee chairman \uc2 John Yarmuth\uc2 , a Democrat of Kentucky, said it was \u8220\'20\'1Ccomical\u8221\'20\'1D to listen to Republican attacks on the plan. The House speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , argued that many Republicans were eager to vote for similar stimulus measures when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president and said they were only opposed now because it was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in office.\par \par \uc2 Congress has already spent $3tn in coronavirus aid, and many Republican lawmakers are wary the economy needs another nearly $2tn infusion as more Americans are vaccinated and businesses and schools begin to cautiously reopen.\par \par \uc2 Yet Pelosi did not rule out the possibility of another Covid relief bill, telling reporters that future relief legislation would be determined by the course of the virus.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Pelosi hails 'historic' relief package on eve of House vote\par \par \uc2 The House speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , hailed \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s $1.9tn stimulus plan as a \u8220\'20\'1Cremarkable, historic, transformative piece of legislation\u8221\'20\'1D on the eve of a House vote on the measure.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m so excited, I just can\u8217\'20\'19t hide it,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi said during a press conference with senior Democrats on Tuesday afternoon, who took turns extolling what they said was the \u8220\'20\'1Chistoric\u8221\'20\'1D nature of the legislation and its impact on reducing poverty in America.\par \par \uc2 Several Democratic leaders compared it to the passage of the Affordable Care Act, saying the plan would not only \u8220\'20\'1Ccrush\u8221\'20\'1D the virus and the economic fallout, but would look forward to tackle long-standing racial and gender inequalities in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Smiling under her mask, she expressed full confidence that Democrats had the votes to pass the bill.\par \par \uc2 Asked about possible defections from progressive members disappointed that the Senate narrowed a version of the bill, initially proposed by Biden and passed by the House, Pelosi shook her head and said \u8220\'20\'1Cno\u8221\'20\'1D repeatedly. The bill would head to Biden\u8217\'20\'19s desk after the vote on Wednesday, she said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.59pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Sam Levine reports:\par \par \uc2 Former president \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  criticized a raft of new restrictions in his home state of Georgia that would make it harder to cast a ballot.\par \par \uc2 The proposals, which include eliminating no-excuse absentee voting and requiring voters to provide identification information when they vote by mail, are advancing through the GOP-controlled state legislature.\par \par \uc2 They come after Georgia saw record turnout, including surges among Black and other minority voters. Many of the measures under consideration would harm Black voters the most, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/georgias-proposed-voting-restrictions-will-harm-black-voters-most"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to an analysis}}}\uc2  by the Brennan Center for Justice.\par \par \uc2 enltr"...As our state legislators seek to turn back the clock through legislation that will restrict access to voting for many Georgians, I am disheartened, saddened, and angry. Many of the proposed changes are reactions to allegations of fraud for which no evidence was produced..." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/1DyecHFl2z"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/1DyecHFl2z}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The \uc2 Carter Center\uc2  (@CarterCenter) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CarterCenter/status/1369362396775673861?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 9, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs our state legislators seek to turn back the clock through legislation that will restrict access to voting for many Georgians, I am disheartened, saddened, and angry,\u8221\'20\'1D Carter, a former Georgia governor, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMany of the proposed changes are reactions to allegations of fraud for which no evidence was produced \u8211\'20\'13 allegations that were, in fact, refuted through various audits, recounts and other measures. The proposed changes appear to be rooted in partisan interests, not in the interests of all Georgia voters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Carter also addressed a 2005 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ucdenver.instructure.com/courses/3034/files/378056?module_item_id=188418"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bipartisan report}}}\uc2  drafted by a commission he co-chaired that says \u8220\'20\'1Cabsentee ballots remain the largest source of potential voter fraud\u8221\'20\'1D. Republicans have seized on that line as a key talking point in advancing restrictions on mail-in ballots.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the 16 years since the report\u8217\'20\'19s release, vote-by-mail practices have progressed significantly as new technologies have been developed,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIn light of these advances, I believe that voting by mail can be conducted in a manner that ensures election integrity. This is just one of several ways to expand access to the voting process for voters across the state, regardless of political affiliation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAmerican democracy means every eligible person has the right to vote in an election that is fair, open and secure. It should be flexible enough to meet the electorate\u8217\'20\'19s changing needs. As Georgians, we must protect these values,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. \u8220\'20\'1CWe must not lose the progress we have made. We must not promote confidence among one segment of the electorate by restricting the participation of others. Our goal always should be to increase, not decrease, voter participation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.39pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , was asked about the lack of Republican support for the coronavirus relief bill in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Psaki said \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would \u8220\'20\'1Ccontinue to leave the door open to working with Republicans \u8221\'20\'1D as he attempts to advance his legislative agenda.\par \par \uc2 But so far, Republicans have generally shown little interest in working in a bipartisan fashion to tackle America\u8217\'20\'19s problems.\par \par \uc2 The White House press briefing has now concluded.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Facing more questions about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s immigration policies, the White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , reiterated that now is not the time to come to the United States.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are still digging our way out of a dismantled and immoral and ineffective immigration policy that was being implemented by the last administration,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to take us some time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 According to multiple reports, more than 3,000 migrant children are currently being detained by the US along the southern border, as the number of unaccompanied minors trying to enter the country has surged.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.18pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Yikes: press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  was asked whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s dog, Major, would be euthanized after he caused a minor injury for someone in the White House.\par \par \uc2 Psaki indicated he would not be. \u8220\'20\'1CMajor Biden is a member of the family,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki said.\par \par \uc2 The press secretary also would not confirm whether it was a Secret Service agent who was injured by Major, as CNN reported.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.10pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Attention all dog lovers: \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  has provided an update on the whereabouts of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s two German Shepherds, Champ and Major.\par \par \uc2 The White House press secretary confirmed details of the CNN report that Major was involved in an incident when he was surprised by an \u8220\'20\'1Cunfamiliar person\u8221\'20\'1D at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Major\u8217\'20\'19s reaction to the person resulted in a \u8220\'20\'1Cminor injury\u8221\'20\'1D, Psaki said. The injury was treated by the White House medical unit, and it required no further medical attention.\par \par \uc2 Champ and Major Biden are seen on the South Lawn of the White House on 25 January. Photograph: Jim Watson/AFP/Getty Images\par \par \uc2 Psaki said the dogs were already scheduled to return to Delaware for a few days, while Jill Biden completes her west coast trip to military bases.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe dogs will return to the White House soon,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki said.\par \par \uc2 That will be a relief for the many Americans who were thrilled that dogs had returned to the White House, given that the Trump family had no pets.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Biden's name will not appear on stimulus checks, White House says\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s name will not appear on the stimulus checks distributed as part of the $1.9tn coronavirus relief bill, said \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , White House press secretary.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe didn\u8217\'20\'19t think that was a priority or a necessary step,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki said. \u8220\'20\'1CHis focus was on getting them out as quickly as possible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s name appeared on the first round of stimulus checks last year, an unprecedented move that sparked intense criticism among Democrats.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.59pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Psaki grilled on surge of unaccompanied migrant children at the border\par \par \uc2 The White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , was grilled on the situation at the US-Mexican border, as the number of unaccompanied migrant children trying to enter the country has surged.\par \par \uc2 Psaki refused to confirm reports that more than 3,000 migrant children are being detained along the southern border.\par \par \uc2 The press secretary also would not say whether the situation at the border qualified as a \u8220\'20\'1Ccrisis\u8221\'20\'1D, insisting she did not want to put \u8220\'20\'1Clabels\u8221\'20\'1D on the issue.\par \par \uc2 The secretary of homeland security, Alejandro Mayorkas, previously said that he does not consider the border situation to be a crisis, sparking criticism among Republicans.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will meet virtually with the leaders of Japan, Australia and India on Friday, White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  confirmed at her briefing.\par \par \uc2 This will mark the first such meeting between leaders of the nations that make up \u8220\'20\'1Cthe Quad\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Biden has already held virtual meetings with Canadian prime minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  and Mexican president \uc2 Andr\u233\'00\'E9s Manuel L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Bharat Ramamurti\uc2 , the deputy director of the National Economic Council, was asked about inflation concerns in connection to the likely passage of the $1.9tn coronavirus relief bill.\par \par \uc2 Ramamurti reiterated that the Biden administration was more concerned about the possibility of doing too little rather than too much to address the economic fallout from the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The senior adviser added that the White House will \u8220\'20\'1Calways be carefully monitoring inflation\u8221\'20\'1D to make the best decisions for the US economy.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  is now holding her daily briefing, and she is joined by \uc2 Bharat Ramamurti\uc2 , the deputy director of the National Economic Council.\par \par \uc2 Ramamurti highlighted \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s visit to a local Washington business earlier today, noting that the business received a loan from the Paycheck Protection Program, which was established by the first coronavirus relief bill.\par \par \uc2 Ramamurti said the Biden administration has worked diligently to ensure that PPP loans go to the small businesses that need them most.\par \par \uc2 According to Ramamurti, the administration has already approved more than 300,000 loans for businesses with fewer than five employees.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Jury selection got under way this morning in the trial of former Minneapolis police officer Derek Chauvin, accused of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/jun/09/george-floyd-houston-funeral-tribute"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 murdering George Floyd}}}\uc2  last May.\par \par \uc2 The process {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/08/george-floyd-trial-delayed-for-decision-on-extra-charge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was delayed}}}\uc2  yesterday for legal machinations over {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/06/george-floyd-trial-derek-chauvin-third-degree-murder-charges"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the ruling}}}\uc2  by the court of appeals last Friday that the judge in the case, Peter Cahill, must reinstate an earlier charge from the case of third-degree murder, alongside second degree murder and second degree manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 Protesters march in Minneapolis to demand justice for \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  on the first day of Derek Chauvin\u8217\'20\'19s trial yesterday. Photograph: Tim Evans/NurPhoto/REX/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 The start of proceedings this morning in downtown Minneapolis illustrates why the unusually long time of three weeks has been provided to sit a jury.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press reports:\par \par \uc2 Jury selection began with the first potential juror excused after she revealed during questioning that she thought the way the officer acted was \u8220\'20\'1C not fair \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The woman, a mother of three from Mexico, said she saw bystander video showing Derek Chauvin kneeling on Floyd\u8217\'20\'19s neck, but she didn\u8217\'20\'19t understand why the officer didn\u8217\'20\'19t get up when Floyd said he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/may/26/george-floyd-killing-police-video-fbi-investigation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 couldn\u8217\'20\'19t breathe}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s not fair because we are humans, you know?\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Defense attorney Eric Nelson noted that the woman said on her questionnaire that she wanted to be on the jury \u8220\'20\'1Cto give my opinion of the unjust death of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Potential jurors must show they can set aside their opinions on the case and view the evidence fairly.\par \par \uc2 The woman said she would be willing to change her mind if she saw evidence from a different perspective, but Nelson used one of his 15 peremptory challenges to dismiss her without providing a reason.\par \par \uc2 Cahill ruled on several pretrial motions, setting parameters for trial testimony. Jurors will hear when Chauvin stopped working for the police department, but they will not be told that he was fired or that the city made a \u8220\'20\'1C substantial offer \u8221\'20\'1D to settle a lawsuit from Floyd\u8217\'20\'19s family. Those details will not be allowed because they could imply guilt, Cahill said.\par \par \uc2 The city had no immediate comment when asked about the settlement offer. A message left with an attorney for the Floyd family was not immediately returned.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s our weekend {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/06/minneapolis-george-floyd-my-home-town"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dispatch}}}\uc2  from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/amudalat-ajasa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amudalat Ajasa}}}\uc2  in Minneapolis:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/06/minneapolis-george-floyd-my-home-town"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Minneapolis on trial: how the year of George Floyd changed me and my city}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.57pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Today so far\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s where the day stands so far:\par \par \uc2 * The House will hold its final vote on the $1.9tn coronavirus relief bill tomorrow morning, House majority leader \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s office said. The Democratic-controlled House is expected to pass the bill, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has said he will sign the legislation as soon as it reaches his desk.\par \par \uc2 * A coalition of American environmental groups has urged the US to cut 50% of emissions by 2030, in order to address the climate crisis and inspire similar action from America\u8217\'20\'19s allies.\par \par \uc2 * Biden\u8217\'20\'19s promise of a more humane US immigration system is facing its first big test. Congressional Republicans have criticized the Democratic president as the number of unaccompanied minors arriving at the US-Mexican border has surged.\par \par \uc2 The blog will have more coming up, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.40pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  paid a visit to WS Jenks and Sons hardware store in Washington, DC, to highlight the work of the Paycheck Protection Program.\par \par \uc2 The PPP is a small business loan program that was approved by the first coronavirus relief bill, and the Biden administration has been working to ensure the money from the program goes to actual small businesses rather than larger companies.\par \par \uc2 enltrPresident Biden visits W.S. Jenks & Son hardware store in NE DC, which received a PPP loan {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/zL1LJvvuax"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/zL1LJvvuax}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jordan Fabian (@Jordanfabian) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Jordanfabian/status/1369339141532565504?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 9, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 WS Jenks and Son, which opened in 1866 and is the oldest hardware store in Washington, received a PPP loan.\par \par \uc2 During the visit, reporters tried to ask the president about the situation at the US-Mexican border, but Biden did not respond.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 House majority leader \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s office has confirmed to \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  that the chamber has received the coronavirus relief bill from the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The House rules committee is now holding a meeting on the legislation, paving the way for a final vote tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 Hoyer\u8217\'20\'19s office said the House will convene tomorrow at 9 am ET to take up the bill, which is expected to pass and then go to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s desk for his signature.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Senator Tom Cotton has repeatedly attacked Democrats who voted for the Covid-19 relief bill for giving money to \u8220\'20\'1Cmurderers and rapists\u8221\'20\'1D in prison, citing Dzhokhar Tsarnaev, convicted in the Boston Marathon bombing.\par \par \uc2 The Arkansas Republican has neglected to mention, however, that during \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s presidency {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/03/08/politics/fact-check-tom-cotton-prisoners-pandemic-relief-checks-trump-biden/index.html?utm_term=1615257669792c4f7b2b714bd&utm_source=cnn_Reliable+Sources+-+March+8+2021&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=1615257669793&bt_ee=rY7Ot3a5KEC6wlX78dKfL8WPMOO6%2Fs3qRp%2FYdIkhiIbuLT2l0OogcPCi%2FJJiJ4hX&bt_ts=1615257669793"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he twice voted for Covid bills}}}\uc2  that provided payments for prisoners.\par \par \uc2 Prisoners\u8217\'20\'19 advocates say payments are warranted, as many will be released into a situation where the pandemic has ravaged the US economy, leading to high unemployment and many families struggling to pay for basic necessities.\par \par \uc2 Payments also decrease the burden on prisoners\u8217\'20\'19 families, who often have to provide for them after they are released.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CProviding stimulus funds to incarcerated people helps protect the health and wellbeing of those behind bars and provides relief to their loved ones at home,\u8221\'20\'1D the Prison Policy Initiative said last year.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/09/tom-cotton-payments-prisoners-vote-under-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tom Cotton attacks relief payments to prisoners but backed them under Trump}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Progressive congresswoman \uc2 Bonnie Watson Coleman\uc2  said she will vote for the coronavirus relief bill, despite serious concerns about the changes made by the Senate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile I will continue to pressure my party to live up to its banner as the party of the people, I cannot ignore the immediate need for relief,\u8221\'20\'1D Watson Coleman said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 undltr {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/VODEIHERDU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/VODEIHERDU}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Rep. Bonnie Watson Coleman\uc2  (@RepBonnie) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RepBonnie/status/1369325300467793931?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 9, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Watson Coleman raised the possibility of opposing the bill because of the \u8220\'20\'1Coutrageous\u8221\'20\'1D changes made by the Senate, including limiting eligibility for the $1,400 direct payments and scrapping the minimum wage increase.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat are we doing here? I\u8217\'20\'19m frankly disgusted with some of my colleagues and question whether I can support this bill,\u8221\'20\'1D Watson Coleman said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The House is scheduled to hold its final vote on the bill tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 enltrThis trend is outrageous:\par \par \uc2 Eliminating $15/hr\par \par \uc2 Reducing thresholds for payments (cutting off ~400k New Jerseyans)\par \par \uc2 Cuts to weekly payments\par \par \uc2 What are we doing here? I'm frankly disgusted with some of my colleagues and question whether I can support this bill.\par \par \uc2 1/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/r9dqZpuCbU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/r9dqZpuCbU}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Rep. Bonnie Watson Coleman\uc2  (@RepBonnie)\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RepBonnie/status/1367963544008941571?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 House will vote tomorrow morning on coronavirus relief bill\par \par \uc2 Okay, now it\u8217\'20\'19s official: the House will hold its final vote on the $1.9 trillion coronavirus relief package tomorrow, not today.\par \par \uc2 House majority leader \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  said moments ago that the House will take up the bill at 9 am ET tomorrow morning, per \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 enltrHoyer this morning in his weekly pen and pad announced the potential timing of House vote on final $1.9T COVID-19 relief bill: \u8220\'20\'1COur expectation is, maybe late this afternoon we would adopt the rule...We will then tomorrow at 9am consider the American Rescue Plan and pass that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Craig Caplan (@CraigCaplan) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CraigCaplan/status/1369322533120585730?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 9, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The Senate, which passed the relief package on Saturday, sent the bill to the House this morning, and the House rules committee will soon hold a meeting on the legislation.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic-controlled House is expected to pass the bill, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has said he will sign it as soon as it gets to his desk.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is scheduled to deliver his first primetime address as president on Thursday, the White House announced yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The speech is meant to mark one year since the start of the country\u8217\'20\'19s shutdowns linked to the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe will discuss the many sacrifices the American people have made over the last year and the grave loss communities and families across the country have suffered,\u8221\'20\'1D press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Biden is also expected to tout the passage of his $1.9 trillion coronavirus relief bill, which he is expected to sign this week.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 House Democratic caucus chair \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  said he is \u8220\'20\'1C110% confident\u8221\'20\'1D that Democrats have the votes to pass the coronavirus relief bill.\par \par \uc2 When the House originally voted on its version of the relief bill, the final vote was 219 to 212, with two Democrats joining all Republicans in opposing the measure.\par \par \uc2 The final vote on the relief package is also expected to fall along party lines, as it did in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Three days after the coronavirus relief package was passed by the Senate, the bill has now arrived in the House, according to the Washington Post.\par \par \uc2 enltrPer 2 sources the American Rescue Act has completed its roughly 70 hour trip across the Capitol. Timing of Rules meeting/floor vote still TBD.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Mike DeBonis (@mikedebonis) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mikedebonis/status/1369307168420331528?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 9, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The bill will now go to the House rules committee, and the panel will then send the legislation to the floor for a final vote.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s unclear whether that final vote will occur this evening or tomorrow morning. Democratic leaders have indicated they do not want the vote to occur late at night.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has said he will sign the relief bill into law as soon as it reaches his desk.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.17pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is expected to sign the $1.9 trillion coronavirus relief bill as soon as it passes the House.\par \par \uc2 Asked yesterday about when he would sign the legislation, the president told reporters, \u8220\'20\'1CAs soon as I can get it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The package, which passed the Senate on Saturday, includes expanded unemployment benefits through early September and $1,400 direct payments for most Americans.\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said yesterday that the Biden administration expects most of the checks to be distributed by the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 House may vote on coronavirus relief bill today, Hoyer confirms\par \par \uc2 House majority leader \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  has confirmed that the chamber may vote on the coronavirus relief bill today, depending on when the Senate sends the bill.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMembers are advised that the House may consider the Senate Amendment to H.R. 1319 \u8211\'20\'13 American Rescue Plan Act of 2021 today. Further information will be provided as soon as it becomes available,\u8221\'20\'1D the Democratic leader said in a floor update to members.\par \par \uc2 House leadership signaled yesterday that the vote would be postponed until tomorrow, and that may still be the case depending on how quickly the House receives the bill.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.15pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The First Dogs will soon return to the White House, according to NBC News.\par \par \uc2 The Bidens \u8217\'20\'19 two dogs, Champ and Major, are not at the White House right now, but a spokesperson told NBC News they will return once the first lady returns from her trip to military bases on the west coast this week.\par \par \uc2 enltrA WH official tells NBC News, \u8220\'20\'1CYes, they will be back\u8221\'20\'1D referring to the First Couple\u8217\'20\'19s dogs Champ and Major on living at the White House.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith the First Lady traveling for three days, Champ and Major went to Delaware to stay with family friends.\u8221\'20\'1D(??WH) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/eLLhR8ipEZ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/eLLhR8ipEZ}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kelly O'Donnell (@KellyO) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/KellyO/status/1369286655144828930?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 9, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 CNN {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/03/08/politics/president-joe-biden-white-house-dogs/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  last night that the dogs had been sent back to Delaware after one of the dogs, Major, was involved in a \u8220\'20\'1Cbiting incident\u8221\'20\'1D at the White House.\par \par \uc2 The dogs\u8217\'20\'19 arrival at the White House in January was met with much excitement in the US, given that the Trumps did not have any pets.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 This is Joan Greve in Washington, taking over for Martin Belam.\par \par \uc2 The House may still vote on the $1.9 trillion coronavirus relief bill today, after Democratic leaders indicated yesterday that the vote would probably be postponed until Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 According to Politico, the House rules committee may have time to meet today and send the bill to the floor for a vote, assuming the Senate sends it over in time.\par \par \uc2 enltrIf House receives coronavirus relief bill in time for Rules to meet this afternoon and prep it, they\u8217\'20\'19ll vote this evening.\par \par \uc2 If not, vote tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Heather Caygle (@heatherscope) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/heatherscope/status/1369291767670050816?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 9, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 If the Senate does not send it over soon, then the full House vote will likely will be pushed until tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 The Senate passed the relief bill on Saturday, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said yesterday that he would sign the bill \u8220\'20\'1Cas soon as I can get it\u8221\'20\'1D. The president is scheduled to deliver a primetime address on Thursday, and he will likely use the speech to tout the passage of the relief bill.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.45pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Roughly 4 in 10 Americans say they\u8217\'20\'19re still feeling the financial impact of the loss of a job or income within their household as the economic recovery remains uneven one year into the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 A new poll by the Associated Press-NORC Center for Public Affairs Research provides further evidence that the pandemic has been devastating for some Americans, while leaving others virtually unscathed or even in better shape, at least when it comes to their finances. The outcome often depended on the type of job a person had and their income level before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Ken Sweet and Emily Swanson wrute that the pandemic has particularly hurt Black and Latino households, as well as younger Americans, some of whom are now going through the second major economic crisis of their adult lives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI just felt like we were already in a harder position, so (the pandemic) kind of threw us even more under the dirt,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kennard Taylor, a 20-year-old Black college student at Jackson College. Taylor lost his job as a server in the campus cafeteria in the first weeks of the pandemic and struggled to make rent and car payments while continuing his studies. He had to move back in with his family.\par \par \uc2 The poll shows that about half of Americans say they have experienced at least one form of household income loss during the pandemic, including 25% who have experienced a household layoff and 31% who say someone in the household was scheduled for fewer hours. Overall, 44% said their household experienced income loss from the pandemic that is still having an impact on their finances.\par \par \uc2 The poll results are consistent with recent economic data. Roughly 745,000 Americans filed for unemployment benefits the week of 22 February, according to the Labor Department, and roughly 18 million Americans remain on the unemployment rolls.\par \par \uc2 Thirty percent of Americans say their current household income is lower than it was when the pandemic began, while 16% say it is higher and 53% say there\u8217\'20\'19s been no change. About half of those who experienced any form of household income loss during the pandemic say their current household income is lower than it was.\par \par \uc2 The poll\u8217\'20\'19s findings reflect what some economists have called a \u8220\'20\'1CK-shaped recovery,\u8221\'20\'1D where there have been divergent fortunes among Americans. Those with office jobs were able to transition to working from home while those who worked in hard-hit industries such as entertainment, dining, travel and other industries have continued to struggle. The poor have struggled to recover financially compared to the wealthy and Black and Latino households have not bounced back as well as their white counterparts.\par \par \uc2 About 1 in 10 Americans say they couldn\u8217\'20\'19t make a housing payment in the last month because of the pandemic, and roughly as many say that of a credit card bill. Overall, about a quarter of Americans say they\u8217\'20\'19ve been unable to pay one or more bills in the last month.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Martin Pengelly here with one of those stories that gives this live blogger sleepless nights\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Wall Street Journal editors working from home in Connecticut fell under suspicion on Monday, when the paper published an unusual correction.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe stray word \u8216\'20\'18Yay!\u8217\'20\'19 was inadvertently inserted,\u8221\'20\'1D it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/corrections-amplifications-11615165446"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Cduring editing of an article on Friday about Connecticut\u8217\'20\'19s Covid-19 restrictions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/connecticut-rolls-back-covid-19-business-restrictions-11614896738"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The piece in question}}}\uc2  was printed in Friday\u8217\'20\'19s paper. It reported a state decision to lift all capacity limits on offices, shops and restaurants from 19 March, though a mask mandate will stay in place.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not Texas,\u8221\'20\'1D the paper quoted Democratic governor \uc2 Ned Lamont\uc2  telling reporters. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not Mississippi. We are maintaining the masks. We know what works, and masks work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It was not clear from the online correction if the mystery man or woman who inserted the stray \u8220\'20\'1CYay!\u8221\'20\'1D was delighted by news of restrictions lifting, or by Lamont\u8217\'20\'19s expression of faith in face masks, or by the simple fact of Connecticut not being Mississippi or Texas.\par \par \uc2 Online images of the Journal\u8217\'20\'19s print edition, however, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://ereader.wsj.net/?editionStart=The+Wall+Street+Journal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 revealed}}}\uc2  the \u8220\'20\'1CYay!\u8221\'20\'1D to have been inserted after Lamont\u8217\'20\'19s statement that \u8220\'20\'1Cmasks work\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.32pm GMT\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s plenty of opposition to that coronavirus relief plan. Senate Republican leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has argued the package is missing the moment \u8212\'20\'14 too big at a time when the virus is showing signs of easing and the economy is poised to come \u8220\'20\'1Croaring\u8221\'20\'1D back. The Senate spent months under McConnell\u8217\'20\'19s own majority leadership without advancing any further Covid rescue plan.\par \par \uc2 McConnell, who indulged former president Trump\u8217\'20\'19s fanciful attempts to overturn November\u8217\'20\'19s election result, says that instead of working across the aisle toward unity, as Biden has promised, Democrats are \u8220\'20\'1Cramming through what they call \u8216\'20\'18the most progressive domestic legislation in a generation,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D quoting the White House chief of staff.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey explained their intent very clearly: to exploit this crisis as \u8216\'20\'18a tremendous opportunity to restructure things to fit our vision,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D McConnell says. This is the first Covid-19 bill that had zero support from Republicans in the House or Senate, Boak and Mascaro of the AP remind us.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are poised to portray the spending as bloated and inefficient, much the way they attacked the \uc2 Obama\uc2 -era recovery act during the 2009 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, much of the aid is temporary, set to expire in a year or so, leaving Congress to assess Biden\u8217\'20\'19s approach ahead of the next election season.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The phrase \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m from the government and I\u8217\'20\'19m here to help\u8221\'20\'1D became a well-worn punchline in the Reagan era. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is attempting to turn that image around four decades later, and stake his presidency on the idea that the government can use his $1.9 trillion coronavirus relief plan not only to stop a pandemic and jobs crisis but also to catapult the country forward to tackle deep issues of poverty, inequality and more.\par \par \uc2 Josh Boak and Lisa Mascaro write for the Associated Press that the provisions in the 628-page bill add up to one of the largest enhancements to the social safety net in decades, pushing the US into uncharted territory, although many of the measures would be familiar and indeed seem commonplace to European governments.\par \par \uc2 Besides stopping the pandemic and jumpstarting hiring, money in the rescue package \u8212\'20\'14 now awaiting final approval in the House \u8212\'20\'14 is supposed to start fixing income inequality, halve child poverty, feed the hungry, save pensions, sustain public transit, let schools reopen with confidence and help repair state and local government finances. And Biden is betting that the government can do all of this with the speed of a nation mobilizing for war without touching a tripwire of inflation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople have lost faith government can do good for them,\u8221\'20\'1D says Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer, who spoke daily with Biden while ushering the bill through the Senate last weekend. Now, as vaccines become more available and other changes take place, \u8220\'20\'1Cpeople are going to see that government actually is making their lives better \u8212\'20\'14 which is how Americans used to think of it, and we\u8217\'20\'19ve gotten away from it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sweeping in scope, Biden\u8217\'20\'19s plan largely relies on existing health care and tax credits, rather than new programs, but it expands that standard fare in ambitious new ways that are designed to reach more people who are suffering in an unprecedented time.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe haven\u8217\'20\'19t done this before,\u8221\'20\'1D said Syracuse University economics professor Len Burman, a co-founder of the Tax Policy Center. \u8220\'20\'1CIf it actually does work the way it does in theory and the economy is back at full employment in a year, that would be amazing. It would save a lot of hardship and suffering.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Final passage of the bill is expected this week \u8212\'20\'14 before expanded unemployment benefits are set to expire mid-March. But Biden\u8217\'20\'19s signing celebration will just be the start. His administration will have to show that the funds can be spent effectively and efficiently, helping those in need while giving the broader public enough confidence to awaken growth through hiring and spending.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Melanie Zanona at Politico this morning is running the rule over some of the eager young Trumpist Republicans in the House who might be eyeing up the Senate seats that are about to become vacant in the next election cycle. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2021/03/09/trump-allies-congress-474509"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 She writes}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 At least half a dozen of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s staunchest allies in the House are exploring bids for higher office, eager to carry the Trump mantle into the Senate \u8212\'20\'14 as well as into governors\u8217\'20\'19 mansions. A wave of retirements by veteran Senate Republicans has created fresh opportunities for the House\u8217\'20\'19s hard-liners in deep red states such as Alabama, Ohio and Missouri. But even in states won by President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , such as Arizona and Georgia, some of the former president\u8217\'20\'19s most loyal devotees are willing to test their political fortunes, hoping to seize on a deep but baseless belief on the right that the election was stolen.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s pretty clear that our more liberal, establishment brethren in the Senate have not been faring well,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rep Mo Brooks, who is considering a bid for the upper-chamber seat of retiring Republican \uc2 Richard Shelby\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThose were the only ones that lost in 2020. And our conservatives won. So that\u8217\'20\'19s a pretty good sign as to what the American electorate prefers.\par \par \uc2 Among the Republicans considering a Senate run are Brooks, who spearheaded the effort to challenge the election results while Shelby voted to certify Biden\u8217\'20\'19s win; Rep Andy Biggs of Arizona, who chairs the ultra-conservative House Freedom Caucus and hails from a state where the legislature amplified Trump\u8217\'20\'19s false voter fraud claims; and Rep Warren Davidson of Ohio, a hardliner who replaced former Speaker \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  in Congress.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Trump policy and platform is the direction of the party,\u8221\'20\'1D Biggs said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo I think people that have embraced the America First policy. They really have a good shot at winning their constituencies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2021/03/09/trump-allies-congress-474509"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Politico \u8211\'20\'13 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s House GOP fans don his mantle as they seek higher office}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US urged to cut 50% of emissions by 2030 to spur other countries to action\par \par \uc2 The US needs to commit to slashing its planet-heating emissions by at least half by the end of the decade to address the climate crisis and spur other countries to greater action, a coalition of American environmental groups has urged.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\u8217\'20\'19s administration is set to unveil a new national emissions reduction target at a climate meeting it has convened with other major economic powers on Earth Day, 22 April, which it hopes will galvanize countries that are currently dangerously lagging in efforts to stave off disastrous climate change.\par \par \uc2 A motley selection of environmental groups and leaders have said the US goal must be no lower than a 50% cut in its greenhouse gas emissions by 2030, based on 2005 levels. This will, the groups argue, put America on track to meet Biden\u8217\'20\'19s aspiration of net zero emissions by 2050, as well as provide a major push to countries and businesses that were bereft of American climate leadership during \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s presidency.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe target has to be ambitious enough to show US leadership, but also credible, it can\u8217\'20\'19t just be plucked from thin air,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Nat Keohane\uc2 , vice-president for international climate at the Environmental Defense Fund (EDF). \u8220\'20\'1CThis is ambitious but also feasible. We need to show the US is bringing everything it can to this fight.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.edf.org/sites/default/files/documents/Recapturing%20U.S.%20Leadership%20on%20Climate.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new EDF report}}}\uc2  calls for a \u8220\'20\'1Cwhole of government effort\u8221\'20\'1D to combat the climate crisis, with all cars sold in the US to be zero emissions from 2035, a clean electricity standard to shift the grid to renewable energy sources such as solar and wind, and new regulations to restrict methane emissions from oil and gas drilling.\par \par \uc2 Other environmental groups, including the \uc2 Union of Concerned Scientists\uc2 , \uc2 World Resources Institute\uc2  and National Resources Defense Council, have also rallied to the idea of a 50% cut, along with figures such as \uc2 Jay Inslee\uc2 , the governor of Washington, and \uc2 Michael Bloomberg\uc2 , former mayor of New York City, as crucial to curb ever-worsening wildfires, floods and heatwaves that are suffered disproportionately by underserved Americans of color.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe see this important opportunity to bolster equity and fairness,\u8221\'20\'1D said Starla Yeh, a clean energy policy specialist at the Natural Resources Defense Council. \u8220\'20\'1CThe goal is not only achievable but cost effective. The more progress we make this decade, the better off we will be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read more of Oliver Milman\u8217\'20\'19s report here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/09/us-climate-crisis-cut-emissions-environment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US urged to cut 50% of emissions by 2030 to spur other countries to action}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The publisher of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://viewer.gutools.co.uk/us-news/2020/oct/17/american-crisis-review-andrew-cuomo-new-york-covid-trump-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 American Crisis}}}\uc2 , \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s book about the coronavirus pandemic, will cease promotional efforts as the governor struggles under a New York crisis of his own making.\par \par \uc2 Cuomo has been accused of sexual harassment by multiple women, of workplace bullying by multiple former aides, and of conspiring to cover-up Covid-19 deaths in nursing homes as the pandemic hit his state last year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 New York Times{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/08/nyregion/cuomo-book.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  Crown\u8217\'20\'19s decision regarding Cuomo\u8217\'20\'19s book, a spokesperson telling the paper there were \u8220\'20\'1Cno plans\u8221\'20\'1D for a reprint, citing \u8220\'20\'1Cthe ongoing investigation into [New York state] reporting of Covid-related fatalities in nursing homes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Cuomo did not immediately comment. He has defended his handling of the nursing homes issue and apologised for behaviour described by women who claim harassment. But he has refused to resign, despite calls from top state Democrats. On Monday, Republicans in the state assembly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nypost.com/2021/03/08/assembly-republicans-to-launch-impeachment-effort-against-gov-cuomo/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 began}}}\uc2  an attempt to impeach him.\par \par \uc2 American Crisis was published in October. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://viewer.gutools.co.uk/us-news/2020/oct/17/american-crisis-review-andrew-cuomo-new-york-covid-trump-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In its review}}}\uc2 , \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  pointed to how it came to be written.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEarly in the pandemic,\u8221\'20\'1D Lloyd Green wrote, \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s daily briefings emerged as must-see television, counter-programming to the campaign commercials that masqueraded as presidential press conferences [by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ]. The New York governor was forthright and reassuring, even as the body count mounted.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But Green also wrote that Cuomo \u8220\'20\'1Cconfronts criticism of his order directing that older Covid patients be sent to nursing homes rather than hospitals, which may have contributed to New York\u8217\'20\'19s high death toll. Although he pushes back, such claims are not going away.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ag.ny.gov/press-release/2021/attorney-general-james-releases-report-nursing-homes-response-covid-19"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 emerged}}}\uc2  that in summer 2020, when Cuomo signed to write his book, the state under-reported deaths in nursing homes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ag.ny.gov/press-release/2021/attorney-general-james-releases-report-nursing-homes-response-covid-19"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 by as much as half}}}\uc2 , 9,250 deaths of residents being {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://viewer.gutools.co.uk/us-news/2021/mar/05/andrew-cuomo-nursing-home-deaths-aides-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 written out}}}\uc2  of the record. Federal prosecutors are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/02/17/nyregion/cuomo-nursing-homes-deaths.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly}}}\uc2  looking into the matter.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 You\u8217\'20\'19ll find plenty of commentary about a post-Covid \u8220\'20\'1Cnew normal\u8221\'20\'1D and the latest output from the Axios-Ipsos coronavirus index poll, which has been running since 13 March last year, has a clue as to how that might look in the US \u8211\'20\'13 and it still involves face masks. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/axios-ipsos-poll-one-year-of-covid-1dd5b7ac-2f84-42c1-825d-b4b94db6dd69.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 David Nather writes}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 The poll found that more Americans are expecting the outbreak to be over sooner rather than later, as vaccinations ramp up throughout the country \u8212\'20\'14 but that very few are ready to end the precautions that have upended their lives.\par \par \uc2 The vast majority now know someone who has gotten the virus \u8212\'20\'14 and one in three knows someone who has died from it.\par \par \uc2 Just 7% of respondents said they plan to stop wearing face masks in public after they\u8217\'20\'19ve been vaccinated, and only 13% said they plan to stop social distancing.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, 81% said they\u8217\'20\'19ll keep wearing face masks, and 66% said they\u8217\'20\'19ll keep social distancing, until the pandemic ends \u8212\'20\'14 even after they\u8217\'20\'19ve gotten the shot.\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/axios-ipsos-poll-one-year-of-covid-1dd5b7ac-2f84-42c1-825d-b4b94db6dd69.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Axios \u8211\'20\'13 America looks for the exits after a year of Covid}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.44am GMT\par \par \uc2 Gov Newsom set to address California in State of the State speech while facing recall battle\par \par \uc2 Democratic Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  is under pressure to resign in New York over allegations of sexual harassment and a scandal involving Covid deaths in nursing homes. At the other end of the country, another Democratic Governor, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , is also potentially looking at an exit from power.\par \par \uc2 Newsom is set to give perhaps the most important speech of his political life today \u8211\'20\'13 an upbeat address aimed at nearly 40 million people exhausted by a year of coronavirus restrictions in California\u8217\'20\'19s State of the State.\par \par \uc2 Kathleen Ronayne reports from Sacramento for the Associated Press that Newsom\u8217\'20\'19s popularity has fallen significantly after reaching record highs at the start of the pandemic and he\u8217\'20\'19s likely to face a recall election later this year driven by critics of his stewardship during the crisis.\par \par \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  last week visiting a California vaccination center. Photograph: David Middlecamp/AP\par \par \uc2 Dr. Mindy Romero, founder and director of the Center for Inclusive Democracy at the University of Southern California, said the speech is an opportunity and a test. Newsom needs to assert his focus on vaccination equity, reassure the general population that California is on the right track and back up his claims that his pandemic restrictions were the right choice.\par \par \uc2 And the often wonky-sounding governor must speak plainly, Romero said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe has to really make the case that he has positively changed the tide in California, that he really has protected Californians,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The speech traditionally is delivered in the Capitol to a joint session of the Legislature. This time, Newsom will speak from Dodger Stadium, which has served as a mass testing and vaccination center. It comes a year after Newsom enacted the nation\u8217\'20\'19s first statewide stay-at-home order.\par \par \uc2 The anti-Newsom effort is driven by Republicans hoping to regain a political foothold in California by tapping into widespread frustration over Covid-driven business restrictions and school closures. Organizers say they have more than the 1.5 million signatures needed to force an election, likely late in the year.\par \par \uc2 Newsom is coming off a strong week that saw him outline a speedier reopening plan tied to vaccinating 2 million of California\u8217\'20\'19s most vulnerable residents, sign a law aimed at getting the youngest kids back in classrooms by 1 April, and announce that outdoor sports stadiums, theme parks and concert venues can reopen with limited attendance next month.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 Lisa Monaco and Vanita Gupta to face Senate confirmation hearings for Justice Department jobs\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s nominees for the No. 2 and No. 3 Justice Department jobs face the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  today, and will likely be questioned on topics from civil rights to protecting the United States from domestic extremist attacks.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Monaco, a former prosecutor who also served as homeland security and counterterrorism adviser to former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , is Biden\u8217\'20\'19s pick for deputy attorney general - a sweeping role that entails overseeing the department\u8217\'20\'19s criminal and national security matters as well as its 93 US attorneys.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Monaco was introduced as a Biden pick in Delaware in January. Photograph: REX/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 Vanita Gupta is nominated as associate attorney general, a job that oversees the department\u8217\'20\'19s civil and civil rights divisions, as well as antitrust, environmental, grant-making and community policing matters.\par \par \uc2 Vanita Gupta speaks during a House Judiciary Committee hearing last year. Photograph: Reuters\par \par \uc2 Sarah Lynch reports for Reuters that both women have garnered bipartisan support for their nominations, though Gupta has drawn some opposition from conservative groups over her progressive views on criminal justice reform and is expected to face a tougher confirmation battle than Monaco. That will follow a pattern where nominees of color have faced a tougher path to confirmation this year than their white colleagues.\par \par \uc2 The committee previously approved Biden\u8217\'20\'19s attorney general nominee, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , in a bipartisan 15-7 vote, and the Senate is expected to confirm him to the post as soon as tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 If confirmed as deputy attorney general, Monaco would help oversee the department\u8217\'20\'19s sprawling investigation into the deadly attack on the US Capitol by a mob of former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s supporters.\par \par \uc2 In her prepared opening statement released by the Judiciary Committee, Monaco said the Justice Department was at an \u8220\'20\'1Cinflection point... as we battle violent extremism - foreign and domestic - and mounting cyber threats from nation states and criminals alike.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gupta previously served as acting assistant attorney general of the Civil Rights Division during the Obama administration, where she oversaw high-profile investigations into systemic abuses by police departments in Baltimore and Ferguson, Missouri.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf confirmed, I will aggressively ensure that the Justice Department is independent from partisan influence,\u8221\'20\'1D Gupta said in her prepared opening remarks.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.11am GMT\par \par \uc2 Emily Holden and Cooper McKim have this for us this morning, asking: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/09/republican-study-wyoming-biden-climate-plans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 new favorite study trashes Biden\u8217\'20\'19s climate plans \u8211\'20\'13 but who\u8217\'20\'19s behind it}}}\uc2  ?\par \par \uc2 Wyoming\u8217\'20\'19s US representative, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , envisions a dark future for her home state under {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/joebiden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joe Biden}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If the new administration extends its pause on new oil and gas drilling on public land, it would endanger Wyoming\u8217\'20\'19s economy, kill 18,000 jobs and cause the energy state to lose out on critical education, infrastructure and healthcare funding. Biden would be \u8220\'20\'1Ccutting off a major lifeline that Americans have relied on to survive during this time\u8221\'20\'1D, she has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cheney.house.gov/2021/01/27/cheney-biden-executive-orders-endanger-wyomings-economy-and-our-national-security/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But there is a problem with Cheney\u8217\'20\'19s forecast. The numbers she is relying on came from an analysis that is the brainchild of the oil and gas industry.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Western Energy Alliance\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which represents 200 western oil and gas companies \u8211\'20\'13 proposed the $114,000 publicly funded {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wyoenergy.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/12/Final-Report-Federal-Leasing-Drilling-Ban-Policies-121420.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 analysis}}}\uc2  to state officials, tried to provide matching dollars for it and stayed involved throughout its development, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://beta.documentcloud.org/documents/20499462-wy-study-documents-for-publication#document/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 public records}}}\uc2  obtained by Documented and shared with Floodlight and Wyoming Public Media.\par \par \uc2 In February 2020, a Wyoming state senator, who is also the president of an oil company, proposed the spending. The \uc2 Western Energy Alliance\uc2  sought to help fund the study but was unable because the industry was in serious {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2020/apr/20/over-a-barrel-how-oil-prices-dropped-below-zero#:~:text=US%20oil%20prices%20turned%20negative,the%20fastest%20plunge%20in%20history."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decline}}}\uc2 . It did, however, spend $8,000 publicizing the report, as was first reported by Politico.\par \par \uc2 Records show Governor Mark Gordon \u8217\'20\'19s office was aware of and never disclosed the group\u8217\'20\'19s deep involvement in the study.\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 Western Energy Alliance\uc2  is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.eenews.net/energywire/stories/1063725719/search?keyword=western+energy+"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  spending thousands more}}}\uc2  to amplify the warnings in an ad campaign against Biden\u8217\'20\'19s climate policies. The numbers have been cited dozens of times in local and national newspapers, including in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/03/climate/wyoming-coal-country-wind-farm.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times}}}\uc2  in a reference to Wyoming officials\u8217\'20\'19 projections.\par \par \uc2 While industry-funded research is not uncommon, transparency advocates say it is increasingly being used to produce conclusions favorable to oil and gas companies in order to shape public opinion.\par \par \uc2 Read more of here Emily Holden and Cooper McKim\u8217\'20\'19s report here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/09/republican-study-wyoming-biden-climate-plans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 new favorite study trashes Biden\u8217\'20\'19s climate plans \u8211\'20\'13 but who\u8217\'20\'19s behind it?}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/09/republican-study-wyoming-biden-climate-plans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans new favorite study trashes Biden's climate plans \u8211\'20\'13 but who's behind it?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.10am GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  yesterday pledged to combat sexual assault in the US military as he announced the nomination of two female officers, Gen Jacqueline Van Ovost and Lt Gen Laura Richardson, to become four-star commanders.\par \par \uc2 The president, who spoke on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/international-womens-day"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 International Women\u8217\'20\'19s Day}}}\uc2 , said: \u8220\'20\'1CSexual assault is abhorrent and wrong at any time. And in our military, so much of unit cohesion is built on trusting your fellow service members to have your back \u8211\'20\'13 there\u8217\'20\'19s nothing less than a threat to our national security.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.59am GMT\par \par \uc2 The funding war within the Republican party intensified last night, with a statement from twice-impeached former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  asking supporters to fund him directly rather than the party. Justine Coleman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/542244-trump-vows-no-more-money-for-rinos-while-encouraging-donations-to-his-pac"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reports}}}\uc2  for the Hill:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo more money for RINOS,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump said in a statement, referring to \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans in name only.\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CThey do nothing but hurt the Republican Party and our great voting base\u8212\'20\'14they will never lead us to Greatness.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSend your donation to Save America PAC at DonaldJTrump.com,\u8221\'20\'1D the statement continued. \u8220\'20\'1CWe will bring it all back stronger than ever before!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The statement came days after Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers sent a cease-and-desist letter to the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  (\uc2 RNC\uc2 ), the National Republican Senatorial Committee and the National Republican Congressional Committee to stop using his name and likeness for fundraising and merchandise sales.\par \par \uc2 RNC chief counsel Justin Riemer wrote in a letter that the party \u8220\'20\'1Chas every right to refer to public figures as it engages in core, First Amendment-protected political speech, and it will continue to do so in pursuit of these common goals.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/542244-trump-vows-no-more-money-for-rinos-while-encouraging-donations-to-his-pac"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Hill \u8211\'20\'13 Trump vows \u8216\'20\'18No more money for RINOS,\u8217\'20\'19 instead encouraging donations to his PAC}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 NBC News have carried {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/news/investigations/record-number-unaccompanied-migrant-children-held-facilities-meant-adults-n1260097"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 some extra detail}}}\uc2  about the conditions in which the children are being kept, and it is not a ringing endorsement of a new dawn for immigration under Joe Biden\u8217\'20\'19s administration:\par \par \uc2 Nearly half of the children \u8212\'20\'14 1,400 \u8212\'20\'14 have been held beyond the three-day legal limit. The CBP holding cells, sometimes known as \u8220\'20\'1Chieleras,\u8221\'20\'1D or iceboxes, are not designed for children. They are typically small concrete rooms with concrete or metal benches and no beds. In addition, nearly 170 of the detained unaccompanied children are younger than 13, a source said.\par \par \uc2 The arrival of the childen in the US is in part because of the undoing of Trump-era immigration policies:\par \par \uc2 Many of the children arriving at the border initially immigrated during the Trump administration but were expelled under an order from the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  meant to protect immigrants and U.S. residents from Covid-19. The Biden administration reversed the policy for unaccompanied children.\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/news/investigations/record-number-unaccompanied-migrant-children-held-facilities-meant-adults-n1260097"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NBC News \u8211\'20\'13 Record number of unaccompanied migrant children held in facilities meant for adults}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.26am GMT\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of focus in the US media this morning on the challenge the Biden administration is facing on the country\u8217\'20\'19s southern border, with numbers of migrants increasing as the new president sets out on his stated aim of building a more humane immigration system. Zolan Kanno-Youngs and Michael Shear {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/08/us/politics/immigration-mexico-border-biden.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report for the New York Times}}}\uc2  in particular on a surge of unaccompanied children:\par \par \uc2 The number of migrant children in custody along the border has tripled in the past two weeks to more than 3,250, according to federal immigration agency documents obtained by The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , and many of them are being held in jail-like facilities for longer than the three days allowed by law.\par \par \uc2 The problem for the administration is both the number of children crossing the border and what to do with them once they are in custody. Under the law, the children are supposed to be moved to shelters run by the \uc2 Health and Human Services Department\uc2 , but because of the pandemic the shelters until last week were limiting how many children they could accommodate.\par \par \uc2 The growing number of unaccompanied children is just one element of an escalating problem at the border. Border agents encountered a migrant at the border about 78,000 times in January \u8212\'20\'14 more than double the rate at the same time a year ago and higher than in any January in a decade.\par \par \uc2 Immigration authorities are expected to announce this week that there were close to 100,000 apprehensions, including encounters at port entries, in February, according to people familiar with the agency\u8217\'20\'19s latest data. An additional 19,000 migrants, including adults and children, have been caught by border agents since March 1.\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/08/us/politics/immigration-mexico-border-biden.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times \u8211\'20\'13 Biden faces challenge from surge of migrants at the border}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s live coverage of US politics. Here\u8217\'20\'19s a catch-up on what happened yesterday, and what we might expect to see today\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 * President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s promise of a more humane US immigration system is facing its first big test. More than 3,250 children are now detained at the border \u8211\'20\'13 some in facilities only intended for adults. The number has tripled in just two weeks\par \par \uc2 * Biden will deliver a primetime address on Thursday to mark one year since the start of coronavirus lockdowns, the White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , announced. The speech will be Biden\u8217\'20\'19s first primetime address since becoming president.\par \par \uc2 * There were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/ng-interactive/2020/sep/21/coronavirus-covid-19-map-us-cases-deaths-state-by-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 50,237 new Covid cases yesterday}}}\uc2 , taking the total US caseload to over 29 million since the pandemic began. There were 719 further deaths, bring the total death toll to 525,381.\par \par \uc2 * The CDC issued new guidance that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/08/covid-guidelines-new-rules-vaccine-cdc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fully vaccinated Americans can gather indoors without masks}}}\uc2 . So far more than 31.3 million people have had both shots.\par \par \uc2 * Biden said he would sign the $1.9tn coronavirus relief bill \u8220\'20\'1Cas soon as I can get it\u8221\'20\'1D. The relief bill passed the Senate on Saturday, and the House is now expected to take up the legislation tomorrow. There\u8217\'20\'19s a deadline of 14 March, when some existing benefits stop unless they are renewed.\par \par \uc2 * The president {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/08/biden-us-white-house-us-military"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pledged to tackle what he called the \u8220\'20\'1Cscourge\u8221\'20\'1D of sexual assault in the US military}}}\uc2  while also ordering a review of rules from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s administration dealing with such crimes on college campuses.\par \par \uc2 * The judge overseeing the trial of a former Minneapolis police officer accused of murdering George Floyd{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/08/george-floyd-trial-delayed-for-decision-on-extra-charge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 delayed the start of jury selection for at least a day}}}\uc2  while an appeal proceeds over the possible reinstatement of an additional charge.\par \par \uc2 * Georgia Democrats sharply criticized the state\u8217\'20\'19s Republicans for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/08/georgia-senate-bill-voting-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 advancing restrictions on voting rights}}}\uc2 , which activists have said will disproportionately impact Black voters. The state senate narrowly approved a bill yesterday that would roll back a policy that made absentee voting easier.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is offering us a slight mystery tour today, as the location of his planned visit to a small business that has benefited from a Paycheck Protection Program loan is down as \u8220\'20\'1CTo be determined\u8221\'20\'1D in his schedule \u8211\'20\'13 though it is anticipated that he is staying in Washington DC for the trip. That\u8217\'20\'19s at 11.45am.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  hosts her regular press briefing at 1.30pm today, and she\u8217\'20\'19s accompanied by deputy director of the National Economic Council, \uc2 Bharat Ramamurti\par \par \uc2 2021-03-09T10:20:04Z 2021-03-10T02:21:56Z {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://media.guim.co.uk/652e52b07d944cf227915ba4a1aead719c533504/0_0_5900_3541/500.jpg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://media.guim.co.uk/652e52b07d944cf227915ba4a1aead719c533504/0_0_5900_3541/500.jpg}}}\uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  speaks during a news conference with House Democrats on 9 March. Champ and Major Biden are seen on the South Lawn of the White House on 25 January. Protesters march in Minneapolis to demand justice for \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  on the first day of Derek Chauvin\u8217\'20\'19s trial yesterday. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  last week visiting a California vaccination center. Lisa Monaco was introduced as a Biden pick in Delaware in January. Vanita Gupta speaks during a House Judiciary Committee hearing last year.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210309eh39001gw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
