\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Senate's debate on $1.9tn Covid relief hits delays over unemployment benefits \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Gabrielle Canon (now), Joan E Greve and Martin Belam (earlier) \par \uc2 12,703 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 March 2021\par 01:19 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  sues Trump over Capitol attackCDC director urges continued mask usageSanders issues urgent call to Senate to raise minimum wagePoll: Biden has 60% approval ratingUS gains 379,000 jobs as more states reopenSign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.14am GMT\par \par \uc2 Today so far\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s it for me tonight! Thanks for reading along.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what happened over the last few hours:\par \par \uc2 * The largest monthly number of migrants in roughly 15 years was detained at the US-Mexico border in February, as the Biden administration prepares to undo parts of his predecessor\u8217\'20\'19s immigration policies.\par \par \uc2 * Former state department aide, Federico Klein, was arrested and charged for his alleged participation in the 6 January pro-Trump attack on the US Capitol.\par \par \uc2 * Herd immunity from Covid might be reached as early as this summer, if current trends continue. But health officials warn against going back to business as usual too quickly.\par \par \uc2 * After a year of the Covid crisis, surveys show a staggering 40% of Americans are struggling financially.\par \par \uc2 Have a good night!\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.19am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.12am GMT\par \par \uc2 Senate deal expected soon\par \par \uc2 As the Senate showdown continues into the night, CNN reports that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/politics/live-news/stimulus-update-us-relief-bill-03-05-21/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a deal may be coming soon}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator Joe Manchin has reportedly accepted a final agreement on an amendment that will offer an additional $300 a week in unemployment benefits through 6 September.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s longer than the House\u8217\'20\'19s version, which extended the extra funds into the end of August.\par \par \uc2 CNN reports:\par \par \uc2 This agreement also provides tax relief to workers who received unemployment insurance compensation by making the first $10,200 of unemployment benefits non-taxable for the first time to prevent surprise bills for the unemployed at end of year, which was not in the House-passed legislation. This provision applies only to households with incomes under $150k.\par \par \uc2 enltrHere's how UI unfolded -->\par \par \uc2 1/ Biden: $400/week through Sept\par \par \uc2 2/ House Ds: $400/week through August\par \par \uc2 3/ Senate Deal 1 (early today): $300/week through Sept. + up to $10K in tax forgiveness\par \par \uc2 4/ Senate Deal 2 w/ Manchin (just now): $300/week thru Sept. 6, + ~$10K tax forgiveness\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jeff Stein (@JStein_WaPo) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JStein_WaPo/status/1368004499214852098?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 6, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.33am GMT\par \par \uc2 Senators are still stuck on relief bill\par \par \uc2 The senate remains at a standstill as Democratic lawmakers race to pass the $1.9tn Covid relief bill without any Republican support.\par \par \uc2 Joe Manchin, the West Virginia senator, who signaled that he might side with Republicans on an amendment for unemployment benefits, has refused to comment on how he will vote. CNN reports that he\u8217\'20\'19s left the Senate floor and is now inside the Senate majority leader Chuck Schumer\u8217\'20\'19s office.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe Manchin surprise:\par \par \uc2 - Dems fear he'll back the GOP plan to pare back jobless benefits\par \par \uc2 - GOP working to line up support from all 50 members\par \par \uc2 - Dems working to get him to back WH-Dem deal\par \par \uc2 - Manchin concerned about $10,200 in tax-free jobless benefits\par \par \uc2 - Senate in a standstill {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/dTUo66IKer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/dTUo66IKer}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Manu Raju (@mkraju) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mkraju/status/1367934047054336000?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Tom Carper\uc2 , a Delaware Democrat, the lead sponsor on the amendment, reportedly told the Republican senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  that negotiations were still slow-going.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re stuck. And I don\u8217\'20\'19t know what it\u8217\'20\'19s going to take to get unstuck,\u8221\'20\'1D Carper said, according to CNN:\par \par \uc2 Carper refused to comment on where things stand, only telling CNN: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s gotta get done.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Carper amendment would codify a deal reached between the White House and Democratic leaders to extend jobless benefits at $300 a week through September. It would also ensure that the first $10,200 of jobless benefits would not be taxed.\par \par \uc2 The competing Portman amendment only extends the unemployment benefits through 18 July, and they would be taxed.\par \par \uc2 enltrGOP Senator Roger Wicker gives reporters "the scoop from the cloakroom," which is that Manchin is done negotiating, and Democrats will have to deal with him voting for the Portman amendment. He says senior Democrats have accepted this, but not Biden and Schumer.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Grace Segers (@Grace_Segers) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Grace_Segers/status/1367985744137101321?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.08am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.01am GMT\par \par \uc2 Now nearly a year into the Covid crisis, a staggering 40% of Americans report that the pandemic has had a lingering impact on their pocketbooks.\par \par \uc2 The surveys, reflected in new report {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/financial-struggles-covid-pandemic-one-year/?ftag=CNM-00-10aab7e&linkId=112742399"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 released}}}\uc2  today by the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2 , are just the latest evidence that inequalities already existing in the US were only compounded by the crisis. Minorities and low-income earners were the hardest hit by job losses and instability over the past year.\par \par \uc2 enltr48% of lower-income Americans in households that have lost income during the pandemic have taken on debt to help make ends meet. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/TI53TrHSE6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/TI53TrHSE6}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/1rJLLxjnsG"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/1rJLLxjnsG}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  (@pewresearch) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/pewresearch/status/1367965717958647817?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Roughly 60% of white adults told researchers their financial situation was excellent or good, according to the report, while 66% of Black and 59% of Hispanic Americans said their finances were in poor or fair shape.\par \par \uc2 From Pew:\par \par \uc2 Lower-income adults, as well as Hispanic and Asian Americans and adults younger than 30, are among the most likely to say they or someone in their household has lost a job or taken a pay cut since the outbreak began in February 2020. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/social-trends/2021/03/05/a-year-into-the-pandemic-long-term-financial-impact-weighs-heavily-on-many-americans/?utm_content=bufferee252&utm_medium=social&utm_source=twitter.com&utm_campaign=buffer#fn-29739-1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1}}}\uc2  Among those who\u8217\'20\'19ve had these experiences, lower-income and Black adults are particularly likely to say they have taken on debt or put off paying their bills in order to cover lost wages or salary.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Researchers also found that close of half of those who reported that their financial situation has worsened because of Covid don\u8217\'20\'19t think they will get back to where they were for three years or more.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.12am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A new CNN {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/03/05/health/herd-immunity-usa-vaccines-alone/index.html?utm_content=2021-03-05T23%3A17%3A01&utm_medium=social&utm_source=twcnnbrk&utm_term=link"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 analysis}}}\uc2  of federal data is projecting the US could reach herd immunity to Covid by summer \u8211\'20\'13 and maybe even sooner.\par \par \uc2 enltrUS could reach herd immunity by summer through vaccinations alone, CNN analysis finds {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/shXNLgAyXU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/shXNLgAyXU}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Audrey Irvine (@audreycnn) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/audreycnn/status/1367982457723699203?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 With the current pace of roughly 2 million shots going out each day, and an expected bump from the newly authorized single-shot \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine, experts now estimate 70% of US residents will be fully vaccinated by the end of July. That may be enough to stop the spread.\par \par \uc2 With transmissions on the decline, several states have already begun rolling back restrictions. California announced today that theme parks and sports stadiums will be back in business (with limitations) as early as 1 April \u8211\'20\'13 right in time for some MLB teams to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mercurynews.com/2021/03/03/covid-gov-newsom-expects-giants-as-fans-will-be-in-stands-for-opening-day/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 participate in Opening Day.}}}\par \par \uc2 Arizona, Maine, and Connecticut also eased business and travel restrictions this week, and Texas and Mississippi shared plans to end mask mandates.\par \par \uc2 Heath officials have warned against rushing into going back to business as usual, though, cautioning legislators that they need to remain vigilant. A new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/70/wr/mm7010e3.htm?s_cid=mm7010e3_w"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  from the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , released today, states that policies allowing indoor restaurant dining were associated with increases in daily death rates up to 100 days after they were implemented. Mandated mask-wearing decreased Covid cases and deaths within 20 days.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.11am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Federico Klein, a former state department aide who worked under the Trump administration, has a cockroach problem: the creepy-crawly brown insects have apparently been making themselves at home the cell Klein\u8217\'20\'19s been staying in, after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/former-state-department-aide-arrested-connection-capitol-riot-n1259689"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he was charged for assaulting officers}}}\uc2  during the pro-Trump riot at the US Capitol on 6 January.\par \par \uc2 Klein, 42, is being held at a jail in Washington DC, facing six charges, including two that carry 20-year maximum sentences.\par \par \uc2 NBC News {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/justice-department/federico-klein-former-trump-appointee-charged-capitol-riot-requests-jail-n1259767"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reports}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 When told he would remain in custody until next week\u8217\'20\'19s hearing, he said, \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m wondering if there\u8217\'20\'19s a place that I could stay in detention where I don\u8217\'20\'19t have cockroaches crawling everywhere while I attempt to sleep. I haven\u8217\'20\'19t slept very much.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://int.nyt.com/data/documenttools/f-b-i-statement-of-facts-federico-g/0b50c0cd4b5a59e9/full.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 court documents}}}\uc2 , Klein was recorded on video shoving a riot shield into police officers and attempting to take gear from them as he pushed to gain entry into the Capitol. Klein, who was still a federal employee at the time and possessed a top secret security clearance, was hired by the state department in 2017 and worked on Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 2016 campaign.\par \par \uc2 enltrWOW: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ktbenner?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @ktbenner}}}\uc2  gets the actual arrest affidavit for former State Dept aide Federico KLEIN. He was still employed there on Jan. 6, held a Top Secret clearance that was renewed in 2019 and is accused of multiple assaults on \uc2 Capitol police\uc2 . {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/tqF3wyw42J"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/tqF3wyw42J}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kyle Cheney (@kyledcheney) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/kyledcheney/status/1367757098759684096?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Roughly 100,000 migrants were detained at the US-Mexico border last month, Reuters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-immigration-border/u-s-detained-nearly-100000-migrants-at-u-s-mexico-border-in-february-sources-idUSKCN2AX2FE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reports}}}\uc2 , marking the highest number of arrests in the month of February in the last 15 years, and an increase of 22,000 from January.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have been critical of the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s plans to roll back many of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s immigration policies, and open more opportunities for migrants to enter the US. From Reuters:\par \par \uc2 Kevin McCarthy, the top Republican in the House of Representatives, sent a letter to Biden on Friday that requested a meeting to discuss the issue, saying he had \u8220\'20\'1Cgreat concern\u8221\'20\'1D with the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s approach to border.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe must acknowledge the border crisis, develop a plan, and, in no uncertain terms, strongly discourage individuals from Mexico and Central America from ever making the dangerous journey to our southern border,\u8221\'20\'1D McCarthy wrote in the letter.\par \par \uc2 Advocates are hoping the new president will not only take a softer stance on immigration than his predecessor, but also hold the former administration accountable for allegations of abuse and misconduct against Customs and Border Protection as they carried out those policies.\par \par \uc2 The American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/immigration/aclu-asks-dhs-take-action-complaints-abuse-misconduct-u-s-n1259657"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed 13 complaints}}}\uc2  against the agency, which is housed under the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , hoping to push officials to punish officers involved and enact reforms.\par \par \uc2 White House officials are also planning to visit the southern border in order to brief Biden, but details are scant on when that trip will take place.\par \par \uc2 As Martin Pengelly pointed out {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2021/mar/05/joe-biden-covid-coronavirus-relief-bill-senate-kamala-harris-republicans-delay-live-updates?page=with:block-604284f28f08711f62509f96#block-604284f28f08711f62509f96"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 earlier today,}}}\uc2  Trump weighed in from Florida, calling the influx a \u8220\'20\'1Cborder nightmare\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 . {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PressSec?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @PressSec}}}\uc2  pushing back on former Pres Trump's attack on Pres Biden's immigration policies says: "We don't take our advice or counsel from former President Trump on immigration policy."\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Yamiche Alcindor (@Yamiche) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Yamiche/status/1367905591570690053?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 11.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 10.14pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Gabrielle Canon here, taking you through the remainder of Friday from the west coast! But before I do, here\u8217\'20\'19s an update from my colleague Martin Pengelly:\par \par \uc2 Remember the great debate over derp ? \uc2 Ben Jacobs\uc2 , once of this parish, does and he\u8217\'20\'19s written {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gen.medium.com/the-return-of-derp-880623820a2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a fine Medium piece about it}}}\uc2 , in light of the more than slightly ridiculous fuss this week over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s comment about Republicans and \u8220\'20\'1Cneanderthal thinking\u8221\'20\'1D about mask mandates in the age of Covid.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the video of Joe saying it\u8217\'20\'19s so:\par \par \uc2 And here\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/04/biden-mask-mandate-texas-neanderthal-thinking-greg-abbott"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 some coverage}}}\uc2  of Republicans saying Joe should have said it ain\u8217\'20\'19t so.\par \par \uc2 And here\u8217\'20\'19s the start of Ben\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gen.medium.com/the-return-of-derp-880623820a2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dissection of the whole sorry mess}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Out of the foggy mists of time, Neanderthals emerged this week. With them came the dim outlines of a world far distant from the present: the world of 2012.\par \par \uc2 Suddenly, it is the era of derp again. Derp was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nymag.com/intelligencer/2013/06/derp-meaning-origin.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the defining complaint}}}\uc2  about politics in the early teens of the 21st century. With a relatively prosperous country and both parties nominating well-disciplined candidates who were clearly qualified for the Oval Office, the 24-hour cable news cycle needed to cover something. So we got \u8220\'20\'1Cderp,\u8221\'20\'1D a word coined by the creators of South Park that became used to describe the unbearable stupidity of political fights happening for their own sake.\par \par \uc2 As Ben says, a prime example of derp circa 2012 sprung up over a joke \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  made {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/blogs/politico44/2012/03/contra-obama-rutherford-b-hayes-loved-the-telephone-117592"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about Rutherford B Hayes}}}\uc2 . There are not many jokes about Rutherford B Hayes. There are not many jokes, about Rutherford B Hayes or otherwise, in this long Guardian report from 1877 about how he came to be president. But as I am nothing if not a history dad, I\u8217\'20\'19m going to demand that you read it:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/1877/mar/03/uselections2000.usa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Original Guardian report: The 1877 US elections}}}\par \par \uc2 This is a slightly more sprightly telling, from last summer and by me, interviewing the great historian Eric Foner. When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  started trying to overturn the election, a lot of it came to ring rather true\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/aug/23/1876-election-constitutional-crisis-republicans-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A disputed election, a constitutional crisis, polarisation \u8230\'20\'26 welcome to 1876}}}\par \par \uc2 10.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Today so far\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s it from me today. My west coast colleague Gabrielle Canon will take over the blog for the next few hours.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s where the day stands so far:\par \par \uc2 * The US economy added 379,000 jobs last month, according to the latest report from the labor department. The unemployment rate dropped slightly to 6.2%.\par \par \uc2 * The Senate \u8220\'20\'1Cvote-a-rama\u8221\'20\'1D on the coronavirus relief bill has been stalled for about six hours, amid a disagreement over unemployment benefits. Senate Democrats are trying to convince Joe Manchin to back a proposal from \uc2 Tom Carper\uc2 , which would lower the federal unemployment benefits from $400 a week to $300 a week but extend the benefits through the end of September (rather than the end of August). Manchin had been considering supporting a similar proposal from Republican Rob Portman, which would let the benefits end in July.\par \par \uc2 * Senator Bernie Sanders\u8217\'20\'19 proposal to raise the federal minimum wage to $15 an hour appears to have failed. The Senate vote on the proposal to add the minimum wage increase to the relief bill has stood at 42 to 58 for hours now. Democratic leaders are leaving the vote open as they negotiate with Manchin, but the minimum wage proposal will almost certainly be rejected, given that it needed 60 votes to pass.\par \par \uc2 * Chuck Schumer pledged the Senate would stay in session until the coronavirus relief bill passes. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Senate is going to take a lot of votes. But we are going to power through and finish this bill, however long it takes,\u8221\'20\'1D the Democratic majority leader said this morning. \u8220\'20\'1CThe American people are counting on us and our nation depends on it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gabrielle will have more coming up, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 9.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Senate is preparing for a long weekend, as Democrats try to pass \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s $1.9 trillion coronavirus relief bill.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to an NBC News reporter about the voting schedule, Republican Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2  asked, \u8220\'20\'1CDid you bring your pillow?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The vote-a-rama was already expected to go on for hours, given that Republicans have signaled they plan to introduce many amendments to force Democrats to take some difficult votes before the bill is passed.\par \par \uc2 And that was before the process stalled for more than five hours today, as Democrats try to hammer out an agreement on the federal unemployment benefits in the bill.\par \par \uc2 Senate majority leader Chuck Schumer said earlier today, \u8220\'20\'1CThe Senate is going to take a lot of votes. But we are going to power through and finish this bill, however long it takes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 9.23pm GMT\par \par \uc2 According to Punchbowl News, Senator Dan Sullivan, a Republican of Alaska, has had to leave Washington because of a family issue.\par \par \uc2 NEWS \u8212\'20\'14 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/DanSullivan_AK?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @DanSullivan_AK}}}\uc2  had to leave Washington for a family issue. This comes in the middle of the covid relief debate. From {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JakeSherman?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @JakeSherman}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/bresreports?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @bresreports}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Punchbowl News (@PunchbowlNews) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PunchbowlNews/status/1367947257027452928?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Sullivan\u8217\'20\'19s absence from Washington means that Republicans will have one less vote as they try to advance amendments to the coronavirus relief bill.\par \par \uc2 But most Republican amendments were already expected to fail, and the proposals will still force Democrats to take difficult votes on issues like immigration and energy policy.\par \par \uc2 9.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Time-check: the Senate vote on Bernie Sanders \u8217\'20\'19 minimum wage proposal has now been open for more than five hours.\par \par \uc2 The vote has stood at 42 to 58 for hours now, meaning the measure will almost certainly be rejected.\par \par \uc2 But Senate Democrats are keeping the vote open as they work to convince Joe Manchin to support \uc2 Tom Carper\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s proposal on the expanded unemployment benefits in the coronavirus relief bill.\par \par \uc2 Manchin had previously been considering supporting a proposal from Republican Senator Rob Portman, which would lower the federal unemployment benefit from $400 a week to $300 a week.\par \par \uc2 Walking by reporters on Capitol Hill moments ago, Portman said he was on the phone with Manchin to get a sense of whether the moderate Democrat was still planning to support his proposal.\par \par \uc2 Portman wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t talk to reporters a minute ago. Why? He had Manchin on the phone\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Manu Raju (@mkraju) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mkraju/status/1367941130046803973?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 8.45pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is now holding a roundtable discussion on his $1.9tn coronavirus relief bill at the White House.\par \par \uc2 The White House said of the event: \u8220\'20\'1CThe president will participate in a roundtable with individuals who will benefit from receiving relief checks thanks to the American Rescue Plan. They will discuss how these relief checks will help relieve some of the hardship they have experienced due to Covid.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The roundtable is being moderated by the senior White House adviser and former Democratic congressman \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 8.29pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Progressive congresswoman \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  criticized Senate Democrats for limiting the eligibility for the direct payments in the coronavirus relief bill.\par \par \uc2 In the version of the relief bill passed by the House, the checks completely phase out for individuals making $100,000 a year, but the Senate bill lowers that income threshold to $80,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 Rep. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/IlhanMN?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @IlhanMN}}}\uc2  (D-MN) on lower caps for next round of stimulus checks:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe obviously are now ultimately sending money to less people than the Trump administration and the Senate majority Republicans \u8230\'20\'26\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/KrM2Owk2wm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/KrM2Owk2wm}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1367929521559724036?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Omar, a Democrat of Minnesota, noted that the change meant about 17 million fewer Americans would receive checks from this relief package, in comparison to the two rounds of payments that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  approved.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe obviously are now ultimately sending money to less people than the Trump administration and the Senate-majority Republicans were willing to,\u8221\'20\'1D Omar said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not the promise that we made,\u8221\'20\'1D the congresswoman added. \u8220\'20\'1CSo ultimately it is a failure when we compromise ourselves out of delivering on behalf of the American people and keeping our promises.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 8.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Senate vote-a-rama stalls over unemployment benefits\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s where things stand in the Senate: the vote-a-rama has now been stalled for more than three hours.\par \par \uc2 It appears Senate Democrats are keeping the vote on the $15 minimum wage open to give themselves more time to negotiate a compromise over the expanded unemployment benefits in the relief bill.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Tom Carper\uc2  had proposed lowering the federal unemployment benefit from $400 a week to $300 a week, in exchange for extending the benefits until the end of September (rather than August) and making $10,200 of unemployment benefits tax exempt.\par \par \uc2 But according to multiple reports, Senator Joe Manchin has signaled he may support a Republican proposal to lower the benefits to $300 a week without either of the other parts of Carper\u8217\'20\'19s measure.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations over the issue continue, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 7.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was out earlier with another statement, issued from his Florida bolthole and about one of his favourite subjects: immigration.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur border is now totally out of control thanks to the disastrous leadership of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D began a former president well versed in struggles to deal with conditions at the southern border and, one might argue, disastrous leadership.\par \par \uc2 The rest of the statement was a rant about not treating \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  agents nicely, a muddled restatement of hardline Trump administration policy positions, and claims that Biden had both caused a \u8220\'20\'1C spiralling tsunami \u8221\'20\'1D and unleashed a \u8220\'20\'1Cborder nightmare\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Among actions since taking office, Biden has lifted the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/05/us-immigration-biden-remain-in-mexico-asylum-seekers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Remain in Mexico policy}}}\uc2  for those seeking asylum in the US \u8211\'20\'13 see below \u8211\'20\'13 and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/feb/27/us-mexico-border-family-separations-parents-contacted-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sought to reunite children}}}\uc2  with their parents after they were separated at the southern border under one of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s most controversial initiatives.\par \par \uc2 Some actions by federal authorities under Biden remain controversial, however. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/feb/24/biden-is-locking-up-migrant-children-will-the-world-still-care-with-trump-gone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s Moustafa Bayoumi}}}\uc2 , writing for Guardian US last month:\par \par \uc2 This week, the Biden administration did the unthinkable. It {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/national/immigrant-children-camp-texas-biden/2021/02/22/05dfd58c-7533-11eb-8115-9ad5e9c02117_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reopened a Trump-era detention site for migrant children. }}}\uc2  The detention center, a reconverted camp for oil field workers in Carrizo Springs, Texas, is expected to hold 700 children between the ages of 13 and 17, and dozens of kids have already arrived there.\par \par \uc2 This is an awful development, reminding me of some of the worst abuses of the Trump years.\par \par \uc2 At the White House today, press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  was asked about Trump\u8217\'20\'19s statement. She said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t take our advice or counsel from former President Trump on immigration policy \u8230\'20\'26 We\u8217\'20\'19re gonna chart our own path forward, and that includes treating children with humanity.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/05/us-immigration-biden-remain-in-mexico-asylum-seekers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 'Only we know what we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen': migrants re-enter US after Biden lifts Remain in Mexico}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 7.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  sues Trump over Capitol attack\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s post-presidency legal jeopardy is a favourite subject among liberals traumatised by his four years in power, and today \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2 , a California congressman who briefly ran for his party\u8217\'20\'19s presidential nomination but more memorably served as a House manager in both impeachment trials, has sought to add to the pile.\par \par \uc2 In a lawsuit filed in Washington DC, the Democrat {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/swalwell-sues-trump-alleged-role-capitol-assault/story?id=76275852"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 accused}}}\uc2  Trump, his lawyer \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and Mo Brooks, an Alabama congressman, of making \u8220\'20\'1Ca clear call to action\u8221\'20\'1D before the Capitol riot on 6 January, to which Trump supporters responded by storming the halls of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s some of what the suit says:\par \par \uc2 Trump directly incited the violence at the Capitol that followed and then watched approvingly as the building was overrun. As Trump was instructing them to go to the Capitol, insurgents were already forcing their way through barricades, attempting to breach the building, while blasting Trump\u8217\'20\'19s speech on a bullhorn.\par \par \uc2 Trump aide Jason Miller responded, telling ABC News: \u8220\'20\'1CAfter failing miserably with two impeachment hoaxes, [Swalwell is] attacking our greatest president with yet another witch hunt. It\u8217\'20\'19s a disgrace that a compromised member of Congress like Swalwell still sits on the House intelligence committee.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump has already been sued over the riot by a Democrat in Congress, \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2 , who was joined in the action by the National \uc2 Association for the Advancement of Colored People\uc2 . The former president was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.yahoo.com/trump-served-civil-rights-lawsuit-165719200.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 served}}}\uc2  in that case this week.\par \par \uc2 Other cases to worry Trump include investigations into his financial affairs in New York and his attempts to overturn his election defeat in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 On the subject of Trump and the Capitol riot, meanwhile, here\u8217\'20\'19s some further reading from Kari Paul:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/05/election-misinformation-trump-rightwing-super-spreader-study"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rightwing 'super-spreader': study finds handful of accounts spread bulk of election misinformation}}}\par \par \uc2 7.05pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Today so far\par \par \uc2 The White House briefing has now concluded. Here\u8217\'20\'19s where the day stands so far:\par \par \uc2 * The US economy added 379,000 jobs last month, according to the latest report from the labor department. The unemployment rate dropped slightly to 6.2%.\par \par \uc2 * The Senate has started its \u8220\'20\'1Cvote-a-rama\u8221\'20\'1D on the coronavirus relief bill. Senator Bernie Sanders has already introduced a proposal to add a $15 federal minimum wage to the relief package, but that measure appears to have failed.\par \par \uc2 * Chuck Schumer pledged the Senate would stay in session until the coronavirus relief bill passes. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Senate is going to take a lot of votes. But we are going to power through and finish this bill, however long it takes,\u8221\'20\'1D the Democratic majority leader said this morning. \u8220\'20\'1CThe American people are counting on us and our nation depends on it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The blog will have more coming up, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 6.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , rejected the notion that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was \u8220\'20\'1Csnubbing\u8221\'20\'1D lawmakers by delaying his first address to a joint session of Congress.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not a snubbing happening here,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are in the middle of a global pandemic.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 "It's not a snubbing," press sec. Psaki says when asked about Pres. Biden addressing Congress.\par \par \uc2 "We are in the middle of a global pandemic...We intend on the president delivering a joint session... but we don't have a date for that." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/R89HWMj6Jp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/R89HWMj6Jp}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 ABC News Politics (@ABCPolitics) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ABCPolitics/status/1367910904013197314?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Psaki added that Biden intended to address Congress soon, and he is in close consultation with congressional leaders to determine a date for that speech.\par \par \uc2 The White House has previously indicated that Biden wants to wait to address Congress until after he signs the coronavirus relief bill.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 6.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  was asked about whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would soon speak to Turkish President \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Psaki said the two leaders would speak \u8220\'20\'1Cat some point,\u8221\'20\'1D but she did not give a clear sense of when that might happen.\par \par \uc2 6.40pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , said \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot engaged in conversations or negotiations about lowering the threshold for the minimum wage\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Psaki reiterated that Biden strongly supports Bernie Sanders\u8217\'20\'19 proposal to raise the federal minimum wage to $15 an hour, but she said the president is currently focused on passing the coronavirus relief bill.\par \par \uc2 Psaki\u8217\'20\'19s comments come as the Senate appears to have rejected Sanders\u8217\'20\'19 amendment to add a minimum wage provision to the relief package.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 6.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , was asked about whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was concerned that it seems like his coronavirus relief bill will not attract bipartisan support in Congress.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBipartisanship is not determined by a single zip code in Washington DC,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki replied.\par \par \uc2 The press secretary noted that polls have shown a large majority of the American people support the relief package, and she argued those polls were a better reflection of the bipartisan support for the bill.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 6.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , was asked about the Detroit mayor\u8217\'20\'19s rejection of a shipment of the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  coronavirus vaccine because of its lower efficacy rate compared to the Moderna and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccines.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur team has been in touch with the mayor. There\u8217\'20\'19s been a bit of a misunderstanding,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki said.\par \par \uc2 Echoing public health experts, the press secretary once again encouraged Americans to receive whichever of the three approved coronavirus vaccines that is made available to them.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 6.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , said a group of senior officials will soon travel to the US-Mexican border to provide \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  with a briefing on the influx of unaccompanied migrant children at the border.\par \par \uc2 Citing security concerns, Psaki would not provide details on which officials were going or where specifically along the border they would be traveling to.\par \par \uc2 When a reporter noted that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has criticized Biden\u8217\'20\'19s immigration agenda, Psaki replied: \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t take our advice or counsel from former president Trump on immigration policy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.45pm GMT\par \par \uc2 6.18pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Tthe White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , was once again asked about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s views on the Senate filibuster.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHis view and his position haven\u8217\'20\'19t changed,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki said.\par \par \uc2 Biden has previously voiced opposition to the idea of scrapping the filibuster, although he has left himself some wiggle room if Republicans attempt to obstruct all of his agenda, which progressives have said is a virtual certainty.\par \par \uc2 As of now, Democrats don\u8217\'20\'19t have the votes to eliminate the filibuster because at least two moderate Democratic senators, Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 , has said they are against the idea.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.45pm GMT\par \par \uc2 6.14pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will hold his first full press conference as president \u8220\'20\'1Cbefore the end of the month\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Biden has received some criticism for not yet holding a solo press conference, as many reporters have noted that \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had already held press conferences by this point in their presidencies.\par \par \uc2 Psaki defended the timing of the press conference, saying Biden has been focused on responding to the coronavirus pandemic and the resulting economic fallout.\par \par \uc2 The press secretary also noted Biden frequently takes questions from reporters after his events, but the president usually just answers one or two questions before departing.\par \par \uc2 6.11pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will travel to Baltimore on Wednesday to meet with the CEOs of \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  and Merck, press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  just announced.\par \par \uc2 The trip comes days after the president announced Merck would start producing \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s coronavirus vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Biden said he would invoke the Defense Production Act to help Merck facilities get the needed resources to start producing the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 6.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , is now holding her daily briefing, and she started the event by commenting on the latest jobs report.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile it shows some progress, it also shows the long road ahead,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki said.\par \par \uc2 The press secretary noted that the US economy still has about 9.5m fewer jobs than it did in February 2020, before the coronavirus pandemic started.\par \par \uc2 The jobs report will be discussed when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  receives an economic briefing from the treasury secretary, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , later today, Psaki said.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 5.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Former Cuomo aide says he is 'textbook abuser'\par \par \uc2 Richard Luscombe reports for \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 A former aide to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/andrew-cuomo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Andrew Cuomo}}}\uc2  who has accused the New York governor of sexual harassment has said she believes he is a \u8220\'20\'1Ctextbook abuser\u8221\'20\'1D who knew she was a survivor of sexual violence and nevertheless made inappropriate advances.\par \par \uc2 Charlotte Bennett, 25, Cuomo\u8217\'20\'19s former executive assistant and health policy adviser, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/cuomo-acuser-charlotte-bennett-interview-sexual-harassment/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told CBS Evening News}}}\uc2  on Thursday that Cuomo was trying to proposition her for sex during an \u8220\'20\'1Cuncomfortable\u8221\'20\'1D encounter in his office last spring, and that she felt she \u8220\'20\'1Chad to get out of this room as soon as possible\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 CUOMO ACCUSER SPEAKS OUT: In her first television interview, Charlotte Bennett, a former aide to \uc2 Gov. Andrew Cuomo\uc2 , speaks with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NorahODonnell?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @NorahODonnell}}}\uc2  about her allegations of sexual harassment against the governor, providing the most detailed account yet from one of Cuomo's accusers. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/MOS0iViBfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/MOS0iViBfw}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 CBS Evening News (@CBSEveningNews) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CBSEveningNews/status/1367623754008965123?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 4, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 She said it was one of multiple incidents in which the 63-year-old governor, who is also facing similar allegations from two other women, acted inappropriately.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe is a textbook abuser,\u8221\'20\'1D Bennett said, when asked how she would describe Cuomo. \u8220\'20\'1CHe lets his temper and his anger rule the office, but he was very sweet to me for a year in the hope that maybe one day when he came on to me I would think we were friends or that it was appropriate or that it was OK.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/05/andrew-cuomo-charlotte-bennett-interview-alleged-harassment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Former Cuomo aide says he is 'textbook abuser' and details alleged harassment }}}\par \par \uc2 5.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Tina Smith\uc2 , a Democrat of Minnesota, has announced her support for eliminating the Senate filibuster.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve made up my mind,\u8221\'20\'1D Smith said in a tweet. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to move this country forward, and that\u8217\'20\'19s why I\u8217\'20\'19ve decided to come out in support of eliminating the filibuster.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ve made up my mind.\par \par \uc2 We need to move this country forward, and that\u8217\'20\'19s why I\u8217\'20\'19ve decided to come out in support of eliminating the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Senator \uc2 Tina Smith\uc2  (@SenTinaSmith) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenTinaSmith/status/1367868344158875654?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Many progressives have called on Senate Democrats to eliminate the filibuster, a mechanism that could allow Republicans to hold up much of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s agenda.\par \par \uc2 With the filibuster in place, most bills need 60 votes to pass the Senate, a very cumbersome task when the chamber is evenly divided between Democrats and Republicans. If the filibuster were eliminated, the Senate could pass bills with a simple majority.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats would need all of their Senate caucus members to support eliminating the filibuster in order to get rid of it, and centrists like Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  have consistently said they oppose the idea.\par \par \uc2 4.55pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The White House has announced its support for the Democratic amendment to alter the expanded unemployment benefits in the coronavirus relief bill.\par \par \uc2 According to reports, the amendment would lower the federal unemployment benefits to $300 a week, rather than the $400 a week approved in the House bill.\par \par \uc2 But the benefits will also expire at the end of September, rather than August, and $10,200 of unemployment benefits will be tax-exempt, protecting unemployed Americans from hefty tax bills.\par \par \uc2 The compromise amendment achieves that while helping to address the surprise tax bills that many are facing by eliminating the first $10,200 of UI benefits from taxation for 2020. Combined, this amendment would provide more relief to the unemployed than the current legislation.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  (@PressSec) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PressSec/status/1367875430716477443?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 The White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , said of the proposed amendment: \u8220\'20\'1CThe president believes it is critical to extend expanded unemployment benefits through the end of September to help Americans who are struggling, as the president proposed in the American Rescue Plan.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CThe compromise amendment achieves that while helping to address the surprise tax bills that many are facing by eliminating the first $10,200 of UI benefits from taxation for 2020. Combined, this amendment would provide more relief to the unemployed than the current legislation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 4.39pm GMT\par \par \uc2 White House senior adviser \uc2 Andy Slavitt\uc2  closed the briefing by emphasizing that there is still much work left to be done to get coronavirus under control in the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re not done,\u8221\'20\'1D Slavitt said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re making progress, but there are also disturbing signs on the horizon.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Slavitt added that the Biden administration is working on identifying \u8220\'20\'1Ctrusted messengers\u8221\'20\'1D to boost confidence in coronavirus vaccines in their local communities.\par \par \uc2 4.32pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A reporter asked the White House coronavirus response team about the Detroit mayor\u8217\'20\'19s decision to reject a shipment of the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine because of its lower efficacy rate compared to the Moderna and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccines.\par \par \uc2 White House senior adviser \uc2 Andy Slavitt\uc2  said that the mayor had not actually rejected the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine, describing the incident as a \u8220\'20\'1Cmisunderstanding\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Dr Anthony Fauci\uc2  reiterated that Americans should receive whichever of the three approved vaccines is offered to them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have a highly efficacious group of three vaccines,\u8221\'20\'1D Fauci said. \u8220\'20\'1CI would just take the vaccine that is most readily available.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 4.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Dr Rochelle Walensky\uc2  said the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  would \u8220\'20\'1Csoon\u8221\'20\'1D release guidance on best practices for those who have already been vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 But the CDC director would not provide further clarity on when the guidance might be released.\par \par \uc2 4.23pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Dr Anthony Fauci\uc2  echoed the director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, urging Americans to continue taking every possible precaution to limit their risk of contracting coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 Fauci noted that some European countries are currently seeing a rise in cases after relaxing some of the restrictions means to limit the spread of the virus.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.55pm GMT\par \par \uc2 4.17pm GMT\par \par \uc2 CDC director emphasizes importance of continued mask usage\par \par \uc2 The director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, \uc2 Dr Rochelle Walensky\uc2 , emphasized the importance of following public health guidelines as coronavirus vaccinations ramp up.\par \par \uc2 Walensky acknowledged there is a \u8220\'20\'1Clight at the end of the tunnel\u8221\'20\'1D, but she warned that abandoning precautions too early could cause a setback in the US pandemic response.\par \par \uc2 CDC Director \uc2 Rochelle Walensky\uc2 : \u8220\'20\'1CWe have seen this movie before. When prevention measures like mask mandates are rolled back, cases go up.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/aJQIu1UU9Q"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/aJQIu1UU9Q}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1367871556161011716?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have seen this movie before,\u8221\'20\'1D the CDC director said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen prevention measures like mask mandates are rolled back, cases go up.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Walensky\u8217\'20\'19s comments come as some states have started relaxing their coronavirus-related restrictions. The Republican governors of Texas and Mississippi announced on Tuesday that they were rescinding mask mandates in their states.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 4.11pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The White House coronavirus response team is now holding its briefing to provide an update on the vaccine distribution process.\par \par \uc2 Andy Slavitt\uc2 , a senior White House adviser, announced that Fema is opening two new mass-vaccination sites in Atlanta and Cleveland.\par \par \uc2 The two sites will be located at the \uc2 Atlanta Falcons\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 stadium and the Wolstein Center at \uc2 Cleveland State University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 According to Slavitt, the two vaccination sites will have the capacity to administer 6,000 shots a day.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 3.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Echoing comments from the White House, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said the new jobs report was promising, but she argued it was still vital that the Senate pass \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s coronavirus relief bill.\par \par \uc2 The Feb. jobs report is promising, but millions of Americans still face unemployment & economic crises. Decisive action is imperative \u8211\'20\'13 which is why {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/HouseDemocrats?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @HouseDemocrats}}}\uc2  passed the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/AmericanRescuePlan?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #AmericanRescuePlan}}}\uc2  to stimulate the economy, cut child poverty in half & lift ~12M out of poverty. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/odPG7oQXH0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/odPG7oQXH0}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  (@SpeakerPelosi) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SpeakerPelosi/status/1367862166330740737?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe February jobs report shows some progress, but much more is needed to address the daily reality of joblessness and financial insecurity facing millions of Americans,\u8221\'20\'1D the House speaker said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe need for decisive action to save lives and livelihoods could not be more urgent \u8211\'20\'13 which is why, last week, House Democrats passed the Biden American Rescue Plan.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Pelosi also criticized Republicans for not supporting the relief package, despite polls showing the majority of Americans back the bill.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic speaker said: \u8220\'20\'1CSenate Republicans must end their obstruction and delay tactics, which demonstrate a great disdain for the suffering of the American people, and work with the Senate Democratic majority to pass and send this bill to the president\u8217\'20\'19s desk.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.15pm GMT\par \par \uc2 3.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Sanders delivers fiery speech urging Senate to raise minimum wage\par \par \uc2 In a fiery speech during debate over the American Rescue Plan, Senator Bernie Sanders implored Congress to raise the federal minimum wage to $15 an hour, calling it \u8220\'20\'1Cdisgraceful\u8221\'20\'1D that lawmakers have allowed tens of millions of American workers to live on \u8220\'20\'1Cstarvation wages\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNobody in America can survive on $7.25 an hour, $9 an hour or $12 an hour,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need an economy in which all of our workers earn at least a living wage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He added: \u8220\'20\'1CNow is the time to raise the minimum wage to a living wage \u8211\'20\'13 at least $15 an hour. A job in the United States of America should lift you out of poverty, not keep you in it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sen. Bernie Sanders (I-VT) gives forceful speech on his proposed amendment to raise the federal minimum wage to $15/hour:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a bill which will answer a profound question: Are we living in a democratic society \u8230\'20\'26 ?\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/qrz4LjQFWq"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/qrz4LjQFWq}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1367848776614961153?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Last week, the Senate parliamentarian determined that a provision raising the minimum wage to $15 an hour was inadmissible under the rules of a special budgetary procedure Democrats are using to pass the $1.9tn coronavirus relief bill on a party-line vote.\par \par \uc2 Sanders, backed by many progressives in the House, has called on Democrats to \u8220\'20\'1Cignore\u8221\'20\'1D the decision by the parliamentarian, citing the urgent need to help low-wage workers who have been disproportionately affected by the economic havoc wrought by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Later this morning, Sanders was poised to introduce an amendment that would write a minimum wage hike in the stimulus bill. Though the measure, which would raise the federal minimum wage from $7.25 to $15 an hour over a five-year period, is unlikely to pass, it may reveal how much support the effort has.\par \par \uc2 A provision raising the minimum wage was included in a version of the stimulus bill advanced by the House last week, despite the parliamentarian\u8217\'20\'19s ruling.\par \par \uc2 Sanders also made a forceful case for enacting the Covid bill, which is expected to pass with only Democratic support.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a bill which will answer a profound question: are we living in a democratic society where the \uc2 US Congress\uc2  will respond to the needs of working families rather than just the wealthy and large corporations and their lobbyists?\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s what today is about.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 3.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Ron Wyden\uc2 , a Democrat of Oregon, told the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  that Democrats are coalescing around a plan to extend expanded unemployment benefits through 4 October.\par \par \uc2 Ron Wyden\uc2  tells me in interview Dems are coalescing around a possible UI change: $300 a week through October 4, but with tax forgiveness on 2020 UI benefits up to $10,200. (Usual in flux caveats apply.)\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Emily Cochrane (@ESCochrane) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ESCochrane/status/1367853201161076738?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 As of now, the $400 weekly federal unemployment benefits are expected to expire in August, assuming the Senate can pass the relief bill.\par \par \uc2 But senators like Wyden have warned that could create an \u8220\'20\'1CAugust cliff\u8221\'20\'1D, where the benefits expire, leaving Americans in the lurch, while Congress is out of session for the summer.\par \par \uc2 Wyden told the Times that under the new plan, the benefits would be extended, at the lower rate of $300 a week, until October, avoiding the August cliff.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the negotiations are still in flux, so we\u8217\'20\'19ll have to wait to see what the final bill says.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 3.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s really \u8220\'20\'1Cchoose your own adventure\u8221\'20\'1D when it comes to interpreting the latest jobs report, which showed the US gained 379,000 jobs last month.\par \par \uc2 If you ask the Senate minority leader, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the jobs report shows that the coronavirus relief bill is unnecessary because states are already recovering from the economic fallout of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 McConnell claimed that Democrats had \u8220\'20\'1Cinherited a tide that was already turning\u8221\'20\'1D, a rather ironic assertion given how frequently \uc2 Donald Trump{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2018/09/12/646708799/fact-check-who-gets-credit-for-the-booming-u-s-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claimed credit}}}\uc2  for an economic recovery that started under \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We're already set to come roaring back \u8212\'20\'14 not due to this liberal spending spree. Democrats inherited a tide that was already turning.\par \par \uc2 We could have worked together to do something smart & bipartisan to finish this fight ASAP.\par \par \uc2 Democrats decided to do something else.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Leader McConnell (@LeaderMcConnell) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LeaderMcConnell/status/1367846452001714182?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 But according to the White House chief of staff, Ron Klain, the latest jobs report underscores how slowly the US economy is recovering from the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Klain noted that, at the current rate of job growth, the US will not regain all the jobs lost since the start of the pandemic until April 2023.\par \par \uc2 If you think today's jobs report is "good enough," then know that at this pace (+379,000 jobs/month), it would take until April 2023 to get back to where we were in February 2020.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Ronald Klain (@WHCOS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WHCOS/status/1367845935636709385?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 2.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Senate was originally supposed to debate the coronavirus relief bill for 20 hours before moving on to the vote-a-rama.\par \par \uc2 But this morning, the Democratic senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2  asked for unanimous consent to limit debate to three hours, and no one objected.\par \par \uc2 Republicans were not in the chamber as \uc2 Van Hollen\uc2  proposed limiting debate, so some congressional reporters suggested that Democrats had outplayed their colleagues by raising the issue when they had the chamber to themselves.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans have pushed back against those claims, saying they wanted to quickly move on to the vote-a-rama, during which they are expected to introduce many amendments.\par \par \uc2 From a Washington Post reporter:\par \par \uc2 R's disputing any suggestion they got rolled here -- say they wanted to use 3 hours for debate and that's what they're doing, then on to amendments. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/eFjXv71cV1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/eFjXv71cV1}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Erica Werner (@ericawerner) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ericawerner/status/1367842501630046214?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 2.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Schumer says Senate will 'power through and finish' coronavirus relief bill\par \par \uc2 The Senate majority leader, Chuck Schumer, said the chamber would \u8220\'20\'1Cpower through and finish\u8221\'20\'1D passing the coronavirus relief bill, no matter how long it takes.\par \par \uc2 Because of the number of amendments that Republicans are expected to introduce, the final Senate passage of the bill probably won\u8217\'20\'19t happen until sometime this weekend.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Senate is going to take a lot of votes. But we are going to power through and finish this bill, however long it takes,\u8221\'20\'1D Schumer said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe American people are counting on us and our nation depends on it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer thanks Senate staff for 10-hour reading of COVID relief bill, and gives a nod to Sen. Ron Johnson (R-WI), who forced the procedure:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd as for our friend from Wisconsin, I hope he enjoyed his Thursday evening.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/LBPkf0xpzU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/LBPkf0xpzU}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1367839343398780932?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Schumer also thanked the Senate clerks for reading the full text of the bill aloud, which took more than 10 hours and concluded early this morning.\par \par \uc2 The Senate usually skips reading the full text of the bill, but Republican Senator Ron Johnson objected to Schumer\u8217\'20\'19s motion to dispense with the reading, forcing the clerks to read all 628 pages aloud.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThank you, thank you, thank you for your efforts yesterday and every day,\u8221\'20\'1D Schumer told the clerks today. He added this pointed remark to Johnson: \u8220\'20\'1CAnd, as for our friend from Wisconsin, I hope he enjoyed his Thursday evening.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 2.14pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Senate begins debate on coronavirus relief bill\par \par \uc2 This is Joan Greve in Washington, taking over for Martin Belam.\par \par \uc2 The Senate is now beginning its three hours of debate over the coronavirus relief package, a day after Vice-President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  broke a Senate tie to allow the chamber to take up the bill.\par \par \uc2 After the three-hour debate, the Senate will begin its \u8220\'20\'1Cvote-a-rama\u8221\'20\'1D on the relief bill.\par \par \uc2 During the vote-a-rama, any senator can introduce an amendment to the bill. Republicans are expected to introduce many amendments, forcing Democrats to take difficult votes on issues like immigration.\par \par \uc2 But the first amendment of the vote-a-rama will come from Senator Bernie Sanders, who is seeking to add a provision that will increase the federal minimum wage to $15 an hour.\par \par \uc2 The version of the relief bill that passed the House on Saturday included the minimum wage proposal, but it was stripped out of the Senate version after the chamber\u8217\'20\'19s parliamentarian ruled that it did not meet the requirements for passage via reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 The minimum wage proposal is expected to fail, but it will be interesting to see which Democratic senators oppose Sanders\u8217\'20\'19 amendment.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s all still coming up, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 1.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US gains 379,000 jobs as more states reopen economies\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Dominic Rushe and Michael Sainato report:\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/useconomy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US economy}}}\uc2  bounced back strongly in February adding 379,000 jobs as more states reopened for business and more vaccines against the coronavirus became available.\par \par \uc2 The number was the largest gains the Department of Labor has recorded since November and came after jobs were lost in December and a lackluster January report when just 49,000 new jobs were added. The unemployment rate dropped slightly to 6.2%.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/coronavirus-outbreak"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Coronavirus}}}\uc2  infection rates remain at high levels and close to 520,000 people have now died of Covid-19 but states including Texas, Massachusetts and New York have all moved to roll back business closures as more vaccine becomes available.\par \par \uc2 The latest job report means the US is still close to 10m jobs short of where it was before the pandemic hit and troubling signs remain in the employment market.\par \par \uc2 Nearly all, 355,000 jobs, of February\u8217\'20\'19s gains were made in the leisure and hospitality industry as coronavirus restrictions eased and venues reopened. Other sectors, including local government, education and mining, lost jobs. The gains elsewhere were small.\par \par \uc2 The stark disparity in unemployment by race remained. The unemployment rate for white Americans was 5.6%, for Blacks it was 9.9%, and for Latinos 8.5%. The rate for teenage unemployment was 13.9%.\par \par \uc2 Read more of Dominic Rushe and Michael Sainato\u8217\'20\'19s report here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/mar/05/us-gains-jobs-states-reopen-economies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US gains 379,000 jobs as more states reopen economies}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/mar/05/us-gains-jobs-states-reopen-economies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US gains 379,000 jobs as more states reopen economies}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has rounded out his White House staff with a host of new appointments. The White House announced six additional staffers to its National Economic Council, including Columbia University professor \uc2 Tim Wu\uc2 , who coined the term \u8220\'20\'1Cnet neutrality\u8221\'20\'1D and has warned against an economy dominated by a few giant firms.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPutting this \uc2 twitter\uc2  feed on hold for now - so long!\u8221\'20\'1D Wu, a former senior enforcement counsel to New York\u8217\'20\'19s attorney general and a former Federal Trade Commission adviser, said in a post on his appointment.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Democrats have already begun talks with the White House on ways to crack down on tech companies, including making them responsible for disinformation and addressing their market power.\par \par \uc2 Several Republicans have also sought to hit back at big tech, including efforts to scrap a law known as Section 230 that shields online companies for liability over users\u8217\'20\'19 posted content.\par \par \uc2 Reuters report that in the White House statement on new staff, Biden also named 13 additions to his Domestic Policy Council and two more staffers to the White House Covid-19 response team.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has 60% job approval rating \u8211\'20\'13 poll\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press this morning are describing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as enjoying \u8220\'20\'1Can early presidential honeymoon\u8221\'20\'1D, citing a new poll which says 60% of Americans are approving of his job performance thus far.\par \par \uc2 Julie Pace and Emily Swanson write that less than two months into his presidency, Biden has made the pandemic his central focus, urging Americans to follow stringent social distancing and mask guidelines and vowing to speed up distribution of critical vaccines. He\u8217\'20\'19s also argued that until the spread of the virus is under control, the economy won\u8217\'20\'19t fully recover. That\u8217\'20\'19s led to an even higher rating for him than his rating as a president \u8211\'20\'13 fully 70% of Americans (including 44% of Republicans) \u8211\'20\'13 approve of his handling of pandemic.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a more partisan divergence on the economy though. Only 17% of Republicans think he has handling that well. That sort of attitude may be one of the reasons that the Senate Republican caucus believes it will not alienate supporters by trying to hold up the $1.9 trillion Democratic-backed Covid rescue plan which will be debated from 9am EST (1400 GMT) today.\par \par \uc2 1.17pm GMT\par \par \uc2 New York City based writer Ross Barkan has this for us to day on the fate of \uc2 Gov Andrew Cuomo\uc2 , saying {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/mar/05/andrew-cuomo-sexual-harassment-nursing-home-deaths-accused"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 karma is coming for him, with a vengeance}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  is on the front page of every New York City newspaper, a headliner of the nightly newscasts, and a constant subject of debate and intrigue on CNN and MSNBC. Corporate media abhors a vacuum. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was still president of the United States, Cuomo could count on the idiocy and scandal in the White House to distract from whatever came out of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/new-york"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s how he became a star in the first place. Trump\u8217\'20\'19s federal response to the pandemic was so plainly inept and horrendous, any questions about failure on the local level could always be deflected, especially by eager, Cuomo-worshiping Democrats. One salacious, incendiary or perplexing Trump tweet could seize a headline and give cover to all of those, like Cuomo, who were failing out of view.\par \par \uc2 Cuomo is dying by the sword he once lived by. The Cuomo scandals are perfect for cable TV because they are both legitimate and compelling. There is a natural narrative arc, a rising and falling action; these media companies helped create a myth, and now they will tear it down. The myth, in any sane world, would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/feb/18/cuomo-gate-nixonian-scandal-nursing-homes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 never have existed}}}\uc2  in the first place. But that\u8217\'20\'19s where we are.\par \par \uc2 Many politicians in New York are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nymag.com/intelligencer/article/everyone-calling-for-cuomo-resign.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 now calling}}}\uc2  for Cuomo to resign. Once so commanding, the governor now hides, refusing to appear on TV or talk to the press. His schedule is emptied out. He is hoping this all blows over. But that\u8217\'20\'19s not quite how the modern media work. If there is a void to fill, it will be filled, and the distractions of Trump are no longer there to bail Cuomo out.\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/mar/05/andrew-cuomo-sexual-harassment-nursing-home-deaths-accused"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ross Barkan \u8211\'20\'13 Andrew Cuomo was never a hero. Karma is coming for him, with a vengeance}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/mar/05/andrew-cuomo-sexual-harassment-nursing-home-deaths-accused"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Andrew Cuomo was never a hero. Karma is coming for him, with a vengeance | Ross Barkan}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Over at CNN, Stephen Collinson offers this analysis of the Senate antics over the Covid relief bill, saying it sets the scene for what we can expect from the next two years of the Biden administration. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://us.cnn.com/2021/03/05/politics/covid-relief-americans-washington/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 He writes}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 The clash over Covid relief also reflects the changing politics of the pandemic, with Biden warning the crisis is far from over and Republicans arguing that a sudden surge in vaccine distribution and a fall in new cases makes more huge government aid superfluous.\par \par \uc2 Senate debate on the American Recovery Plan will start for real on Friday after Sen Ron Johnson, who is leading a circus-like GOP bid to slow down the bill, forced Senate clerks to read the bill aloud \u8211\'20\'13 a process that began Thursday afternoon and was a 10-hour marathon. For a while, Johnson was the only senator in the chamber as luckless Senate clerks plowed through the monster text \u8212\'20\'14 reflecting the grandstanding inherent in a gambit that seemed scripted for conservative cable news shows.\par \par \uc2 By trying to highlight what he sees as massive overspending with a stalling effort, Johnson \u8211\'20\'13 who was most recently seen selling delusional claims that the red-hatted invaders of the US Capitol on January 6 were not Trump supporters \u8211\'20\'13 is focusing attention on a GOP conference apparently committed to politics of obstruction.\par \par \uc2 Though the bill is widely popular in the country, Republicans \u8211\'20\'13 who have chosen to root their hopes of a rebound in former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s radical base \u8211\'20\'13 appear convinced they will pay no price for trying to block it.\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://us.cnn.com/2021/03/05/politics/covid-relief-americans-washington/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CNN \u8211\'20\'13 Desperate Americans wait as Washington duels over Covid-19 relief bill}}}\par \par \uc2 12.28pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The national rush to vaccinate teachers in hopes of soon reopening pandemic-shuttered schools is running into one basic problem: Almost no one knows how many are getting the shots, or refusing to get them.\par \par \uc2 Casey Smith writes for the Associated Press that states and many districts have not been keeping track of school employee vaccinations, even as the federal government asks them to prioritize teachers nationwide. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  directed all state governments this week to administer at least one coronavirus vaccination to every teacher, school employee and child-care worker by the end of March\par \par \uc2 Vaccines are not required for educators to return to school buildings, but the absence of data complicates efforts to address parents\u8217\'20\'19 concerns about health risk levels and some teachers unions\u8217\'20\'19 calls for widespread vaccinations as a condition of reopening schools.\par \par \uc2 The number of school staff members receiving vaccinations and refusal rates are unclear in several large districts where teachers were prioritized, including Las Vegas, Chicago and Louisville, Kentucky. Some state agencies and districts have said privacy concerns prevent them from tracking or publishing teacher vaccination data. Others say vaccine administration sites are not tracking recipients\u8217\'20\'19 occupations and they are not in position to survey employees themselves.\par \par \uc2 No states are publicly reporting the percentage of teachers and school staff that have been vaccinated, according to a \uc2 Johns Hopkins University\uc2  analysis published Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Education leaders are missing out on an opportunity to address hesitancy about when it\u8217\'20\'19s safe to go back, said Megan Collins, co-director of the Johns Hopkins Consortium for School-Based Health Solutions. Increased transparency could influence back-to-school decision making, she said, and would likely make teachers and students more willing to return to classrooms.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re seeing a substantial disconnect. There are states not prioritizing teachers for vaccine that are fully open for in-person instruction, and others that are prioritizing teachers for vaccines, but aren\u8217\'20\'19t open at all,\u8221\'20\'1D Collins said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf states are going to use teacher vaccinations as a part of the process for safely returning to classrooms, it\u8217\'20\'19s very important then to be able to communicate that information so people know that teachers are actually getting vaccines.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 12.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Lester Black reports for us this morning as part of our {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/series/americas-dirty-divide"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 America\u8217\'20\'19s dirty divide}}}\uc2  series, which is examining the country\u8217\'20\'19s vast environmental inequalities and how climate change will make things worse:\par \par \uc2 Scott Schuyler doesn\u8217\'20\'19t need to see the Skagit River to know something is wrong. As he walks down the river\u8217\'20\'19s steep embankment, wet rock and moss under each step, he can hear the problem. \u8220\'20\'1CThe river should be singing to us right now, it should be free flowing,\u8221\'20\'1D Schuyler says as cold February rain drops silently disappear into his quilted blue jacket. \u8220\'20\'1CThe river has been stolen from us. It has been harvested for money.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Schuyler is a member of the Upper Skagit Indian tribe, which has lived along the Skagit River for at least 8,400 years and considers it to be sacred. A century ago, Seattle\u8217\'20\'19s public utility dammed the river in three spots, creating a hydroelectric complex that provides 18% of the city\u8217\'20\'19s energy. On this particular two-mile stretch near the Canadian border, the entire river has been diverted into a hydroelectric tunnel, reducing this wide riverbed to a stretch of sleepy pools.\par \par \uc2 The tribe wants {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/seattle"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Seattle}}}\uc2  to remove the Gorge Dam, the lowest of the three dams, and return the river to the section the city de-watered. The tribe says Seattle\u8217\'20\'19s century of hydroelectric work on the Skagit has contributed to a sharp drop in river\u8217\'20\'19s salmon runs, which has ripple effects across the region. \u8220\'20\'1COur people are a fishing people, a salmon people. The salmon are disappearing and it\u8217\'20\'19s hurting our people,\u8221\'20\'1D Schuyler said.\par \par \uc2 The city\u8217\'20\'19s federal license to operate the dams expires in 2025, and in order to obtain a renewal, the city is required to work with various other stakeholders \u8211\'20\'13 including the federal and state agencies and the Upper Skagit Indian Tribe \u8211\'20\'13 to study the dam\u8217\'20\'19s effect on the river.\par \par \uc2 But less than a year into the reapplication process, the city has found itself in disagreement with almost every stakeholder involved, including powerful state and federal agencies. The battle over the Skagit River and its dams is in some ways a proxy battle over the hidden expense of fighting climate change. On one side are governments and utility companies looking to hydropower as a lower-carbon source of energy; on the other are conservation-minded scientists and other stakeholders, often Indigenous people who live along targeted rivers, who decry dams as devastating to the local environments.\par \par \uc2 Read more of Lester Black\u8217\'20\'19s report for our {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/series/americas-dirty-divide"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 America\u8217\'20\'19s dirty divide}}}\uc2  series here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/05/upper-skagit-tribe-gorge-dam-seattle"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The river was stolen from us\u8217\'20\'19: a tribe\u8217\'20\'19s battle to retake the Skagit River}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/05/upper-skagit-tribe-gorge-dam-seattle"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The river was stolen from us\u8217\'20\'19: a tribe's battle to retake the Skagit River }}}\par \par \uc2 12.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Former US Attorney Joyce Vance has published {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.msnbc.com/opinion/gop-has-enlisted-supreme-court-its-anti-voting-rights-crusade-n1259670?cid=sm_npd_ms_tw_ma"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a strongly-worded op-ed at MSNBC}}}\uc2  about Republican efforts to suppress the vote. It argues that Republican lies about voter fraud are giving way to naked grasping for power. It opens:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re living in a time where one political party openly believes it\u8217\'20\'19s more important to win elections than it is to let Americans choose their own representatives in free and fair elections. And whether they\u8217\'20\'19re going to get away with it is shaping up to be one of the most important issues the country faces in the post-Trump presidency era.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court isn\u8217\'20\'19t a venue where you typically expect to hear the quiet part said out loud. But that was what happened Tuesday, when an attorney for the Arizona Republican Party, Michael Carvin, advised the court that provisions that made it easier for eligible Americans to vote put \u8220\'20\'1Cus at a competitive disadvantage relative to Democrats.\u8221\'20\'1D He was implicitly characterizing laws that make voting more difficult for likely Democratic voters, often people of color, as the difference between winning and losing elections.\par \par \uc2 Historically, restrictive voting measures have been justified as necessary to keep a shadowy group of people who are allegedly intent on casting fraudulent ballots from stealing elections. But those people never seem to materialize.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s clear that Republican operatives and legislatures have adopted voter suppression through restrictive legislation as a political strategy. Now that a lawyer has confirmed before the Supreme Court that it\u8217\'20\'19s really just about winning elections, what\u8217\'20\'19s a constitutional republic to do?\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.msnbc.com/opinion/gop-has-enlisted-supreme-court-its-anti-voting-rights-crusade-n1259670?cid=sm_npd_ms_tw_ma"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 MSNBC \u8211\'20\'13 Joyce Vance \u8211\'20\'13 The GOP has enlisted the Supreme Court in its anti-voting rights crusade}}}\par \par \uc2 11.44am GMT\par \par \uc2 Eric Sweeney {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/05/arkansas-black-sororities-fraternities-covid-vaccines"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reports for us this morning}}}\uc2  on the hundreds of people who have gotten Covid vaccine shots thanks to a partnership between the Arkansas health department and historically Black social groups:\par \par \uc2 Soon after Arkansas began allowing people over 70 to receive the Covid-19 vaccine in January, Wanda King heard from her aunt and cousin, who fell in that age group, that they were struggling to get their shots.\par \par \uc2 So, she turned to her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aka1908.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Alpha Kappa Alpha sorority}}}\uc2  sister {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.linkedin.com/in/michelle-r-smith-a9a8a786"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Michelle Smith}}}\uc2 , who is the director of the office of health equity and HIV elimination at the Arkansas department of health. Smith helped King locate a pharmacist in the town of Brinkley, 12 miles away, who was willing to join them in hosting a clinic in Cotton Plant in late February that would offer 180 vaccination slots.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI immediately got on the phone and started spreading the word, putting it on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , and asking people to help me spread the word,\u8221\'20\'1D King says. She recruited other sorority sisters to help coordinate sign-ups, purchase snacks, masks and hand sanitizer, and greet people on the day of the event.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnything we can do to roll out the red carpet for these individuals, that\u8217\'20\'19s what we want to do,\u8221\'20\'1D she adds.\par \par \uc2 The clinic in Cotton Plant is part of a broader effort by the state health department and Arkansas chapters of historically Black sororities and fraternities, known as the Divine Nine, working together to get Black Arkansans vaccinated. Through the partnership, hundreds have gotten their shots so far.\par \par \uc2 Public service is one of the key tenets of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.naacpconnect.org/blog/entry/from-the-burning-sands-of-the-divine-nine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Black Greek Letter Organizations}}}\uc2 , formerly known as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nphchq.com/millennium1/about/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Pan-Hellenic Council}}}\uc2 . Membership in these organizations is lifelong, and they have been involved with health initiatives for more than 100 years. Smith says the Covid -19 vaccination effort is an extension of that.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you want inroads into the Black community, you start with the church, and then the next step is the fraternities and sororities,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are trusted leaders in the community, so if we want to get information out to a larger swath of people, we go to these groups.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read more of Erica Sweeney\u8217\'20\'19s report here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/05/arkansas-black-sororities-fraternities-covid-vaccines"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The sororities and fraternities helping Black Americans get vaccinated}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/05/arkansas-black-sororities-fraternities-covid-vaccines"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The sororities and fraternities helping Black Americans get vaccinated}}}\par \par \uc2 11.41am GMT\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s been quite the to-and-fro between the White House and \uc2 Texas Gov Greg Abbott\uc2  over mask mandates the last days. Kim Chandler at Associated Press writes that another governor, Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2  has resisted political pressure and angry constituents over her state\u8217\'20\'19s mask order.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMaybe they don\u8217\'20\'19t have access to the same information I have. We want to be abundantly clear and abundantly safe before we drop the mask mandate,\u8221\'20\'1D Ivey said when asked about fellow Republicans including the Alabama Senate and the lieutenant governor who urged her to end the order.\par \par \uc2 Ivey issued Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s mask order in July and announced yesterday that she would extend it five more weeks until 9 April.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to get past Easter and hopefully allow more Alabamians to get their first shot before we take a step some other states have taken to remove the mask order altogether and lift other restrictions. Folks, we are not there yet, but goodness knows we\u8217\'20\'19re getting closer,\u8221\'20\'1D Ivey said at a Thursday news conference.\par \par \uc2 Ivey\u8217\'20\'19s announcement came days after Mississippi and Texas dropped their mandates, decisions President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  called \u8220\'20\'1CNeanderthal thinking.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s governor took issue with the criticism. \u8220\'20\'1CMississippians don\u8217\'20\'19t need handlers. As numbers drop, they can assess their choices and listen to experts. I guess I just think we should trust Americans, not insult them,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Gov Tate Reeves\uc2  responded on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dr Michael Saag, an infectious disease specialist who contracted Covid-19 early in the pandemic and now treats patients with the illness, said Ivey deserves credit for standing up to calls to lift the order from fellow Republicans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think it was a bold step forward considering the pressure she was under,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. But rather than setting a firm deadline for the requirement to expire, Saag said, it would be better to see where both caseloads and vaccinations totals are next month and then make a decision.\par \par \uc2 Ivey made a tongue-in-cheek quip about the heaping doses of criticism she has received from some over masks. \u8220\'20\'1CY\u8217\'20\'19all, I\u8217\'20\'19m not trying to be Governor Mee-Maw as some on social media have called me. I\u8217\'20\'19m just trying to urge you to use the common sense the good Lord gave each of us to be smart and considerate of others,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 11.15am GMT\par \par \uc2 Senate to debate as Republicans attempt to derail $1.9tn Covid relief bill\par \par \uc2 Susan Cornwell and Makini Brice write for Reuters today that the Senate debate will be \u8220\'20\'1Ccontentious\u8221\'20\'1D today. That feels like an understatement.\par \par \uc2 They note that the Senate is expected to debate the bill three hours, before considering a multitude of amendments, which could require a marathon voting session, before taking a vote on final passage in a process that could extend into the weekend. Republicans are expected to use procedural maneuvers to slow the process, as demonstrated by Sen Ron Johnson of Wisconsin insisting on the bill being read in full yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The relief legislation includes funding for vaccines and medical supplies, extends jobless assistance and provides a new round of emergency financial aid to households, small businesses and state and local governments. Opinion polls indicate broad public support.\par \par \uc2 If the Senate approves the bill, it will have to be sent back to the Democratic-controlled House of Representatives for final passage. Democrats hope Biden can sign the bill into law before 14 March, some of the current benefits run out.\par \par \uc2 With no votes to spare, Senate Democrats have tweaked the measure to ensure all 50 of their members would support it. Those changes would steer more aid to smaller US states and add money for infrastructure projects, among other adjustments.\par \par \uc2 But efforts by some senators to alter temporary federal unemployment benefits failed. The Senate bill keeps the House plan for $400 per-week payments through 29 August. It was unclear whether any senators would try to change that figure, possibly to $300, during the amendment process in coming days.\par \par \uc2 In the Senate, bills usually require the support of 60 senators. But the coronavirus relief bill is being advanced under a legislative procedure known as reconciliation that allows passage with a simple majority vote.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.17pm GMT\par \par \uc2 11.04am GMT\par \par \uc2 A handful of rightwing \u8220\'20\'1Csuper-spreaders\u8221\'20\'1D on social media were responsible for the bulk of election misinformation in the run-up to the Capitol attack, according to a new study that also sheds light on the staggering reach of falsehoods pushed by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A report from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.eipartnership.net/about"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Election Integrity Partnership}}}\uc2  (EIP), a group that includes Stanford and the \uc2 University of Washington\uc2 , analyzed social media platforms including \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , and TikTok during several months before and after the 2020 elections.\par \par \uc2 It found that \u8220\'20\'1Csuper-spreaders\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 responsible for the most frequent and most impactful misinformation campaigns \u8211\'20\'13 included Trump and his two elder sons, as well as other members of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2  and the rightwing media.\par \par \uc2 The study\u8217\'20\'19s authors and other researchers say the findings underscore the need to disable such accounts to stop the spread of misinformation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf there is a limit to how much content moderators can tackle, have them focus on reducing harm by eliminating the most effective spreaders of misinformation,\u8221\'20\'1D said said Lisa Fazio, an assistant professor at Vanderbilt University who studies the psychology of fake news but was not involved EIP report. \u8220\'20\'1CRather than trying to enforce the rules equally across all users, focus enforcement on the most powerful accounts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The report analyzed social media posts featuring words like \u8220\'20\'1Celection\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cvoting\u8221\'20\'1D to track key misinformation narratives related to the the 2020 election, including claims of mail carriers throwing away ballots, legitimate ballots strategically not being counted, and other false or unproven stories.\par \par \uc2 The report studied how these narratives developed and the effect they had. It found during this time period, popular rightwing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/twitter"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Twitter}}}\uc2  accounts \u8220\'20\'1Ctransformed one-off stories, sometimes based on honest voter concerns or genuine misunderstandings, into cohesive narratives of systemic election fraud\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, the \u8220\'20\'1Cfalse claims and narratives coalesced into the meta-narrative of a \u8216\'20\'18stolen election\u8217\'20\'19, which later propelled the January 6 insurrection\u8221\'20\'1D, the report said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe 2020 election demonstrated that actors \u8211\'20\'13 both foreign and domestic \u8211\'20\'13 remain committed to weaponizing viral false and misleading narratives to undermine confidence in the US electoral system and erode Americans\u8217\'20\'19 faith in our democracy,\u8221\'20\'1D the authors concluded.\par \par \uc2 Read more of Kari Paul\u8217\'20\'19s report here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/05/election-misinformation-trump-rightwing-super-spreader-study"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A few rightwing \u8216\'20\'18super-spreaders\u8217\'20\'19 fueled bulk of election falsehoods, study says}}}\par \par \uc2 10.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 If you fancy something to listen to today, can I recommend our Politics Weekly Extra podcast?\par \par \uc2 This week guest host Rafael Behr puts some epoch-defining questions to the former US ambassador to Nato Nicholas Burns. How does the new president convince the Europeans that America is reliable? How does Washington begin to engage with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Russia? Does \u8216\'20\'18the west\u8217\'20\'19 exist any more?\par \par \uc2 10.45am GMT\par \par \uc2 If you aren\u8217\'20\'19t clear why the Senate spent a lot of yesterday listening to the entire text of the $1.9 trillion Covid rescue plan being read out, rather than debating it, then Philip Bump has you covered at the Washington Post. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2021/03/04/while-senate-reads-coronavirus-relief-bill-nearly-1400-americans-may-die-virus/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 He writes}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 After passing the House, the $1.9 trillion bill is awaiting a vote in the Senate. But that won\u8217\'20\'19t happen for a while yet, not because there aren\u8217\'20\'19t the votes to pass it but, instead, because Sen Ron Johnson decided to force the chamber to read the 628-page bill in its entirety. The effect isn\u8217\'20\'19t to change the outcome. Instead, it\u8217\'20\'19s to delay the inevitable.\par \par \uc2 Normally, the Senate or House dispenses with the required reading of legislation. For those looking to throw up any possible roadblock to a bill\u8217\'20\'19s passage, though, forcing the bill to be read (which can be done at the request of any member) is an effective tool.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s meant to be a nuisance. But it carries an additional weight this time. At this moment, on this issue, time can be measured in human lives. On average, nearly 2,000 people a day are dying from covid-19, the disease caused by the virus. That\u8217\'20\'19s a death about once every 44 seconds.\par \par \uc2 I just objected to skipping past the reading of the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 628-page bill that was just introduced minutes ago.\par \par \uc2 If they\u8217\'20\'19re going to add nearly $2T to the national debt at least we should know what\u8217\'20\'19s in the bill.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Senator Ron Johnson (@SenRonJohnson) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenRonJohnson/status/1367576129557712896?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 4, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Republican Senator Ron Johnson is delaying:\par \par \uc2 Direct payments\par \par \uc2 Billions for schools and vaccine distribution\par \par \uc2 Extended UI\par \par \uc2 Huge benefits for families\par \par \uc2 Housing assistance\par \par \uc2 And much more\par \par \uc2 Why? Sen. Johnson says Americans need ZERO additional help from the COVID pandemic. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/HOif1nK2M6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/HOif1nK2M6}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Senate Democrats (@SenateDems) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenateDems/status/1367679927173197826?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 5, 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Around 80 Americans would have died from Covid for each hour the Senate spent reading the bill yesterday.\par \par \uc2 10.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 Welcome to our US politics live blog for Friday, after a late night in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 * The Senate will reconvene at 9am EST (1400 GMT) to debate the $1.9 trillion Covid relief bill. It was read in full yesterday, with the process not finishing until the early hours.\par \par \uc2 * Republicans wanted it read in full because they argued that a lot of the measures don\u8217\'20\'19t directly apply to Covid. Senate Democrats {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenateDems/status/1367670464068390913"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 thanked}}}\uc2  Republicans for the move, saying it showed to the American people exactly what help their opposition was trying to delay.\par \par \uc2 * Republicans are so determined to delay progress that Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  had to come to the chamber yesterday to break a Senate tie to even begin the debate .\par \par \uc2 * Senate majority leader Chuck Schumer pledged that the chamber would stay in session to pass the bill this weekend, \u8220\'20\'1Cno matter how long it takes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * The White House defended President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s criticism of Republican governors of Texas and Mississippi, after the president called their decisions to end Covid mask mandates \u8220\'20\'1CNeanderthal thinking\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * The US \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  has requested a two-month extension to the national guard\u8217\'20\'19s mission at the Capitol, though \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  downplayed the security threats at the Capitol which had caused the House to rearrange their votes this week.\par \par \uc2 * A Turkish court trying 26 Saudi nationals in absentia for the murder of Jamal Khashoggi has refused to admit as evidence a recent US intelligence report implicating the kingdom\u8217\'20\'19s crown prince, \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * Biden will keep pushing the Covid relief agenda today, as he holds a roundtable on his American Rescue Plan at 3.15pm ET today. He\u8217\'20\'19ll also receive the president\u8217\'20\'19s daily brief, and lunch with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s media briefing today is at 12.30, and the Covid response team will face the press earlier, at 11am.\par \par \uc2 2021-03-05T10:33:56Z2021-03-06T01:22:14Z{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://media.guim.co.uk/062e3ad6abf97a8d510462f58c6fa2e7e4c1cd09/0_150_4500_2700/500.jpgThe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://media.guim.co.uk/062e3ad6abf97a8d510462f58c6fa2e7e4c1cd09/0_150_4500_2700/500.jpgThe}}}\uc2  Capitol is seen from the Russell Senate Office building on 5 March.Joe Biden participates in a roundtable discussion on his $1.9tn coronavirus aid bill.Bernie Sanders prepares to attend a confirmation hearing on Capitol Hill.Scott Schuyler stands in front of the Gorge Dam, which diverts the entire Skagit River into a hydroelectric tunnel.Alabama Governor \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2  receiving a Covid vaccine dose earlier in the year.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020210305eh35001jn\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Date\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/2021 to 06/10/2026\cell